window.statoblastDocsSearch = [{"fragment":"","heading":"","keywords":["overview","lifecycle","market","components"],"path":"explanation/system-overview.html","sectionTitle":"Start here","summary":"A five-minute introduction to Zoltar, Statoblast, Trading, and OpenOracle.","text":"Start here Zoltar and Statoblast are Ethereum protocols for prediction markets, designed to make outcome manipulation costly.","title":"System overview","topic":"Introduction","weight":1},{"fragment":"problem","heading":"The problem","keywords":[],"path":"explanation/system-overview.html","sectionTitle":"Start here","summary":"A five-minute introduction to Zoltar, Statoblast, Trading, and OpenOracle.","text":"A prediction market lets people trade on a question such as “Will the fictional Aurora spacecraft launch before 31 December 2030?” Its shares are worth something only after someone decides what actually happened. That decision is the weak point: whoever controls it controls the money. Zoltar and Statoblast make the decision expensive to corrupt in two ways. First, anyone who reports an outcome must stake REP , the protocol’s reputation token, behind it. A report can win when it has the strongest backing at settlement. Reporters whose outcome loses forfeit their stake. Whether that pressure keeps reports honest depends on the participation assumptions . Second, when reporters cannot agree, the universe , the ledger that holds REP balances and the markets that use them, can fork into one child per possible answer. Even a dishonest REP majority cannot stop others from moving REP to a child that records the honest answer. See the limits of a fork .","title":"System overview","topic":"Introduction","weight":0},{"fragment":"vocabulary","heading":"How markets are backed","keywords":[],"path":"explanation/system-overview.html","sectionTitle":"Start here","summary":"A five-minute introduction to Zoltar, Statoblast, Trading, and OpenOracle.","text":"Security pool A shared capital and settlement system that backs admitted questions. Each owner keeps an individual REP account and commitment; each question keeps separate outcome shares and accounting. A security pool can hold one fixed question or many. Question ledger The question contract inside a security pool. It exposes outcome shares, reporting and redemption; security pool modules hold REP and collateral.","title":"System overview","topic":"Introduction","weight":0},{"fragment":"components","heading":"Four components","keywords":[],"path":"explanation/system-overview.html","sectionTitle":"Start here","summary":"A five-minute introduction to Zoltar, Statoblast, Trading, and OpenOracle.","text":"Zoltar The question and REP registry. It records questions, universes, REP, and forks. It never judges which answer is true. Statoblast core The market. It deploys a question ledger for one question and universe in one security pool, mints and redeems shares, settles ordinary disputes locally, and moves markets into child universes after a fork. Trading An exchange for Statoblast shares. It is one of possibly many; it neither creates nor resolves markets. OpenOracle A price feed. It supplies a contestable REP price in the security pool’s collateral token so the protocol can check whether REP still covers its obligations. It has no say in market outcomes.","title":"System overview","topic":"Introduction","weight":0},{"fragment":"lifecycle","heading":"One market, start to finish","keywords":[],"path":"explanation/system-overview.html","sectionTitle":"Start here","summary":"A five-minute introduction to Zoltar, Statoblast, Trading, and OpenOracle.","text":"Someone registers the Aurora question in Zoltar and admits it to a security pool under that security pool’s admission policy. Owners deposit REP and authorize shared commitments. Traders purchase complete sets through the security pool: one Yes, one No and one Invalid share at the question’s current backing rate. The collateral token goes to central settlement custody, with a separate balance for the question. Traders can exchange those shares; reporting begins after the question ends. Register question Admit question to security pool Deposit REP into the security pool Mint complete sets Trade shares Question ends First REP deposit starts the Escalation Game Local resolution or Zoltar fork Choose branches and materialize child claims Repair missing collateral through a Collateral Repair Auction Redeem Market lifecycle. Local resolution skips migration and collateral repair. Reporters stake REP behind an answer in the question ledger's Escalation Game , a dispute round whose deadline moves later as the contested amount grows. When the deadline finally passes, the market resolves if exactly one answer holds more REP than every other, and winning shares redeem the question’s backing from central settlement custody. Usually that is the end of the story. If two Aurora answers reach the non-decision threshold , the universe can split into Invalid, Yes, and No children. Each child receives only the collateral allocated to it. Forks and migration explains branch choices, claim preparation, and repair. Follow a market through Statoblast · Understand branching universes · Look up a term","title":"System overview","topic":"Introduction","weight":0},{"fragment":"","heading":"","keywords":["contracts","architecture","calls","assets"],"path":"explanation/contract-architecture.html","sectionTitle":"Start here","summary":"Which Zoltar and Statoblast contracts call each other, deploy each other, and move assets.","text":"Start here Users call question ledgers , the Statoblast contracts that expose each market's shares and reporting. Each question belongs to a security pool , which holds cash in its central settlement engine. Question ledgers rely on Zoltar for universes and REP , on a shared price feed for REP prices in the security pool’s collateral token, and on the QuestionForker after a fork. Related reading: the generated transaction reference for exact caller and prerequisite rules, How Statoblast works for lifecycle context, and the invariant catalog for cross-contract safety properties.","title":"Contract architecture","topic":"Architecture","weight":1},{"fragment":"product-layers","heading":"Product layers","keywords":[],"path":"explanation/contract-architecture.html","sectionTitle":"Start here","summary":"Which Zoltar and Statoblast contracts call each other, deploy each other, and move assets.","text":"Zoltar — questions, universes, REP, forks Statoblast core — security pools, question ledgers, collateral, shares, resolution, migration Trading — one implementation of swaps and liquidity around shares OpenOracle — contestable REP prices in the security pool’s collateral token augurScan and bots — indexing, evidence, and permissionless operations Product-layer map. These products share protocol data but retain separate responsibilities and deployment boundaries.","title":"Contract architecture","topic":"Architecture","weight":0},{"fragment":"conceptual-model","heading":"What each contract owns","keywords":[],"path":"explanation/contract-architecture.html","sectionTitle":"Start here","summary":"Which Zoltar and Statoblast contracts call each other, deploy each other, and move assets.","text":"ZoltarQuestionData stores questions; Zoltar manages universes, forks and universe-specific REP. SecurityPool registers admitted questions and coordinates shared operations. A fixed single-question security pool uses this same architecture. SecurityPoolFactory establishes canonical security pools. A registered question ledger must belong to one of those pools. The capital ledger records each owner's REP principal, commitment, health target and earned income. Its commitment index supports bounded health and retirement operations. The settlement engine and book track question demand, collateral, fee streams, minimum backing floors and recovery assets. A QuestionLedger is a question adapter. Its ShareToken records outcome balances across that question's universe lineage; its EscalationGame resolves reports. Reporter REP has separate custody. The shared OpenOracle feed accepts independently funded price candidates. Question ledgers read that shared evidence directly. The security pool fork coordinator records branch choices and prepares shared branch accounting. The uniform auction sells freshly unchosen provider REP for security pool collateral when a branch needs repair.","title":"Contract architecture","topic":"Architecture","weight":0},{"fragment":"overview","heading":"Purchase and resolution","keywords":[],"path":"explanation/contract-architecture.html","sectionTitle":"Start here","summary":"Which Zoltar and Statoblast contracts call each other, deploy each other, and move assets.","text":"A trader purchases complete sets through the security pool. Admission, fresh price, healthy capacity, fees and the question's backing floor are checked atomically. The question adapter mints distinct outcome shares. After final resolution, winning claims redeem against their question's settlement accounting. Returning complete sets earlier reduces demand and can free commitment for retirement.","title":"Contract architecture","topic":"Architecture","weight":0},{"fragment":"edges","heading":"Fork boundaries","keywords":[],"path":"explanation/contract-architecture.html","sectionTitle":"Start here","summary":"Which Zoltar and Statoblast contracts call each other, deploy each other, and move assets.","text":"Owners choose once per security pool and universe. Shared roots and branch balances avoid a migration transaction per owner per question. A question adapter and reporter continuation are materialized only when used. Token holders still migrate their own claims through each intervening universe. See the fork lifecycle , uniform auctions , and the contract inventory for module interfaces and limits.","title":"Contract architecture","topic":"Architecture","weight":0},{"fragment":"","heading":"","keywords":["glossary","terms","REP","universe","underwriting commitment","haircut","trading"],"path":"reference/glossary.html","sectionTitle":"Start here","summary":"Canonical definitions for protocol assets, states, and mechanisms.","text":"Reference Canonical, plain-language definitions for terms used throughout the documentation.","title":"Protocol glossary","topic":"Introduction","weight":1},{"fragment":"universes-and-rep","heading":"Universes and REP","keywords":[],"path":"reference/glossary.html","sectionTitle":"Start here","summary":"Canonical definitions for protocol assets, states, and mechanisms.","text":"Universe A Zoltar ledger and identity for universe-specific REP and application state. Universe 0 is the initialized root; later universes are descendants created through forks. Child universe A deterministic descendant that represents one valid answer to its parent's fork question. In a binary-market example, a Statoblast fork on its own question creates Invalid, Yes, and No children. If an unrelated fork interrupts that market, its children instead follow the unrelated categorical or scalar fork question's valid answers. Question A global Zoltar object that identifies a proposition, its end time, and its answer options. Outcome One possible answer to a question. Statoblast binary markets use Invalid, Yes, and No. Invalid The legitimate third outcome for ambiguous, malformed, or otherwise unanswerable market wording. REP Universe-specific reputation tokens used as reporting, dispute, and security capital. Theoretical REP supply Zoltar's per-universe REP supply figure, from which it derives the fork and non-decision thresholds. Burns reduce it, and each child universe starts from its parent's supply after the fork threshold burn plus the fork initiator's migration balance. Fork threshold The amount of parent-universe REP that a fork initiator supplies and burns to begin a fork, derived by getForkThresholdAttoRep from that universe's theoretical REP supply. See migration accounting . Fork haircut The part of the fork threshold that Zoltar burns without crediting to the initiator's migration balance: ⌊fork threshold ÷ forkBurnDivisor ⌋, at most 20%. Contract comments call it the burn haircut. REP added to a migration balance after the fork starts is credited in full. Migration balance A parent-universe credit ( migrationRepBalanceAttoRep ) created when REP is burned or committed for a fork. The same credited amount can be minted independently in each valid child, while per-child accounting prevents an account from consuming more than that amount in any one child. Child REP REP minted for one child universe. Its ledger and economic value are separate from parent and sibling REP.","title":"Protocol glossary","topic":"Introduction","weight":0},{"fragment":"security-pools","heading":"Security pools, question ledgers, shares, and vaults","keywords":[],"path":"reference/glossary.html","sectionTitle":"Start here","summary":"Canonical definitions for protocol assets, states, and mechanisms.","text":"Security pool A shared REP capital ledger and ERC-20 settlement system backing questions admitted by its configured policy. Owners retain individual commitments and health settings; questions have separate outcome shares and settlement balances. A security pool with one fixed question uses the same architecture as a security pool admitting many questions. Question ledger The security-pool-bound contract for one question in one universe. It exposes shares, local reporting and redemption; its security pool modules hold capital and settlement cash. Lineage An origin question ledger and every child question ledger forked from it. Question ledgers in one lineage share an origin ID and one outcome-share token contract. Origin question ledger A question ledger first registered in a security pool, before any child question ledger is prepared for its lineage. Settlement collateral The question’s backing in the security pool settlement book. Complete sets and winning shares redeem their proportional value; holding fees reduce backing only above the configured floor. Open interest The exposure created by outstanding question claims. Security pool settlement demand aggregates the obligations that healthy underwriting commitments must support. Complete set One matching unit each of Invalid, Yes, and No shares, minted against security pool collateral and redeemable together while the question ledger is operational. Outcome share An ERC-1155 token representing one outcome claim in one universe-specific question ledger. Vault An owner’s capital account: its own REP principal, commitment and fee claims in the shared capital ledger. An auction winner may control a separate vault contract that owns such an account. Underwriting commitment An owner’s authorized amount of collateral obligations. Healthy commitments contribute to shared security pool capacity and earn fees; losses from a distressed position stay with that owner. Security multiplier The security pool’s fixed protocol backing multiplier, expressed in basis points. Owners can select an additional personal health target; the two settings have different purposes. Vault health Whether an owner’s total free and pledged principal covers its commitment at the protocol backing requirement and liquidation REP price. See liquidations . Health target An owner-selected target of at least 100% relative to the protocol backing requirement. It constrains commitment changes and surplus withdrawals; falling below that personal buffer alone does not trigger liquidation. Minting capacity The healthy security pool commitment available to support additional settlement demand. Purchases also require a current funded price and an eligible question. Utilization The fraction of shared capacity already supporting settlement demand. The security pool’s configured annual holding-fee rate does not vary with utilization. Fee reserve Fees retained for underwriters rather than for outcome-share redemption. Accumulators credit eligible owners without visiting every question when an owner claims. Holding fee The configured linear charge on a question’s fee principal while its stream is eligible to run. It reduces share backing over time but stops before crossing the minimum backing floor. See fees .","title":"Protocol glossary","topic":"Introduction","weight":0},{"fragment":"escalation","heading":"Escalation Game","keywords":[],"path":"reference/glossary.html","sectionTitle":"Start here","summary":"Canonical definitions for protocol assets, states, and mechanisms.","text":"Escalation Game The local Statoblast dispute mechanism in which REP-backed balances accumulate behind Invalid, Yes, and No. Start bond The minimum opening Escalation Game deposit, based on the larger of 1 REP and theoretical REP supply at question ledger deployment divided by 10 7 . See Escalation Game deposit rules for exceptions and continuations. Binding capital The median of the three outcome balances. See Escalation Game deadlines for its role in finality. Required support The rising REP level that closes the Escalation Game when it exceeds binding capital. See Escalation Game deadlines for the clock and totalCostAttoRep for the contract value. Non-decision threshold The balance that two outcomes must each reach for the Escalation Game to enter non-decision: half of the universe's fork threshold, rounded up, read when the game opens and fixed for that game and any game carried from it. Non-decision The Escalation Game state in which two outcomes have each reached the non-decision threshold ( NonDecisionState.Local ). The game picks no winner, and anyone can fork the universe on the question ledger's own question. Escalation reward REP a winning Escalation Game deposit receives on top of its returned principal. It is a share of the runner-up’s forfeited stake, not paid by the protocol: three fifths of binding capital, split across winning deposits up to the reward-eligible cap. See the EscalationGame reference . Reward-eligible cap One and a half times binding capital. Only the portion of winning Escalation Game deposits up to this cap earns an escalation reward ; deposits beyond it return principal only. See the EscalationGame reference . Escalation haircut Two fifths of binding capital, burned at Escalation Game settlement instead of being paid to winners as an escalation reward, unless the question ledger's own fork already paid it through the fork haircut. The contracts call it the winner haircut ( burnEscalationWinnerHaircut ).","title":"Protocol glossary","topic":"Introduction","weight":0},{"fragment":"forks-and-repair","heading":"Forks and repair","keywords":[],"path":"reference/glossary.html","sectionTitle":"Start here","summary":"Canonical definitions for protocol assets, states, and mechanisms.","text":"Question forker The shared QuestionForker contract that prepares question children and their inherited reporting state after security pool branch migration. Branch-choice cutoff The deadline for security pool owners to record their branch choice, eight weeks after the parent universe forks. Native REP migration can continue after this deadline. Carried game A child question’s continuation of an unresolved parent Escalation Game. Frozen history authenticates inherited claims; new wallet REP deposits use the continuation’s current rules. Fork continuation The preparation needed before a child question resumes an inherited unresolved reporting game. Purchases wait until the question and shared branch are ready. Collateral Repair Auction A uniform-price auction of a security pool branch’s saleable REP for the selected collateral to repair branch claims. Winning bids automatically own separately accounted capital positions. It does not select truth.","title":"Protocol glossary","topic":"Introduction","weight":0},{"fragment":"liquidation","heading":"Liquidation","keywords":[],"path":"reference/glossary.html","sectionTitle":"Start here","summary":"Canonical definitions for protocol assets, states, and mechanisms.","text":"Target vault The unhealthy vault whose underwriting commitment a liquidation reduces. Receiver vault The vault that receives transferred commitment and a takeover reward taken from the target vault’s pledged REP, supplies the remaining backing and earns future fees. It must remain healthy on its whole resulting commitment; the takeover reward remains pledged principal. Takeover reward REP a receiver vault gains by taking over distressed commitment. It is taken from the target vault ’s pledged REP, not paid by the protocol, and grows linearly from zero to all of that REP over one hour from the start of liquidation; a partial takeover receives a proportional share. Liquidation penalty Pledged REP the target vault loses to the receiver as the takeover reward . Operator An account submitting permissionless maintenance transactions, such as a funded takeover of distressed commitment. A takeover receiver must authorize its own capital and satisfy the transaction bounds.","title":"Protocol glossary","topic":"Introduction","weight":0},{"fragment":"prices","heading":"Prices","keywords":[],"path":"reference/glossary.html","sectionTitle":"Start here","summary":"Canonical definitions for protocol assets, states, and mechanisms.","text":"OpenOracle An economically contestable price-reporting contract used by Statoblast for fresh REP-per-collateral-token prices, not market outcomes. Shared price feed The canonical REP/collateral price source for every question in one security pool and universe. Sponsors fund OpenOracle reports directly through it; accepted reports may execute their sponsor’s bounded capital operations.","title":"Protocol glossary","topic":"Introduction","weight":0},{"fragment":"trading","heading":"Trading","keywords":[],"path":"reference/glossary.html","sectionTitle":"Start here","summary":"Canonical definitions for protocol assets, states, and mechanisms.","text":"Trading pool The constant-product pair ( TwoWayConstantProductPair , called the pair in the Trading reference) that trades one question ledger's Yes and No shares against each other. The Trading factory creates at most one per question ledger. Trading router The TwoWayConstantProductRouter contract that turns collateral into a position and a position back into collateral by combining question ledger complete-set calls with trading-pool swaps. WETH-backed security pools also support native-ETH convenience paths. Long shares The Yes or No shares a trader holds on the side they expect to win. Entering through the trading router also leaves the trader one Invalid share for each complete set it minted, fewer than the long shares received. Insured exit A trading-router exit that swaps some long shares for the opposite outcome, then redeems them with the trader's Invalid shares as complete sets for collateral.","title":"Protocol glossary","topic":"Introduction","weight":0},{"fragment":"","heading":"","keywords":["ABI","transactions","callers","events"],"path":"reference/contracts.html","sectionTitle":"Reference","summary":"Index of the per-contract interaction reference.","text":"Open a contract to see its read surface, any security assumptions or trust boundary, and each state-changing entrypoint with its caller, prerequisites, effects, and primary signals. The entrypoint count is the number of state-changing function signatures a contract answers, counting each overload, each selector its fallback() routes to a delegate, and receive() .","title":"Contract interactions","topic":"Contracts","weight":1},{"fragment":"table-columns","heading":"Table columns","keywords":[],"path":"reference/contracts.html","sectionTitle":"Reference","summary":"Index of the per-contract interaction reference.","text":"Transaction : the function name with its parameter names in ABI order. Overloads appear as separate signatures. Caller : the addresses the contract accepts as msg.sender . Main prerequisites : the conditions the call requires, quoting a revert string where a caller needs it to tell failures apart. State or asset effect : what a successful call changes. Primary signals : the events a successful call emits, each with the condition under which it is emitted, including events emitted by contracts the call reaches. A row says so when a call can succeed without emitting an event. Contract Purpose State-changing entrypoints ZoltarQuestionData Creates immutable, content-addressed scalar or categorical questions and exposes their display metadata. 1 Zoltar Registers universe forks, charges the fork haircut , and mints branch-specific child REP . 6 ReputationToken Implements universe-specific ERC-20 REP, ERC-2612 permits, and ERC-3009 transfers while enforcing the supply ceiling maintained by Zoltar. 10 SecurityPoolFactory Deploys every security pool from pinned creation code and module deployers, and deploys and registers their question ledgers and shared price feeds. 7 QuestionLedger Question ledger for one shared security pool, question and universe. 21 QuestionForker Executes question forks and lazy child question preparation using shared security pool state. Child-game trust boundary 5 EscalationGame Escrows outcome REP, raises the running resolution cost, detects non-decision , and settles local or carried deposits. 16 SharedPoolPriceFeed Canonical REP/collateral reporting and price source for every question in one security pool and universe. 7 ShareToken Stores universe-aware ERC-1155 outcome shares and materializes a holder's persistent source entitlement in selected fork branches. 12","title":"Contract interactions","topic":"Contracts","weight":0},{"fragment":"","heading":"","keywords":["questions","categorical","scalar","encoding"],"path":"reference/contracts/zoltarquestiondata.html","sectionTitle":"Reference","summary":"Read surface and the createQuestion entrypoint.","text":"Creates immutable, content-addressed scalar or categorical questions and exposes their display metadata. ZoltarQuestionData source contract","title":"ZoltarQuestionData","topic":"Contracts","weight":1},{"fragment":"read-surface","heading":"Read surface","keywords":[],"path":"reference/contracts/zoltarquestiondata.html","sectionTitle":"Reference","summary":"Read surface and the createQuestion entrypoint.","text":"Use getQuestionId before submission; questionCreatedTimestamp and questions for direct lookup; getQuestionCount and getQuestions for indexed or paged discovery; and getQuestionEndDate , getOutcomeLabels , splitUint256IntoTwoWithInvalid , hasNonZeroScalarReservedBits , isMalformedAnswerOption , and getAnswerOptionName when validating or displaying answers. In the QuestionData tuple, startTime and endTime are uint48 , while numTicks is uint120 ; clients must use these exact widths because they determine the getQuestionId and createQuestion selectors. An unregistered question has no valid scalar answer: both answer getters treat everything except canonical Invalid as malformed. When a scalar range spans fewer 18-decimal units than it has ticks, a tick whose value is rounded down is named with its index, for example 0 (tick 1 of 2) , so distinct answers never share a name.","title":"ZoltarQuestionData","topic":"Contracts","weight":0},{"fragment":"interactions","heading":"State-changing interactions","keywords":[],"path":"reference/contracts/zoltarquestiondata.html","sectionTitle":"Reference","summary":"Read surface and the createQuestion entrypoint.","text":"The contract index defines the columns. Transaction Caller Main prerequisites State or asset effect Primary signals createQuestion(questionData, outcomeOptions) Anyone Question ID not already created; end time is on or after start time. Scalar questions use no labels, require display maximum greater than minimum, and positive ticks. Categorical labels use 1–64 printable ASCII characters with single interior spaces, must be unique ignoring ASCII case, and cannot use the reserved protocol names Invalid or Malformed ( Reserved protocol outcome label ). Their keccak256(abi.encode(label)) values are strictly descending. Stores the question at its deterministic content hash, records the creation timestamp, appends it to discovery order, and stores categorical labels when supplied. QuestionCreated","title":"ZoltarQuestionData","topic":"Contracts","weight":0},{"fragment":"","heading":"","keywords":["fork","universe","migration","child REP"],"path":"reference/contracts/zoltar.html","sectionTitle":"Reference","summary":"Read surface and the forkUniverse, burnRep, deployChild, and migration-balance entrypoints.","text":"Registers universe forks, charges the fork haircut , and mints branch-specific child REP . Zoltar source contract","title":"Zoltar","topic":"Contracts","weight":1},{"fragment":"read-surface","heading":"Read surface","keywords":[],"path":"reference/contracts/zoltar.html","sectionTitle":"Reference","summary":"Read surface and the forkUniverse, burnRep, deployChild, and migration-balance entrypoints.","text":"Use universes , forkThresholdDivisor , forkBurnDivisor , zoltarQuestionData , genesisReputationToken , childReputationTokenCount , getForkTime , getForkChoiceDeadline , getForkRecapitalizationDeadline , getEarliestForkTime , forkQuestionMatches , getRepToken , getForkThresholdAttoRep , getNonDecisionThresholdAttoRep , getUniverseTheoreticalSupplyAttoRep , getChildUniverseId , getDeployedChildUniverses , getMigrationRepBalanceAttoRep , and getChildMigrationRepAmountsAttoRep to reconstruct universe and migration state. The choice and recap deadlines are the native fork time plus eight and nine weeks. A child may next fork at its parent fork time plus ten weeks; origin universes have no parent cooldown, and getEarliestForkTime reverts with Unknown universe for an ID with no REP token. Late preparation never moves these timestamps. The child migration getter accepts an array of child universe IDs and returns cumulative amounts in the same order, with zero for unused IDs. Before the first genesis fork, theoretical supply follows the external REP token minus Zoltar burns; that fork freezes the child admission caps. Later external supply changes do not enlarge them. The fork threshold is the live universe theoretical supply divided by forkThresholdDivisor , rounded up, so every nonzero-supply universe has a positive fork cost. UniverseInitialized contains the genesis supply snapshot. Read the current supply from getUniverseTheoreticalSupplyAttoRep and discover children through DeployChild and ChildReputationTokenInitialized . The constructor enforces the genesis REP supply and divisor bounds. Genesis REP uses ordinary ERC-20 approvals because the configured mainnet genesis tokens, Augur V2’s REP Yes and REP No fork child tokens, do not implement ERC-2612 or ERC-3009. Mainnet genesis tokens explains their supply growth and the low REP No fork threshold.","title":"Zoltar","topic":"Contracts","weight":0},{"fragment":"security-assumptions","heading":"Security assumptions","keywords":[],"path":"reference/contracts/zoltar.html","sectionTitle":"Reference","summary":"Read surface and the forkUniverse, burnRep, deployChild, and migration-balance entrypoints.","text":"These calls depend on A15 intended question selection and A25 safe immutable parameters .","title":"Zoltar","topic":"Contracts","weight":0},{"fragment":"interactions","heading":"State-changing interactions","keywords":[],"path":"reference/contracts/zoltar.html","sectionTitle":"Reference","summary":"Read surface and the forkUniverse, burnRep, deployChild, and migration-balance entrypoints.","text":"The contract index defines the columns. Transaction Caller Main prerequisites State or asset effect Primary signals forkUniverse(universeId, questionId) Any address able to fund the current fork threshold Initialized and unforked universe; existing ended question; sufficient caller REP; a child must have reached its parent fork timestamp plus ten weeks. Genesis REP requires allowance; child REP is burned directly without allowance. Records the fork, removes threshold REP from the parent universe, and credits the caller with the threshold minus the uncredited fork haircut. The REP burn events (genesis REP Transfer to the burn address, or child REP Transfer and Burn ), then UniverseForked burnRep(universeId, amountAttoRep) Any REP holder; the caller can burn only its own balance Initialized universe; positive amount; sufficient caller REP and theoretical supply. Genesis REP requires allowance. Permanently removes REP without creating migration credit; escalation settlement uses this to burn the escalation haircut when its own fork has not already paid it. The REP burn events (genesis REP Transfer to the burn address, or child REP Transfer and Burn ), then RepBurned deployChild(universeId, outcomeIndex) Anyone Parent forked; outcome is well formed; child is not already deployed. Deploys the deterministic child REP token and initializes the child universe. TheoreticalSupplySet , ReputationTokenInitialized , DeployChild , and ChildReputationTokenInitialized addRepToMigrationBalance(universeId, amountAttoRep) Parent REP holder Universe forked; sufficient caller REP. Genesis REP requires allowance; child REP is burned directly without allowance. Burns or sinks additional parent REP and increases the caller's reusable migration balance. The REP burn events (genesis REP Transfer to the burn address, or child REP Transfer and Burn ), then MigrationRepAdded prepareAndSplitMigrationRep(universeId, amountAttoRep, outcomeIndexes, preparationAttoRep) Parent REP holder Forked universe; positive split amount; nonempty valid outcomes; sufficient parent REP for the exact preparationAttoRep supplied and sufficient migration credit for every split. Outcome order is unrestricted. Genesis REP requires allowance for preparation; child REP needs no allowance. Adds the supplied preparation amount to migration credit, then splits the requested REP into the supplied outcomes using the existing migration checks. Both steps revert together on failure. Callers compute any preparation shortfall from cumulative child migration amounts, rather than current child token holdings. The addRepToMigrationBalance signals when preparationAttoRep is positive; TheoreticalSupplySet , ReputationTokenInitialized , DeployChild , and ChildReputationTokenInitialized when children are deployed; child REP Transfer , Mint , and MigrationRepSplit for each destination splitMigrationRep(universeId, amountAttoRep, outcomeIndexes) Migration-balance holder Universe forked; positive split amount; nonempty list of well-formed outcomes; the cumulative amount per child must not exceed the caller's migration balance. Mints amountAttoRep of child REP into every selected branch, deploying missing children lazily. TheoreticalSupplySet , ReputationTokenInitialized , DeployChild , and ChildReputationTokenInitialized when children are deployed; child REP Transfer and Mint , then MigrationRepSplit , per selected branch","title":"Zoltar","topic":"Contracts","weight":0},{"fragment":"","heading":"","keywords":["REP","ERC-20","permit","supply"],"path":"reference/contracts/reputationtoken.html","sectionTitle":"Reference","summary":"Read surface and the ERC-20, ERC-2612 permit, and ERC-3009 authorization entrypoints.","text":"Implements universe-specific ERC-20 REP, ERC-2612 permits, and ERC-3009 transfers while enforcing the supply ceiling maintained by Zoltar. ReputationToken source contract","title":"ReputationToken","topic":"Contracts","weight":1},{"fragment":"read-surface","heading":"Read surface","keywords":[],"path":"reference/contracts/reputationtoken.html","sectionTitle":"Reference","summary":"Read surface and the ERC-20, ERC-2612 permit, and ERC-3009 authorization entrypoints.","text":"Use getTotalTheoreticalSupply , zoltar , universeId , and repNumber to identify the token and its supply ceiling; the standard ERC-20 reads name , symbol , decimals , totalSupply , balanceOf , and allowance for balances; and nonces , DOMAIN_SEPARATOR , and authorizationState to build and check ERC-2612 and ERC-3009 signatures.","title":"ReputationToken","topic":"Contracts","weight":0},{"fragment":"interactions","heading":"State-changing interactions","keywords":[],"path":"reference/contracts/reputationtoken.html","sectionTitle":"Reference","summary":"Read surface and the ERC-20, ERC-2612 permit, and ERC-3009 authorization entrypoints.","text":"The contract index defines the columns. Transaction Caller Main prerequisites State or asset effect Primary signals initialize(universeId, totalTheoreticalSupplyAttoRep, repNumber) Zoltar only Called once by Zoltar as part of child-universe creation; universe, REP number, and theoretical supply are nonzero; supply does not exceed 11 million REP. Atomically assigns the child universe, global display sequence, name, symbol, and theoretical-supply ceiling used to bound migration mints. TheoreticalSupplySet and ReputationTokenInitialized mint(account, valueAttoRep) Zoltar only account is nonzero; resulting ERC-20 supply does not exceed theoretical supply. Mints branch REP to an account. Mint and ERC-20 Transfer burn(account, valueAttoRep) Zoltar only account is nonzero and has sufficient REP; theoretical supply covers the burn. Burns account REP and reduces both actual and theoretical supply by the same amount. Burn and ERC-20 Transfer transfer(to, value) REP holder Destination is nonzero; caller has sufficient balance. Moves REP from the caller without changing actual or theoretical supply. Transfer approve(spender, value) Any REP account setting its own allowance Spender is nonzero. Replaces the named spender allowance without moving REP. Approval transferFrom(from, to, value) A spender with sufficient allowance from from Source and destination are nonzero; source has sufficient balance; caller has sufficient allowance, including when caller equals source. Moves REP from from ; a finite allowance decreases by value , while an infinite allowance remains unchanged. Neither allowance path emits Approval . Transfer only permit(owner, spender, value, deadline, v, r, s) Anyone presenting the owner's valid ERC-2612 signature Deadline has not passed; signature matches the current chain, token domain, owner, spender, value, and nonce. Sets the signed allowance and advances the owner's permit nonce. Approval transferWithAuthorization(from, to, value, validAfter, validBefore, nonce, v, r, s) Anyone presenting the source's valid ERC-3009 signature The strict validity window is open; the nonce is unused; signature fields and source balance are valid. Consumes the authorization nonce and transfers the exact signed amount to the signed recipient. AuthorizationUsed and Transfer receiveWithAuthorization(from, to, value, validAfter, validBefore, nonce, v, r, s) The signed recipient presenting the source's valid ERC-3009 signature Caller equals the signed recipient; the strict validity window is open; the nonce is unused; signature fields and source balance are valid. Consumes the authorization nonce and transfers the exact signed amount to the caller-bound recipient. AuthorizationUsed and Transfer cancelAuthorization(authorizer, nonce, v, r, s) Anyone presenting the authorizer's valid ERC-3009 cancellation signature The nonce is unused and the cancellation signature matches the authorizer. Marks the authorization nonce as consumed without transferring tokens. AuthorizationCanceled","title":"ReputationToken","topic":"Contracts","weight":0},{"fragment":"","heading":"","keywords":["factory","deployment","registration","CREATE2"],"path":"reference/contracts/securitypoolfactory.html","sectionTitle":"Reference","summary":"Read surface and the origin and child pool deployment entrypoints.","text":"Deploys every security pool from pinned creation code and module deployers, and deploys and registers their question ledgers and shared price feeds. No standalone deployment entry point exists. SecurityPoolFactory source contract","title":"SecurityPoolFactory","topic":"Contracts","weight":1},{"fragment":"read-surface","heading":"Read surface","keywords":[],"path":"reference/contracts/securitypoolfactory.html","sectionTitle":"Reference","summary":"Read surface and the origin and child pool deployment entrypoints.","text":"Each question derives its start bond as max(1 REP, universe theoretical REP supply / 10,000,000). questionLedgerDeploymentCount and strict questionLedgerDeploymentsRange enumerate canonical question ledgers. isSecurityPool is true only for a security pool this factory deployed, and securityPoolCreationCodeHash is the hash of the only creation code it deploys. securityPoolCount and strict securityPoolsRange enumerate, in registration order, the factory-deployed security pools that have configured their namespace; a deployed security pool is listed when its final setup step calls initializeSecurityPoolPriceFeed . getQuestionLedgerId , getQuestionLedger , getQuestionLedgerOriginId and getQuestionLedgerHasInheritedForkOutcome identify their lineage. getQuestionOriginId includes the caller security pool namespace. securityPoolOf returns the factory-deployed security pool a ledger belongs to; sharedPriceFeeds is indexed by security pool and universe. maximumReportPriorityFeeAttoEthPerGas exposes the configured feed-factory ceiling used to reject over-cap security pool creation before setup. zoltar identifies the protocol root.","title":"SecurityPoolFactory","topic":"Contracts","weight":0},{"fragment":"interactions","heading":"State-changing interactions","keywords":[],"path":"reference/contracts/securitypoolfactory.html","sectionTitle":"Reference","summary":"Read surface and the origin and child pool deployment entrypoints.","text":"The contract index defines the columns. Transaction Caller Main prerequisites State or asset effect Primary signals deploySecurityPool(creationCode, configuration) Anyone keccak256(creationCode) equals securityPoolCreationCodeHash ( Security pool creation code is not canonical ); configuration is exactly 13 words ( Security pool configuration length invalid ). The security pool constructor’s own checks apply and revert the deployment, including that every pinned module deployer holds code. Deploys a SecurityPool whose leading constructor arguments are this factory and its nine pinned module deployers, records it in isSecurityPool , and returns its address. The deployment uses CREATE2 with a salt derived from the caller and the number of security pools that caller has deployed, so the address depends on its creator and no other deployment can occupy it. configuration is the ABI encoding of the origin configuration, question horizon, admission policy and economics, in that order: the only values the creator chooses. SecurityPoolDeployed with the security pool and the caller. Setup continues through the security pool’s prepareNext . initializeSecurityPoolPriceFeed(universeId, priorityFee) A security pool this factory deployed, within its own caller-address namespace The universe has a REP token and has not forked. A repeated call must match the first call’s REP token and priority fee. The feed starts without an accepted price. The caller satisfies isSecurityPool ( Security pool is not canonical ), so no other contract can open a namespace. This call neither registers a question nor allocates REP. The first call creates the caller namespace’s oracle configuration from the universe’s REP token and the priority fee, and appends the caller to the security pool registry. Each call creates the namespace’s feed for that universe or returns the existing one. Security pool setup calls this in its last stage, before any question is admitted. Returns the canonical feed address. The first setup emits SecurityPoolRegistered . Each new feed emits SharedPriceFeedRegistered with the security pool, universe and feed address. initializeSecurityPoolPriceFeedForUniverse(universeId) A security pool, within its own caller-address namespace The caller has configured its namespace; the caller’s security pool clock recognizes the target universe; the target REP token exists. Creates or retrieves the caller namespace’s feed for an already registered child universe, using the oracle configuration from the namespace’s first initializeSecurityPoolPriceFeed call. No question needs to be deployed first. Returns the canonical feed address. A newly deployed feed emits SharedPriceFeedRegistered . prepareSecurityPoolPriceFeed(source, universeId) Anyone Canonical source question ledger registered to a security pool; the security pool clock recognizes the target; the target REP token exists. Creates or retrieves a target universe feed in the canonical source question ledger’s security pool namespace without moving provider capital. Returns the canonical feed address. A newly deployed feed emits SharedPriceFeedRegistered . deploySecurityPoolQuestionLedger(universeId, questionId, statoblastSecurityMultiplierBps, initialReportPriorityFeeAttoEthPerGas) A security pool with a configured namespace, within its own caller-address namespace The caller has configured its namespace ( Security pool configuration missing ), which only a factory-deployed security pool can do. The ordinary origin deployment checks apply; no lineage for this question/configuration has been claimed in the caller namespace. The namespace is derived from msg.sender and cannot be supplied on behalf of another security pool. Creates a separate origin ledger and share token in the caller namespace, referencing the canonical namespace/universe shared REP/collateral feed. Security pool admission calls this from the security pool contract, after its immutable admission policy allows the question; it then enforces the question horizon, with failure reverting deployment. QuestionLedgerRegistered and DeployQuestionLedger prepareSecurityPoolChildPriceFeed(parent, outcomeIndex) Anyone The parent is a canonical question ledger in PoolForked state. Its namespace and oracle configuration determine the feed; Zoltar determines the selected child universe and its REP token. Prepares the child universe’s REP token and shared feed in a separate transaction before creating a child question ledger. Repeated preparation returns the same feed without moving capital or selecting any owner’s migration. Returns the canonical child feed address. A newly deployed feed emits SharedPriceFeedRegistered . Child REP deployment may emit Zoltar events. deployChildQuestionLedger(parent, shareToken, universeId, questionId, statoblastSecurityMultiplierBps) QuestionForker only ( Only question forker ) Parent is the canonical question ledger for its lineage; supplied share token equals the parent share token; no question ledger has already been claimed for the parent origin in the target universe; a security pool child feed has already been prepared; deployment arguments satisfy downstream constructors and wiring. Creates and registers a canonical child question ledger with the parent share token and its canonical security pool branch feed. QuestionLedgerRegistered , then DeployQuestionLedger","title":"SecurityPoolFactory","topic":"Contracts","weight":0},{"fragment":"","heading":"","keywords":["securityPool","question","complete set","reporting","settlement"],"path":"reference/contracts/questionledger.html","sectionTitle":"Reference","summary":"Question-ledger reads, complete-set purchases and redemptions, wallet reporting, and shared-fork entrypoints.","text":"Question ledger for one shared security pool, question and universe. Shares and reporting are question-specific; provider capital, settlement cash and fees are accounted centrally. QuestionLedger source contract","title":"QuestionLedger","topic":"Contracts","weight":1},{"fragment":"read-surface","heading":"Read surface","keywords":[],"path":"reference/contracts/questionledger.html","sectionTitle":"Reference","summary":"Question-ledger reads, complete-set purchases and redemptions, wallet reporting, and shared-fork entrypoints.","text":"Read security pool owner positions from the capital ledger and question fee streams from the settlement book. Amounts named AttoEth use raw units of the security pool collateral token; no token metadata is required. After security pool binding, question storage mirrors settlement cash, share supply and lastSettlementSecurityPoolTime , the security pool time of its last checkpoint. Fee stream deadlines use native time; question storage does not enumerate owners. additionalMintCapacityAttoEth is zero once the question ends or reporting starts; otherwise it returns available shared capacity, excluding existing question collateral. attoSharesToAttoEth , attoSharesToAttoRep and attoEthToAttoShares quote central settlement including fork recovery rights. isEscalationResolved recognizes inherited final outcomes and locally finalized reports. The settlement book tracks each question’s fee stream. An unreported question can continue paying fees after its calendar end until reporting starts. Additional validated configuration and accounting getters: questionId , universeId , initialEscalationGameDepositAttoRep , zoltar , parent , shareToken , repToken , priceFeed , openOracle , escalationGameFactory , accountingModule , escalationGame , questionData , questionForker , securityPoolFactory , settlementCollateralAttoEth , statoblastSecurityMultiplierBps , shareTokenSupplyAttoShares , lastSettlementSecurityPoolTime , awaitingForkContinuation , systemState , securityPoolCustody .","title":"QuestionLedger","topic":"Contracts","weight":0},{"fragment":"security-assumptions","heading":"Security assumptions","keywords":[],"path":"reference/contracts/questionledger.html","sectionTitle":"Reference","summary":"Question-ledger reads, complete-set purchases and redemptions, wallet reporting, and shared-fork entrypoints.","text":"Price-sensitive withdrawal, commitment-increase, and liquidation calls depend on A16 timely inclusion , A21 REP and selected collateral behavior , A19 observable correctable price , and A06 lifecycle executors . User-initiated question ledger calls additionally depend on A28 account authority .","title":"QuestionLedger","topic":"Contracts","weight":0},{"fragment":"interactions","heading":"State-changing interactions","keywords":[],"path":"reference/contracts/questionledger.html","sectionTitle":"Reference","summary":"Question-ledger reads, complete-set purchases and redemptions, wallet reporting, and shared-fork entrypoints.","text":"The contract index defines the columns. See the security pool module boundaries for capital, settlement, and reporter custody. Transaction Caller Main prerequisites State or asset effect Primary signals setSecurityPoolCustody(custody) The canonical security pool Nonzero custody; not already bound; caller matches factory registration. Binds its central capital ledger once and registers the question fee stream. Relevant share, reporting or central accounting events; no standalone vault accounting. creditEscalationSurplus(amountAttoRep) The active escalation game Caller matches the configured game; REP has already been transferred. Transfers irrevocable surplus REP to the security pool for vested provider earnings. Reporter principal remains separate. Relevant share, reporting or central accounting events; no standalone vault accounting. refreshSecurityPoolFees() Anyone Central accounting is bound. Refreshes the question linear stream and settlement mirrors. Relevant share, reporting or central accounting events; no standalone vault accounting. burnEscalationWinnerHaircut(amountAttoRep) The active escalation game Caller matches the configured game; REP has already been transferred. Burns the winning settlement haircut through Zoltar. Relevant share, reporting or central accounting events; no standalone vault accounting. createCompleteSet(cash, recipient, minimumShares, fee, premium) The registered security pool purchase route Operational unforked universe, reporting not started, question not ended, authenticated purchase and sufficient healthy backing. Completes central settlement and purchase-fee funding, then mints question complete sets directly to the recipient. Relevant share, reporting or central accounting events; no standalone vault accounting. redeemCompleteSet(amountAttoShares) A complete-set holder Operational unforked universe; holder has all outcome shares. Burns complete sets and pays their current collateral and recovery REP entitlement. Released exposure becomes available to the security pool. Relevant share, reporting or central accounting events; no standalone vault accounting. redeemShares() A winning-share holder Operational question with a final outcome; finalized claims retain their protected settlement path. Burns winning shares and pays their central collateral and recovery REP entitlement. Relevant share, reporting or central accounting events; no standalone vault accounting. depositWalletRepToEscalationGame(outcome, maximumDepositAttoRep) Any reporter Valid outcome, wallet allowance and balance, operational reporting window; no inherited fixed outcome. Transfers accepted wallet REP into separate reporter custody and records the chosen outcome; starts the game if needed, deploying it unless EscalationGameFactory.prepareEscalationGame already did. EscalationGameSet when the game is created, followed by reporter deposit and REP transfer events. depositWalletRepToEscalationGameWithLead(outcome, maximumDepositAttoRep) Any reporter Ordinary wallet reporting prerequisites; the resulting outcome balance exceeds every other balance or fills its threshold ( Deposit does not lead ). An opposing top-up that invalidates this condition causes the whole transaction to revert. Performs the ordinary wallet deposit only if it leaves the chosen outcome strictly leading, or fills its non-decision threshold. The reporting UI uses this protected form when the caller requests a leading result. The ordinary wallet deposit signals; none survive a failed lead condition. withdrawFromEscalationGame(outcome, depositIndexes) Anyone, for one recorded depositor Operational question and final outcome; unresolved parent claims follow the child settlement path. Settles the supplied local deposit indexes. Payout goes to the recorded owner; losing claims receive no REP. Relevant share, reporting or central accounting events; no standalone vault accounting. withdrawForkedEscalationDeposits(outcome, proofs) Anyone, for one recorded depositor Operational finalized question, valid unconsumed proofs, one depositor per batch. Settles authenticated inherited leaves without migrating every reporter. Payouts go to the recorded owner. Relevant share, reporting or central accounting events; no standalone vault accounting. updateSettlementCollateral() Anyone Central accounting is bound. Checkpoints central question fees and updates the local settlement mirror. Relevant share, reporting or central accounting events; no standalone vault accounting. setStartingParams() The factory Factory authentication; deployment initialization. Initializes the question fee clock. Relevant share, reporting or central accounting events; no standalone vault accounting. activateForkMode() QuestionForker only No inherited fixed outcome; the central fork checks apply. Freezes shared security pool accounting and marks the question forked. SystemStateSet on the transition to forked, and none for a repeated call; relevant share, reporting or central accounting events; no standalone vault accounting. initializeForkedEscalationGame(startBondAttoRep, nonDecisionThresholdAttoRep, elapsedAtFork, fixedQuestionOutcome) QuestionForker only No existing game. Deploys a continuation game with the inherited bond, threshold, elapsed time and outcome. Relevant share, reporting or central accounting events; no standalone vault accounting. initializeForkCarryOutcome(sourceGame, snapshotId, peaks, leafCount, carryTotalAttoRep, resolutionBalanceAttoRep, nullifierRoot) QuestionForker only A game is configured; it is a fork continuation with fewer than three initialized outcomes; leaf counts fit the MMR; every call supplies the same source and nonzero snapshot ID, with the next outcome’s data; the third call verifies the full commitment. Installs the next outcome of the continuation game’s canonical snapshot. Three calls install immutable inherited roots and leaf counts, plus append-only current peaks, carry totals and resolution balances. Canonical live sources require empty nullifier roots and cannot export finalized claims. ForkCarryCheckpoint on completion of the third outcome resumeForkedEscalationGame() Anyone Complete authenticated carry snapshot and funded central reporter custody. Resumes a prepared continuation from the shared child branch activation timestamp. Relevant share, reporting or central accounting events; no standalone vault accounting. setAwaitingForkContinuation(shouldAwait) QuestionForker only No lifecycle or value-change guard. Stores whether complete-set minting must wait for continuation initialization, then refreshes bound security pool fee accounting. AwaitingForkContinuationSet , including for a repeated value; bound question ledgers then emit central checkpoints and stream replacement when applicable setSystemState(newState) QuestionForker only No transition or value-change guard. Replaces the question ledger lifecycle state directly, then refreshes bound security pool fee accounting. SystemStateSet , including for a repeated state; bound question ledgers then emit central checkpoints and stream replacement when applicable setShareTokenSupplyAttoShares(newShareTokenSupplyAttoShares) QuestionForker only Caller is the immutable forker. Initializes the child question share-supply mirror. Relevant share, reporting or central accounting events; no standalone vault accounting. authorizeChildQuestionLedger(pool) QuestionForker only This parent question ledger is already authorized; candidate reports this share token; candidate universe has no different canonical question ledger. No question ledger lifecycle guard. Asks the lineage share token to establish pool as the canonical authorized question ledger for its universe; reauthorizing the same question ledger is a no-op. AuthorizationUpdated only on first authorization; no event when already authorized","title":"QuestionLedger","topic":"Contracts","weight":0},{"fragment":"","heading":"","keywords":["fork","migration","child pool","auction"],"path":"reference/contracts/questionforker.html","sectionTitle":"Reference","summary":"Read surface, the child-game trust boundary, question fork activation, reporter continuations, and owner-selected capital branches.","text":"Executes question forks and lazy child question preparation using shared security pool state. QuestionForker source contract","title":"QuestionForker","topic":"Contracts","weight":1},{"fragment":"read-surface","heading":"Read surface","keywords":[],"path":"reference/contracts/questionforker.html","sectionTitle":"Reference","summary":"Read surface, the child-game trust boundary, question fork activation, reporter continuations, and owner-selected capital branches.","text":"zoltar identifies the protocol root. getQuestionOutcome returns the fixed or finalized question outcome, or None while unresolved; the shared security pool clock protects pre-fork final claims. getOwnForkAdmissionShortfall returns the reporter ledger and the REP an own-question fork pulls from its caller.","title":"QuestionForker","topic":"Contracts","weight":0},{"fragment":"child-game-trust-boundary","heading":"Child-game trust boundary","keywords":[],"path":"reference/contracts/questionforker.html","sectionTitle":"Reference","summary":"Read surface, the child-game trust boundary, question fork activation, reporter continuations, and owner-selected capital branches.","text":"The forker binds Zoltar immutably at deployment. Security pool paths authenticate the source question through its factory namespace and central books before changing capital or installing claims. Fork preparation executes in the forker itself without delegate dispatch.","title":"QuestionForker","topic":"Contracts","weight":0},{"fragment":"interactions","heading":"State-changing interactions","keywords":[],"path":"reference/contracts/questionforker.html","sectionTitle":"Reference","summary":"Read surface, the child-game trust boundary, question fork activation, reporter continuations, and owner-selected capital branches.","text":"The contract index defines the columns. Child question ledgers and their fixed-size reporter snapshots are prepared lazily. See shared fork accounting for capital and reporter migration. Transaction Caller Main prerequisites State or asset effect Primary signals prepareSecurityPoolQuestion(source, targetUniverse) Anyone Canonical bound source; its universe has forked; the security pool recognizes a ready target branch and the source reporter identity. Factory and share-token bindings are verified. Deploys and binds the canonical target question contract and share-token namespace separately from report-history installation. A forked target can be prepared for frozen share recovery without activating a game. Canonical question ledger deployment and authorization events when the target is new. materializeSecurityPoolQuestion(source, targetUniverse) Anyone The preparation guards apply. A target whose native universe has already forked can activate only a protected final reporter or trader claim owned by that universe. Already operational targets return without duplicate installation. Prepares the target if absent. With inherited reporting, installs one outcome of the fixed 64-peak snapshot per call; the third call activates the question and resumes reporting from the child branch activation timestamp. Without reporter history, activates immediately. Late installation does not postpone finality. An already-final game must agree with the inherited reporter and outstanding-share settlement deadlines, including the fixed-outcome response period, or activation reverts. Preparation may be a separate transaction to bound deployment gas. Canonical deployment events when needed, then game continuation and carry checkpoint events when a reporter game is installed. initiateQuestionLedgerFork(questionLedger) Anyone The universe has forked and the question was not final before that fork. Marks the unresolved question forked and freezes its shared books. Shared fork, question deployment and reporting continuation events as applicable. forkZoltarWithOwnEscalationGame(questionLedger) Anyone Canonical reporter question has non-decision; the caller has approved the reporter ledger for any shortfall getOwnForkAdmissionShortfall reports; universe is not already forked. Uses this question’s reporter custody to pay native fork admission and freezes its security pool. A game fixes its non-decision threshold when it opens, but the genesis fork threshold follows the live external supply until the first fork, so the escrow can fall short. The caller then pays the shortfall from REP approved to the reporter ledger. That REP joins the question’s escrow with no separate claim. Shared fork, question deployment and reporting continuation events as applicable. createChildUniverse(questionLedger, outcomeIndex) Anyone Canonical source in a forked universe; target universe and question identity must match; the security pool’s shared branch for the target universe is ready ( Child settlement not ready ). Deploys the native child universe when it does not exist yet, then materializes the immediate security pool child question using the shared branch and lazy carry installation. When the target universe has itself already forked and the question cannot activate there, the call stops after preparing the child ledger in fork mode, which is all that share migration through that universe needs. Shared fork, question deployment and reporting continuation events as applicable.","title":"QuestionForker","topic":"Contracts","weight":0},{"fragment":"","heading":"","keywords":["escalation","dispute","deposit","non-decision"],"path":"reference/contracts/escalationgame.html","sectionTitle":"Reference","summary":"Read surface and the start, deposit, withdrawal, proof-based claim, fork-carry, and fallback-routed entrypoints, with accounting examples.","text":"Escrows outcome REP, raises the running resolution cost, detects non-decision , and settles local or carried deposits. EscalationGame source contract","title":"EscalationGame","topic":"Contracts","weight":1},{"fragment":"read-surface","heading":"Read surface","keywords":[],"path":"reference/contracts/escalationgame.html","sectionTitle":"Reference","summary":"Read surface and the start, deposit, withdrawal, proof-based claim, fork-carry, and fallback-routed entrypoints, with accounting examples.","text":"Base getters are questionLedger , repToken , activationTime , nonDecisionThresholdAttoRep , startBondAttoRep , nonDecisionTimestamp , nonDecisionState , forkContinuation , forkElapsedAtStart , forkResumedAt , fixedQuestionOutcome , nodes , disputeStakedRepByVaultAttoRep , totalDisputeStakedAttoRep , ordinarySettlementInitialized , ordinaryWinningReserveAttoRep , creditedSurplusAttoRep , settlementForkThresholdAttoRep , and winnerHaircutPaidByFork . Every accepted deposit, and a continuation’s resume in an unforked universe, records the universe fork threshold in settlementForkThresholdAttoRep . A final continuation resumed after its universe forked again records nonDecisionThresholdAttoRep instead, preserving unscaled entitlements; winning claims scale by that value, so REP burned after the game’s last change cannot lower any payout. Ordinary settlement fixes the winner liability; losing local deposits and escrow read as zero afterward, while raw historical nodes remain stored. The fallback accepts only the three signed deposit selectors delegated to EscalationGameDepositDelegate ; other unknown selectors revert. Canonical game activation requires security pool reporter custody. Inherited claims use the authenticated leaf’s unchanged principal and reward interval, independently of ancestry depth or claim order. Membership proofs and per-game consumed-deposit checks remain mandatory. Canonical games report appendOnlyCarry as true: live carry roots only append, and finalized claims do not rewrite historical membership or require nullifier witnesses. Standalone games retain mutable carry roots and nullifier proofs. disputeStakedRepByVaultAttoRep is nominal reporter principal, not collateral value. getDisputeBackingAttoRep returns actual game REP; Inherited carry remains aggregate commitment state until proof settlement. Use previewLeadingDepositOnOutcome for a required lead and previewDepositOnOutcome , computeIterativeAttritionCostAttoRep , computeTimeSinceStartFromAttritionCostAttoRep , computeCurveFinalityElapsed , totalCostAttoRep , getEscalationGameEndDate , getQuestionResolution , getFinalQuestionResolution , hasReachedNonDecision , canTriggerOwnFork , getBindingCapitalAttoRep , getOutcomeBalancesAttoRep , getDepositsByOutcome , getDepositsByOutcomeLength , forkCarrySnapshotInitialized , forkCarrySnapshotOutcomesInitialized , getOutcomeState , getForkCarrySnapshot , getForkCarryRoots , isForkCarryFundingComplete , getCarryLeafPageByOutcome , getProofConsumedCarriedDepositIndexesByOutcome , and getLocalUnresolvedPrincipalByVaultAndOutcome for calculations, lifecycle authorization, pages, carry state, and escrow. getCarryLeafPageByOutcome limits visited nodes by maxEntries , including consumed nodes; empty or short pages may return a nonzero continuation cursor. A nonzero start cursor must be an issued node of the requested outcome. Wallet reports and finalized withdrawals route through QuestionLedger ; after an ordinary game starts, wallet-funded deposits use depositRepOnOutcome , depositRepOnOutcomeWithPermit , or depositRepOnOutcomeWithAuthorization and mint no pool backing units.","title":"EscalationGame","topic":"Contracts","weight":0},{"fragment":"interactions","heading":"State-changing interactions","keywords":[],"path":"reference/contracts/escalationgame.html","sectionTitle":"Reference","summary":"Read surface and the start, deposit, withdrawal, proof-based claim, fork-carry, and fallback-routed entrypoints, with accounting examples.","text":"The contract index defines the columns. Rows marked \"through fallback() \" are not in the compiled ABI: the fallback forwards the signed and lead-protected deposit selectors to EscalationGameDepositDelegate by delegatecall , returning or reverting with the delegate output. Every other unknown selector reverts. A call restricted to the owning question ledger or its forker reverts without reason data for any other caller. See escalation deadlines and continuations and carry proofs . Transaction Caller Main prerequisites State or asset effect Primary signals start(startBondAttoRep, nonDecisionThresholdAttoRep) Immutable owner, the EscalationGameFactory , only ( Only owner ), during the first report Game not already started, and nonDecisionThresholdAttoRep > startBondAttoRep > 0 ( Invalid game start ). Initializes a local game and sets activation three days after this call. The factory deploys the game in the same transaction, or starts the unstarted game that the permissionless EscalationGameFactory.prepareEscalationGame(questionLedger) deployed earlier at the same address; preparation never starts the clock. For ordinary question ledger games, the factory lowers an oversized configured bond to nonDecisionThresholdAttoRep - 1 before this call. GameStarted startFromFork(startBondAttoRep, nonDecisionThresholdAttoRep, elapsedAtFork, fixedQuestionOutcome, winnerHaircutPaidByFork, forkCarryInitialBackingAttoRep) Immutable owner, the EscalationGameFactory , only ( Only owner ), during continuation deployment, including a game prepared earlier Game not already started, and nonDecisionThresholdAttoRep > startBondAttoRep > 0 ( Invalid game start ); elapsedAtFork is a signed number of seconds between minus three days and seven weeks, inclusive ( Invalid fork elapsed time ). Initializes a continuation with inherited elapsed time, an optional fixed matching child outcome, and immutable fork-time escalation-haircut and backing accounting. GameContinuedFromFork resumeFromFork() Owning QuestionLedger only ( Only pool ) Fork-continuation mode; not previously resumed; aggregate REP funding complete ( isForkCarryFundingComplete ). All three carry outcomes must be initialized ( Fork snapshot incomplete ), even for empty history. When the canonical factory records nonzero initial backing, the live reporter balance must cover that actual escrow after source surplus and native fork burn. Without recorded initial backing, it must cover effective inherited unresolved principal plus local unresolved principal across all three outcomes. Resumes once carry funding is complete. Standalone continuations use the resume transaction timestamp. Bound continuations use the child branch activation timestamp, shared by every inherited game. Without a fixed outcome, the deadline adds only remaining curve time; a bound fixed outcome follows its inherited security pool deadline (three days after activation when the current fork fixes that question); a standalone fixed outcome ends three days after resume. Late question materialization never moves the anchor. If the game is final and its universe has already forked again, resuming records the non-decision threshold as its settlement threshold so later migration cannot reduce payouts. Final resolution also checks whether the native universe forked before that deadline. ForkContinuationResumed depositRepOnOutcome(outcome, maximumDepositAttoRep) Any REP holder Current game is ordinary or a resumed security pool continuation without a fixed outcome; provisional resolution None ; question ledger operational; universe unforked; outcome non- None ; caller allowance to this game covers the accepted REP; the requested amount is at least the start bond, and preview accepts a positive amount within the remaining threshold room, which may be one attoREP short to avoid a tie or below the start bond when it fills the threshold ( Invalid deposit preview ). Transfers accepted REP from the caller into dispute escrow and appends its nominal deposit and carry leaf, without minting reporter backing units. Ordinary games release increases in the minimum outcome balance to vested REP income from the security pool. Continuations do not release this ordinary surplus. REP Transfer , LocalDepositAppended , DepositOnOutcome , EscalationSurplusAccrued when the ordinary minimum increases, and NonDecisionReached when the deposit reaches non-decision depositRepOnOutcomeWithPermitAndLead(outcome, maximumDepositAttoRep, deadline, v, r, s) through fallback() Any child-universe REP holder Permit-funded deposit prerequisites and the lead or threshold condition. A failed condition rolls back the permit nonce, allowance, transfer and deposit. Performs the permit-funded deposit with the same atomic lead or threshold condition as depositRepOnOutcomeWithLead . Permit-funded deposit signals; none survive a failed lead condition. depositRepOnOutcomeWithLead(outcome, maximumDepositAttoRep) Any REP holder The ordinary direct-deposit prerequisites. The resulting outcome balance exceeds every competing balance or equals the non-decision threshold ( Deposit does not lead ). previewLeadingDepositOnOutcome quotes this bound. Performs the ordinary wallet deposit only when its result leads or reaches the non-decision threshold. A reordered reply that would become trailing support reverts without spending REP. The ordinary depositRepOnOutcome signals. depositRepOnOutcomeWithAuthorizationAndLead(owner, outcome, maximumDepositAttoRep, validAfter, validBefore, nonce, v, r, s) Anyone relaying the owner's ERC-3009 signature The depositRepOnOutcomeWithLead and child-universe authorization prerequisites. Compute the operation hash and token nonce as for the unguarded authorization method, substituting depositRepOnOutcomeWithAuthorizationAndLead.selector . Pulls and records the owner’s previewed REP only when its outcome leads or completes the non-decision threshold. The authorization is bound to this guarded selector and cannot be submitted through the unguarded authorization method. REP AuthorizationUsed and the ordinary deposit signals. depositRepOnOutcomeWithPermit(outcome, maximumDepositAttoRep, deadline, v, r, s) through fallback() Any child-universe REP holder The depositRepOnOutcome game, question ledger, and preview prerequisites; the question ledger universe is not genesis ( Genesis REP does not support permit ); the permit succeeds or the caller allowance to this game covers the previewed amount ( Game permit and allowance insufficient ). Calls the REP token permit for the previewed amount from the caller to this game, ignores a failed permit when the existing allowance already covers that amount, then performs the depositRepOnOutcome deposit. REP Approval when the permit succeeds, then the depositRepOnOutcome signals depositRepOnOutcomeWithAuthorization(owner, outcome, maximumDepositAttoRep, validAfter, validBefore, nonce, v, r, s) through fallback() Anyone relaying the owner's ERC-3009 signature The depositRepOnOutcome game, question ledger, and preview prerequisites; the question ledger universe is not genesis ( Genesis REP does not support authorization ); the signature authorizes the token nonce keccak256(abi.encode(nonce, operationHash, owner)) , where operationHash is keccak256(abi.encode(depositRepOnOutcomeWithAuthorization.selector, owner, questionLedger, universeId, questionId, outcome, maximumDepositAttoRep, previewedAmount)) . Pulls the previewed amount from owner to this game through the REP token receiveWithAuthorization and records the deposit for owner . REP AuthorizationUsed and Transfer , LocalDepositAppended , DepositOnOutcome , EscalationSurplusAccrued when the ordinary minimum increases, and NonDecisionReached when the deposit reaches non-decision recordDepositFromQuestionLedger(depositor, outcome, amountAttoRep, expectedCumulativeAttoRep) Owning QuestionLedger only ( Only pool ) Explicit non-decision state is None ; provisional resolution None ( Question resolved ); valid outcome; preview and accepted cumulative amount match; room remains below threshold. Appends the nominal deposit and carry leaf. In ordinary games, it releases any increase in the minimum outcome balance through the same security-pool-income path as depositRepOnOutcome ; only the new report’s retained contribution receives collateral credit. Continuations release no ordinary surplus. LocalDepositAppended , DepositOnOutcome , EscalationSurplusAccrued when the ordinary minimum increases, and NonDecisionReached when the deposit reaches non-decision withdrawDeposit(depositIndex, outcome) , the uint256 overload Owning QuestionLedger only ( Only pool ) Explicit non-decision state is None ; non- None supplied outcome; game final; game and question ledger final outcomes match; valid local deposit index, unsettled unless it is a retired ordinary loser. Settles one local deposit after resolution. An ordinary first claim freezes the winning liability and burns terminal excess beyond the surplus already paid at source; ordinary losers retire together and a valid losing index returns zero repeatedly. A winner consumes its reserved payout and burn and pays the immutable depositor. Continuation losers still consume their individual escrow. OrdinarySettlementInitialized on first ordinary settlement. Winners and continuation losers emit CarryDepositConsumed and VaultEscrowUpdated ; winners also emit ClaimDeposit and positive payout or burn signals. Retired ordinary losers emit no per-deposit settlement event. initializeForkCarryOutcome(sourceGame, snapshotId, peaks, leafCount, carryTotalAttoRep, resolutionBalanceAttoRep, nullifierRoot) Owning QuestionLedger only Fork-continuation mode; fewer than three initialized outcomes; each leaf count fits the MMR; every call supplies the same source and nonzero snapshot ID, with the next outcome’s data. The third call requires the hash of all normalized data to match that ID. Installs one outcome’s inherited root, mutable peaks, leaf count, carry total, resolution balance, and normalized nullifier root. Canonical live continuations require an empty nullifier root; finalized sources cannot export live claims. The nonzero full snapshot identity and source are fixed on the first call. The third call verifies the installed data against that identity before marking the snapshot complete. Two or more threshold-full inherited balances then set nonDecisionState to InheritedThresholdTie without creating a local timestamp. ForkCarryCheckpoint after all three outcomes; additionally InheritedThresholdTie when the completed balances meet the non-decision threshold claimDepositForWinning(depositIndex, outcome) Owning QuestionLedger or its QuestionForker Non- None supplied outcome and valid unsettled local deposit with sufficient escrow. All canonical games require final resolution. An ordinary non-fork game also checks the actual winning outcome; continuation paths rely on the trusted caller to select the winner. Consumes a selected local deposit as a winner, consumes its vault escrow, burns the computed escalation haircut when nonzero, and transfers the remaining positive REP payout to the deposit's immutable depositor. CarryDepositConsumed , VaultEscrowUpdated , ClaimDeposit with transferredRep = true ; REP payout Transfer and escalation-haircut burn signals only when their amounts are positive withdrawDeposit(proof, outcome) , the CarriedDepositProof overload Owning QuestionLedger or its QuestionForker Non- None supplied outcome; game final and matching the question ledger final outcome; supplied outcome is the winner; parent deposit was not directly claimed; valid membership proof and an unconsumed deposit identity; standalone games also require a nullifier proof. Consumes an inherited proof, transfers any positive winning payout, and burns the positive escalation haircut unless the fork already paid it. CarryDepositConsumed and ClaimDeposit with transferredRep = true ; REP payout Transfer and escalation-haircut burn signals only when positive settleOrdinaryGame() Anyone Ordinary game, not a fork continuation, with nonDecisionState = None and a final outcome. Game REP must cover the initial winner reserve ( Winner reserve underfunded ). Freezes the final outcome, reserves the cumulative winning payout and burn liability at the recorded settlementForkThresholdAttoRep , and retires all losing local escrow without visiting deposits. Each later winning claim subtracts its payout and burn from that reserve. Burns terminal REP above the reserve; the ordinary minimum-outcome surplus was already paid irrevocably as deposits arrived. A repeated call with no surplus succeeds. See reporter liability invariant . OrdinarySettlementInitialized once; OrdinaryResidualRepBurned and REP Transfer only when surplus is positive sweepResidualRepToQuestionLedger() Anyone Final outcome and positive releasable surplus ( No sweepable REP for an ordinary no-op). Ordinary settlement preserves all unclaimed winning liabilities. Continuations require no effective unresolved principal and, after retiring inherited aggregate escrow, no remaining local stake or vault escrow. Applies ordinary winner-reserve settlement when applicable. Sends terminal surplus through the question ledger to burn it, with no credit to capital owners. OrdinarySettlementInitialized if first ordinary settlement, then OrdinaryResidualRepBurned ; ForkContinuationResidualRepBurned for a fork continuation","title":"EscalationGame","topic":"Contracts","weight":0},{"fragment":"accounting-examples","heading":"Accounting examples","keywords":[],"path":"reference/contracts/escalationgame.html","sectionTitle":"Reference","summary":"Read surface and the start, deposit, withdrawal, proof-based claim, fork-carry, and fallback-routed entrypoints, with accounting examples.","text":"","title":"EscalationGame","topic":"Contracts","weight":0},{"fragment":"required-support-threshold","heading":"Required support threshold","keywords":[],"path":"reference/contracts/escalationgame.html","sectionTitle":"Reference","summary":"Read surface and the start, deposit, withdrawal, proof-based claim, fork-carry, and fallback-routed entrypoints, with accounting examples.","text":"The contract waits ACTIVATION_DELAY (3 days) after start() before the escalation clock begins. From day 3 the cumulative binding-capital requirement rises from the configured start bond S to the non-decision threshold T over the 49-day ESCALATION_TIME_LENGTH , reaching T on day 52. The contract evaluates a fixed-point curve; this expression is its readable idealization. A ( d ) = 0 for 0 ≤ d ≤ 3 = S × exp ( ln ( T S ) × d − 3 49 ) for 3 < d < 52 = T for d ≥ 52","title":"EscalationGame","topic":"Contracts","weight":0},{"fragment":"winning-deposit-payout","heading":"Winning-deposit payout","keywords":[],"path":"reference/contracts/escalationgame.html","sectionTitle":"Reference","summary":"Read surface and the start, deposit, withdrawal, proof-based claim, fork-carry, and fallback-routed entrypoints, with accounting examples.","text":"For an ordinary settlement, binding capital is the median outcome balance at settlement; no outcome balance can exceed the non-decision threshold. The reward-eligible cap is binding capital plus binding capital divided by EXCESS_REWARD_WINDOW_DIVISOR (2), so only the portion of winning deposits up to 1.5 × binding capital participates in the escalation reward. Of the binding capital, 3 / 5 funds the reward pool and 2 / 5 funds the escalation-haircut pool; both are shared pro rata across the reward-eligible principal, which is the smaller of the winning outcome balance and that cap. Let binding capital be 10 REP , so the reward-eligible cap is 15 REP , the reward pool is 6 REP , and the escalation-haircut pool is 4 REP . If the winning outcome holds 15 REP and one deposit contributed 5 REP as the final deposit, from cumulative 10 REP to 15 REP: Principal returned: 5 REP . Escalation reward: the difference of the rounded cumulative reward allocations at 15 REP and 10 REP is 2 REP . Winning payout: 5 + 2 = 7 REP . Haircut burned: the difference of the rounded cumulative burn allocations is 1.333333333333333334 REP at attoREP precision. A winning deposit position above 15 REP returns its principal without an escalation reward. When the Zoltar fork threshold the game recorded at its last deposit is below the game's non-decision threshold, each cumulative payout is scaled by their ratio before taking the difference. If this game's non-decision threshold were 20 REP and the fork threshold 16 REP , the 7 REP payout becomes 5.6 REP after subtracting the two scaled cumulative payouts at attoREP precision. Fork scaling can therefore reduce the final transfer below principal.","title":"EscalationGame","topic":"Contracts","weight":0},{"fragment":"","heading":"","keywords":["OpenOracle","price","shared feed"],"path":"reference/contracts/sharedpoolpricefeed.html","sectionTitle":"Reference","summary":"Shared universe price reads, sponsor-funded report requests, recovery, and authenticated exposure checkpoints.","text":"Canonical REP/collateral reporting and price source for every question in one security pool and universe. SharedPoolPriceFeed source contract","title":"SharedPoolPriceFeed","topic":"Contracts","weight":1},{"fragment":"read-surface","heading":"Read surface","keywords":[],"path":"reference/contracts/sharedpoolpricefeed.html","sectionTitle":"Reference","summary":"Shared universe price reads, sponsor-funded report requests, recovery, and authenticated exposure checkpoints.","text":"Accepted price and freshness: lastPrice , lastSettlementTimestamp , priceValidUntil and isPriceValid . Pending reports do not extend freshness. Reports retain isolated sponsor-owned escrows. Configuration, exposure and candidate getters: reportCandidates , reportOutcome , universeId , reputationToken , aggregateExposureAttoEth , validateExposure , conservativePrice , liquidationPrice , minimumToken1ReportAttoEth , minimumToken2ReportAttoRep , minimumToken1ReportAttoEthForPrice , getSettlementCallbackGasLimit , getRequestPriceCostAttoEth , reportAuthorization , priceValidityWindowSeconds , timeType , trackDisputes , OPEN_INTEREST_DIVIDER , REPORT_OPERATION_GAS_OVERHEAD , MAXIMUM_REPORT_OPERATION_DATA_BYTES , FEED_SETTLEMENT_GAS_OVERHEAD , securityPoolFactory , securityPool , openOracle , collateralToken , minimumReportCollateral , weth , initialReportPriorityFeeAttoEthPerGas , targetPriceErrorForDispute , settlementTime , disputeDelay , protocolFee , feePercentage , multiplier , protocolFeeRecipient , escalationHaltMultiplierBps , maxSettlementBaseFeeMultiplierBps , pendingReportId , pendingReportSponsor , pendingReportMaxSettlementBaseFeeAttoEthPerGas , pendingExposureLimitAttoEth , acceptedExposureLimitAttoEth , acceptedRepExposureLimitAttoRep , acceptedEpoch , sponsorOperationReport .","title":"SharedPoolPriceFeed","topic":"Contracts","weight":0},{"fragment":"security-assumptions","heading":"Security assumptions","keywords":[],"path":"reference/contracts/sharedpoolpricefeed.html","sectionTitle":"Reference","summary":"Shared universe price reads, sponsor-funded report requests, recovery, and authenticated exposure checkpoints.","text":"Report liveness depends on A16 timely inclusion , A17 corrector capability , A18 independent correction incentive , A19 observable correctable price , and A06 lifecycle executors .","title":"SharedPoolPriceFeed","topic":"Contracts","weight":0},{"fragment":"interactions","heading":"State-changing interactions","keywords":[],"path":"reference/contracts/sharedpoolpricefeed.html","sectionTitle":"Reference","summary":"Shared universe price reads, sponsor-funded report requests, recovery, and authenticated exposure checkpoints.","text":"The contract index defines the columns. Transaction Caller Main prerequisites State or asset effect Primary signals requestSecurityPoolPrice(proposedRepPerEthPrice, requestedInitialAttoWeth, bountyAttoEth) Anyone Approve this feed for exact collateral and REP; positive price; sufficient report stake; msg.value covers the committed bounty; excess ETH is refunded after candidate storage is complete; reporting window open. Opens an independently funded report. Collateral and REP move directly from the caller to its isolated escrow; the caller receives returned funding. PriceRequested , ReportFundingAssigned requestSecurityPoolPriceWithOperations(proposedRepPerEthPrice, requestedInitialAttoWeth, bountyAttoEth, batch) Anyone Same funding checks; unexpired batch, price bounds and sufficient bounded callback gas. Opens a report and retains the caller as sponsor for ordered bounded capital operations after acceptance. PriceRequested , ReportFundingAssigned , ReportOperationAttempted syncExposure() Canonical question ledgers only Caller belongs to this security pool and universe; this is the factory-registered feed. Emits changed live aggregate exposure. The factory mapping, ledger factory, universe, REP and feed must agree. ExposureCheckpoint createReportEscrow(payer, sponsor, wethAmount, repAmount, haltAmount, bounty, callbackGasLimit) This feed only Authenticated self-call from report creation; OpenOracle amount and bounty widths already checked. Creates the report escrow and pulls caller-funded tokens into it atomically. OpenOracle report creation settleReport(reportId, amount1, amount2) The registered report escrow only Known pending candidate; authenticated escrow callback. Validates report economics, freshness, base-fee ceiling and exposure. An acceptance records the candidate and canonical prices before sponsor operations use the canonical price. PriceCandidateAccepted , PriceReported , PriceReportRejected , PriceReportDeferred , ReportOperationAttempted recoverReport(reportId) Anyone Known unprocessed report with nonzero settlement timestamp. Accepting its price or attempting operations under a fresh canonical price requires base fee at or below its cap; other invalid candidates can retire above the cap. Retries a settled candidate using stored OpenOracle amounts and timestamps. Funding withdrawal remains separate. PendingReportRecovered and report outcome events recoverSettledPendingReport() Anyone Latest candidate is settled and recoverable. Recovers the latest pending candidate through recoverReport. PendingReportRecovered and report outcome events","title":"SharedPoolPriceFeed","topic":"Contracts","weight":0},{"fragment":"","heading":"","keywords":["shares","ERC-1155","migrate","outcome"],"path":"reference/contracts/sharetoken.html","sectionTitle":"Reference","summary":"Read surface and the ERC-1155 transfer, migrate, authorize, and complete-set entrypoints.","text":"Stores universe-aware ERC-1155 outcome shares and materializes a holder's persistent source entitlement in selected fork branches. ShareToken source contract","title":"ShareToken","topic":"Contracts","weight":1},{"fragment":"read-surface","heading":"Read surface","keywords":[],"path":"reference/contracts/sharetoken.html","sectionTitle":"Reference","summary":"Read surface and the ERC-1155 transfer, migrate, authorize, and complete-set entrypoints.","text":"Base and relationship getters are name , symbol , zoltar , canonicalQuestionLedgerByUniverse , recoveredCompleteSetShares , _balances , _supplies , and _operatorApprovals . Standard ERC-1155 reads are supportsInterface , balanceOf , totalSupply , balanceOfBatch , and isApprovedForAll ; protocol-specific reads are isAuthorized , totalSupplyForOutcome , maximumOutcomeSupply , balanceOfOutcome , balanceOfShares , getMigratedShareAmountAttoShares , getTokenId , getTokenIds , unpackTokenId , isSecurityPoolRecoveryEligible , and balanceOfSecurityPoolRecoveryRights . The balance getter returns unspent Invalid, Yes and No recovery rights, or all zeros when ineligible. See the source recovery rules for eligibility and complete-set recovery. Received rights are included independently of the holder’s source-token balances.","title":"ShareToken","topic":"Contracts","weight":0},{"fragment":"interactions","heading":"State-changing interactions","keywords":[],"path":"reference/contracts/sharetoken.html","sectionTitle":"Reference","summary":"Read surface and the ERC-1155 transfer, migrate, authorize, and complete-set entrypoints.","text":"The contract index defines the columns. Transaction Caller Main prerequisites State or asset effect Primary signals setApprovalForAll(operator, approved) Any token account setting its own operator approval The operator differs from the caller and from the token itself, which isApprovedForAll always reports as an operator. Sets or clears the operator's authority over all of the caller's outcome-token balances. ApprovalForAll safeTransferFrom(from, to, id, value) and safeTransferFrom(from, to, id, value, data) Share holder or approved ERC-1155 operator Caller holds the source balance or has operator approval; the source account has neither materialized that token into a child branch nor spent source-backed recovery rights by transferring or consuming them; destination is nonzero; the source balance is sufficient; under A22 asset-recipient compatibility , a contract recipient accepts the ERC-1155 callback. Transfers one outcome-token balance without changing supply. TransferSingle safeBatchTransferFrom(from, to, ids, values) and safeBatchTransferFrom(from, to, ids, values, data) Source account or approved ERC-1155 operator ID and value array lengths match; caller is the source account or has operator approval; destination is nonzero; no listed source token is locked by child materialization or spending source-backed recovery rights; source balances are sufficient. Under A22 asset-recipient compatibility , a contract recipient must accept the ERC-1155 callback, including for an empty batch. Transfers each listed outcome-token balance without changing supply. Empty batches change no balances. TransferBatch , including empty batches transferSecurityPoolRecoveryRights(sourceUniverse, recipient, amountsAttoShares) Recovery-right holder transferring only their own rights; ERC-1155 operator approval does not authorize this call Canonical source is a security pool question in a forked universe with isSecurityPoolRecoveryEligible(sourceUniverse) == true ( No live source recovery right ); recipient is a different nonzero account; at least one amount is positive; every amount fits its corresponding unspent recovery-right balance. Transfers the specified Invalid, Yes and No recovery rights independently. Source and child token balances and every branch migration entitlement remain intact. Received credits are spent first; spending source-backed rights locks that source token against transfer. No token-receiver callback is invoked. SecurityPoolRecoveryRightsTransferred consumeSecurityPoolRecoveryCompleteSets(sourceUniverse, holder, amountAttoShares) The canonical source question ledger’s security pool or its configured fork coordinator Canonical source is a security pool question in a forked universe with isSecurityPoolRecoveryEligible(sourceUniverse) == true ( No live source recovery right ); positive amount no greater than any of the holder’s three unspent recovery-right balances. The coordinator authenticates the holder and calculates the corresponding cash payout. Consumes equal amounts of Invalid, Yes and No recovery rights, using independently received credits before source-backed rights. Spending a source-backed right locks that source token against transfer; spending received credits alone does not. Source balances and every branch’s migration rights remain intact. Returns cumulative consumed shares. SecurityPoolRecoveryRightsConsumed migrate(fromId, targetOutcomeIndexes) Holder of the source token ID Source universe forked; for a security pool question, isSecurityPoolRecoveryEligible(sourceUniverse) is true ( ShareToken question finalized before fork ): shares of a question whose claims were final before the fork redeem in the source universe; a canonical source question ledger that is not yet PoolForked has no final outcome and no inherited fixed outcome, because fork-mode activation rejects both; positive source balance; nonempty, strictly increasing, well-formed outcomes; every target in a multi-target call already has a canonical child question ledger; a single target without one requires the security pool’s fork branch into that universe to be active ( Fork branch not active: run prepareBranch, startBranch and finalizeBranch first ); each target is an immediate native child, including when its bound question contract skips intermediate reporter ancestors; at least one selected child has an unmaterialized balance; under A22 asset-recipient compatibility , a contract holder accepts onERC1155Received for every target mint. If needed, first puts the source question ledger into fork mode and records its fork snapshot; this applies to an operational source and to a ledger that was prepared in its universe but never activated before that universe forked. A single-target call may lazily materialize that child's question ledger once the security pool's fork branch into that universe is active; it never prepares, starts, or activates the branch itself, so the call stays within the transaction gas limit. It keeps and locks the holder's source entitlement, then mints each selected child-universe token ID up to the current source balance. Later source additions materialize only the unminted delta. A contract holder receives the ERC-1155 single-receiver callback for each mint; rejection rolls back the mint and preceding fork or child setup. Canonical child preparation may emit QuestionLedgerRegistered , DeployQuestionLedger , and share-token AuthorizationUpdated . Successful materialization emits ERC-1155 TransferSingle and Migrate for each target. authorize(questionLedgerCandidate) Initially authorized SecurityPoolFactory for an origin question ledger; an authorized parent QuestionLedger for a child question ledger Caller is already authorized; the candidate reports this exact share token; its universe has no different canonical question ledger. Establishes the candidate as canonicalQuestionLedgerByUniverse for its universe and adds it to the set allowed to mint, burn, and authorize descendants. Reauthorizing the same candidate is a no-op. AuthorizationUpdated on first authorization; no event when the same candidate is already authorized mintCompleteSets(universeId, account, amountAttoShares) An authorized QuestionLedger Caller is authorized; account is nonzero; amountAttoShares is positive; under A22 asset-recipient compatibility , a contract account accepts onERC1155BatchReceived . Mints amountAttoShares each of Invalid, Yes, and No to account , then invokes its ERC-1155 batch-receiver callback when it is a contract. Rejection rolls back the mint and the authorized question ledger's surrounding transaction. TransferBatch on a successful callback burnCompleteSets(universeId, owner, amountAttoShares) An authorized QuestionLedger Caller is authorized; owner is nonzero and has at least amountAttoShares of every outcome. Each remaining source balance must still cover its already-separated recovery rights. Burns amountAttoShares each of Invalid, Yes, and No from owner ; global outcome supplies may differ. TransferBatch burnTokenIdAndGetRemainingSupply(tokenId, owner) An authorized QuestionLedger owner is nonzero; caller is authorized; no source-backed recovery rights have already been separated for this token. Burns owner 's full balance of tokenId and returns the burned amount and that token ID's remaining supply. TransferSingle , including when the burned balance is zero","title":"ShareToken","topic":"Contracts","weight":0},{"fragment":"","heading":"","keywords":["invariants","safety","liveness","properties"],"path":"reference/invariants.html","sectionTitle":"Reference","summary":"Contract, lifecycle, cross-contract, liveness, and economic properties.","text":"Each entry identifies a property, where it must hold, and the strongest evidence for its enforcement. See the Statoblast security model for assumptions A01–A28. Four connected layers run from local authority guards through cross-contract conservation and lifecycle liveness to economic security. A failure in an earlier layer invalidates the safety claims built above it. Invariant Dependency Correct callers are necessary but insufficient. Their actions must conserve assets, every mandatory state transition must remain reachable, and the named participation and inclusion assumptions must hold where the protocol deliberately relies on economic incentives. Matches All invariants listed below Filter invariants Type Any type Enforcement status Any status Subsystem Any subsystem Expand visible Collapse all Reset filters No invariants match the selected filters.","title":"Statoblast and Zoltar invariants","topic":"Safety","weight":1},{"fragment":"standing","heading":"Classification and scope","keywords":[],"path":"reference/invariants.html","sectionTitle":"Reference","summary":"Contract, lifecycle, cross-contract, liveness, and economic properties.","text":"Each entry separates the kind of property from its current enforcement status. A status describes the strongest claim supported by the local implementation and tests; it is not a formal proof. Type Meaning Safety A forbidden caller, state, transition, or replay cannot succeed. Conservation Assets, liabilities, supplies, and claims reconcile without duplication or loss. Liveness A required permissionless action remains executable under the stated conditions. Enforcement status Meaning Contract guard A direct authorization, bound, or state check enforces the property locally. Tested property The cited regression suite checks this condition on concrete inputs or state transitions. Reviewed preservation Several contracts cooperate to preserve the property in reviewed flows and tests. External assumption Solidity cannot guarantee the property without honest participation, liquidity, transaction inclusion, or external token behavior. Automated tooling guard Release, deployment, or CI tooling rejects a mismatched artifact, address, or runtime-code identity before it is accepted. The index lists the core universe properties and security pool accounting boundaries. Identifiers name the property being reviewed; they are not contract entrypoints.","title":"Statoblast and Zoltar invariants","topic":"Safety","weight":0},{"fragment":"standing","heading":"Terms used in the catalog","keywords":[],"path":"reference/invariants.html","sectionTitle":"Reference","summary":"Contract, lifecycle, cross-contract, liveness, and economic properties.","text":"Ledger question The question whose outcome determines the question ledger 's winning outcome shares and Escalation Game payouts. Fork question The ended question recorded by the universe fork. It may differ from the ledger question. Child branch A well-formed answer encoding for the fork question, used to derive one child universe from its parent. Question ledgers' binary question paths specifically use Invalid, Yes, and No. Continuation game A child Escalation Game initialized from an unresolved parent snapshot. See fork continuation . Carry Unresolved deposits carried across a fork. See Merkle Mountain Range carry proofs . Nullifier A record that prevents a carried deposit from being claimed twice. Fixed payout outcome The ledger question's outcome a continuation game must use after its deadline, regardless of its copied balance leader. Direct fork A caller invokes Zoltar's universe-fork entrypoint directly rather than through a question ledger's own non-decision path. Recursive fork A fork of a child universe created by an earlier fork. Tracked balance An amount recorded by protocol accounting; it excludes unsolicited assets unless a named transition explicitly credits them. Consumed claim A deposit, bid, or proof that has already been settled, refunded, exported, or otherwise made unusable.","title":"Statoblast and Zoltar invariants","topic":"Safety","weight":0},{"fragment":"invariant-index","heading":"Invariant index","keywords":[],"path":"reference/invariants.html","sectionTitle":"Reference","summary":"Contract, lifecycle, cross-contract, liveness, and economic properties.","text":"For pair and router properties, see Trading invariants . Identifier Property Type Enforcement status UNI-01 One fork per universe Safety Contract guard UNI-02 Valid fork questions Safety Contract guard UNI-03 Fork threshold burn and credit Conservation Contract guard UNI-04 Deterministic child universe IDs Safety Contract guard UNI-05 Well-formed child outcomes Safety Contract guard UNI-06 Per-child migration mint limit Safety Contract guard UNI-07 Branch-specific child REP Conservation Reviewed preservation UNI-08 Child theoretical supply snapshot Conservation Reviewed preservation UNI-09 Child REP supply coherence Conservation Reviewed preservation QST-01 Question registry and classifier coherence Safety Reviewed preservation AUTHORITY Security pool identity and question admission Safety Contract guard POOL-ACCOUNTING Separate capital, settlement and reporter custody Conservation Reviewed preservation CAPITAL-HEALTH Owner commitments and loss isolation Safety Reviewed preservation CAPITAL-EXIT Retirement and withdrawal accounting Conservation Reviewed preservation SHARE-SETTLEMENT Question shares and backing floor Conservation Reviewed preservation ESCALATION Reporter liabilities and historical proofs Conservation Reviewed preservation FORKS Shared fork choices and lazy questions Safety Reviewed preservation AUCTIONS Uniform branch auction and automatic owners Conservation Reviewed preservation AUCTION-REFUNDS Bid and refund ownership Conservation Reviewed preservation ORACLE Shared price acceptance and exposure Safety Reviewed preservation LIFECYCLE Bounded work and explicit recovery Liveness Reviewed preservation EXTERNAL-CALLS Authority before external effects Safety Reviewed preservation INV-01 Healthy price boundary Safety Tested property INV-02 REP grid per lot Conservation Tested property INV-09 Retained commitment Safety Tested property INV-10 Distress age after materialization Safety Tested property INV-03 Maximum removable fee units Conservation Tested property INV-04 Minimum required cash Conservation Tested property INV-05 Incumbent value on issuance Conservation Tested property INV-06 Immediate unit redemption Conservation Tested property INV-07 Remaining backing after redemption Conservation Tested property INV-08 Checkpoint frequency Conservation Tested property INV-46 Funding target through runoff Conservation Tested property INV-47 New shares and recovery REP Conservation Tested property INV-50 Base purchase fee Conservation Tested property INV-59 Deferred service cash Conservation Tested property INV-60 Recovery REP through subsequent forks Conservation Tested property INV-11 Cash follows chosen commitment Conservation Tested property INV-12 Rounding across fully chosen branches Conservation Tested property INV-13 Partial recovery across questions Conservation Tested property INV-14 Transferable recovery rights Conservation Tested property INV-15 Branch clock conversion Safety Tested property INV-16 First inherited fixing Safety Tested property INV-17 Prepaid reporting horizon Conservation Tested property INV-49 Fork admission shortfall Safety Tested property INV-18 Longest fundable prefix Safety Tested property INV-19 Gross and net proceeds Conservation Tested property INV-20 Closing observation Safety Tested property INV-21 Winner cursor Conservation Tested property INV-22 Ownership through detachment Conservation Tested property INV-51 Auction tree height and weights Safety Tested property INV-54 Exact early refund eligibility Safety Tested property INV-61 Order of materialization Conservation Tested property INV-23 Median and provisional outcome Safety Tested property INV-24 Last open curve second Safety Tested property INV-25 Partitioned ordinary payouts Conservation Tested property INV-26 Carry proof geometry Safety Tested property INV-27 Outward price bounds Safety Tested property INV-28 Canonical observation updates Safety Tested property INV-29 Deferred report recovery Safety Tested property INV-30 Growth-proof withdrawal floor Safety Tested property INV-31 Sponsor operation order Safety Tested property INV-48 Two-asset report funding Safety Tested property INV-58 Escrow delivery rights Conservation Tested property INV-39 Unused child mint capacity Conservation Tested property INV-40 Threshold steps Safety Tested property INV-41 Adjacent historical checkpoints Safety Tested property INV-42 Order of maturity updates Safety Tested property INV-43 Creation bytecode round trip Safety Tested property INV-44 Deployment mask Safety Tested property INV-45 Scalar label uniqueness Safety Tested property INV-52 Frozen history allocation Safety Tested property INV-53 Canonical event replay Safety Tested property INV-57 Authorization namespaces Safety Tested property","title":"Statoblast and Zoltar invariants","topic":"Safety","weight":0},{"fragment":"zoltar-invariants","heading":"Zoltar invariants","keywords":[],"path":"reference/invariants.html","sectionTitle":"Reference","summary":"Contract, lifecycle, cross-contract, liveness, and economic properties.","text":"These properties govern core universe creation, REP supply, universe forks, deterministic child identities, and branch-specific REP migration.","title":"Statoblast and Zoltar invariants","topic":"Safety","weight":0},{"fragment":"universes","heading":"Universes, REP supply, and migration","keywords":[],"path":"reference/invariants.html","sectionTitle":"Reference","summary":"Contract, lifecycle, cross-contract, liveness, and economic properties.","text":"","title":"Statoblast and Zoltar invariants","topic":"Safety","weight":0},{"fragment":"uni-01","heading":"UNI-01 One fork per universe","keywords":[],"path":"reference/invariants.html","sectionTitle":"Reference","summary":"Contract, lifecycle, cross-contract, liveness, and economic properties.","text":"Required property An initialized universe records at most one fork time and one fork question. Example: After a universe forks on question 17, a second call attempting to fork it on question 22 reverts. Type Safety Enforcement status Contract guard Primary evidence Zoltar.forkUniverse","title":"Statoblast and Zoltar invariants","topic":"Safety","weight":0},{"fragment":"uni-02","heading":"UNI-02 Valid fork questions","keywords":[],"path":"reference/invariants.html","sectionTitle":"Reference","summary":"Contract, lifecycle, cross-contract, liveness, and economic properties.","text":"Required property forkUniverse accepts only a question that exists in ZoltarQuestionData and whose end time has been reached ( block.timestamp >= endTime ). A question ledger may separately require the fork question to equal its ledger question. Zoltar intentionally imposes no question-creation age, universe registration, or security-pool-relevance condition. Example: At exactly a known question's end time, a caller may fork a universe even when that question came from another universe or was created with an already-past end time; an unknown or still-open question is rejected. Requiring a universe-relevant or sufficiently aged question is not a Zoltar invariant. Type Safety Enforcement status Contract guard Primary evidence forkUniverse , question model , question-end fork boundary test Related assumption A15 intended question selection","title":"Statoblast and Zoltar invariants","topic":"Safety","weight":0},{"fragment":"uni-03","heading":"UNI-03 Fork threshold burn and credit","keywords":[],"path":"reference/invariants.html","sectionTitle":"Reference","summary":"Contract, lifecycle, cross-contract, liveness, and economic properties.","text":"Required property The fork threshold is ⌈parent theoretical supply / forkThresholdDivisor⌉ for nonzero supply, and zero only when supply is zero. Starting a fork burns that amount of the initiator's parent REP, reduces the parent theoretical supply by the same amount, and credits the threshold minus ⌊threshold / forkBurnDivisor⌋ for migration. The configured forkBurnDivisor is at least 5 , so the uncredited fork haircut cannot exceed 20%. Example: With a 1,000 REP theoretical supply, threshold divisor 20, and burn divisor 5, the initiator commits 50 parent REP, pays a 10 REP fork haircut, and receives 40 REP of migration credit. Type Conservation Enforcement status Contract guard Primary evidence getForkThresholdAttoRep and forkUniverse , fork-threshold rounding test","title":"Statoblast and Zoltar invariants","topic":"Safety","weight":0},{"fragment":"uni-04","heading":"UNI-04 Deterministic child universe IDs","keywords":[],"path":"reference/invariants.html","sectionTitle":"Reference","summary":"Contract, lifecycle, cross-contract, liveness, and economic properties.","text":"Required property A child universe identity is exactly the truncated hash of its parent universe and fork outcome; one parent-outcome pair maps to one child. Example: Repeatedly deriving the Yes child of the same parent returns the same universe ID, while deriving its No child returns a different ID. Type Safety Enforcement status Contract guard Primary evidence getChildUniverseId , deterministic child-ID test","title":"Statoblast and Zoltar invariants","topic":"Safety","weight":0},{"fragment":"uni-05","heading":"UNI-05 Well-formed child outcomes","keywords":[],"path":"reference/invariants.html","sectionTitle":"Reference","summary":"Contract, lifecycle, cross-contract, liveness, and economic properties.","text":"Required property Only well-formed outcomes of the recorded fork question can deploy child universes or receive migrated REP. Example: For a binary categorical fork question whose valid indexes are 0, 1, and 2, passing index 3 cannot deploy a child or receive child REP. Type Safety Enforcement status Contract guard Primary evidence deployChild and _splitRep , malformed-outcome rejection tests","title":"Statoblast and Zoltar invariants","topic":"Safety","weight":0},{"fragment":"uni-06","heading":"UNI-06 Per-child migration mint limit","keywords":[],"path":"reference/invariants.html","sectionTitle":"Reference","summary":"Contract, lifecycle, cross-contract, liveness, and economic properties.","text":"Required property For one migrator and one child, cumulative child REP minted never exceeds that migrator's parent migration balance. Example: A migrator with 40 REP of migration credit cannot mint 25 REP and later mint another 20 REP into the same child. Type Safety Enforcement status Contract guard Primary evidence childMigrationRepAmountsAttoRep","title":"Statoblast and Zoltar invariants","topic":"Safety","weight":0},{"fragment":"uni-07","heading":"UNI-07 Branch-specific child REP","keywords":[],"path":"reference/invariants.html","sectionTitle":"Reference","summary":"Contract, lifecycle, cross-contract, liveness, and economic properties.","text":"Required property One burned parent migration balance may mint the same credited amount into several selected children. Each minted balance belongs to a different child REP token, and migration never restores the burned parent REP. Example: A 40 REP migration credit may mint 40 Yes-child REP and 40 No-child REP, but neither balance is parent REP and the burned parent balance remains zero. Type Conservation Enforcement status Reviewed preservation Primary evidence _splitRep , ReputationToken","title":"Statoblast and Zoltar invariants","topic":"Safety","weight":0},{"fragment":"uni-08","heading":"UNI-08 Child theoretical supply snapshot","keywords":[],"path":"reference/invariants.html","sectionTitle":"Reference","summary":"Contract, lifecycle, cross-contract, liveness, and economic properties.","text":"Required property The child theoretical-supply snapshot equals the parent's theoretical supply after the threshold burn plus the initiator's migration credit net of the fork haircut. Each child therefore excludes the uncredited fork haircut. Example: If a fork removes 50 parent REP and credits 40 migration REP, each child's theoretical snapshot is 10 REP below the pre-fork supply. Type Conservation Enforcement status Reviewed preservation Primary evidence childUniverseTheoreticalSupplySnapshotsAttoRep , common child supply snapshot test","title":"Statoblast and Zoltar invariants","topic":"Safety","weight":0},{"fragment":"uni-09","heading":"UNI-09 Child REP supply coherence","keywords":[],"path":"reference/invariants.html","sectionTitle":"Reference","summary":"Contract, lifecycle, cross-contract, liveness, and economic properties.","text":"Required property For each child REP token, total minted supply equals the sum of holder balances, never exceeds the child's theoretical-supply snapshot, and uses the same theoretical maximum recorded by the child universe. Example: If two migrators mint 12 and 7 child REP, the child token supply is 19 REP, both balances sum to 19 REP, and minting does not change the child's theoretical maximum. Type Conservation Enforcement status Reviewed preservation Primary evidence splitMigrationRep and child universe accounting , ReputationToken supply , aggregate child-supply invariant test","title":"Statoblast and Zoltar invariants","topic":"Safety","weight":0},{"fragment":"questions","heading":"Questions and answer classification","keywords":[],"path":"reference/invariants.html","sectionTitle":"Reference","summary":"Contract, lifecycle, cross-contract, liveness, and economic properties.","text":"","title":"Statoblast and Zoltar invariants","topic":"Safety","weight":0},{"fragment":"qst-01","heading":"QST-01 Question registry and classifier coherence","keywords":[],"path":"reference/invariants.html","sectionTitle":"Reference","summary":"Contract, lifecycle, cross-contract, liveness, and economic properties.","text":"Required property Every successful question creation appends its deterministic ID exactly once and leaves prior question data and labels immutable. For every stored question and answer, the malformed classifier is true exactly when the public answer name is Malformed . Example: Appending a scalar question after two categorical questions preserves the first two records and their order; a scalar answer with reserved bits is both classified and named malformed. Type Safety Enforcement status Reviewed preservation Primary evidence createQuestion , getQuestions , and answer classification , registry and classifier invariant test","title":"Statoblast and Zoltar invariants","topic":"Safety","weight":0},{"fragment":"statoblast-invariants","heading":"Shared security pool invariants","keywords":[],"path":"reference/invariants.html","sectionTitle":"Reference","summary":"Contract, lifecycle, cross-contract, liveness, and economic properties.","text":"These properties apply to every security pool, including fixed one-question security pools. The module reference describes the corresponding accounting and authority boundaries.","title":"Statoblast and Zoltar invariants","topic":"Safety","weight":0},{"fragment":"authority","heading":"AUTHORITY Security pool identity and question admission","keywords":[],"path":"reference/invariants.html","sectionTitle":"Reference","summary":"Contract, lifecycle, cross-contract, liveness, and economic properties.","text":"Required property The canonical factory deploys every security pool from its pinned creation code and module deployers, and only a security pool it deployed can open a security pool namespace. Every registered question ledger therefore belongs to a factory-deployed security pool, and the factory namespace binds each ledger to it. The immutable admission policy controls registration. A fixed one-question security pool uses the same capital, settlement and reporting contracts as an open security pool. Type Safety Enforcement status Contract guard Primary evidence SecurityPoolFactory.sol , SecurityPool.sol , auditSecurityPoolAuthenticity.test.ts and securityPool.test.ts","title":"Statoblast and Zoltar invariants","topic":"Safety","weight":0},{"fragment":"pool-accounting","heading":"POOL-ACCOUNTING Separate capital, settlement and reporter custody","keywords":[],"path":"reference/invariants.html","sectionTitle":"Reference","summary":"Contract, lifecycle, cross-contract, liveness, and economic properties.","text":"Required property Provider REP and commitments belong to the capital ledger; question cash and outcome supply belong to the settlement book; reporter REP belongs to the reporter ledger. A reporting deposit does not acquire underwriting rights, and a trader purchase does not create a provider position. Type Conservation Enforcement status Reviewed preservation Primary evidence SecurityPoolSettlement.sol and securityPoolSettlement.test.ts","title":"Statoblast and Zoltar invariants","topic":"Safety","weight":0},{"fragment":"capital-health","heading":"CAPITAL-HEALTH Owner commitments and loss isolation","keywords":[],"path":"reference/invariants.html","sectionTitle":"Reference","summary":"Contract, lifecycle, cross-contract, liveness, and economic properties.","text":"Required property Each owner authorizes its commitment and health target. Health counts that owner’s free and pledged principal against its full commitment. A liquidation affects the target’s position; other owners do not absorb its penalty. Unvested REP income is excluded from principal health. Type Safety Enforcement status Reviewed preservation Primary evidence SecurityPoolCapitalLedger.sol and securityPoolCapitalTakeoverBounds.test.ts","title":"Statoblast and Zoltar invariants","topic":"Safety","weight":0},{"fragment":"capital-exit","heading":"CAPITAL-EXIT Retirement and withdrawal accounting","keywords":[],"path":"reference/invariants.html","sectionTitle":"Reference","summary":"Contract, lifecycle, cross-contract, liveness, and economic properties.","text":"Required property An owner cannot withdraw principal needed by its live commitment or pending risk. Retirement immediately removes capacity available to new purchases while the settlement book tracks when the old demand becomes releasable. Owners do not claim separately for every question. Type Conservation Enforcement status Reviewed preservation Primary evidence SecurityPoolCapitalLifecycleDelegate.sol and securityPoolCapitalLifecycle.test.ts","title":"Statoblast and Zoltar invariants","topic":"Safety","weight":0},{"fragment":"share-settlement","heading":"SHARE-SETTLEMENT Question shares and backing floor","keywords":[],"path":"reference/invariants.html","sectionTitle":"Reference","summary":"Contract, lifecycle, cross-contract, liveness, and economic properties.","text":"Required property Each question has its own outcome shares and cash entitlement. Purchases check healthy aggregate capacity and the configured minimum backing floor. Complete-set returns release their demand; fees stop before settlement backing would fall below the floor. Finalized claims use their protected settlement state. Type Conservation Enforcement status Reviewed preservation Primary evidence SecurityPoolSettlementBook.sol and securityPoolSettlementBook.test.ts","title":"Statoblast and Zoltar invariants","topic":"Safety","weight":0},{"fragment":"escalation","heading":"ESCALATION Reporter liabilities and historical proofs","keywords":[],"path":"reference/invariants.html","sectionTitle":"Reference","summary":"Contract, lifecycle, cross-contract, liveness, and economic properties.","text":"Required property Ordinary settlement fixes winning payout liabilities and required burns before paying individual claims. Inherited claims authenticate frozen Merkle-mountain-range membership and reject consumed deposit identities. Standalone games also require nullifier proofs; see the carry proof reference . Replaying a consumed proof cannot pay again. Type Conservation Enforcement status Reviewed preservation Primary evidence EscalationGameSettlement.sol and forkMigrationClaims.test.ts","title":"Statoblast and Zoltar invariants","topic":"Safety","weight":0},{"fragment":"forks","heading":"FORKS Shared fork choices and lazy questions","keywords":[],"path":"reference/invariants.html","sectionTitle":"Reference","summary":"Contract, lifecycle, cross-contract, liveness, and economic properties.","text":"Required property Each owner records one branch choice before the fixed cutoff. Shared books snapshot the branch once; question ledgers and reporter snapshots are materialized on demand. Materialization cannot extend the native choice, auction or activation deadlines. Type Safety Enforcement status Reviewed preservation Primary evidence SecurityPoolForkCoordinator.sol and securityPoolForkCoordinator.test.ts","title":"Statoblast and Zoltar invariants","topic":"Safety","weight":0},{"fragment":"auctions","heading":"AUCTIONS Uniform branch auction and automatic owners","keywords":[],"path":"reference/invariants.html","sectionTitle":"Reference","summary":"Contract, lifecycle, cross-contract, liveness, and economic properties.","text":"Required property Only freshly unchosen provider REP is auction inventory; reporter escrow and previously owed recovery REP are excluded. Winners pay the same grid-rounded REP-per-lot price, and every winning lot receives at least the REP that backs it at the closing conservative price. Winning principal and obligations enter aggregate accounting at closing even if a bidder delays activating individual controls. Type Conservation Enforcement status Reviewed preservation Primary evidence SecurityPoolUniformPriceRepairAuction.sol and securityPoolCapitalAuction.test.ts","title":"Statoblast and Zoltar invariants","topic":"Safety","weight":0},{"fragment":"auction-refunds","heading":"AUCTION-REFUNDS Bid and refund ownership","keywords":[],"path":"reference/invariants.html","sectionTitle":"Reference","summary":"Contract, lifecycle, cross-contract, liveness, and economic properties.","text":"Required property Early refund eligibility must be proven by the auction’s irreversible losing condition. Refund credits belong to the bidder and are withdrawn independently of winner activation. The auction cannot require an unbounded scan of losing bids before honest winners can act. Type Conservation Enforcement status Reviewed preservation Primary evidence SecurityPoolUniformPriceRepairAuction.sol and securityPoolAuctionSafetyBounds.test.ts","title":"Statoblast and Zoltar invariants","topic":"Safety","weight":0},{"fragment":"oracle","heading":"ORACLE Shared price acceptance and exposure","keywords":[],"path":"reference/invariants.html","sectionTitle":"Reference","summary":"Contract, lifecycle, cross-contract, liveness, and economic properties.","text":"Required property Independent report candidates cannot reserve exclusive access. Acceptance checks price freshness, funded coverage and the security pool reporting window and atomically checkpoints capital under the previous price. A rejected or disputed renewal cannot extend the accepted price’s expiry. Type Safety Enforcement status Reviewed preservation Primary evidence SharedPoolPriceFeed.sol and securityPoolSharedFeed.test.ts","title":"Statoblast and Zoltar invariants","topic":"Safety","weight":0},{"fragment":"lifecycle","heading":"LIFECYCLE Bounded work and explicit recovery","keywords":[],"path":"reference/invariants.html","sectionTitle":"Reference","summary":"Contract, lifecycle, cross-contract, liveness, and economic properties.","text":"Required property Capital and settlement operations use fixed-width indexes and aggregate accounting rather than scanning every owner or question. User-supplied claim batches are divisible. Inherited claims preserve the authenticated leaf’s principal and reward coordinates at every fork depth; settlement requires membership proofs and consumed-identity checks, with additional nullifier proofs for standalone games. Type Liveness Enforcement status Reviewed preservation Primary evidence securityPoolAuctionGas.test.ts","title":"Statoblast and Zoltar invariants","topic":"Safety","weight":0},{"fragment":"external-calls","heading":"EXTERNAL-CALLS Authority before external effects","keywords":[],"path":"reference/invariants.html","sectionTitle":"Reference","summary":"Contract, lifecycle, cross-contract, liveness, and economic properties.","text":"Required property Canonical module bindings and caller guards protect delegated accounting. Transaction failures roll back coupled state changes. External REP and oracle assumptions remain explicit in the security model; these invariants do not guarantee an honest market price or transaction inclusion. Type Safety Enforcement status Reviewed preservation Primary evidence SecurityPool.sol and securityPoolDelivery.test.ts","title":"Statoblast and Zoltar invariants","topic":"Safety","weight":0},{"fragment":"capital-health-and-retirement","heading":"Capital health and retirement","keywords":[],"path":"reference/invariants.html","sectionTitle":"Reference","summary":"Contract, lifecycle, cross-contract, liveness, and economic properties.","text":"","title":"Statoblast and Zoltar invariants","topic":"Safety","weight":0},{"fragment":"inv-01","heading":"INV-01 Healthy price boundary","keywords":[],"path":"reference/invariants.html","sectionTitle":"Reference","summary":"Contract, lifecycle, cross-contract, liveness, and economic properties.","text":"Required property For positive commitment K , multiplier M , price p and target health H ≥ 10,000 , backing R suffices exactly when p ≤ maximumHealthyPrice(⌊R × 10,000 / H⌋, K, M) . At the price immediately above the boundary the same backing is insufficient. Type Safety Enforcement status Tested property Primary evidence securityPoolCommitmentIndex.test.ts","title":"Statoblast and Zoltar invariants","topic":"Safety","weight":0},{"fragment":"inv-02","heading":"INV-02 REP grid per lot","keywords":[],"path":"reference/invariants.html","sectionTitle":"Reference","summary":"Contract, lifecycle, cross-contract, liveness, and economic properties.","text":"Required property Let the auction REP grid be M / gcd(M, 10,000) . For positive commitment per lot and grid-aligned REP per lot, the protocol-floor maximum healthy price is identical for one lot and any representable multiple. An owner’s voluntary health target can introduce a separate rounding step. Type Conservation Enforcement status Tested property Primary evidence securityPoolCommitmentIndex.test.ts","title":"Statoblast and Zoltar invariants","topic":"Safety","weight":0},{"fragment":"inv-09","heading":"INV-09 Retained commitment","keywords":[],"path":"reference/invariants.html","sectionTitle":"Reference","summary":"Contract, lifecycle, cross-contract, liveness, and economic properties.","text":"Required property A retirement request for part of an owner’s commitment removes only the requested reduction from eligibility for new purchases. The healthy retained part stays eligible. Completing a retirement cannot withdraw capital already needed to back live demand. Type Safety Enforcement status Tested property Primary evidence securityPoolCommitmentIndex.test.ts","title":"Statoblast and Zoltar invariants","topic":"Safety","weight":0},{"fragment":"inv-10","heading":"INV-10 Distress age after materialization","keywords":[],"path":"reference/invariants.html","sectionTitle":"Reference","summary":"Contract, lifecycle, cross-contract, liveness, and economic properties.","text":"Required property Splitting an auction position transfers its original adverse-observation history to the materialized lots. Ordinary new owners and imported positions have their own first applicable observation. A later return to the same price does not reconstruct the prior distress age. Type Safety Enforcement status Tested property Primary evidence securityPoolAuctionPositionSplit.test.ts","title":"Statoblast and Zoltar invariants","topic":"Safety","weight":0},{"fragment":"settlement-units-and-fees","heading":"Settlement units and fees","keywords":[],"path":"reference/invariants.html","sectionTitle":"Reference","summary":"Contract, lifecycle, cross-contract, liveness, and economic properties.","text":"","title":"Statoblast and Zoltar invariants","topic":"Safety","weight":0},{"fragment":"inv-03","heading":"INV-03 Maximum removable fee units","keywords":[],"path":"reference/invariants.html","sectionTitle":"Reference","summary":"Contract, lifecycle, cross-contract, liveness, and economic properties.","text":"Required property For an already funded question with nonzero net cash and total book units, the fee budget is the largest unit removal that preserves its rounded minimum backing at the quoted book price. Removing the next unit breaks that floor when another unit exists and the required floor is positive. An underfunded question has zero fee budget. Type Conservation Enforcement status Tested property Primary evidence invariantMath.test.ts","title":"Statoblast and Zoltar invariants","topic":"Safety","weight":0},{"fragment":"inv-04","heading":"INV-04 Minimum required cash","keywords":[],"path":"reference/invariants.html","sectionTitle":"Reference","summary":"Contract, lifecycle, cross-contract, liveness, and economic properties.","text":"Required property Required cash is the least raw amount c such that (c × S − r) × d ≥ u × n , where S = 10⁹ , r is the carried fee remainder, u the units, and n/d the target ratio. Computing this without the carried remainder can change admission by one raw unit. Type Conservation Enforcement status Tested property Primary evidence invariantMath.test.ts","title":"Statoblast and Zoltar invariants","topic":"Safety","weight":0},{"fragment":"inv-05","heading":"INV-05 Incumbent value on issuance","keywords":[],"path":"reference/invariants.html","sectionTitle":"Reference","summary":"Contract, lifecycle, cross-contract, liveness, and economic properties.","text":"Required property Minting units against a funded book weakly increases the incumbent net cash per unit. With pre-mint net scaled cash N , units U , deposit C in raw cash units, settlement scale S = 10⁹ , and new units a , the exact rounding residual satisfies 0 ≤ C × S × U − a × N < N . Type Conservation Enforcement status Tested property Primary evidence invariantMath.test.ts","title":"Statoblast and Zoltar invariants","topic":"Safety","weight":0},{"fragment":"inv-06","heading":"INV-06 Immediate unit redemption","keywords":[],"path":"reference/invariants.html","sectionTitle":"Reference","summary":"Contract, lifecycle, cross-contract, liveness, and economic properties.","text":"Required property Immediately redeeming exactly the new units from a successful funded-book mint returns either C or C−1 raw units, holding fee remainder and book composition otherwise fixed. This bound applies to the two unit-conversion functions. A full user transaction can also pay fees or acquire other rights. Type Conservation Enforcement status Tested property Primary evidence invariantMath.test.ts","title":"Statoblast and Zoltar invariants","topic":"Safety","weight":0},{"fragment":"inv-07","heading":"INV-07 Remaining backing after redemption","keywords":[],"path":"reference/invariants.html","sectionTitle":"Reference","summary":"Contract, lifecycle, cross-contract, liveness, and economic properties.","text":"Required property A partial redemption can only increase or preserve net backing per unit for the remaining book. With b<U burned, the amount paid is rounded down and the scaled rounding residue is strictly below U × S in the cross-multiplied comparison. The last burn returns all remaining raw cash. Type Conservation Enforcement status Tested property Primary evidence invariantMath.test.ts","title":"Statoblast and Zoltar invariants","topic":"Safety","weight":0},{"fragment":"inv-08","heading":"INV-08 Checkpoint frequency","keywords":[],"path":"reference/invariants.html","sectionTitle":"Reference","summary":"Contract, lifecycle, cross-contract, liveness, and economic properties.","text":"Required property With a fixed set of funded capital fee streams and fixed eligibility, subdividing a time interval into checkpoints gives exactly the same cumulative vested amount for each token. Fractional accrual survives intermediate checkpoints. At the last stream expiry all funded amounts have vested. Type Conservation Enforcement status Tested property Primary evidence securityPoolCapitalFeeStreams.test.ts","title":"Statoblast and Zoltar invariants","topic":"Safety","weight":0},{"fragment":"inv-46","heading":"INV-46 Funding target through runoff","keywords":[],"path":"reference/invariants.html","sectionTitle":"Reference","summary":"Contract, lifecycle, cross-contract, liveness, and economic properties.","text":"Required property Forking an underfunded settlement book with live units preserves its funding target. Repeated partial or zero funding cannot lower the amount required for those same liabilities. A book with unchanged units is still in runoff at one raw unit below the target and is funded at equality. Type Conservation Enforcement status Tested property Primary evidence securityPoolSettlementBook.test.ts","title":"Statoblast and Zoltar invariants","topic":"Safety","weight":0},{"fragment":"inv-47","heading":"INV-47 New shares and recovery REP","keywords":[],"path":"reference/invariants.html","sectionTitle":"Reference","summary":"Contract, lifecycle, cross-contract, liveness, and economic properties.","text":"Required property If a live question owns all book units and all associated recovery REP R , issuing a new shares against prior supply s>0 requires exactly ⌈R × a / s⌉ new REP. A genuinely new question with zero prior outcome supply has a zero recovery-REP quote. Type Conservation Enforcement status Tested property Primary evidence securityPoolSettlementBook.test.ts","title":"Statoblast and Zoltar invariants","topic":"Safety","weight":0},{"fragment":"inv-50","heading":"INV-50 Base purchase fee","keywords":[],"path":"reference/invariants.html","sectionTitle":"Reference","summary":"Contract, lifecycle, cross-contract, liveness, and economic properties.","text":"Required property Base purchase fees depend on the configured fee minimum, security multiplier, fee rate and purchase cash. Under unchanged configuration a temporary increase in idle healthy underwriting capacity cannot discount the base fee. Round the backing value up before charging the percentage. Prepaid resolution premium is a separate component. Type Conservation Enforcement status Tested property Primary evidence securityPoolResolutionEconomics.test.ts","title":"Statoblast and Zoltar invariants","topic":"Safety","weight":0},{"fragment":"inv-59","heading":"INV-59 Deferred service cash","keywords":[],"path":"reference/invariants.html","sectionTitle":"Reference","summary":"Contract, lifecycle, cross-contract, liveness, and economic properties.","text":"Required property Deferred service cash has an owner or a fixed burn destination. Claiming a capital ledger’s assigned cash cannot consume its neighbor’s allocation. Flushing a pending burn pays only the burn address. If delivery reverts, the pending amount remains claimable and can be flushed after delivery resumes. Type Conservation Enforcement status Tested property Primary evidence securityPoolCollateral.test.ts and securityPoolPreparationPremium.test.ts","title":"Statoblast and Zoltar invariants","topic":"Safety","weight":0},{"fragment":"inv-60","heading":"INV-60 Recovery REP through subsequent forks","keywords":[],"path":"reference/invariants.html","sectionTitle":"Reference","summary":"Contract, lifecycle, cross-contract, liveness, and economic properties.","text":"Required property For each recovery fork, the increment in newly surrendered REP belongs to the question units present at that fork. Subsequent holding fees cannot retroactively shrink that allocation. Inherited recovery REP retains its previous ownership through another fork, including after partial burns. Type Conservation Enforcement status Tested property Primary evidence securityPoolSettlementRecoveryFees.test.ts","title":"Statoblast and Zoltar invariants","topic":"Safety","weight":0},{"fragment":"fork-choices-and-recovery","heading":"Fork choices and recovery","keywords":[],"path":"reference/invariants.html","sectionTitle":"Reference","summary":"Contract, lifecycle, cross-contract, liveness, and economic properties.","text":"","title":"Statoblast and Zoltar invariants","topic":"Safety","weight":0},{"fragment":"inv-11","heading":"INV-11 Cash follows chosen commitment","keywords":[],"path":"reference/invariants.html","sectionTitle":"Reference","summary":"Contract, lifecycle, cross-contract, liveness, and economic properties.","text":"Required property At a frozen source with cash C and commitment K>0 , a branch with chosen commitment k receives ⌊C × k / K⌋ before child funding adjustments. Grouping or reordering owners who make the same choices cannot change that amount. Type Conservation Enforcement status Tested property Primary evidence securityPoolForkCoordinator.test.ts","title":"Statoblast and Zoltar invariants","topic":"Safety","weight":0},{"fragment":"inv-12","heading":"INV-12 Rounding across fully chosen branches","keywords":[],"path":"reference/invariants.html","sectionTitle":"Reference","summary":"Contract, lifecycle, cross-contract, liveness, and economic properties.","text":"Required property If all source commitment chooses among b>0 nonempty branches, unassigned cash from rounding is between zero and b−1 raw units. If any commitment remains unchosen, this bound no longer applies to the entire neutral cash balance. Type Conservation Enforcement status Tested property Primary evidence securityPoolForkCoordinator.test.ts","title":"Statoblast and Zoltar invariants","topic":"Safety","weight":0},{"fragment":"inv-13","heading":"INV-13 Partial recovery across questions","keywords":[],"path":"reference/invariants.html","sectionTitle":"Reference","summary":"Contract, lifecycle, cross-contract, liveness, and economic properties.","text":"Required property For eligible recovery questions at a fixed source snapshot, neutral cash N , total units U>0 , question units uᵢ , supply sᵢ>0 and cumulative recovered shares xᵢ , total cash paid from zero recovered shares equals ⌊N × Σ ⌊uᵢ × xᵢ / sᵢ⌋ / U⌋ . Splitting or reordering claims that end at the same vector leaves total payout unchanged. Individual claimants may receive different rounding units. Type Conservation Enforcement status Tested property Primary evidence securityPoolResolutionPremiumBook.test.ts","title":"Statoblast and Zoltar invariants","topic":"Safety","weight":0},{"fragment":"inv-14","heading":"INV-14 Transferable recovery rights","keywords":[],"path":"reference/invariants.html","sectionTitle":"Reference","summary":"Contract, lifecycle, cross-contract, liveness, and economic properties.","text":"Required property In an eligible source, a holder’s recovery rights for each outcome equal source shares less previously detached rights, plus independently received credits. Forwarding credits spends them first. Consuming x complete sets reduces each of the three rights by exactly x and leaves child shares unchanged. Type Conservation Enforcement status Tested property Primary evidence shareTokenSecurityPoolRecovery.test.ts","title":"Statoblast and Zoltar invariants","topic":"Safety","weight":0},{"fragment":"inv-15","heading":"INV-15 Branch clock conversion","keywords":[],"path":"reference/invariants.html","sectionTitle":"Reference","summary":"Contract, lifecycle, cross-contract, liveness, and economic properties.","text":"Required property Logical and native time conversions are mutual inverses on the running segment at or after the anchors, within the timestamp domain. All native times in a preparation plateau map to the same logical instant. A claim involving elapsed reporting time must use the correct clock. Type Safety Enforcement status Tested property Primary evidence fixedOutcomeEncoding.test.ts","title":"Statoblast and Zoltar invariants","topic":"Safety","weight":0},{"fragment":"inv-16","heading":"INV-16 First inherited fixing","keywords":[],"path":"reference/invariants.html","sectionTitle":"Reference","summary":"Contract, lifecycle, cross-contract, liveness, and economic properties.","text":"Required property The first ancestor that fixes a question determines the inherited outcome on that branch. A later fork on the same question cannot overwrite it. A fixed Invalid outcome remains distinguishable from absence. Siblings can have distinct fixed outcomes. Type Safety Enforcement status Tested property Primary evidence fixedOutcomeEncoding.test.ts","title":"Statoblast and Zoltar invariants","topic":"Safety","weight":0},{"fragment":"inv-17","heading":"INV-17 Prepaid reporting horizon","keywords":[],"path":"reference/invariants.html","sectionTitle":"Reference","summary":"Contract, lifecycle, cross-contract, liveness, and economic properties.","text":"Required property The first schedule fixes a question’s prepaid reporting horizon. Timely disputes may redistribute future earnings only through that horizon. An early-finality refund becomes effective strictly after its boundary. A refunded inherited balance cannot be revived in a descendant. Type Conservation Enforcement status Tested property Primary evidence securityPoolResolutionPremiumBook.test.ts","title":"Statoblast and Zoltar invariants","topic":"Safety","weight":0},{"fragment":"inv-49","heading":"INV-49 Fork admission shortfall","keywords":[],"path":"reference/invariants.html","sectionTitle":"Reference","summary":"Contract, lifecycle, cross-contract, liveness, and economic properties.","text":"Required property Funding a reporting-triggered fork with question escrow e>0 and current threshold T pulls max(0,T−e) from the payer. This top-up issues no separate winning claim. The triggering question’s post-admission balance is max(e,T)−⌊T/forkBurnDivisor⌋ . Unrelated question balances receive no admission debit. Type Safety Enforcement status Tested property Primary evidence ownForkAdmissionShortfall.test.ts","title":"Statoblast and Zoltar invariants","topic":"Safety","weight":0},{"fragment":"auction-clearing-and-ownership","heading":"Auction clearing and ownership","keywords":[],"path":"reference/invariants.html","sectionTitle":"Reference","summary":"Contract, lifecycle, cross-contract, liveness, and economic properties.","text":"","title":"Statoblast and Zoltar invariants","topic":"Safety","weight":0},{"fragment":"inv-18","heading":"INV-18 Longest fundable prefix","keywords":[],"path":"reference/invariants.html","sectionTitle":"Reference","summary":"Contract, lifecycle, cross-contract, liveness, and economic properties.","text":"Required property Sort eligible lots by bid minimum, then submission ID. For inventory I , grid-aligned closing floor F and the minimum pⱼ on the jth lot, a prefix of length j is fundable exactly when j × max(F,pⱼ) ≤ I and it fits the raise limit. The result is the longest such prefix. Raising the floor alone cannot increase the fill. Type Safety Enforcement status Tested property Primary evidence auditAuctionEngineFuzz.test.ts","title":"Statoblast and Zoltar invariants","topic":"Safety","weight":0},{"fragment":"inv-19","heading":"INV-19 Gross and net proceeds","keywords":[],"path":"reference/invariants.html","sectionTitle":"Reference","summary":"Contract, lifecycle, cross-contract, liveness, and economic properties.","text":"Required property The bid engine’s gross cash equals filled lots times lot size. In the repair adapter, cash delivered to the fork equals the lesser of gross cash and the original shortfall. The difference goes to the burn address. Engine gross, adapter net and burn must reconcile even with a one-unit shortfall. Type Conservation Enforcement status Tested property Primary evidence securityPoolAuctionFeeLotSizing.test.ts","title":"Statoblast and Zoltar invariants","topic":"Safety","weight":0},{"fragment":"inv-20","heading":"INV-20 Closing observation","keywords":[],"path":"reference/invariants.html","sectionTitle":"Reference","summary":"Contract, lifecycle, cross-contract, liveness, and economic properties.","text":"Required property Closing eligibility uses the close instant. The native cooldown places a reachable child fork after that instant. Such a fork cannot cancel an eligible auction if nobody has finalized it yet. A final-day observation can qualify even if its short ordinary price window has expired by the time finalization runs. Type Safety Enforcement status Tested property Primary evidence securityPoolCollateralRepairAuction.test.ts","title":"Statoblast and Zoltar invariants","topic":"Safety","weight":0},{"fragment":"inv-21","heading":"INV-21 Winner cursor","keywords":[],"path":"reference/invariants.html","sectionTitle":"Reference","summary":"Contract, lifecycle, cross-contract, liveness, and economic properties.","text":"Required property From a frozen auction book, repeatedly requesting the next unmaterialized bid visits each remaining winner exactly once in price and time priority. Settlement or materialization between cursor calls can remove a winner from future results. Bid IDs themselves need not increase. Type Conservation Enforcement status Tested property Primary evidence auditAuctionEngineFuzz.test.ts","title":"Statoblast and Zoltar invariants","topic":"Safety","weight":0},{"fragment":"inv-22","heading":"INV-22 Ownership through detachment","keywords":[],"path":"reference/invariants.html","sectionTitle":"Reference","summary":"Contract, lifecycle, cross-contract, liveness, and economic properties.","text":"Required property A winner detached from the aggregate position owns its accrued fees through detachment. Further fees credited to the remaining winners cannot flow back to it. The two tokens’ budgets each reconcile to detached credits plus remaining credits. Type Conservation Enforcement status Tested property Primary evidence securityPoolAuctionPositionSplit.test.ts","title":"Statoblast and Zoltar invariants","topic":"Safety","weight":0},{"fragment":"inv-51","heading":"INV-51 Auction tree height and weights","keywords":[],"path":"reference/invariants.html","sectionTitle":"Reference","summary":"Contract, lifecycle, cross-contract, liveness, and economic properties.","text":"Required property Every auction bid tree node has stored height 1 + max(leftHeight,rightHeight) , children differing by at most one level, and subtree lots equal to left lots plus its own plus right lots. Its traversal is ordered by minimum REP and then bid ID. The height of a valid tree of at most 2⁹⁶−1 bid IDs is at most 137, with a leaf at height one. Type Safety Enforcement status Tested property Primary evidence auditAuctionEngineFuzz.test.ts","title":"Statoblast and Zoltar invariants","topic":"Safety","weight":0},{"fragment":"inv-54","heading":"INV-54 Exact early refund eligibility","keywords":[],"path":"reference/invariants.html","sectionTitle":"Reference","summary":"Contract, lifecycle, cross-contract, liveness, and economic properties.","text":"Required property For an existing unsettled bid in an open auction, immediate early-refund eligibility is equivalent to receiving zero lots if bidding stopped now and the engine closed at its original maximum raise with floor zero. Once this most favorable close cannot include the bid, extensions of bidding, a higher floor, or a smaller raise limit cannot restore it. Type Safety Enforcement status Tested property Primary evidence auditAuctionEngineFuzz.test.ts","title":"Statoblast and Zoltar invariants","topic":"Safety","weight":0},{"fragment":"inv-61","heading":"INV-61 Order of materialization","keywords":[],"path":"reference/invariants.html","sectionTitle":"Reference","summary":"Contract, lifecycle, cross-contract, liveness, and economic properties.","text":"Required property If a fixed cash and REP award goes to the aggregate auction position before any further winner detaches, the individual amounts that belong to each winner are independent of detachment order. Each matches its pre-detachment preview. Later fee accrual changes the set of eligible owners and begins a new comparison. Type Conservation Enforcement status Tested property Primary evidence auditAuctionEngineFuzz.test.ts","title":"Statoblast and Zoltar invariants","topic":"Safety","weight":0},{"fragment":"reporting-and-price-observations","heading":"Reporting and price observations","keywords":[],"path":"reference/invariants.html","sectionTitle":"Reference","summary":"Contract, lifecycle, cross-contract, liveness, and economic properties.","text":"","title":"Statoblast and Zoltar invariants","topic":"Safety","weight":0},{"fragment":"inv-23","heading":"INV-23 Median and provisional outcome","keywords":[],"path":"reference/invariants.html","sectionTitle":"Reference","summary":"Contract, lifecycle, cross-contract, liveness, and economic properties.","text":"Required property For three ordinary outcome balances, reaching the non-decision threshold is equivalent to the median reaching it. At current cost at or below the median, the provisional result is None. Above the median a strict leader wins, a top tie remains None, and an empty game at positive cost gives Invalid. Type Safety Enforcement status Tested property Primary evidence reportingArithmetic.test.ts","title":"Statoblast and Zoltar invariants","topic":"Safety","weight":0},{"fragment":"inv-24","heading":"INV-24 Last open curve second","keywords":[],"path":"reference/invariants.html","sectionTitle":"Reference","summary":"Contract, lifecycle, cross-contract, liveness, and economic properties.","text":"Required property On an ordinary increasing attrition curve with an interior median, the last open second t satisfies cost(t) ≤ median < cost(t+1) . Changing the leader’s excess while preserving the median and all curve parameters cannot move that boundary. Fixed outcomes and endpoint cases have separate rules. Type Safety Enforcement status Tested property Primary evidence escalationCurveCache.test.ts","title":"Statoblast and Zoltar invariants","topic":"Safety","weight":0},{"fragment":"inv-25","heading":"INV-25 Partitioned ordinary payouts","keywords":[],"path":"reference/invariants.html","sectionTitle":"Reference","summary":"Contract, lifecycle, cross-contract, liveness, and economic properties.","text":"Required property For ordinary winning deposits, both REP paid and REP burned are differences on fixed cumulative curves. Partitioning a winning interval at the reward-window edge or a haircut rounding boundary preserves both totals exactly. Type Conservation Enforcement status Tested property Primary evidence reportingArithmetic.test.ts","title":"Statoblast and Zoltar invariants","topic":"Safety","weight":0},{"fragment":"inv-26","heading":"INV-26 Carry proof geometry","keywords":[],"path":"reference/invariants.html","sectionTitle":"Reference","summary":"Contract, lifecycle, cross-contract, liveness, and economic properties.","text":"Required property For every supported carry leaf count, the mountains partition the leaf interval without overlap or gaps. The containing peak fixes a leaf’s offset and branch orientation. Its proof contains exactly peakHeight + popcount(leafCount) − 1 sibling hashes. Type Safety Enforcement status Tested property Primary evidence reportingArithmetic.test.ts","title":"Statoblast and Zoltar invariants","topic":"Safety","weight":0},{"fragment":"inv-27","heading":"INV-27 Outward price bounds","keywords":[],"path":"reference/invariants.html","sectionTitle":"Reference","summary":"Contract, lifecycle, cross-contract, liveness, and economic properties.","text":"Required property With raw price p , percentage precision D and uncertainty 0 ≤ e < D , the liquidation bound is ⌊pD/(D+e)⌋ and the conservative bound is ⌈pD/(D−e)⌉ . Increasing only uncertainty weakly widens this interval. At zero uncertainty both equal p . Type Safety Enforcement status Tested property Primary evidence invariantMath.test.ts","title":"Statoblast and Zoltar invariants","topic":"Safety","weight":0},{"fragment":"inv-28","heading":"INV-28 Canonical observation updates","keywords":[],"path":"reference/invariants.html","sectionTitle":"Reference","summary":"Contract, lifecycle, cross-contract, liveness, and economic properties.","text":"Required property Canonical price, accepted epoch, observation timestamp and both funded exposure limits update together on a strictly newer accepted observation. An equal-time accepted report may authorize its sponsor’s operations but cannot overwrite that tuple or extend expiry. With different prices at the same timestamp, the first accepted observation matters. Type Safety Enforcement status Tested property Primary evidence securityPoolSharedFeed.test.ts","title":"Statoblast and Zoltar invariants","topic":"Safety","weight":0},{"fragment":"inv-29","heading":"INV-29 Deferred report recovery","keywords":[],"path":"reference/invariants.html","sectionTitle":"Reference","summary":"Contract, lifecycle, cross-contract, liveness, and economic properties.","text":"Required property Recovery of a deferred report uses its original settlement time. Running recovery later cannot buy another validity window. An upstream settlement and a terminal local result are distinct states. Once terminal, the report cannot run the local batch a second time. Type Safety Enforcement status Tested property Primary evidence securityPoolSharedFeed.test.ts","title":"Statoblast and Zoltar invariants","topic":"Safety","weight":0},{"fragment":"inv-30","heading":"INV-30 Growth-proof withdrawal floor","keywords":[],"path":"reference/invariants.html","sectionTitle":"Reference","summary":"Contract, lifecycle, cross-contract, liveness, and economic properties.","text":"Required property A secondary accepted growth proof can constrain the sponsoring owner’s withdrawals during that accepted epoch. The owner’s minimum price reflects the higher applicable price. A later accepted epoch invalidates the earlier overlay. Type Safety Enforcement status Tested property Primary evidence securityPoolSharedFeed.test.ts","title":"Statoblast and Zoltar invariants","topic":"Safety","weight":0},{"fragment":"inv-31","heading":"INV-31 Sponsor operation order","keywords":[],"path":"reference/invariants.html","sectionTitle":"Reference","summary":"Contract, lifecycle, cross-contract, liveness, and economic properties.","text":"Required property Ordinary failure of one budgeted sponsor operation leaves later eligible operations executable in declared order and under the sponsor’s identity. The caller who settles a report cannot gain the sponsor’s owner rights. Type Safety Enforcement status Tested property Primary evidence securityPoolReportOperations.test.ts","title":"Statoblast and Zoltar invariants","topic":"Safety","weight":0},{"fragment":"inv-48","heading":"INV-48 Two-asset report funding","keywords":[],"path":"reference/invariants.html","sectionTitle":"Reference","summary":"Contract, lifecycle, cross-contract, liveness, and economic properties.","text":"Required property With the same price feed exposures, let C be its collateral-side minimum, R the REP-side minimum, P>0 the proposed price, and S=10¹⁸ . The minimum report collateral a is the smallest integer with both a ≥ C and a × P ≥ R × S . Raising only P cannot increase a . The actual initial REP transfer rounds up separately. Type Safety Enforcement status Tested property Primary evidence securityPoolCollateralRepairAuction.test.ts","title":"Statoblast and Zoltar invariants","topic":"Safety","weight":0},{"fragment":"inv-58","heading":"INV-58 Escrow delivery rights","keywords":[],"path":"reference/invariants.html","sectionTitle":"Reference","summary":"Contract, lifecycle, cross-contract, liveness, and economic properties.","text":"Required property Anyone may deliver a released report token to its sponsor. Only the sponsor may redirect the delivery. When one report token cannot be delivered, independently withdrawing the other preserves both its recipient restriction and the unwithdrawn claim. Type Conservation Enforcement status Tested property Primary evidence securityPoolCollateral.test.ts","title":"Statoblast and Zoltar invariants","topic":"Safety","weight":0},{"fragment":"rep-history-and-deployment","heading":"REP, history and deployment","keywords":[],"path":"reference/invariants.html","sectionTitle":"Reference","summary":"Contract, lifecycle, cross-contract, liveness, and economic properties.","text":"","title":"Statoblast and Zoltar invariants","topic":"Safety","weight":0},{"fragment":"inv-39","heading":"INV-39 Unused child mint capacity","keywords":[],"path":"reference/invariants.html","sectionTitle":"Reference","summary":"Contract, lifecycle, cross-contract, liveness, and economic properties.","text":"Required property For an initialized child REP token, current theoretical supply less current circulating supply equals initial theoretical supply less all successful mints since initialization . A child burn lowers both current supplies equally and never restores minting headroom. A transfer changes neither. Type Conservation Enforcement status Tested property Primary evidence reputationAuthorization.test.ts","title":"Statoblast and Zoltar invariants","topic":"Safety","weight":0},{"fragment":"inv-40","heading":"INV-40 Threshold steps","keywords":[],"path":"reference/invariants.html","sectionTitle":"Reference","summary":"Contract, lifecycle, cross-contract, liveness, and economic properties.","text":"Required property For positive theoretical supply S and fork divisor D , the fork and non-decision thresholds are ⌈S/D⌉ and ⌈S/(2D)⌉ . For either divisor d ∈ {D,2D} , the least supply reduction that lowers the corresponding threshold is 1 + ((S−1) mod d) . One less reduction leaves it unchanged. Type Safety Enforcement status Tested property Primary evidence zoltar.test.ts","title":"Statoblast and Zoltar invariants","topic":"Safety","weight":0},{"fragment":"inv-41","heading":"INV-41 Adjacent historical checkpoints","keywords":[],"path":"reference/invariants.html","sectionTitle":"Reference","summary":"Contract, lifecycle, cross-contract, liveness, and economic properties.","text":"Required property On a common ordered timestamp history, the persistent map’s floor identifies the greatest timestamp at or below a probe, and the frozen history’s ceiling identifies the least at or above it. At an exact key they select the same recorded item. Outside the applicable range the corresponding lookup returns its absence sentinel. Type Safety Enforcement status Tested property Primary evidence securityPoolSettlementBook.test.ts","title":"Statoblast and Zoltar invariants","topic":"Safety","weight":0},{"fragment":"inv-42","heading":"INV-42 Order of maturity updates","keywords":[],"path":"reference/invariants.html","sectionTitle":"Reference","summary":"Contract, lifecycle, cross-contract, liveness, and economic properties.","text":"Required property Maturity index updates are additive per timestamp modulo 2²⁵⁶, independently for units and correction. Reordering identical deltas gives the same point and inclusive-prefix answers. An update followed by its additive inverse restores those answers, and old roots remain queryable. Root IDs may differ. Type Safety Enforcement status Tested property Primary evidence securityPoolMaturityMap.test.ts","title":"Statoblast and Zoltar invariants","topic":"Safety","weight":0},{"fragment":"inv-43","heading":"INV-43 Creation bytecode round trip","keywords":[],"path":"reference/invariants.html","sectionTitle":"Reference","summary":"Contract, lifecycle, cross-contract, liveness, and economic properties.","text":"Required property Storing then loading any supported creation bytecode preserves every byte. The first carrier has a STOP byte and the first ⌊length/2⌋ bytes. The second has a STOP and the rest. A one-byte input therefore has a STOP-only first carrier. Type Safety Enforcement status Tested property Primary evidence creationCodeStorage.test.ts","title":"Statoblast and Zoltar invariants","topic":"Safety","weight":0},{"fragment":"inv-44","heading":"INV-44 Deployment mask","keywords":[],"path":"reference/invariants.html","sectionTitle":"Reference","summary":"Contract, lifecycle, cross-contract, liveness, and economic properties.","text":"Required property Deployment-mask bit i equals runtime-code presence at the configured address in slot i at read time. Duplicate addresses have equal bits, unused high bits are zero, and bit 255 is valid. It says nothing about the identity of that runtime. Type Safety Enforcement status Tested property Primary evidence deploymentStatusOracle.test.ts","title":"Statoblast and Zoltar invariants","topic":"Safety","weight":0},{"fragment":"inv-45","heading":"INV-45 Scalar label uniqueness","keywords":[],"path":"reference/invariants.html","sectionTitle":"Reference","summary":"Contract, lifecycle, cross-contract, liveness, and economic properties.","text":"Required property Within one scalar question, valid ticks have unique display labels even when successive ticks round to the same numerical display value. The underlying displayed numerical value is nondecreasing and both endpoints are exact. Compare numerical values separately from the disambiguating tick text. Type Safety Enforcement status Tested property Primary evidence questionData.test.ts","title":"Statoblast and Zoltar invariants","topic":"Safety","weight":0},{"fragment":"inv-52","heading":"INV-52 Frozen history allocation","keywords":[],"path":"reference/invariants.html","sectionTitle":"Reference","summary":"Contract, lifecycle, cross-contract, liveness, and economic properties.","text":"Required property Each accepted frozen-history append allocates exactly one new node. It may join the two leading equal-sized trees. Every prior head remains independently queryable, so branching history does not require copying earlier checkpoints. Type Safety Enforcement status Tested property Primary evidence securityPoolSettlementBook.test.ts","title":"Statoblast and Zoltar invariants","topic":"Safety","weight":0},{"fragment":"inv-53","heading":"INV-53 Canonical event replay","keywords":[],"path":"reference/invariants.html","sectionTitle":"Reference","summary":"Contract, lifecycle, cross-contract, liveness, and economic properties.","text":"Required property Replaying the canonical log history reconstructs the corresponding published state for each deployment. Contract-local report, bid and game IDs require the emitter as part of their identity. Removing an orphaned branch and replaying the canonical replacement must yield the same result as replaying that canonical history from scratch. Type Safety Enforcement status Tested property Primary evidence eventReplay.test.ts","title":"Statoblast and Zoltar invariants","topic":"Safety","weight":0},{"fragment":"inv-57","heading":"INV-57 Authorization namespaces","keywords":[],"path":"reference/invariants.html","sectionTitle":"Reference","summary":"Contract, lifecycle, cross-contract, liveness, and economic properties.","text":"Required property The ERC-3009 authorization key is local to the token and authorizer. Transfer, receive and cancellation consume that key. Its consumption leaves the ERC-2612 permit counter unchanged. Two holders may independently use the same bytes32 ID. The permit deadline is inclusive and both ends of the authorization time window are exclusive. Type Safety Enforcement status Tested property Primary evidence reputationAuthorization.test.ts","title":"Statoblast and Zoltar invariants","topic":"Safety","weight":0},{"fragment":"test-evidence","heading":"Executable evidence","keywords":[],"path":"reference/invariants.html","sectionTitle":"Reference","summary":"Contract, lifecycle, cross-contract, liveness, and economic properties.","text":"Each suite exercises the stated property over concrete fixtures or generated sequences. Gas bounds apply to the measured operations and fixture sizes.","title":"Statoblast and Zoltar invariants","topic":"Safety","weight":0},{"fragment":"test-evidence","heading":"Questions and universes","keywords":[],"path":"reference/invariants.html","sectionTitle":"Reference","summary":"Contract, lifecycle, cross-contract, liveness, and economic properties.","text":"Registry Creating a question appends its deterministic ID once and preserves earlier records and labels. Adjacent pages reconstruct the same registry slice. questionData.test.ts . Answers The malformed predicate agrees with the public answer name for categorical, scalar and reserved-bit cases. Invalid is a supported answer. questionData.test.ts . Fork finality and eligibility An initialized universe forks once on a registered, ended question. The end timestamp itself is eligible. An unrelated ledger question can continue in a child. zoltar.test.ts , auditE2ELazyAndMultiQuestion.test.ts . Fork accounting The threshold burn, initiator credit and common child supply snapshot reconcile using the rounding rules in UNI-03 and UNI-08. A positive theoretical supply produces a positive threshold. zoltar.test.ts . Child identity Deployment, enumeration and migration agree on the child derived from the parent and outcome. Malformed answers cannot acquire children. zoltar.test.ts . Migration and supply Cumulative minting is capped independently for each migrator and child. Summed holder balances equal actual child supply, bounded by the child snapshot. zoltar.test.ts , auditForkMigrationTwoGenerations.test.ts .","title":"Statoblast and Zoltar invariants","topic":"Safety","weight":0},{"fragment":"test-evidence","heading":"Deployment and authority","keywords":[],"path":"reference/invariants.html","sectionTitle":"Reference","summary":"Contract, lifecycle, cross-contract, liveness, and economic properties.","text":"Canonical identity Predicted and deployed contract addresses agree. Registration binds each question ledger to its canonical pool and immutable admission policy. securityPoolModuleDeployment.test.ts , auditSecurityPoolAuthenticity.test.ts , securityPoolAdmission.test.ts . Host isolation A shared delegate acts on its authorized host. Direct callers and other pools cannot mutate that host’s accounting. privilegedAuthorization.test.ts , securityPoolCapitalLifecycle.test.ts , securityPoolModuleDeployment.test.ts . Spending authority Share transfers require the owner or an approved operator. Signed REP authorizations bind their domain, fields, validity interval and nonce. Cancellation and prior use preclude replay. erc1155.test.ts , reputationAuthorization.test.ts . Atomicity Failed delivery or insufficient capacity preserves the balances, supplies and accounting touched by the transaction. A receiver callback cannot exercise the same entitlement twice. securityPoolDelivery.test.ts , securityPoolTransactionRollback.test.ts , pairRouter.integration.test.ts .","title":"Statoblast and Zoltar invariants","topic":"Safety","weight":0},{"fragment":"test-evidence","heading":"Capital and underwriting","keywords":[],"path":"reference/invariants.html","sectionTitle":"Reference","summary":"Contract, lifecycle, cross-contract, liveness, and economic properties.","text":"Custody domains Underwriting capital, reporter deposits and question cash have separate entitlements. Settling reporter claims cannot create provider collateral. escalationGameForkThreshold.test.ts , statoblastInvariant.test.ts , auditReporterLedgerFuzz.test.ts . Owner totals Owner principal and commitments reconcile to the capital ledger through deposits, takeovers and exits. Winning auction lots enter totals before the owner activates them. auditCapitalLedgerFuzz.test.ts , securityPoolCapitalAuction.test.ts . Health Withdrawal and takeover limits use the owner’s full commitment, eligible principal and applicable price. Earned REP income remains separate from principal. securityPoolHealthPolicy.test.ts , securityPoolCapitalTakeoverBounds.test.ts . Shared capacity Two questions cannot spend the same final unit of healthy capacity. A complete-set return releases that unit for a subsequent purchase on either question. statoblastInvariant.test.ts . Retirement Retired capacity stops supporting new purchases. Principal securing outstanding demand remains subject to retirement and withdrawal limits. securityPoolCapitalSetCommitment.test.ts , securityPoolCapitalLifecycle.test.ts . Loss isolation A takeover transfers only the target’s eligible principal and obligations, within the receiver’s consent. Other owners retain their entitlements. auditCapitalLedgerFuzz.test.ts , securityPoolCapitalTakeoverBounds.test.ts . Fees Funded streams reconcile to vested income and remaining amounts in each asset. Checkpoint frequency and same-second funding preserve entitlements across price changes and forks. securityPoolCapitalFeeStreams.test.ts , auditForkFeeConservation.test.ts , auditChildPoolFeeEpoch.test.ts . Donations Forced native currency cannot enlarge tracked question cash, purchasing capacity or provider principal. Trading pair donations follow the separate reserve rule in the trading catalog . statoblastInvariant.test.ts , pairRouter.integration.test.ts .","title":"Statoblast and Zoltar invariants","topic":"Safety","weight":0},{"fragment":"test-evidence","heading":"Question settlement and shares","keywords":[],"path":"reference/invariants.html","sectionTitle":"Reference","summary":"Contract, lifecycle, cross-contract, liveness, and economic properties.","text":"Identity and supply Share IDs round-trip within their token contract. In the zero-fee two-question model, each purchase or return changes all three outcome supplies equally and leaves the other question’s supply intact. erc1155.test.ts , statoblastInvariant.test.ts . Cash and demand Credited cash less authorized outflows equals tracked question cash. In the zero-fee fixture, aggregate demand and settlement collateral equal the sum across questions after every modeled operation. statoblastInvariant.test.ts , auditSettlementCustodyFuzz.test.ts . Backing floor Fees stop before consuming protected backing. Partial burns, replacement and finality preserve the rounded backing floor. securityPoolSettlementBook.test.ts . Settlement finality Finalized question entitlements survive subsequent settlement activity and forks. Redeeming shares consumes the corresponding entitlement once. securityPoolSettlementBook.test.ts , ordinarySettlementRecovery.test.ts . Rounding Partial redemptions remain within the settlement budget. The final eligible burn receives the residual, including a fractional cash or REP remainder. securityPoolSettlementBook.test.ts , auditSettlementRecoveryFuzz.test.ts . Recovery rights The source entitlement bounds migration to each branch. Transfers, partial forwarding and complete-set recovery preserve branch rights and consume the source recovery right once. shareTokenSecurityPoolRecovery.test.ts , forkMigrationRecovery.test.ts .","title":"Statoblast and Zoltar invariants","topic":"Safety","weight":0},{"fragment":"test-evidence","heading":"Reporting and carry","keywords":[],"path":"reference/invariants.html","sectionTitle":"Reference","summary":"Contract, lifecycle, cross-contract, liveness, and economic properties.","text":"Deposits and liabilities Authenticated deposits retain their owner, outcome, principal and reward coordinates. Ordinary finality reserves winning claims and retires losing principal before individual payouts. auditReporterLedgerFuzz.test.ts , localAccounting.test.ts , audit4EscalationPayoutConservation.test.ts . Outcome finality A fixed continuation outcome determines the payout even if the copied balance leader differs. A later fork cannot rewrite a finalized payout basis. fixedContinuationSecondFork.test.ts , securityPoolContinuationFinality.test.ts . Historical proofs Inherited claims authenticate membership in the frozen history and retain principal and reward coordinates through recursive forks. Consumed identities cannot be claimed again within the same branch. carriedProofPayouts.test.ts , recursiveCarry.test.ts , auditE2EContinuationMixedClaims.test.ts . Claim partitioning Paying two ordinary claims together or separately in reverse order leaves the same wallet, reporter escrow and underwriting balances. Recursive claims preserve principal in either claim order. escalationGameForkThreshold.test.ts , recursiveCarry.test.ts .","title":"Statoblast and Zoltar invariants","topic":"Safety","weight":0},{"fragment":"test-evidence","heading":"Fork coordination","keywords":[],"path":"reference/invariants.html","sectionTitle":"Reference","summary":"Contract, lifecycle, cross-contract, liveness, and economic properties.","text":"Choice and clocks Each owner chooses one branch before the shared cutoff. Delayed materialization cannot extend native choice, auction or activation deadlines. securityPoolForkCoordinator.test.ts , delayedForkCarry.test.ts . Frozen snapshots Sibling and repeated forks preserve the recorded question state and isolate subsequent changes. A frozen parent history remains usable after a child forks again. securityPoolSettlementSnapshot.test.ts , securityPoolSettlementBook.test.ts , claimCheckpointFreeze.test.ts . Lazy materialization A child materialized after its own fork retains the inherited outcome or clock. The fork question selects the branch while the ledger question determines its payouts. auditE2ELazyAndMultiQuestion.test.ts , finalizedParentMaterialization.test.ts .","title":"Statoblast and Zoltar invariants","topic":"Safety","weight":0},{"fragment":"test-evidence","heading":"Repair auctions","keywords":[],"path":"reference/invariants.html","sectionTitle":"Reference","summary":"Contract, lifecycle, cross-contract, liveness, and economic properties.","text":"Inventory Only eligible unchosen provider REP funds auction inventory. Reporter escrow and REP already owed to recovery holders retain their respective beneficiaries. securityPoolForkCoordinator.test.ts , auditE2EAuctionChildSecondFork.test.ts . Clearing Filled lots respect eligible bids, available inventory and the common grid-rounded clearing price. The closing price determines each lot’s minimum backing. auditAuctionEngineFuzz.test.ts , auditAuctionClosingPriceTick.test.ts . Closing Funded winning principal and obligations enter aggregate accounting at close. Late activation preserves entitlement and cannot duplicate a winning position. securityPoolCapitalAuction.test.ts , auditE2EAuctionChildSecondFork.test.ts . Refunds and progress An early-refundable bid stays a loser. Refunds remain attributable to the bidder and can be withdrawn independently. Winner activation and refund processing stay feasible as the tested bid book grows. auditAuctionEngineFuzz.test.ts , securityPoolAuctionGas.test.ts .","title":"Statoblast and Zoltar invariants","topic":"Safety","weight":0},{"fragment":"test-evidence","heading":"Price reports","keywords":[],"path":"reference/invariants.html","sectionTitle":"Reference","summary":"Contract, lifecycle, cross-contract, liveness, and economic properties.","text":"Report identity and units Only the bound feed and authorized adapter can apply a report. Token amounts and decimals determine quote units, including six-decimal collateral. priceOracleSecurity.test.ts , securityPoolCollateral.test.ts . Acceptance and repricing Acceptance checks funded coverage, reporting window, freshness and exposure. Capital checkpoints accrued entitlements under the prior price before the new price takes effect. securityPoolSharedFeed.test.ts , audit4ReportExposureGrowth.test.ts . Expiry Disputed or rejected renewal cannot extend freshness. Delayed settlement cannot restart the validity clock. securityPoolSharedFeed.test.ts , priceOracleSecurity.test.ts . Candidate independence A pending candidate cannot prevent another funded candidate from succeeding. Sponsors retain separate escrow and refund entitlements. securityPoolSharedFeed.test.ts .","title":"Statoblast and Zoltar invariants","topic":"Safety","weight":0},{"fragment":"test-evidence","heading":"Arithmetic, integrations and progress","keywords":[],"path":"reference/invariants.html","sectionTitle":"Reference","summary":"Contract, lifecycle, cross-contract, liveness, and economic properties.","text":"Reference models Commitment and maturity queries and settlement aggregates agree with independent expectations across updates. Frozen snapshots remain readable after later writes. auditCapitalIndexFuzz.test.ts , securityPoolMaturityMap.test.ts , auditSettlementBookFuzz.test.ts . Numeric boundaries Checked casts preserve representable inputs and reject overflow. Thresholds round up, fee remainders retain their funding, and trading quotes respect their integer bounds. protocolHardening.test.ts , zoltar.test.ts , securityPoolCapitalFeeStreams.test.ts , auditTradingMathFuzz.test.ts . Collateral movement Token transfers must succeed to credit a payment. Refunds and claims reconcile in collateral units and forced native balances remain separate. safeErc20.test.ts , securityPoolCollateral.test.ts , statoblastInvariant.test.ts . Seeder A mint callback must come from the active pool. Exceeding either token maximum rolls back both payments. A subsequent valid seed preserves the seeder’s opening balances. genesisUniswapV3Seeder.test.ts . Bounded and conditional progress Capital, auction and lazy migration gas tests bound costs for the measured fixture sizes. Permissionless execution still depends on transaction inclusion, available proofs and any required external funding. securityPoolCapitalLedgerGas.test.ts , securityPoolAuctionGas.test.ts , lazyShareMigrationGasBound.test.ts .","title":"Statoblast and Zoltar invariants","topic":"Safety","weight":0},{"fragment":"","heading":"","keywords":["assumptions","security","threats","guarantees"],"path":"reference/security-model.html","sectionTitle":"Reference","summary":"Normative participant, market, deployment, client, and cryptographic assumptions.","text":"Statoblast and Zoltar enforce accounting, authorization, and lifecycle rules onchain. Their economic safety and practical liveness still depend on the participant, market, asset, deployment, client, data, Ethereum, and cryptographic assumptions below. An assumption is not a guarantee enforced by the contracts. Each entry says what must remain true, what can fail if it does not, and where the related mechanics are explained. The Statoblast and Zoltar invariants catalog lists contract-enforced properties and their implementation evidence. Participation and coordination Forks, truth, value, and liquidity Oracle correction, inclusion, and operational data Assets, clients, deployment, Ethereum, and cryptography Guarantees deliberately not made","title":"Statoblast security model","topic":"Safety","weight":1},{"fragment":"orientation","heading":"Security-model orientation","keywords":[],"path":"reference/security-model.html","sectionTitle":"Reference","summary":"Normative participant, market, deployment, client, and cryptographic assumptions.","text":"The assets and state at stake are REP , ERC-20 settlement collateral , outcome shares , vault backing, Escalation Game deposits, child-universe accounting, and the integrity of event-derived state. Boundary Examples Trusted protocol components Zoltar, question ledgers , share tokens, shared price feeds , question forkers , and their configured delegates. External dependencies OpenOracle , collateral/REP token behavior, Ethereum execution and finality, RPCs, wallets, and proof data. Untrusted actors Traders, vault owners, liquidators, disputers, bidders, settlers, keepers, and indexers. Failure outcomes Reverted calls, delayed transitions, underfunded child repair, stale prices, incorrect external data, or unreconstructable history.","title":"Statoblast security model","topic":"Safety","weight":0},{"fragment":"security-review-orientation","heading":"Which assumptions support my claim?","keywords":[],"path":"reference/security-model.html","sectionTitle":"Reference","summary":"Normative participant, market, deployment, client, and cryptographic assumptions.","text":"A wrong local outcome can be challenged in time. Check timely evidence , REP for a competing deposit , inclusion before the deadline . A harmful oracle price can be corrected. Check a funded corrector , an independent incentive , a reliable reference market , timely inclusion . A truthful child can cover its collateral shortfall. Check retained backing value , bidder demand and the opening and closing sale conditions . See EG02 on partial repair . An eligible public settlement advances in time. Check an executor , timely inclusion . For the reads, deadlines, and stop conditions behind these decisions, see Monitor security assumptions .","title":"Statoblast security model","topic":"Safety","weight":0},{"fragment":"assumptions-participation","heading":"Participation and coordination","keywords":[],"path":"reference/security-model.html","sectionTitle":"Reference","summary":"Normative participant, market, deployment, client, and cryptographic assumptions.","text":"These assumptions explain why someone performs the economically useful action instead of merely being permitted to perform it.","title":"Statoblast security model","topic":"Safety","weight":0},{"fragment":"assumption-a01","heading":"A01 Participants act on risk-adjusted economic incentives","keywords":[],"path":"reference/security-model.html","sectionTitle":"Reference","summary":"Normative participant, market, deployment, client, and cryptographic assumptions.","text":"The economic model assumes participants weigh expected gains against costs, delay, and risk when deciding whether to act. Failure boundary: A nominally profitable liquidation, dispute, bid, migration, or fork defense may not happen when its risk-adjusted return is unattractive. Monitoring checks . Mechanics: economic roles , liquidation incentives , auction clearing , and oracle incentives .","title":"Statoblast security model","topic":"Safety","weight":0},{"fragment":"assumption-a02","heading":"A02 Privately motivated actions cover their costs","keywords":[],"path":"reference/security-model.html","sectionTitle":"Reference","summary":"Normative participant, market, deployment, client, and cryptographic assumptions.","text":"When timely action depends on private incentives, at least one participant expects the value they earn or protect to exceed their transaction, coordination, and capital costs. Failure boundary: An action can be technically executable but economically abandoned before its deadline. Monitoring checks . Mechanics: Collateral Repair Auction lifecycle , oracle gas parameters , and Escalation Game lifecycle .","title":"Statoblast security model","topic":"Safety","weight":0},{"fragment":"assumption-a03","heading":"A03 Price discovery and contests attract independent participants","keywords":[],"path":"reference/security-model.html","sectionTitle":"Reference","summary":"Normative participant, market, deployment, client, and cryptographic assumptions.","text":"Competitive auction pricing depends on independent bidders, external trading prices depend on independent traders, and contesting a local outcome requires someone willing to fund an opposing outcome. Failure boundary: Thin or controlled participation can remove price discovery, competitive bidding, or an opposing outcome deposit. Monitoring checks . Mechanics: economic roles , fork coordination , and auction participation .","title":"Statoblast security model","topic":"Safety","weight":0},{"fragment":"assumption-a04","heading":"A04 Participants can mobilize REP to raise an alarm","keywords":[],"path":"reference/security-model.html","sectionTitle":"Reference","summary":"Normative participant, market, deployment, client, and cryptographic assumptions.","text":"A participant can deliver enough REP for a competing local deposit before the game closes. A further reply after a change of leader requires enough time at the new median stake to bring more REP into the game. The reward cap encourages a stake margin , but sets no minimum reply time. Failure boundary: A wrong local outcome can become final before better-capitalized participants enter. Monitoring checks . Mechanics: escalation resolution , escalation accounting , and escalation guardrails .","title":"Statoblast security model","topic":"Safety","weight":0},{"fragment":"assumption-a05","heading":"A05 Participants value continued access to the supported Statoblast lineage","keywords":[],"path":"reference/security-model.html","sectionTitle":"Reference","summary":"Normative participant, market, deployment, client, and cryptographic assumptions.","text":"Users and integrations whose branch choices determine durable use would lose enough by abandoning the supported lineage to deter that choice. Estimate what they lose relative to their best available alternative. The model uses the lineage’s configured backing multiplier as its deterrence hurdle for the same secured exposure. Value Statoblast access × BPS_DENOMINATOR ≥ statoblastSecurityMultiplierBps × Value secured Compare one lineage’s continued-use value and secured value at the same pre-attack time. Divide the configured basis points by 10,000 to obtain the multiplier. Failure boundary: Participants may rationally abandon, grief, or coordinate away from a lineage whose continued-use value is too small. Monitoring checks . Mechanics: fork security , fork migration , and Statoblast security boundary .","title":"Statoblast security model","topic":"Safety","weight":0},{"fragment":"assumption-a06","heading":"A06 At least one executor advances every required public transition","keywords":[],"path":"reference/security-model.html","sectionTitle":"Reference","summary":"Normative participant, market, deployment, client, and cryptographic assumptions.","text":"For each required public transition, at least one caller can pay for and submit the transaction in time. Examples include fork activation, auction finalization and oracle settlement or callback recovery. A takeover also needs a willing, funded receiver. Holder-directed migration is covered by A11 . Failure boundary: Permissionless state can remain stalled even though no contract authorization prevents progress. Monitoring checks . Mechanics: lifecycle liveness , fork migration guardrails , and oracle guardrails .","title":"Statoblast security model","topic":"Safety","weight":0},{"fragment":"assumptions-forks","heading":"Forks, truth, value, and liquidity","keywords":[],"path":"reference/security-model.html","sectionTitle":"Reference","summary":"Normative participant, market, deployment, client, and cryptographic assumptions.","text":"These assumptions connect onchain branching and accounting to offchain truth judgments, asset value, liquidity, and migration.","title":"Statoblast security model","topic":"Safety","weight":0},{"fragment":"assumption-a07","heading":"A07 Truthful-child REP retains enough backing value","keywords":[],"path":"reference/security-model.html","sectionTitle":"Reference","summary":"Normative participant, market, deployment, client, and cryptographic assumptions.","text":"For one protected Statoblast origin lineage, let V parent be its pre-fork REP backing value and V child the corresponding backing value in the supported truthful child after fork haircuts, at the repair decision horizon. Both use the same collateral numeraire; unrelated external collateral and REP in other branches are excluded. The multiplier m is the origin's immutable statoblastSecurityMultiplierBps / 10_000 . V child ≥ V parent m At 20_000 BPS , this bound requires retaining at least half the parent backing value; at 30_000 BPS , at least one third. At the minimum admission ratio, that retained value equals the secured collateral before realization costs. This is a valuation assumption, not an onchain parent/child price check or a guarantee of full repair; auction participation and conversion costs are covered by A12 . Failure boundary: Insufficient retained backing value can leave a truthful child's collateral shortfall unrepaired. Monitoring checks . Mechanics: security pool fork migration . Rationale: REP backing and open interest .","title":"Statoblast security model","topic":"Safety","weight":0},{"fragment":"assumption-a08","heading":"A08 REP market capitalization approximates economic value","keywords":[],"path":"reference/security-model.html","sectionTitle":"Reference","summary":"Normative participant, market, deployment, client, and cryptographic assumptions.","text":"When an assessment uses market capitalization to estimate REP discounted cash flow for A14 , it assumes the estimate is accurate enough to preserve the comparison with open interest at the same time and scope. Failure boundary: Thin, manipulated, or speculative pricing can overstate the economic value securing open interest. Monitoring checks . Mechanics: REP economics and economic backing claims .","title":"Statoblast security model","topic":"Safety","weight":0},{"fragment":"assumption-a09","heading":"A09 Users value the universes they judge truthful","keywords":[],"path":"reference/security-model.html","sectionTitle":"Reference","summary":"Normative participant, market, deployment, client, and cryptographic assumptions.","text":"Users prefer the universe or universes they judge truthful, so economic activity and durable asset value concentrate there. This assumption describes the durable allocation of value after users coordinate; A07 bounds backing value at the repair decision horizon. Failure boundary: Coordination on a false branch, or durable fragmentation across branches, defeats the value-concentration security argument. Monitoring checks . Mechanics: branch non-guarantees and security pool fork migration .","title":"Statoblast security model","topic":"Safety","weight":0},{"fragment":"assumption-a10","heading":"A10 Participants can access timely outcome evidence","keywords":[],"path":"reference/security-model.html","sectionTitle":"Reference","summary":"Normative participant, market, deployment, client, and cryptographic assumptions.","text":"Participants can obtain and interpret reliable outcome evidence early enough to fund and submit a response before the relevant deadline. Failure boundary: Missing, delayed, or conflicting evidence can split honest capital or let a false local outcome become final. Monitoring checks . Mechanics: question encoding , invalid outcomes , and local resolution .","title":"Statoblast security model","topic":"Safety","weight":0},{"fragment":"assumption-a11","heading":"A11 Migration is operationally practical","keywords":[],"path":"reference/security-model.html","sectionTitle":"Reference","summary":"Normative participant, market, deployment, client, and cryptographic assumptions.","text":"REP holders can migrate into a child without a native REP migration deadline. Underwriters must choose their branch before the branch-choice cutoff. Share holders must be able to call migration for their own balances once the destination branch is active. Each path depends on the holder or its custodian supporting that branch. Failure boundary: Supported branches can remain illiquid or undercollateralized because economic claims and backing do not move there. Monitoring checks . Mechanics: REP splitting , security pool fork migration , and share migration guardrails .","title":"Statoblast security model","topic":"Safety","weight":0},{"fragment":"assumption-a12","heading":"A12 Bidders will buy enough child REP at the repair auction","keywords":[],"path":"reference/security-model.html","sectionTitle":"Reference","summary":"Normative participant, market, deployment, client, and cryptographic assumptions.","text":"Bidders are willing and able to supply the collateral a repair estimate requires at the offered terms, after discount, fees and capital costs. A full repair requires enough eligible fills to cover the shortfall. Failure boundary: Weak or strategically withheld demand leaves the child operational with impaired collateral. A shared security pool auction sells only the REP priced by its funded lots and can leave much of the shortfall unrepaired. Ordinary surplus withdrawal and retirement remain available to winners, so selling the inventory does not guarantee permanent backing. Monitoring checks . Mechanics: shared security pool auctions , and repair accounting .","title":"Statoblast security model","topic":"Safety","weight":0},{"fragment":"assumption-a13","heading":"A13 REP is sufficiently liquid","keywords":[],"path":"reference/security-model.html","sectionTitle":"Reference","summary":"Normative participant, market, deployment, client, and cryptographic assumptions.","text":"Participants can acquire or sell the required REP quantities without price impact or delay large enough to invalidate reporting, disputing, liquidation, auction, or migration economics. Failure boundary: Capital may exist in principle but cannot be assembled at a usable price before the deadline. Monitoring checks . Mechanics: economic roles , escalation , and oracle positions .","title":"Statoblast security model","topic":"Safety","weight":0},{"fragment":"assumption-a14","heading":"A14 REP economic value exceeds the open interest it secures","keywords":[],"path":"reference/security-model.html","sectionTitle":"Reference","summary":"Normative participant, market, deployment, client, and cryptographic assumptions.","text":"For a stated question ledger, lineage, or system scope at one timestamp, open interest and REP discounted cash flow are valued in the same numeraire, and REP discounted cash flow remains strictly greater. For a question-ledger-level evaluation, open interest means the question ledger’s protocol-accounted settlement collateral unless the analysis explicitly names a broader exposure measure. The REP supply and price basis must also be stated; market capitalization is the observable proxy in A08 . This external relationship is separate from each question ledger’s onchain multiplier-adjusted REP backing check. Open Interest < Discounted Cash Flow REP Record scope, timestamp, common numeraire, price source, REP supply basis, and open-interest definition when evaluating this strict inequality. Failure boundary: REP holders may rationally accept or cause losses larger than the durable REP value placed at risk. Monitoring checks . Mechanics: security pool underwriting , liquidation backing , and oracle exposure analysis .","title":"Statoblast security model","topic":"Safety","weight":0},{"fragment":"assumption-a15","heading":"A15 Question selection remains aligned with user intent","keywords":[],"path":"reference/security-model.html","sectionTitle":"Reference","summary":"Normative participant, market, deployment, client, and cryptographic assumptions.","text":"Interfaces and users verify the immutable question ID and metadata when selecting a market or security pool, and the question has evidence and wording from which participants can reach an outcome or intentionally choose Invalid . Failure boundary: Unverified selection can route users and capital to an unintended market or security pool, while ambiguous wording or unavailable evidence can split local outcome coordination. Monitoring checks . Mechanics: UNI-02 and question identity .","title":"Statoblast security model","topic":"Safety","weight":0},{"fragment":"assumptions-oracle","heading":"Oracle correction, inclusion, and operational data","keywords":[],"path":"reference/security-model.html","sectionTitle":"Reference","summary":"Normative participant, market, deployment, client, and cryptographic assumptions.","text":"These assumptions cover truthful REP/collateral price discovery, deadline access, capital, independent correction, and the offchain information required to act. The shared feed uses the fixed report flags .","title":"Statoblast security model","topic":"Safety","weight":0},{"fragment":"assumption-a16","heading":"A16 Deadline transactions can obtain Ethereum inclusion","keywords":[],"path":"reference/security-model.html","sectionTitle":"Reference","summary":"Normative participant, market, deployment, client, and cryptographic assumptions.","text":"Honest users can submit competitively priced Ethereum transactions and obtain canonical inclusion within every relevant dispute, escalation, migration, auction, and settlement window. Selective exclusion can outlast a correction window even when gas is affordable. High base fees can also defer report acceptance under the settlement base-fee cap . Each onchain comparison assigns the exact deadline timestamp to one phase. Same-block ordering cannot move an action across that boundary: users must target the phase valid at the block timestamp rather than rely on transaction ordering to extend an expired window. Failure boundary: Censorship or congestion lasting through a deadline can finalize a wrong report or prevent a supported action. Monitoring checks . Mechanics: fork deadline guardrails , auction windows , and oracle censorship model .","title":"Statoblast security model","topic":"Safety","weight":0},{"fragment":"assumption-a17","heading":"A17 OpenOracle has an available, adequately capitalized corrector","keywords":[],"path":"reference/security-model.html","sectionTitle":"Reference","summary":"Normative participant, market, deployment, client, and cryptographic assumptions.","text":"For each harmful candidate report, a corrector can recognize the error, fund the replacement position in both assets and get the dispute included during its eligible window. Overlapping windows require enough free capital and execution capacity to cover concurrent reports. Different correctors may cover different windows. Failure boundary: An incorrect report can settle and authorize price-sensitive security pool actions when no capable participant detects, funds, and includes a correction. Monitoring checks . Mechanics: REP/collateral oracle , correction incentives , and attack model .","title":"Statoblast security model","topic":"Safety","weight":0},{"fragment":"assumption-a18","heading":"A18 At least one available corrector has an independent net incentive","keywords":[],"path":"reference/security-model.html","sectionTitle":"Reference","summary":"Normative participant, market, deployment, client, and cryptographic assumptions.","text":"At least one participant satisfying A17 is not under common control with the sponsor-funded shared-feed reporting path, does not share the manipulation payoff, and is not compensated to leave a harmful report uncorrected. The participant's net payoff from correcting is strictly greater than its net payoff from leaving the harmful report uncorrected. Two external submissions are not required for every request. The creation-time collateral floor must sustain that incentive even at zero aggregate exposure, after fees, gas, price impact and capital lockup. Failure boundary: An undersized floor, higher gas fees, or falling collateral value can leave a wrong REP/collateral price uneconomic to dispute. It may settle unchallenged and authorize unsafe price-sensitive actions even when every onchain funding check passes. A sponsor-controlled, bribed, or economically indifferent corrector can cause the same failure. Monitoring checks . Mechanics: settlement validation scope , economic tradeoffs , and oracle invariants .","title":"Statoblast security model","topic":"Safety","weight":0},{"fragment":"assumption-a19","heading":"A19 A robust reference market makes harmful REP/collateral errors correctable","keywords":[],"path":"reference/security-model.html","sectionTitle":"Reference","summary":"Normative participant, market, deployment, client, and cryptographic assumptions.","text":"For the actual pair and required size, independent participants can obtain an executable reference price from a market sufficiently independent of the price being checked. Every deviation large enough to affect the named pool action leaves a profitable correction under A18 . Failure boundary: A harmful deviation below the profitable correction threshold, or a manipulated/illiquid reference market, can settle without an economically rational challenge. Monitoring checks . Mechanics: report sizing , external-payoff model , and settlement limits .","title":"Statoblast security model","topic":"Safety","weight":0},{"fragment":"assumption-a20","heading":"A20 Canonical chain data and proof construction remain available","keywords":[],"path":"reference/security-model.html","sectionTitle":"Reference","summary":"Normative participant, market, deployment, client, and cryptographic assumptions.","text":"Participants can obtain canonical history through the required snapshot and reconstruct the outcome root for an inherited claim. Standalone games also need a nullifier proof. Canonical games use a consumed-deposit check. Failure boundary: A valid dispute, settlement, migration, or inherited claim can become practically unavailable even while its contract entrypoint remains live. Monitoring checks . Mechanics: indexer replay and canonicality , carry proofs , and operator orientation .","title":"Statoblast security model","topic":"Safety","weight":0},{"fragment":"assumptions-technical","heading":"Assets, clients, deployment, Ethereum, and cryptography","keywords":[],"path":"reference/security-model.html","sectionTitle":"Reference","summary":"Normative participant, market, deployment, client, and cryptographic assumptions.","text":"These assumptions define the technical foundation below the protocol’s onchain guards.","title":"Statoblast security model","topic":"Safety","weight":0},{"fragment":"assumption-a21","heading":"A21 REP and selected collateral obey the required accounting model","keywords":[],"path":"reference/security-model.html","sectionTitle":"Reference","summary":"Normative participant, market, deployment, client, and cryptographic assumptions.","text":"The accounting assumptions depend on the asset and the path using it. Genesis REP. The theoretical supply is accurate, transfers deliver the requested 18-decimal units, and the burn sink remains inaccessible. Sepolia uses the reviewed genesis token once its deployment is verified under A23 . Child REP. Canonical children use the reviewed ReputationToken implementation. Selected collateral. Transfers deliver exactly the raw units requested. The token supports the integration’s return-value and allowance conventions, including nonzero allowance changes where required. Rebases, fees, unexpected callbacks, blacklisting, pauses or unauthorized minting can break accounting or block delivery. Pools reject their deployment’s canonical REP as collateral. WETH collateral. Holders can redeem WETH for ETH at parity. The Sepolia deployment checks the published WETH runtime hash. Failure boundary: Origin question ledger, genesis-fork, or oracle accounting can overstate balances, backing, burned supply, or collateral value. Monitoring checks . Mechanics: bounded lifecycle work and oracle token pair .","title":"Statoblast security model","topic":"Safety","weight":0},{"fragment":"assumption-a22","heading":"A22 Participant-controlled recipients can accept required asset deliveries","keywords":[],"path":"reference/security-model.html","sectionTitle":"Reference","summary":"Normative participant, market, deployment, client, and cryptographic assumptions.","text":"Recipients must be able to receive the asset transferred by their chosen path. Security pool fees, collateral redemptions, auction proceeds, and auction refunds use the selected ERC-20 collateral. Native-ETH router exits and OpenOracle ETH withdrawals or settler rewards require a recipient that accepts ETH. Contract recipients also return the required ERC-1155 receiver selector when complete-set creation, share migration, or an outcome-share transfer delivers tokens to them. Failed transfers or rejected callbacks can prevent delivery. Failure boundary: A failed collateral-token transfer, rejected native-ETH delivery, or rejected ERC-1155 callback can make the recipient’s payout, mint, migration, transfer, or claim unavailable even while protocol-wide accounting remains safe. Monitoring checks . Mechanics: router ETH callbacks , router ERC-1155 callbacks , share-token receipt , auction refunds , oracle request refunds , and security pool guardrails .","title":"Statoblast security model","topic":"Safety","weight":0},{"fragment":"assumption-a23","heading":"A23 Users select verified canonical deployments","keywords":[],"path":"reference/security-model.html","sectionTitle":"Reference","summary":"Normative participant, market, deployment, client, and cryptographic assumptions.","text":"The reviewed release is built reproducibly with its pinned compiler and toolchain configuration. The deterministic manifest freshness check rejects address drift, while the deployment plan verifies pinned runtime-code hashes for planned contracts and bootstrap descendants. The canonical factory deploys every security pool from pinned creation code and module deployers, and only those security pools can open a security pool namespace, so isSecurityPool on that factory authenticates a security pool and its modules. Users and integrations must still verify post-deployment wiring, factory and forker provenance, question ledger lineage, question ID, and the manifest against the intended network. Constructor admission alone is not a safety proof. Failure boundary: Toolchain drift, unavailable or mismatched reviewed hashes, permissionless lookalike instances, wrong dependencies, or unintended lineage and question wiring can bypass the reviewed protocol even when the selected bytecode functions as configured. Monitoring checks . Mechanics: release posture , deployment status , compiler configuration , OpenOracle build provenance , and authority invariants .","title":"Statoblast security model","topic":"Safety","weight":0},{"fragment":"assumption-a24","heading":"A24 Clients, RPCs, and wallets preserve user intent and canonical state","keywords":[],"path":"reference/security-model.html","sectionTitle":"Reference","summary":"Normative participant, market, deployment, client, and cryptographic assumptions.","text":"Interfaces, indexers, RPC providers, and wallet software return canonical, reorg-aware state; construct and display the intended chain, target, calldata, parameters, and ETH value; and submit only transactions the user authorizes. Users verify transaction previews rather than treating an application label as an onchain guarantee. Failure boundary: Compromised or stale offchain software can misroute signatures or capital, hide deadlines or events, or cause users to authorize valid but unintended calls without violating contract guards. Monitoring checks . Mechanics: deployment discovery , contract interactions .","title":"Statoblast security model","topic":"Safety","weight":0},{"fragment":"assumption-a25","heading":"A25 Immutable parameters preserve economic and executable security margins","keywords":[],"path":"reference/security-model.html","sectionTitle":"Reference","summary":"Normative participant, market, deployment, client, and cryptographic assumptions.","text":"The selected parameter relationships keep the fork threshold and fork haircut economically meaningful, the start bond affordable, the security pool backing multiplier ( capital health ) large enough for its coverage role, the oracle target error profitable to correct after fees and gas, dispute and settlement windows usable, and callback and transition gas executable. The security pool backing multiplier is independent of the fixed OraclePriceMath factors used to bound the report priority fee. Fork liveness also requires enough REP to have migrated into the active lineage. Thresholds use theoretical supply, so incomplete migration can make the fork backstop unreachable , even for holders of all circulating child REP. Failure boundary: Weak or internally inconsistent immutable parameters can defeat the economic or liveness argument even when the canonical contracts function exactly as configured. Monitoring checks . Mechanics: Statoblast security pool parameters , fork economics , liquidation economics , oracle parameters , and oracle attack model .","title":"Statoblast security model","topic":"Safety","weight":0},{"fragment":"assumption-a26","heading":"A26 Ethereum preserves the execution environment the protocol relies on","keywords":[],"path":"reference/security-model.html","sectionTitle":"Reference","summary":"Normative participant, market, deployment, client, and cryptographic assumptions.","text":"Ethereum continues to provide correct EVM execution and atomic rollback, sufficient canonical finality, usable timestamp and block.basefee semantics, and transaction and block gas limits and gas schedules under which every mandatory immutable transition remains executable. Failure boundary: Deep reorganizations, invalid state execution, adversarial time behavior, or gas repricing can reverse accepted state or strand required transitions. Monitoring checks . Mechanics: contract interactions , atomic external effects and router deadlines , and Escalation Game lifecycle .","title":"Statoblast security model","topic":"Safety","weight":0},{"fragment":"assumption-a27","heading":"A27 Ethereum cryptographic primitives remain secure","keywords":[],"path":"reference/security-model.html","sectionTitle":"Reference","summary":"Normative participant, market, deployment, client, and cryptographic assumptions.","text":"Keccak-256 remains collision- and preimage-resistant at the truncated widths used by the protocol, and CREATE2 identity remains sound. secp256k1 ECDSA signatures remain unforgeable with correct EOA signer recovery for Ethereum transactions and EIP-712 authorizations. Accounts using depositFromPermit2 or granting Permit2 allowances also rely on the fixed singleton, its signature checks, deadlines and nonce enforcement. Verify that implementation under A23 . The standard shared-feed escrow starts reports with direct token approvals. Failure boundary: Forged identities, colliding questions or universes, false carry proofs, forged EOA transactions, incorrect EOA recovery or ERC-1271 acceptance, or broken domain and nonce enforcement can violate otherwise-correct guards or authorize an unintended token transfer. Monitoring checks . Mechanics: universe identity , proof hashing , Permit2 deposit authorization , Permit2 signature interface , EIP-712 authorization signer recovery , pinned Permit2 implementation provenance , and deterministic address guardrails .","title":"Statoblast security model","topic":"Safety","weight":0},{"fragment":"assumption-a28","heading":"A28 Participants retain control of account authority","keywords":[],"path":"reference/security-model.html","sectionTitle":"Reference","summary":"Normative participant, market, deployment, client, and cryptographic assumptions.","text":"Participants protect the private keys, signing devices, session permissions, and delegated ERC-20 or ERC-1155 approvals that authorize their accounts. Account recovery and custody arrangements do not give an unintended party effective control during a security-sensitive window. Failure boundary: A stolen key, compromised signer, or overbroad approval can authorize valid but unwanted transfers, deposits, bids, migrations, or configuration changes without violating a contract guard. Monitoring checks . Mechanics: transaction authority , security pool guardrails .","title":"Statoblast security model","topic":"Safety","weight":0},{"fragment":"excluded-guarantees","heading":"Guarantees deliberately not made","keywords":[],"path":"reference/security-model.html","sectionTitle":"Reference","summary":"Normative participant, market, deployment, client, and cryptographic assumptions.","text":"These outcomes remain possible without contradicting the security model. They should be included in user, operator, and integration risk disclosures. EG01. Zoltar creates valid branches but does not enforce that the fork question is relevant to a user, establish the objectively truthful branch, obtain user consent to a fork, or force users to coordinate on one branch. EG02. A Collateral Repair Auction may raise less than its repair target; the child then activates with migrated collateral plus accepted auction collateral. EG03. Shared security pools bound ordinary commitment growth by the accepted report’s exposure limit and require a separate funded proof for additional growth. Settlement does not prove external price correctness. EG04. Shared security pools isolate concurrent report escrows, but cannot guarantee that any particular disputed report settles within a bounded time. A participant with enough capital can keep correcting one candidate without blocking other independently funded candidates. EG05. The immutable protocol has no administrator who can pause, upgrade, roll back, or rescue an unsafe deployment after launch. EG06. Reporter escrow is separate from saleable auction inventory. A losing escalation claim pays its committed depositor nothing, and reporting claims remain subject to the game and fork rules; segregation does not guarantee a successful report or a particular REP value.","title":"Statoblast security model","topic":"Safety","weight":0},{"fragment":"","heading":"","keywords":["testnet","Sepolia","deploy","private key","spending limit"],"path":"how-to/deploy-testnet-contracts.html","sectionTitle":"How-to guides","summary":"Install the deterministic infrastructure on Sepolia from GitHub Actions or a local shell.","text":"How-to guide The testnet deployer installs the complete deterministic Zoltar and Statoblast infrastructure in one run. It is safe to rerun: a contract already at its target address is skipped only when its runtime bytecode matches, and an unexpected contract at a target address stops the deployment.","title":"Deploy testnet contracts","topic":"Operations","weight":1},{"fragment":"before-you-deploy","heading":"Before you deploy","keywords":[],"path":"how-to/deploy-testnet-contracts.html","sectionTitle":"How-to guides","summary":"Install the deterministic infrastructure on Sepolia from GitHub Actions or a local shell.","text":"Complete the repository setup in the README . Use a dedicated testnet account. To size its balance without deploying, run the deployer once with --max-total-cost-eth=0.000001 : its preflight aborts and the abort message reports the upper-bound cost of the missing steps. A normal run prints the same figure in its Estimated maximum cost log line. Use an HTTPS RPC endpoint. Loopback HTTP endpoints are accepted for local test networks. Make sure the deployer account has no pending transactions. If the testnet rejects the fixed raw legacy transactions that install the canonical CREATE2 deployer and the proxy deployer, make sure both are already present as predeploys. Sepolia is the default target (chain ID 11155111 ). A different testnet must support EIP-1559, the Cancun opcodes used by Zoltar and Uniswap V4, and the Osaka CLZ opcode used by the compiled contracts. The deployer rejects Ethereum mainnet (chain ID 1 ).","title":"Deploy testnet contracts","topic":"Operations","weight":0},{"fragment":"deploy-with-github-actions","heading":"Deploy with GitHub Actions","keywords":[],"path":"how-to/deploy-testnet-contracts.html","sectionTitle":"How-to guides","summary":"Install the deterministic infrastructure on Sepolia from GitHub Actions or a local shell.","text":"Use the Deploy Testnet Contracts workflow for a deployment from main . Create a protected GitHub environment named testnet-deployment . Add the deployer's private key as the environment secret TESTNET_DEPLOYER_PRIVATE_KEY . Optionally add ETHERSCAN_API_KEY as well; without it post-deploy verification skips Etherscan. Open Actions → Deploy Testnet Contracts → Run workflow . Select main , complete the inputs, and enter DEPLOY as the confirmation. Review the job summary for each planned deployment's result and address. It includes transaction hashes for contracts deployed during the run. The job runs only when the workflow is started from main and the confirmation is exactly DEPLOY . Otherwise GitHub skips it without an error and no summary is produced; if the run shows a skipped job, check the branch and confirmation and start it again. Use a public RPC URL without credentials. Workflow inputs are stored in GitHub metadata and are not secret.","title":"Deploy testnet contracts","topic":"Operations","weight":0},{"fragment":"deploy-locally","heading":"Deploy locally","keywords":[],"path":"how-to/deploy-testnet-contracts.html","sectionTitle":"How-to guides","summary":"Install the deterministic infrastructure on Sepolia from GitHub Actions or a local shell.","text":"Load PRIVATE_KEY from a secret manager or a hidden prompt. Never paste the key into a command, because the command may be saved in shell history. In Bash: read -rsp 'Testnet deployer private key: ' PRIVATE_KEY \\ && echo && export PRIVATE_KEY Run the deployer with an explicit RPC endpoint and spending limits: bun run deploy:testnet -- \\ --rpc-url=https://rpc.example \\ --chain-id=11155111 \\ --max-fee-per-gas-nanoeth=100 \\ --max-total-cost-eth=35 Remove the key from the shell when the command finishes: unset PRIVATE_KEY Run bun run deploy:testnet -- --help for all options. Input Default Purpose RPC_URL / --rpc-url Required RPC endpoint for the target network CHAIN_ID / --chain-id 11155111 Expected decimal chain ID MAX_FEE_PER_GAS_NANO_ETH / --max-fee-per-gas-nanoeth 100 Rejects higher RPC fee suggestions. If a canonical deployer is missing, the run aborts when this is below 100 , the fixed gas price of the deployers' raw transactions. MAX_TOTAL_COST_ETH / --max-total-cost-eth 20 Caps the conservative preflight estimate and transaction budget PRIVATE_KEY / --private-key Required 0x -prefixed 32-byte deployer key Preflight estimates an upper bound for the missing steps. If it exceeds MAX_TOTAL_COST_ETH , the command exits before sending any transaction, including on Anvil. The same budget applies during deployment. The run logs each planned contract with Status: deployed , Status: already deployed , or Status: ready (installed without a submitted transaction), then verifies the bootstrap support contracts. A step that fails logs Status: failed and stops the run. After deployment, source verification runs on Sepolia only. A verification failure exits nonzero even though the contracts were deployed. Retry just that stage with bun run verify:contracts -- --chain-id=11155111 . Etherscan requires ETHERSCAN_API_KEY .","title":"Deploy testnet contracts","topic":"Operations","weight":0},{"fragment":"recover-an-interrupted-run","heading":"Recover an interrupted run","keywords":[],"path":"how-to/deploy-testnet-contracts.html","sectionTitle":"How-to guides","summary":"Install the deterministic infrastructure on Sepolia from GitHub Actions or a local shell.","text":"Wait for all pending transactions to settle, then rerun the same command. The deployer revalidates completed contracts and resumes with the first missing step.","title":"Deploy testnet contracts","topic":"Operations","weight":0},{"fragment":"what-gets-deployed","heading":"What gets deployed","keywords":[],"path":"how-to/deploy-testnet-contracts.html","sectionTitle":"How-to guides","summary":"Install the deterministic infrastructure on Sepolia from GitHub Actions or a local shell.","text":"The deployer installs the shared dependencies and both genesis deployments of Zoltar, Statoblast, and Trading. Creating or funding a Uniswap pool is a separate step. See the deployment manifests for addresses and the contract inventory for Uniswap dependencies by chain. The bots use the Yes deployment in the tracked mainnet and Sepolia manifests, so on testnets they work only against Sepolia and Sepolia replays such as a local Anvil node with chain ID 11155111 . A deployment to another testnet is not usable by the bots until that chain has a generated manifest.","title":"Deploy testnet contracts","topic":"Operations","weight":0},{"fragment":"","heading":"","keywords":["release","launch","checklist","genesis REP","manifest"],"path":"how-to/launch-a-release.html","sectionTitle":"How-to guides","summary":"The pre-tag checklist, runtime-hash review, and the testnet genesis REP review.","text":"How-to guide Follow this guide before tagging a Zoltar and Statoblast release. It lists the checks that must pass before the tag and, for Sepolia and deterministic testnets, the genesis REP allocation review. Mainnet uses Augur V2's existing REP Yes and REP No tokens and deploys no genesis REP tokens.","title":"Prepare a protocol release","topic":"Operations","weight":1},{"fragment":"immutable-protocol-release-posture","heading":"Know what a release cannot do","keywords":[],"path":"how-to/launch-a-release.html","sectionTitle":"How-to guides","summary":"The pre-tag checklist, runtime-hash review, and the testnet genesis REP review.","text":"Publish the commit and tag, deterministic addresses, CI and QA results, production UI artifact hash, and known limitations. The contracts are immutable. See EG05 for the limits of a release.","title":"Prepare a protocol release","topic":"Operations","weight":0},{"fragment":"launch-release-checklist","heading":"Run the release checklist","keywords":[],"path":"how-to/launch-a-release.html","sectionTitle":"How-to guides","summary":"The pre-tag checklist, runtime-hash review, and the testnet genesis REP review.","text":"From a fresh dependency install ( bun install --frozen-lockfile ), run bun run check:mainnet-deployment:generated . It fails when any of the four generated deployment manifests is stale. If so, regenerate them with bun ./tooling/contracts/check-mainnet-deployment.mts --write . Review the Yes manifests for mainnet and Sepolia , and the No manifests for mainnet and Sepolia , then rerun the check. Run bun run ui:build:prod . It repeats the manifest check, so it fails if a manifest is still stale. Confirm the build used the pinned compiler configuration in solidity/ts/compile.ts , the exact tool versions in solidity/package.json , and the imported OpenOracle profile in UPSTREAM.md . Review the pinned runtime-code hashes: the EXPECTED_*_RUNTIME_CODE_HASHES maps in ui/zoltarShared/ts/protocol/deployment.ts and ui/statoblastShared/ts/protocol/deployment.ts . Check them in three ways, then publish the reviewed hashes with the release: Run bun ./tooling/testing/bun-test.mts --preload ./bun-test-setup-ui.ts ui/zoltar/ts/tests/protocol/deployment.test.ts ui/statoblast/ts/tests/protocol/deployment.test.ts . It confirms that a clean local build still matches the pinned hashes of the directly compiled steps (the deployment status oracle, Multicall3, ZoltarQuestionData, and six Statoblast artifacts); it does not cover the other pinned contracts. On Sepolia, rerun bun run deploy:testnet . It checks the runtime code of every installed contract against its pinned hash. It is not read-only: it needs PRIVATE_KEY , deploys any missing step, and then runs explorer verification. For mainnet, set MAINNET_RPC_URL to a reader supporting finalized blocks and block-hash code queries. Prepare a JSON object keyed by the lowercase addresses of the Yes REP, No REP, and WETH tokens. Give each entry its independently reviewed runtime hash and a source describing its provenance. Run bun run verify:mainnet-runtime external-token-pins.json release-report.json from the release checkout. It reads both genesis deployments at one finalized block and writes a new report with expected and observed hashes. Missing or mismatched code produces a failing exit status. Publish the passing report from the clean release commit. Once CI passes on the same commit, push a tag in the form v<number> (for example v12 ). Pin all four app exports from the versioned image on a publicly reachable InterPlanetary File System (IPFS) node. The workflow retrieves each app and its assets through an independent HTTPS gateway and checks them against the image. Set the IPFS_GATEWAY repository variable to override https://ipfs.io . If retrieval fails, restore availability and rerun. Confirm the GitHub release records all four content identifiers before announcing. Keep manual notes outside the block managed by the Version deploy workflow . In the production UI release notes, state that ?simulate=1 is a browser-local sandbox. Treat a stale, unavailable, or unsupported live quote as a blocker for the affected client action; never substitute a simulation price for it.","title":"Prepare a protocol release","topic":"Operations","weight":0},{"fragment":"genesis-rep-deployment","heading":"Review genesis REP allocations (Sepolia and deterministic testnets)","keywords":[],"path":"how-to/launch-a-release.html","sectionTitle":"How-to guides","summary":"The pre-tag checklist, runtime-hash review, and the testnet genesis REP review.","text":"Review the holder list and computed total in sepoliaRepAllocations.ts before deployment. Each genesis token divides its 11 million REP cap equally among those holders. Adding a holder reduces every allocation, and rounding can leave less than one attoREP per holder unminted. There is no administrator, post-deployment mint, allocation correction, or recovery entrypoint, so fix an incorrect allocation in the source list before deployment and then regenerate the deterministic manifests. Because constructor arguments are part of CREATE2 init code, any allocation change produces a new genesis REP address and new addresses for every deployment step whose constructor wiring depends on it. Never continue a deployment from a manifest generated for a different allocation list.","title":"Prepare a protocol release","topic":"Operations","weight":0},{"fragment":"","heading":"","keywords":["monitoring","assumptions","operators","signals"],"path":"how-to/monitor-security-assumptions.html","sectionTitle":"How-to guides","summary":"Check the assumptions behind an action: what to read, how often, and how to respond when one is weak.","text":"How-to guide Check that the economic and liveness assumptions behind a deadline-bearing or price-sensitive action still hold before you take it, and keep checking until its last deadline. The contracts cannot tell you when such an assumption has stopped holding.","title":"Monitor security assumptions","topic":"Operations","weight":1},{"fragment":"how-to-use","heading":"Check an action's assumptions","keywords":[],"path":"how-to/monitor-security-assumptions.html","sectionTitle":"How-to guides","summary":"Check the assumptions behind an action: what to read, how often, and how to respond when one is weak.","text":"List the assumptions the action depends on. The security model lists which assumptions protect which mechanism. Find the rows for those assumptions in the table below . Record the lineage, ledger, block number and hash for the decision. Use one block for current state. For A07, record the pre-fork parent and repair-time child snapshots separately. Compare values in one collateral unit. Label each result observed, estimated or unknown. If a read fails or the block hash changes, retry the complete snapshot while time permits. Use canonical block-hash reads where supported and recheck the hash for number-only reads. Discard a stale simulation. If evidence is still missing, apply the row’s response. Repeat the reads on the row's cadence until the action's last deadline has passed. Most on-chain reads are also indexed by augurScan under /operations/… (see the augurScan README ). The liquidator and OpenOracle arbitrager bots in bots/ show live security pool, vault, and report state in their dashboards.","title":"Monitor security assumptions","topic":"Operations","weight":0},{"fragment":"terms","heading":"Terms used in the table","keywords":[],"path":"how-to/monitor-security-assumptions.html","sectionTitle":"How-to guides","summary":"Check the assumptions behind an action: what to read, how often, and how to respond when one is weak.","text":"Corrector An account that disputes an incorrect OpenOracle report; see A17 . Callback-recovery candidate A report OpenOracle has settled but the shared feed has yet to process; see settlement recovery .","title":"Monitor security assumptions","topic":"Operations","weight":0},{"fragment":"assumption-monitoring","heading":"Reads, cadence, and response for each assumption","keywords":[],"path":"how-to/monitor-security-assumptions.html","sectionTitle":"How-to guides","summary":"Check the assumptions behind an action: what to read, how often, and how to respond when one is weak.","text":"Assumptions What to read Review cadence Response when the boundary is weak or unknown Executor incentives and participation — A01, A02, A03, A06 Name the next caller, its benefit, capital costs and ETH for gas. A takeover credits ledger principal and also needs receiver consent. Use the liquidator dashboard to check blockers. Check whether a funded caller will settle, including third-party settlement settings on a bot. For a contest, identify funded opposing support. Use augurScan activity in /operations/reports , /operations/escalations and /operations/auctions to identify participants. Establish independent control separately. For a settled report whose callback failed or was deferred, follow settlement recovery . Continuously for deadline-bearing flows; before launch and after parameter or gas-regime changes Fund or arrange independent executors, reduce exposed value, or disclose that the affected transition may stall Fork lineage value and migration readiness — A05, A07, A09, A11 Fork start: Zoltar getForkTime(universeId) Check native REP migration, each underwriter’s branch-choice deadline and each share holder’s ability to call migration into the active branch. Track the branches actually supported in /operations/forks . Value parent backing at the pre-fork block and child backing at repair. Record both inventories and prices, and the portion of child REP available for sale. For A05, name the participants choosing a branch, the access they lose, their next-best alternative and the secured exposure. Estimate a range. Mark deterrence unknown if that range crosses the hurdle. Before a fork-security claim and throughout migration and coordination windows Do not claim fork deterrence, practical migration, or value concentration; narrow supported lineage and exposure REP value and liquidity versus secured exposure — A08, A13, A14 Theoretical REP supply : Zoltar getUniverseTheoreticalSupplyAttoRep(universeId) Read question settlementCollateralAttoEth() with lastSettlementSecurityPoolTime() , its checkpoint. Define the exposure being valued. Pool oracle exposure and system-wide open interest can differ. Avoid counting the same REP backing independently for several ledgers. REP deposited as underwriting capital: security pool capitalLedger(universeId) , then the rep value from that ledger's totals() For a valid nonzero lastPrice , raw collateral units = raw REP units × 10 18 ÷ lastPrice . Apply the collateral decimals for display. Record the observation time and priceValidUntil . See the six-decimal example . For any required trade, record the pair, direction, size and latest usable time. Quote at that size and account for inventory already held. Security pool and vault risk: augurScan /operations/risk Estimate discounted fee income off-chain. Record the supply and price basis of any market-cap proxy. If plausible valuations straddle the exposure, mark economic coverage unknown. At launch, continuously while open interest exists, and after material price, supply, liquidity, or exposure changes Cap or stop new exposure in clients and disclosures; do not describe REP backing as economically sufficient Outcome evidence and escalation funding — A04, A10 Read getEscalationGameEndDate() , the last open second of the scheduled game. Project the new median and deadline after a change of leader. Check whether responders have time to deliver REP. Required support , which closes the game once it exceeds binding capital while one outcome strictly leads: totalCostAttoRep() Outcome funding: getOutcomeBalancesAttoRep() and getBindingCapitalAttoRep() , or augurScan /operations/escalations Count wallet REP available for deposit. Add only principal the owner can withdraw and deliver before the cutoff. Identify the resolution source and expected publication time. Allow time to interpret it, mobilize REP and obtain inclusion. From question creation through local resolution and any fork window Mark the market or action impaired, surface the deadline, and avoid asserting that honest local correction remains fundable Collateral Repair Auction demand — A12 Collateral Repair Auction bids: auction engine() , then engine bidCount() , bids(index) , and previewFinalization(maximumFilledLots, floorAttoRepPerLot) , or augurScan /operations/auctions Check that the auction opened with a fresh funded quote and at least one day for bidding. Closing needs a funded quote covering both pool collateral exposure and auction REP inventory, observed in the final day at or before close, and no fork at or before close. If ineligible, project zero proceeds even with funded bids. For an eligible close, preview funded lots at the closing REP floor using available saleable inventory and the target lot cap. Collateral credited is the lesser of the shortfall and filled lots × collateral per lot. Estimate bidder costs and market depth separately. Before and during every repair auction Disclose likely incomplete repair and plan operation of the child at only migrated collateral plus collateral raised by the auction Transaction inclusion and Ethereum execution — A16, A26 Base and priority fees: RPC eth_feeHistory Estimate gas units for the actual call, compare transaction and block gas limits, then price the units. Estimate branch preparation and share migration as separate calls. Reorg history: augurScan /operations/integrity Escalation deadline: getEscalationGameEndDate() Collateral Repair Auction close: auction closesAt() Branch-choice cutoff : Zoltar getForkChoiceDeadline(universeId) , eight weeks after the fork For a shared-feed report at time t , disputes open at t + disputeDelay and close strictly before t + settlementTime . Use the deployed values. Take the last eligible second (the scheduled deadline for escalation) and subtract expected inclusion delay to estimate the latest submission time. Subtract the current time for remaining slack. Fee history alone cannot establish inclusion. Continuously while a deadline or mandatory transition is live Raise transaction fees where rational, use independent submission paths, and disclose missed-deadline or stranded-transition risk REP/collateral price correction — A17, A18, A19 Read PriceRequested events from the shared feed returned by SecurityPool.priceFeed(universeId) , including a prepared branch with no question ledger yet, starting at feed deployment. For each report ID, read the feed’s reportOutcome(reportId) . For a requested ID, 1 means accepted, 2 means rejected, and 0 needs follow-up. For each unresolved ID, check OpenOracle storedGame(reportId) and follow settlement recovery if already settled. pendingReportId identifies only the latest candidate and can be zero while older candidates remain unresolved. For the active price, check the feed epoch and expiry. Acceptance at the same eligibility timestamp need not replace the cached price. For each candidate, verify an executing signer on this network, universe and collateral, its approvals and its remaining position capacity. The reference bot can run in dry-run mode and defaults to one open position. Creation-time floor: security pool minimumReportCollateral and the selected collateral token’s units and current ETH value Correction economics: compare obtainable correction gains with OpenOracle fees, current ETH gas costs including approvals and recovery, price impact, and capital lockup under A18 ; do not compare the whole stake with gas alone Net concurrent commitments against each corrector’s REP, collateral and gas funds. Check an independent reference venue at the intended size. Compare the same actor’s payoff from correcting and waiting, including any shared incentives or side payments that can be established. Continuously while a report is pending or a cached price can authorize operations Do not add exposure when correction is uneconomic; disclose that a wrong report can settle despite passing the funding checks. The floor is immutable: a higher minimum requires a new security pool. Token behavior and recipient compatibility — A21, A22 Sepolia genesis REP code: keccak-256 of eth_getCode against reputationToken in EXPECTED_SEPOLIA_DEPLOYMENT_RUNTIME_CODE_HASHES ( ui/zoltarShared/ts/protocol/deployment.ts ) Sepolia WETH code: the same against sepoliaPublished.weth.runtimeCodeHash in scripts/artifacts/uniswap-deployment.json Mainnet genesis REP (Augur V2 REP Yes and REP No fork child tokens) and WETH code: an externally sourced reference hash (no repository pin) Sepolia genesis REP supply: shared/zoltar/ts/deployment/sepoliaRepAllocations.ts against getTotalTheoreticalSupply() From a disposable sender, simulate a withdrawal to check WETH redemption. Exercise the intended recipient’s native-ETH receive path or ERC-1155 callback for the actual delivery. At deployment verification and before first use of a new recipient or integration Reject the deployment or incompatible recipient; do not rely on redirected recovery Deployments, clients, data, and keys — A20, A23, A24, A27, A28 Select the intended chain and Yes or No genesis release. Compare its manifest and wiring with the selected deployment. Check manifest freshness with bun run check:mainnet-deployment:current . Explorer source verification: bun run verify:contracts -- --chain-id=<chain ID> If the status oracle has no code, its mask is unavailable. Set bits indicate code presence only. Read getDeploymentMask() Runtime hashes: release checklist For inherited claims, record the indexer coverage start and snapshot block. Reconstruct the outcome snapshot root from canonical history and check membership. Include the nullifier path for standalone games. Check the signer inventory and on-chain approvals for the owner, spender or operator, asset and chain. Inspect typed-data domains and nonces before signing. At release, on every environment or account change, and continuously for indexers Stop signing or operating, reject an unverifiable deployment, revoke compromised authority where possible, and re-establish canonical state from independent sources Question selection — A15 Read the immutable title, description, dates and outcome labels from QuestionCreated . Compare the full terms with the intended question, or recompute its ID with getQuestionId . Evidence plan and security pool relevance: off-chain review Before security pool selection Reject or warn on the selection; do not represent an admitted question as relevant or resolvable Immutable parameters — A25 Deployed configuration: manifest protocolConfig Fork and non-decision thresholds: Zoltar getForkThresholdAttoRep(universeId) and getNonDecisionThresholdAttoRep(universeId) Oracle costs: shared feed getRequestPriceCostAttoEth() and getSettlementCallbackGasLimit() Compare the fork threshold with minted child totalSupply() , then with REP willing holders can mobilize. A 50 REP threshold with 40 REP minted requires further migration before a fork is reachable. Before deployment and after any change in external gas or market conditions used by the model If migration is insufficient, disclose that the backstop is presently unreachable and recheck after migration. An unsuitable immutable configuration requires another deployment.","title":"Monitor security assumptions","topic":"Operations","weight":0},{"fragment":"","heading":"","keywords":["operators","guardrails","recovery","edge cases"],"path":"reference/operator-guardrails.html","sectionTitle":"Reference","summary":"What each subsystem will and will not accept, mapped to contract sources.","text":"Reference Check these subsystem boundaries before sending a transaction. See the security model for external assumptions and the invariant catalog for cross-contract properties.","title":"Subsystem guardrails","topic":"Safety","weight":1},{"fragment":"security-pool-guardrails","heading":"Security pool capital operations","keywords":[],"path":"reference/operator-guardrails.html","sectionTitle":"Reference","summary":"What each subsystem will and will not accept, mapped to contract sources.","text":"Every question belongs to a shared security pool. Verify the factory registration, capital ledger, settlement module and universe before sending funds. Deposits add principal; setting a commitment separately authorizes risk. Withdrawals and retirement check current owner principal and demand. Complete-set purchases use the registered security pool route and recheck fees, available healthy capacity and backing floor. Before Liquidation takeover , deposit backing REP and set a health target. Verify the funding caps, price epoch, health target and retirement cohort. The reward goes to the receiver vault. See how takeovers and cures work and the capital ledger reference .","title":"Subsystem guardrails","topic":"Safety","weight":0},{"fragment":"share-migration","heading":"Share migration","keywords":[],"path":"reference/operator-guardrails.html","sectionTitle":"Reference","summary":"What each subsystem will and will not accept, mapped to contract sources.","text":"Share tokens retain one question lineage and separate outcome balances per universe. A forked source uses its canonical child question. Activate the shared branch with prepareBranch , startBranch and finalizeBranch before migrating shares into it; until then ShareToken.migrate reverts with Fork branch not active instead of running that work inside the migration. In an active branch a single-target migration materializes a missing child question itself. Finalized claims retain protected source settlement; do not assume every source claim must migrate. See ShareToken entrypoints for migration and recovery calls.","title":"Subsystem guardrails","topic":"Safety","weight":0},{"fragment":"escalation-resolution-and-deposits","heading":"Reporting and settlement","keywords":[],"path":"reference/operator-guardrails.html","sectionTitle":"Reference","summary":"What each subsystem will and will not accept, mapped to contract sources.","text":"For reports through the question ledger, approve wallet REP to that ledger; direct deposits into an existing Escalation Game require approval to the game. The accepted deposit enters separate reporter custody and never becomes provider capital. Finalized local claims use deposit indexes; inherited claims use authenticated frozen-leaf proofs. Payouts go to recorded owners. Batches can be split, and deep lineage proofs use bounded preparation transactions. See Escalation Game entrypoints for deposits and claims.","title":"Subsystem guardrails","topic":"Safety","weight":0},{"fragment":"fork-migration","heading":"Shared forks","keywords":[],"path":"reference/operator-guardrails.html","sectionTitle":"Reference","summary":"What each subsystem will and will not accept, mapped to contract sources.","text":"Owners choose one branch before the native cutoff. The shared coordinator snapshots and prepares each chosen branch once, then lazy owner activation and question materialization apply that state. A missing keeper cannot extend choice or auction deadlines. See the shared fork processing reference for branch preparation.","title":"Subsystem guardrails","topic":"Safety","weight":0},{"fragment":"collateral-repair-auction-operations","heading":"Collateral repair auctions","keywords":[],"path":"reference/operator-guardrails.html","sectionTitle":"Reference","summary":"What each subsystem will and will not accept, mapped to contract sources.","text":"The branch auction sells fresh unchosen provider REP against its aggregate collateral shortfall. Buyers bid lots of the security pool’s collateral token with minimum REP-per-lot limits and their health targets. Winning backing and commitments enter aggregate accounting immediately at closing. Activating a winner enables individual controls; it is not required to count the winner’s obligation. Losing-bid credits and refunds remain independently withdrawable. See the Collateral Repair Auction reference for bid and refund calls.","title":"Subsystem guardrails","topic":"Safety","weight":0},{"fragment":"repeth-oracle-operations","heading":"REP/collateral price reports","keywords":[],"path":"reference/operator-guardrails.html","sectionTitle":"Reference","summary":"What each subsystem will and will not accept, mapped to contract sources.","text":"Verify that the report sponsor can fund its collateral, REP and settlement bounty separately from provider capital. A pending report does not block independent candidates. Capital operations recheck price, owner authorization, health and exposure. See sponsor-authorized operation batches for report-bound calls.","title":"Subsystem guardrails","topic":"Safety","weight":0},{"fragment":"","heading":"","keywords":["inventory","factories","delegates","SecurityPoolUtils","trust boundary"],"path":"reference/contract-inventory.html","sectionTitle":"Reference","summary":"Every production Solidity source by area, the caller and trust boundaries of deployment helpers and delegate modules, and testnet Uniswap dependencies.","text":"Reference This page lists every production Solidity source by area. The contract interaction reference documents the entrypoints of the contracts users and keepers call; this page adds the caller and trust boundaries of the deployment helpers, delegate modules, and libraries that reference does not cover, and the Uniswap contracts a testnet deployment uses.","title":"Contract inventory","topic":"Contracts","weight":1},{"fragment":"caller-and-trust-boundaries","heading":"Caller and trust boundaries","keywords":[],"path":"reference/contract-inventory.html","sectionTitle":"Reference","summary":"Every production Solidity source by area, the caller and trust boundaries of deployment helpers and delegate modules, and testnet Uniswap dependencies.","text":"Deployment helpers are not interchangeable with canonical protocol discovery. Several are intentionally permissionless and bind the caller into each deployed address, through a caller-namespaced salt or the caller in the init code; an application must discover canonical instances through SecurityPoolFactory , recognized question ledgers , and recognized question forker events rather than treating every factory-created address as canonical. Module Direct caller boundary Canonical-use boundary SecurityPool SecurityPoolFactory.deploySecurityPool(creationCode, configuration) is the only way to create a security pool the factory recognizes: it deploys the pinned creation code with the factory and its pinned module deployers, and records the result in isSecurityPool . The creator chooses only the configuration: an immutable ERC-20 collateral token, positive collateral report stake floor, admission policy, question horizon, economic configuration. After construction, four permissionless prepareNext() transactions initialize settlement, reporter/fork modules, capital, then the shared feed and activation. setupStage == 4 is required before accepting user funds or risk. Anyone may register an allowed, open question in the unforked origin universe. After the origin forks, this security pool cannot admit new questions in any branch; see question admission after a fork . getQuestionLedger(questionId, universeId) resolves the registered lineage through the configured factory. Each universe has one capital ledger and one shared REP/collateral feed. The central settlement book owns question cash and supply accounting; the capital ledger owns each provider’s REP and commitments; the reporter ledger separately owns report escrow. Native question contracts expose shares and reporting. Verify isSecurityPool on the canonical factory and the module bindings independently. See the security pool model . Question admission policies ISecurityPoolQuestionAdmissionPolicy.isQuestionAllowed(securityPool, universeId, questionId) is a read-only approval check. SecurityPoolOpenAdmissionPolicy always approves. SecurityPoolFixedAdmissionPolicy(universeId, questionIds) fixes a nonempty, duplicate-free list at construction with no mutation authority. Its paginated getter returns at most 64 entries. Policy approval is required for registration and purchase, including reuse of a registered question. Denial or a failed policy call does not block exits, claims or fork recovery. The UI offers the open and fixed policies; the contract API permits other policies. Security pool economic configuration EconomicsConfig fixes purchaseFeeBps (at most 10,000), annualFeeBps , minimumPurchaseFeeAttoEth , positive minimumCommitmentAttoEth , minimumOutcomeRate , positive rewardVestingSeconds (at least 24 hours) of at most MAXIMUM_REWARD_VESTING_SECONDS (3,650 days). Read these immutable settings from the security pool’s corresponding getters. The creator supplies the security multiplier in OriginConfig.securityMultiplierBps . Each provider selects its own health target through setHealthTarget . Raising it on a live position requires a fresh price and sufficient backing. depositAndSetHealthTarget(amount, targetHealthFactorBps) adds REP and updates the target atomically, applying the same backing check to live target increases. Lowering a target does not increase commitment. The protocol’s liquidation requirement remains unchanged. SecurityPoolCapitalLedger : provider accounts depositFor(owner, amount) is callable only by that owner and adds REP to their entire live position, or to idle principal when they have no commitment. setCommitment(commitment) sets the owner’s whole collateral commitment K, backed by all deposited REP C. Without a pending retirement request, raising or keeping K applies at once, subject to a fresh price and the owner’s health target; a raise must also fit the accepted report’s funded exposure limit. setCommitmentWithReport(reportId, commitment) can instead prove coverage for a raise using a fresh report the shared feed accepted . Its conservative collateral floor stays attached to the owner until the next canonical price epoch, which must cover actual aggregate exposure. Each owner has at most one live position per security pool and universe, active or retiring, with exact C/K amounts. ownerCollateral(owner) returns deposited principal, commitment and currently withdrawable principal, excluding collateral and REP earnings. The chosen health target controls authorization, takeover receipt and pledged-principal withdrawal. Liquidation uses the protocol floor, whose security multiplier is at least 10,500 basis points. SecurityPoolCapitalLifecycleDelegate All capital ledgers in a security pool share a lifecycle delegate for authorization, owner withdrawals, earnings claims, and fork-choice export, import, and backing release. The ledger calls it through delegatecall , using its own storage layout from SecurityPoolCapitalState . Direct calls to delegate mutation entrypoints revert. The ledger’s entrypoints retain caller permissions, reentrancy guards and price/checkpoint requirements. Principal and earnings stay in the ledger. Preparation reports cache prices without starting imported positions’ health history. finishForkImport seals the funded imports and records a health observation only if the cached price is still valid. The capital implementation pins the security pool’s lifecycle and auction delegates. Principal withdrawal and retirement withdrawPrincipal(amount, recipient) withdraws idle principal without a price requirement. A live position must be unforked and have a fresh price; its remaining REP must cover K at ownerConservativePrice(owner) , the protocol multiplier and the saved health target. That price includes an unexpired growth-proof floor when present. Lowering K with setCommitment at a fresh price can cure an uncovered deficit immediately when it meets the economic minimum and capacity permits; a smaller deficit queues the whole reduction, for partial and full exits alike. At a stale price the whole reduction is queued. Zero requests a full exit; a nonzero residual and the queued reduction must each meet minimumCommitmentAttoEth . The owner cannot change or cancel a pending request through setCommitment : until it is served, setCommitment accepts only the pending value, which leaves the request in place and cures an underwater position at a fresh price. Retirement requests are FIFO by immutable request number, independently of health ordering. Adding REP cannot improve priority. Anyone may call serveRetirement(positionId) ; it serves exactly that position’s own request when ready, and the released principal stays with its owner. Principal withdrawal also attempts the owner’s ready request. No call processes positions other than the one named, but an owner cannot leave a ready request reserving spare capacity that its ineligible position no longer supplies. A later owner may complete before earlier owners claim only when spare healthy capacity reserves every earlier requested reduction and their own full healthy reduction. Partial retirement retains all C and returns the residual K to active status; a full exit releases C to its owner. Request sums use a uint64 Fenwick index with at most 65 storage slots per update and 64 per prefix query, independent of queue length. The healthy commitment tree remains separate. Imported fork choices preserve request numbers. Transferring to a new queued receiver, or adding commitment to a queued receiver, places its request at the tail; backing-only top-ups preserve order. previewRetirement(positionId, acceptedPrice, demand) reports readiness and reserved reductions ahead. It is advisory: price, demand and fees are rechecked at execution. New purchases can use healthy active commitments and retained portions of pending requests, without consuming requested reductions. Purchases, redemptions and earnings createCompleteSet(universeId, questionId, cashAttoEth, recipient, minimumShares, maximumFee) requires admission and fresh healthy capacity. Every purchase pays the larger of the flat minimum and the backing-based fee, plus a prepaid resolution premium. purchaseQuote(universeId, questionId, cash) returns the complete fee and its premium component; purchaseFee(cash) returns the base fee including its flat minimum, without the premium. Capacity must cover purchase cash. Ordinary holding fees continue past calendar end until reporting starts or funded units exhaust. The buyer approves the security pool services contract. The transaction transfers cash plus the actual fee in the selected token, rejecting any fee above maximumFee; no unused budget is transferred. quoteMintRecoveryRep(universeId, questionId, cash) quotes the separate REP contribution needed when that question already holds inherited recovery REP. createCompleteSet authorizes no REP spending; use createCompleteSetWithRecoveryRep with the same arguments plus maximumRecoveryAttoRep to fund that contribution. The buyer approves the canonical settlement contract for REP, separately from the service contract’s collateral allowance. The supplied REP belongs only to the purchased question’s claims and preserves existing holders’ REP per share. Redeeming complete sets reduces that question’s cash and aggregate backing demand; the purchase fee is not refunded. Released capacity can serve retiring providers without a claim for every question. Earned fees accrue through aggregate indices; the base purchase fee and reporter surplus vest over the configured duration. A healthy position earns its fraction of total K, including unhealthy K in the denominator. Unassigned collateral and REP are burned. claimEarnings(recipient) pays the owner’s collateral and REP earnings to a recipient the owner chooses, so a token that refuses the owner cannot strand them. Collateral transfer failures do not block fee checkpointing or REP operations. Capital calls services’ withdrawCapitalCash() when a payout needs funds; only services can call settlement’s withdrawServiceFees() . Permissionless flushBurn() on capital and flushBurn(universeId) on services retry quarantined burns. A failed payout reverts and leaves its funded claim available for retry. SecurityPoolCapitalFeeStreams The deployer initializes the clock and rewardDuration when it creates the clone. The bound capital ledger holds the assets and can call addStream(ethAmount, repAmount) to fund a schedule after checkpointing. Equal-second additions share an endpoint; later funding appends a cumulative checkpoint. Lookup excludes released history and searches at most 48 timestamp bits, with the live range bounded by the duration. checkpoint(eligibleThrough) separates releases before and after the eligible cutoff. After a price has been accepted, prepaid streams keep vesting at the last accepted price through stale intervals, with old weights checkpointed before each mutation. Income without eligible backing is quarantined: REP burns immediately and collateral enters pendingBurnCash . It never restarts those amounts for future providers. fundedOutstanding() returns scheduled, unreleased income. Forks preserve existing dates and credit eligible frozen parent commitments at the last accepted price; unassigned parent income burns. Principal and earned claims are excluded. SecurityPoolSettlementBook : claim units and cash pricing Settlement starts with UNIT_SCALE = 10 9 claim units per raw collateral unit. Later purchases pay the current net cash value per unit, including cash retained through redemption rounding. question.units and totals.units are claim weights divided by that scale and rounded up; they are not cash valuations. scaledQuestion , scaledUnits , scaledAvailableFeeBudget , books and frozenBooks expose full-precision weights. The public question view converts fee principal to raw cash and fee rate to raw cash multiplied by UNIT_SCALE per second. The corresponding scaledQuestion fields remain in claim units. Use isCashFunded , requiredCash and requiredForkCash for backing decisions instead of comparing cash with claim weights. validateMintFeePrecision rejects positive-fee purchases whose rate would be zero or lose at least one raw cash unit per year through truncation. Premium weights retain their own fractional precision; fork recovery uses full-precision settlement weights. SecurityPoolServices and SecurityPoolResolutionPremiumBook The service module owns prepaid resolution premiums. It holds no shared budget for keepers or price sponsors. Each question earns its premium only during its funded unresolved interval. A persistent expiry index removes earned units and burns the unused terminal remainder without scanning questions, because the book does not record which buyer paid it. Changes checkpoint earned income under the capital ledger’s old weights. Reporting updates replace only the affected question’s schedule. A stream never runs past the 52-day prepaid window. Forks copy the schedule roots and divide funded cash by chosen commitment; they do not replenish it. A child whose share is zero, or so small that the inherited units exceed 10¹² per unit of cash, starts with an empty premium book, and that share is burned. A surviving child therefore prices new premium at about 10 21 full-precision units per unit of cash at most, and its premium unit domain of 2 208 full-precision units holds about 2 138 raw collateral units of premium even at that ratio. Before imports become operational, the fork coordinator calls checkpointPreparationPremium(child) to burn elapsed inactive premium and any unused terminal remainder. The security pool authenticates native question ledgers, reporters and accepted price reports. The service module credits already-earned capital fees without moving collateral and records each ledger’s pendingCapitalCash . Settlement retains collected fees in pendingServiceFees until the service module withdraws them for an earnings payout or burn. withdrawCapitalCash() pays only the calling ledger’s assigned income; flushBurn(universeId) moves queued unused premium independently. The service module can fund vesting streams and synchronize demand; it cannot change the accepted price or withdraw provider principal. Personal liquidation and bounded accounting Apart from an embedded takeover cure, only the owner cures through setCommitment . takeOver(positionId, commitment, minimumTakeoverReward, maximumOwnRep) enforces the requested minimum takeover reward. After an unpaid cure at the conservative repair price, it transfers a takeover reward from the source’s pledged REP that grows linearly to all of it over one hour and sizes the commitment using the source’s backing after the takeover reward at the conservative price. Smaller fills receive a proportional takeover reward and consume cumulative vesting; they must either restore conservative backing completely or leave the source below the liquidation price so it remains repairable. Maximum terms remain available after one hour. Source principal moves only as the receiver’s pledged takeover reward; old earnings never transfer. A full replacement credits the source owner with any pledged REP not paid as the takeover reward. The receiver supplies remaining backing and earns future fees. A receiver’s resulting commitment must meet the economic minimum; an existing receiver may be active or in the source cohort. An active receiver does not inherit retirement intent; a queued receiver that gains commitment rejoins the request tail. takeOverWithBounds additionally pins price epoch, receiver health target and source cohort and caps resulting principal and commitment. Successful healing cures stand without a transfer. A cure cannot free nonzero principal, including when supported commitment rounds to zero; releasing it requires ordinary retirement. Pledged withdrawals require healthy commitment at least equal to demand. Free principal and earned fees are separate. Admission, fee eligibility and surplus withdrawals use price / (1 − error) , rounded up; owner-requested cures and adverse observations use price / (1 + error) , rounded down, where error is the configured oracle dispute target. Takeover eligibility uses the liquidation bound; the repair amount restores the source at the conservative bound. An owner’s health setting remains separate. Other providers’ C/K balances are unchanged by a target liquidation. Balanced trees index live commitments and health. Finality processing subtracts the previously processed prefix from the current timestamp prefix, using modular arithmetic only for historical totals; each pending deadline’s amount remains checked. SecurityPoolFrozenHistory appends one immutable checkpoint with constant work and resolves claims through at most 48 forest steps and 48 tree steps. Forks copy its head and the processed prefix. Work follows bounded paths and the owner’s single position, without scanning questions, providers or expired streams. Native share migration still requires one action per intervening universe. SecurityPoolCommitmentIndex : liquidation accounting Only the bound capital ledger calls cure(id, price, multiplier, minimumCommitment, demand, target, ownerExit) or clearLiquidation(id) . takeover checks eligibility, applies the conservative-price cure and quotes the transfer backed by the takeover reward in one call. The index updates commitments and records healthy observation epochs while the ledger retains custody. Any owner reduction queues without an immediate cure when its unsupported commitment is below the economic minimum, whether the requested exit is partial or full. Each accepted operating price stores one observation timestamp, without visiting individual owners; preparation prices do not start that history. liquidationStarted(id) derives the first adverse observation after the latest healthy epoch or fresh-price recovery mutation. It returns zero for healthy or retired positions. The capital ledger exposes the same getter. liquidationElapsed(id) counts wall-clock time, including stale-price gaps; a started auction remains executable at the last accepted valuation without renewal; liquidationPenaltyPaid(id) tracks the liquidation penalty already paid from the position during the same distress episode. No starter transaction is required; uninterrupted distress retains its original timestamp, including across stale-price deposits. SecurityPoolForkCoordinator : choices choose(sourceUniverse, outcome) freezes and exports the caller’s single position and its entire deposit, including surplus collateral, into one selected branch. Idle principal without a remaining commitment and old earnings remain in the parent. Choices close eight weeks after the native fork. startBranch(sourceUniverse, outcome) then copies aggregate settlement and reporter roots and migrates REP once for that branch. Choices are immutable once exported and preserve the position’s retirement cohort, request number and requested remaining commitment. The native clock allows activation from week nine and prevents a child fork before week ten. Late preparation does not extend either phase. Parent cash is assigned as sourceCash × chosenCommitment / sourceCommitment , rounded down, independently of excess REP. Unselected child REP belongs to inherited outcome claims; the coordinator never chooses a branch on the owner’s behalf. Shared branch claim recovery prepareBranch , choose , and startBranch can each freeze the source and create the child REP token and capital contracts as needed. The child capital ledger, commitment index, and fee streams are minimal proxies with branch-specific bindings. The shared price feed also uses a minimal proxy and can be prepared independently. startBranch migrates pledged REP and live recovery REP once, splits the requested child once, and assigns chosen collateral to its providers. Fresh unchosen backing may enter the branch auction; old recovery REP remains reserved for inherited claims. finalizeBranch creates the settlement book with assigned parent cash plus accepted auction collateral, installs funded winning commitments, and activates after the fixed deadline. Unsold REP joins claim recovery. An absent or stale price does not prevent activation; eligible holding-fee streams start only with a fresh price in an unforked child. Preparation reporting follows the fixed reporting window . The child settlement book preserves the inherited question units. Collateral redemption uses the question’s settlement units. REP has a separate Q128 entitlement accumulator and per-question corrections, so a partial claim receives its share of REP even when its collateral-unit burn rounds to zero. Finality freezes both entitlements. Live units and funded child REP are each bounded by uint128 ; cumulative REP indices use modular arithmetic across repeated forks. Each fork’s new REP is allocated by question units at that fork, so holding fees charged later in the child do not reduce an inherited question’s REP share. attoSharesToAttoEth and attoSharesToAttoRep quote the two assets through the native question ledger. Cash below the inherited funding target disables new minting and ordinary holding fees; recovery REP remains question-owned through funded minting. If no live claim units exist, surplus child REP is burned. Finalized source claims retain their source assets. SecurityPoolSettlement.topUp(universeId, maximumCashAttoEth) accepts a permissionless donation of the entire positive active cash deficit, read after checkpointing, and returns the amount pulled. It reverts when no deficit remains, the repaired demand would exceed total underwriting commitment, or the live deficit exceeds maximumCashAttoEth , so a redemption or finality that shrinks the deficit before inclusion does not invalidate the donation. It preserves all recovery REP and frozen claims; the sponsor receives no assets or commitments. Fully cash-backed books regain fee eligibility. quoteMintRecoveryRep(bookId, questionId, cashAttoEth) quotes the rounded-up REP needed to preserve an inherited question’s REP per share on minting, and reverts with Recovery REP too large when the book could not hold that REP below 2^128 . The funded custody path pulls that REP from the buyer into settlement, bounded by maximumRecoveryAttoRep , and credits only the purchased question. Existing mint methods have a zero REP budget; fresh questions require no REP contribution. Source recovery eligibility and rights ShareToken.isSecurityPoolRecoveryEligible(sourceUniverse) returns true only for a forked canonical security pool question with live source liabilities. The settlement book’s hasLiveForkRecoveryRight(bookId, questionId, forkTimestamp) requires outstanding outcome supply outside frozen claims. Before sealing, a nonzero finalAfter < forkTimestamp excludes the question once forkTimestamp > minimumFinalAfter , even if finality has not yet been checkpointed. After sealing, the frozen-claim assignment is authoritative. Protected finalized shares retain ordinary source question ledger redemption; transferring or consuming their recovery rights reverts with No live source recovery right . balanceOfSecurityPoolRecoveryRights(sourceUniverse, holder) returns unspent Invalid, Yes and No rights, or all zeros when ineligible; their minimum bounds complete-set cash recovery. Holders combine rights using transferSecurityPoolRecoveryRights(sourceUniverse, recipient, amountsAttoShares) without transferring source shares or child migration rights. recoverUnchosenCash(source, questionId, shares) consumes one right per outcome for each requested share. Protected finalized claims remain in the universe where they finalized. Reporter custody and native question materialization SecurityPoolReporterLedger holds funded per-question report balances in persistent books. Only canonical games can debit their own balances. A fork charges admission to the triggering question only; unrelated report balances cannot fund it. Strictly finalized escrow remains in its source universe; live books migrate once per branch. QuestionForker.prepareSecurityPoolQuestion(source, targetUniverse) creates the canonical question namespace separately from materializeSecurityPoolQuestion , which installs at most one of three fixed outcome snapshots per call when needed; the question activates after the third. Bound questions do not deploy individual Collateral Repair Auctions or migrate provider vaults. Trader tokens retain their immediate-parent migration checks, so skipped native universes still need explicit token migration. SecurityPoolUniformPriceRepairAuction Only its immutable controller starts bidding, submits funded bids, finalizes and settles refunds. Each lot contributes the same fixed collateral amount. Lower minimum REP per lot ranks first. The controller passes finalize(maximumFilledLots, floorAttoRepPerLot) and previewFinalization a floor f , a multiple of the grid ( Auction floor must use the REP price grid ); zero applies none. For inventory I , finalization selects the largest available prefix of F lots, capped by target lots, whose marginal minimum m satisfies F × max(m, f) ≤ I . The floor never changes which bids rank first. Clearing REP per lot is q = max(m, f) and sold REP is F × q , including below target. With no feasible lot, the sale is zero. Bids cannot be cancelled. Each winning lot pledges q REP. Unsold REP I − F × q remains for inherited claims; it does not become winner fee credits. Equal-price bids rank by submission order, so winners are exactly the first F lots in price-then-submission order; at most one bid, the earliest unfilled one at m , fills partially, even when q > m . The engine holds bid collateral; the controller holds sale REP. Refunds are pull credits for the bidder. Before finalization, earlyRefundAttoEth returns the full bid collateral only when the demand ranked before it (lower limits and earlier bids at its limit) B ≥ min(T, ⌊I / p⌋) , where T is target lots and p is the bid’s minimum REP per lot; otherwise it returns zero. Settled, unknown and finalized bids return zero. Refunded bids remain in the tree, permanently outside every winning prefix. The same exclusion condition rejects a new bid at submission, counting every existing bid at its limit as ranked before it. maximumAdmissibleBidAttoRepPerLot() returns the highest grid-aligned limit a new bid can submit against the current book, or zero when none is admissible; SDK quotes and approval guards apply that bound. settleAndWithdrawRefund lets the controller credit one of a bidder’s own refund-eligible bids, unless already credited, and pay that bidder’s entire credit in one transfer; it reverts when nothing is payable, and a rejecting recipient reverts the new credit while older credit stays withdrawable. After finalization, nextUnmaterializedBid(afterBidId) returns the next winner still sharing fee ownership in price order. Zero starts a sweep and also marks its end. A nonzero cursor is excluded from the result and remains valid after its winner is materialized. Zero-weight subtrees skip both losing and detached bids without a history scan. Clearing, individual settlement and fee allocation use logarithmic tree paths without scanning bidders; numeric escrow and fee budgets are bounded by uint128 . SecurityPoolRepairAuction The fork coordinator deploys one controller per eligible branch. It sizes equal collateral lots of lot = ⌈shortfall / ⌊shortfall / minimumCommitmentAttoEth⌋⌉ and offers ⌈shortfall / lot⌉ of them, so their total covers the entire shortfall without requiring it to divide evenly; an auction exists only when the shortfall is at least one minimum commitment. Finalization forwards at most the shortfall to the fork coordinator and burns the rounding surplus of a full fill, which is less than one raw unit per lot. At opening, it can reduce the lot count and enlarge each lot until REP-grid rounding adds less than 1% to the unrounded backing requirement. A single full-shortfall lot may exceed that rounding limit when REP indivisibility requires it. Read lotAttoEth and targetLots after opening for the final bid units. Each bid pulls exactly its whole-lot collateral amount from the bidder. The bidder must approve the auction controller to spend that ERC-20 token. Lots and amounts use raw collateral units. No REP deposit or REP approval is required. The minimum REP per lot must cover requiredRep(lot, openingConservativePrice, M, health) , where M is securityMultiplierBps and health ≥ 10,000 is the chosen target in basis points. This rounds upward after multiplying lot collateral by price and dividing by 10¹⁸ , then after applying M / 10,000 , then health / 10,000 . Bid minima must be positive multiples of grid = M / gcd(M, 10,000) and cannot exceed inventory rounded down to that grid. There is no seller price floor or auction reserve-factor setting; the closing floor below is a minimum REP per lot. Opening requires at least one day before the fixed close, a fresh funded branch price and enough inventory to back one lot at 100% health, rounded up to the grid. The fixed week-nine close uses the last accepted quote only if its settlement-eligibility timestamp lies within the final day; it need not still be fresh at the exact closing second. The sale is cancelled if the universe forked at or before the deadline, no such quote exists, or its accepted exposure limits no longer cover the security pool’s oracle exposure and the inventory. Otherwise the controller finalizes with a floor of requiredRep(lot, closingConservativePrice, M, 10,000) rounded up to the grid: every winning lot receives at least that REP, and fewer lots fill when the inventory cannot provide it. A closing price above the opening price does not cancel the sale; the bidder’s health target applies only at the opening price. Finalization delivers all sold REP into capital; winning commitments equal accepted bid collateral. Pledged REP and fee rights enter aggregate accounting immediately, independent of individual materialization or refunds. settleRefund lets anyone credit a provably excluded bid before finalization, or settle the final unfilled-lot refund afterward. Only the bidder can withdraw its credits, to a recipient of its choice, separately or combined with crediting one of its own eligible bids through settleAndWithdrawRefund ; the combined withdrawal includes credits from the bidder’s other bids. Unsold fresh REP and all old recovery REP remain for inherited claims. Sub-lot shortfalls do not open an auction; the full-deficit donation described above can repair them. SecurityPoolRepairAuctionVault and SecurityPoolCapitalRepairAuctionDelegate Each bid creates a separate vault controlled by its bidder: a minimal proxy to the one implementation the auction deployer publishes, with the bidder, initial ledger, auction and bid identifier fixed in the clone’s own code and the bid identifier as its CREATE2 salt. Each clone keeps its own storage, so per-bid liability isolation is unchanged, and the implementation refuses direct use. Winning principal and commitment first share one protected capital position. materializeRepairAuctionBid splits one exact funded entitlement, preserving health history and historical fees; it pays no keeper reward. The bidder’s vault materializes before its first voluntary operation and then records a permanent flag, so later operations skip the auction lookup and the ledger call; the flag only covers the initial capital ledger, child-universe ledgers after a fork keep their own checks, and it is never set for a zero fill. depositPrincipal pulls approved REP from the controlling wallet. executeAndSweep executes an owner-authorized call and returns the selected collateral, chosen token and ETH balance to the selected recipient. Unmaterialized winners count toward capital, fees and fork snapshots. They cannot avoid liabilities by delaying an individual claim. A winner’s bidder cures through its vault with setCommitment ; takeOverRepairAuctionBid repairs only the affected position, moving the takeover reward from the position’s pledged REP into the receiver’s vault. Bidding requires no ETH execution reserve. Materialization alone earns no reward. Each vault retains an independent health target, liquidation liability and future branch choice. Security pool module deployers SecurityPoolModuleDeployers deploys the capital implementation during security pool setup. Its commitment-index and fee-stream deployers each create a shared implementation at construction. The capital-ledger deployer shares one stateless lifecycle delegate; the fee-stream deployer shares one stateless auction delegate. Each security pool retains separate capital implementations and storage. The capital, settlement-book, service-accounting and fork-coordinator deployers use SecurityPoolCreationCodeDeployer to store creation bytecode in two immutable, STOP-prefixed CreationCodeChunk contracts through the common CreationCodeStorage primitive. The factory pins all nine deployer addresses and supplies them to every security pool it creates. Deployment permission does not grant authority over another security pool’s modules. Canonical bindings and initialization must be verified before use. These deployment boundaries keep creation code and individual initialization transactions within EVM limits. EscalationGameFactory Anyone can call prepareEscalationGame(questionLedger) to deploy an unstarted game bound to that question ledger. The ledger must expose repToken() for preparation and for escalationGameAddress(questionLedger) , which computes the deterministic address. The factory owns the game and starts it through deployEscalationGame on the ordinary reporting path or deployEscalationGameFromFork on the continuation path. Both start paths require the calling question ledger to have a bound reporter security pool and can reuse a prepared game. Ordinary deployment requires a threshold above one attoREP and lowers a start bond at or above that threshold to threshold - 1 . Its constructor receives the separately deployed deposit delegate and requires code at that address. Games share this immutable helper while keeping their own accounting through delegatecall . Deploying the deposit delegate separately keeps factory init code within the EVM limit. The factory has no owner role and no later resumeFromFork relay. Accept a game only after EscalationGameSet from a recognized question ledger. In the supported continuation path, the owning question ledger calls resumeFromFork , which also verifies aggregate funding. ShareTokenFactory Anyone can call deployShareToken to deploy or retrieve a token keyed by keccak256(abi.encode(msg.sender, salt, questionId)) . The CREATE2 salt is the raw salt ; the caller and question id enter through constructor arguments, and that caller is the token's initial authorized address. Origin lineage tokens are the instances created by SecurityPoolFactory and named in DeployQuestionLedger ; children reuse the parent's token. SharedPoolPriceFeedFactory The factory deploys a shared feed implementation per caller and security pool, then universe-bound clones. The canonical security pool factory supplies OpenOracle, the positive report priority fee, and the universe REP token. Feed construction validates the fee against report and escalation limits. The canonical feed is recorded in SecurityPoolFactory.sharedPriceFeeds(securityPool, universeId) and referenced by each ledger in DeployQuestionLedger . Child feeds inherit the security pool’s immutable report configuration. SharedPoolPriceFeedDeploymentWorker and QuestionLedgerDeploymentWorker Deployment workers split large creation code into deployable modules and bind the calling factory. Use canonical factory registration and module bindings; code presence alone is insufficient. QuestionLedgerDeployer and QuestionLedgerDeploymentWorker Only the configured SecurityPoolFactory may call the deployer; only that deployer may call its worker. They are code-size deployment plumbing. The proxy deployer deploys one deterministic QuestionLedgerOperationsDelegate , and its address is passed into the SecurityPoolFactory constructor. The proxy deployer is the deterministic CREATE2 deployment proxy that every canonical deployment goes through; it is not a Solidity source in this repository and appears in the deployment manifests as step proxyDeployer . The factory passes that address through its deployer and worker to every question ledger. Its deterministic address is published as questionLedgerOperationsDelegate . Question ledgers become canonical only when the factory emits QuestionLedgerRegistered and DeployQuestionLedger . QuestionForker Anyone can call the forker to prepare and materialize child question ledgers against shared security pool branch state. It verifies the source ledger in its factory's namespace, target universe ancestry, and child lineage. Consumers identify the canonical factory independently. Reporter and settlement ledgers retain migration custody. EscalationGameDepositDelegate , QuestionLedgerSettlementDelegate , and QuestionLedgerOperationsDelegate EscalationGame delegates share game storage and implement reporting deposits and authenticated historical claims. QuestionLedgerOperationsDelegate handles wallet reporting, continuation resumption, and redemption. These helpers execute in their recognized host context. Accept resulting state and logs only when the recognized game or question ledger executes the matching wrapper through delegatecall . State changes and events then occur in that recognized contract's context. Canonical liquidation enters through the security pool capital ledger; continuation activation enters through QuestionLedger.resumeForkedEscalationGame . QuestionLedger AccountingModule A fixed delegate shares the question ledger storage layout and handles central fee checkpoints, surplus transfers and settlement mirrors. Only events from canonical question addresses describe protocol state. Calling a helper directly does not mutate a registered question. EscalationGameProofVerifier , MerkleMountainRange and SecurityPoolUtils Proof and arithmetic helpers have no independent lifecycle authority. Only an authenticated protocol transaction commits a proof or calculation to state. DeploymentStatusOracle Anyone can read the fixed address list’s code-presence mask. Code presence does not establish readiness. See deployment status verification boundaries .","title":"Contract inventory","topic":"Contracts","weight":0},{"fragment":"internal-arithmetic","heading":"Internal arithmetic","keywords":[],"path":"reference/contract-inventory.html","sectionTitle":"Reference","summary":"Every production Solidity source by area, the caller and trust boundaries of deployment helpers and delegate modules, and testnet Uniswap dependencies.","text":"SecurityPoolUtils.calculateInitialEscalationDepositAttoRep is an internal pure function that derives the first-report bond from theoretical REP supply. It needs no deployed library or linking. Other lifecycle and underwriting math belongs to the corresponding security pool module. UniverseIds.sol derives child-universe IDs from the parent and outcome. OraclePriceMath.sol centralizes conservative and liquidation price bounds and the report-priority-fee ceiling.","title":"Contract inventory","topic":"Contracts","weight":0},{"fragment":"production-contract-inventory","heading":"Production contract inventory","keywords":[],"path":"reference/contract-inventory.html","sectionTitle":"Reference","summary":"Every production Solidity source by area, the caller and trust boundaries of deployment helpers and delegate modules, and testnet Uniswap dependencies.","text":"Area Sources Core and token support CheckedCast.sol , Constants.sol , Context.sol , Create2Address.sol , DecimalStrings.sol , DeploymentStatusOracle.sol , ERC20.sol , GenesisReputationToken.sol , IERC20.sol , IERC20Metadata.sol , IExternalGenesisReputationToken.sol , Pagination.sol , ReputationToken.sol , SafeERC20Ops.sol , Collateral.sol , ICollateralConfig.sol , ScalarOutcomes.sol , Zoltar.sol , ZoltarQuestionData.sol , GenesisUniswapV3Seeder.sol , PredeploymentShareSink.sol , TradingLiquidityToken.sol , TwoWayConstantProductFactory.sol , TwoWayConstantProductMath.sol , TwoWayConstantProductPair.sol , TwoWayConstantProductRouter.sol , AuthorizationSignatures.sol , ERC20Authorization.sol , IERC20Authorization.sol Protocol modules BinaryOutcomes.sol , DelegateCall.sol , EscalationGame.sol , EscalationGameCalculations.sol , EscalationGameCarry.sol , EscalationGameDepositDelegate.sol , EscalationGameEscrow.sol , EscalationGameProofVerifier.sol , EscalationGameRepCustody.sol , EscalationGameSettlement.sol , EscalationGameState.sol , EscalationGameStorage.sol , EscalationGameTypes.sol , MerkleMountainRange.sol , Multicall3.sol , SharedPoolPriceFeed.sol , OraclePriceTypes.sol , QuestionLedger.sol , SecurityPoolRepairAuctionVault.sol , SecurityPoolBoundClones.sol , SecurityPoolCapitalRepairAuctionDelegate.sol , SecurityPoolCapitalFeeStreams.sol , SecurityPoolCapitalLedger.sol , SecurityPoolCapitalLifecycleDelegate.sol , SecurityPoolCapitalState.sol , SecurityPoolCommitmentIndex.sol , SecurityPoolCommitmentTree.sol , SecurityPoolCreationCode.sol , QuestionLedger AccountingModule.sol , SecurityPoolFixedAdmissionPolicy.sol , QuestionForker.sol , SecurityPoolFrozenHistory.sol , QuestionLedger SettlementSync.sol , SecurityPoolMaturityMap.sol , SecurityPoolOpenAdmissionPolicy.sol , QuestionLedgerOperationsDelegate.sol , SecurityPoolPersistentMap.sol , SecurityPoolQuestionMap.sol , SecurityPool.sol , QuestionLedgerAccountingDelegate.sol , SecurityPoolRepairAuction.sol , SecurityPoolForkCoordinator.sol , SecurityPoolModuleDeployers.sol , SecurityPoolPurchase.sol , SecurityPoolServices.sol , SecurityPoolSettlement.sol , SecurityPoolReporterLedger.sol , SecurityPoolResolutionPremiumBook.sol , SecurityPoolSettlementBook.sol , QuestionLedgerSettlementDelegate.sol , SecurityPoolSettlementMath.sol , SecurityPoolSettlementPricing.sol , SecurityPoolSettlementState.sol , QuestionLedgerStorage.sol , SecurityPoolUniformPriceRepairAuction.sol , SecurityPoolUtils.sol , SharedPoolReportEscrow.sol , WETH9.sol , ERC1155.sol , ShareToken.sol , TokenId.sol Factories and deployment Create2Deployment.sol , EscalationGameFactory.sol , SharedPoolPriceFeedFactory.sol , QuestionLedgerDeployer.sol , SecurityPoolFactory.sol , ShareTokenFactory.sol Interfaces ICapitalAccounting.sol , IERC1155.sol , IERC1155Receiver.sol , IERC165.sol , IEscalationGame.sol , IEscalationGameDelegateContexts.sol , ISecurityPoolServiceRoot.sol , ISecurityPoolSettlement.sol , IReportOperations.sol , IQuestionLedger.sol , ISecurityPoolRepairAuctionCapital.sol , IQuestionLedgerDeploymentWorkerConfiguration.sol , IQuestionForker.sol , ISecurityPoolAccounting.sol , ISecurityPoolNative.sol , ISecurityPoolQuestionAdmissionPolicy.sol , ISecurityPoolReporterLedger.sol , IShareToken.sol , IWeth9.sol , IZoltarForkState.sol , ITradingShareToken.sol , ITwoWayConstantProductFactory.sol , ITwoWayConstantProductPair.sol Oracle dependency OpenOracle.sol , ISignatureTransfer.sol , Errors.sol , IERC1363.sol , IERC165.sol , IERC20.sol , IERC20.sol , SafeERC20.sol , Panic.sol , ReentrancyGuard.sol , StorageSlot.sol , IERC165.sol , Math.sol , SafeCast.sol","title":"Contract inventory","topic":"Contracts","weight":0},{"fragment":"testnet-uniswap-dependencies","heading":"Testnet Uniswap dependencies","keywords":[],"path":"reference/contract-inventory.html","sectionTitle":"Reference","summary":"Every production Solidity source by area, the caller and trust boundaries of deployment helpers and delegate modules, and testnet Uniswap dependencies.","text":"The UI, the bots, and the testnet deployer read Uniswap addresses from one registry, shared/core/ts/deployment/uniswapDeployments.ts . Chain WETH, V3 factory, QuoterV2, V4 PoolManager, V4 Quoter SwapRouter Sepolia ( 11155111 ) Uniswap's published contracts ( V3 , V4 ); the deployer requires each to carry Uniswap's exact runtime code. An Anvil node with the Sepolia chain ID receives them by replaying Uniswap's original creation transactions, vendored byte for byte in scripts/artifacts/uniswap-deployment.json . Deterministic SwapRouter (v1) bound to the published factory; Uniswap publishes none on Sepolia. Any other testnet Deterministic WETH plus a complete Uniswap V3 and V4 deployment from the pinned bytecode. Deterministic SwapRouter from the pinned bytecode.","title":"Contract inventory","topic":"Contracts","weight":0},{"fragment":"","heading":"","keywords":["deployment","bitmask","runtime bytecode","status"],"path":"reference/deployment-status.html","sectionTitle":"Reference","summary":"Deployment progress bitmask semantics, per-network bit mappings, and the 256-step limit.","text":"DeploymentStatusOracle is a deployment-progress helper. It does not measure protocol readiness or initialization success. It only reports which configured deployment-step addresses currently contain runtime bytecode.","title":"Deployment status oracle","topic":"Contracts","weight":1},{"fragment":"mask","heading":"What getDeploymentMask() returns","keywords":[],"path":"reference/deployment-status.html","sectionTitle":"Reference","summary":"Deployment progress bitmask semantics, per-network bit mappings, and the 256-step limit.","text":"getDeploymentMask() sets bit i in its uint256 result when the constructor address at zero-based index i contains runtime bytecode. A row of deployment steps maps by index into bit positions in a uint256 mask. Each step sets its bit only when code exists at that configured address. Deployment status mask. Each oracle keeps the constructor’s order for its lifetime.","title":"Deployment status oracle","topic":"Contracts","weight":0},{"fragment":"ordering","heading":"How bits map to deployment steps","keywords":[],"path":"reference/deployment-status.html","sectionTitle":"Reference","summary":"Deployment progress bitmask semantics, per-network bit mappings, and the 256-step limit.","text":"The constructor emits DeploymentAddressesSet(address[] deploymentAddresses) with that exact ordered list. It is the on-chain source for recovering a particular oracle instance's bit mapping. The manifest below describes the current planned deployment and must match the constructor event for that instance. The current user interface (UI) and deployment helpers derive the constructor list from deploymentSteps with one exception: the oracle does not include its own address in its constructor array. The manifest still lists deploymentStatusOracle as a deployment step, but the UI tracks that step out of band and starts consuming mask bits from the remaining addresses. The tables below render the complete bit mappings directly from the canonical mainnet deployment manifest and Sepolia deployment manifest , excluding deploymentStatusOracle . These tables use the Yes manifests. The mainnet No manifest and Sepolia No manifest use the same bit order with their own deployment addresses. Each manifest's ordered deploymentSteps array is the canonical mapping for its network and genesis outcome. The orders differ: Sepolia inserts the genesis REP token at bit 1 , so the same mask names different steps on each network. Select the network before decoding. A second, Zoltar-only oracle is deployed by the Zoltar app's deployment flow and by the testnet deployer for each genesis outcome. The four manifests omit these oracles and the Trading factory and router. The testnet plan identifies the oracles as yes:zoltarDeploymentStatusOracle and no:zoltarDeploymentStatusOracle , and applies the same outcome prefixes to tradingFactory and tradingRouter . Each oracle's constructor list is a prefix of its manifest mapping: the proxy deployer, genesis REP on Sepolia, Multicall3, ZoltarQuestionData , and Zoltar . To decode its mask, select Zoltar-only oracle : only those leading bits exist, and the decoder marks the remaining rows as not tracked instead of missing. Bit Mainnet step id Label Status in entered mask Loading the canonical manifest… Bit Sepolia step id Label Status in entered mask Loading the canonical manifest… If the manifest's constructor order changes, the rendered bit meanings change with it; this page does not maintain a second list. The manifest's derivedContracts list adds no mask bits. Five of its six entries ( questionForker , escalationGameDepositDelegate , escalationGameFactory , securityPoolFactory , and questionLedgerOperationsDelegate ) repeat a deploymentSteps address under a different label, so each already has a bit. Only escalationGameProofVerifier , which EscalationGameFactory deploys in its constructor, has no bit. The tables use the deploymentSteps labels. In the Sepolia manifest the genesis REP step has the id reputationToken and the label GenesisReputationToken . It is the token at network.genesisRepTokenAddress , not a child ReputationToken .","title":"Deployment status oracle","topic":"Contracts","weight":0},{"fragment":"deployment-mask-decoder","heading":"Decode a deployment mask","keywords":[],"path":"reference/deployment-status.html","sectionTitle":"Reference","summary":"Deployment progress bitmask semantics, per-network bit mappings, and the 256-step limit.","text":"Decoded status Loading the canonical mapping… Network Ethereum mainnet Sepolia Oracle Full deployment oracle Zoltar-only oracle Mask value The decoder maps each set bit to the selected network's constructor address at the same index. Retry loading the canonical mapping","title":"Deployment status oracle","topic":"Contracts","weight":0},{"fragment":"limit","heading":"Why the cap is 256 steps","keywords":[],"path":"reference/deployment-status.html","sectionTitle":"Reference","summary":"Deployment progress bitmask semantics, per-network bit mappings, and the 256-step limit.","text":"The constructor requires deploymentAddresses.length <= uint256(type(uint8).max) + 1 , which is 256 . The limit exists because the result is a single uint256 , and each tracked step consumes one bit. Indices 0 through 255 are supported. A deployment process with more than 256 tracked addresses must split status across multiple masks or use a different representation.","title":"Deployment status oracle","topic":"Contracts","weight":0},{"fragment":"interpretation","heading":"Interpretation and verification boundaries","keywords":[],"path":"reference/deployment-status.html","sectionTitle":"Reference","summary":"Deployment progress bitmask semantics, per-network bit mappings, and the 256-step limit.","text":"Evidence What it proves What it does not prove Bit set Runtime bytecode exists at the configured address. Correct bytecode, constructor values, wiring, permissions, or operational readiness. Bit clear No runtime bytecode was observed at that address by this call. Whether the address is intentionally empty or the manifest is wrong. Manifest match The decoder used the intended ordered address list for the network. That deployed code matches the release or that remote procedure call (RPC) responses are honest. Full deployment verification Code hashes, required wiring, permissions, and manifest provenance can be checked together. Economic safety or future operational liveness.","title":"Deployment status oracle","topic":"Contracts","weight":0},{"fragment":"interpretation","heading":"Static decoder test vectors","keywords":[],"path":"reference/deployment-status.html","sectionTitle":"Reference","summary":"Deployment progress bitmask semantics, per-network bit mappings, and the 256-step limit.","text":"Mask Meaning 0x0 No tracked constructor address contains runtime code. 0x1 Only constructor address index 0 is present. 0x5 Constructor address indices 0 and 2 are present. 2^255 Only the highest supported bit, index 255, is present. The interactive tables require JavaScript. The ordered source lists are available in the mainnet deployment manifest and Sepolia deployment manifest ; bit i always corresponds to the i th constructor address after excluding deploymentStatusOracle .","title":"Deployment status oracle","topic":"Contracts","weight":0},{"fragment":"","heading":"","keywords":["MMR","carry proof","hashing","nullifier"],"path":"reference/merkle-mountain-range.html","sectionTitle":"Reference","summary":"Carry-proof encoding, peak bagging, proof shape, nullifier tree, and bounds.","text":"Statoblast uses a Merkle Mountain Range (MMR) only for inherited escalation carry: the unresolved deposits a parent Escalation Game exports when its universe forks. Each child universe 's continuation game, the child Escalation Game that resumes the dispute during fork continuation , verifies withdrawals against those compact snapshots without replaying the full parent history on-chain.","title":"Merkle Mountain Range carry proofs","topic":"Mechanisms","weight":1},{"fragment":"leaf-shape","heading":"Leaf fields","keywords":[],"path":"reference/merkle-mountain-range.html","sectionTitle":"Reference","summary":"Carry-proof encoding, peak bagging, proof shape, nullifier tree, and bounds.","text":"Each leaf commits to one unresolved deposit. The encoding fixes the field order and hashing. Field Why it is committed depositor Binds the payout to the original depositor; see reporter claims , the per-continuation record of claimed carried deposits that prevents a proof from being replayed. cumulativeAmountAttoRep Preserves payout-order prefix data. sourceNodeId Distinguishes otherwise identical leaves copied from different local nodes.","title":"Merkle Mountain Range carry proofs","topic":"Mechanisms","weight":0},{"fragment":"encoding","heading":"Normative encoding","keywords":[],"path":"reference/merkle-mountain-range.html","sectionTitle":"Reference","summary":"Carry-proof encoding, peak bagging, proof shape, nullifier tree, and bounds.","text":"The leaf ABI types are (address,uint8,uint256,uint256,uint256,uint256) , in the order depositor, outcome, amountAttoRep, parentDepositIndex, cumulativeAmountAttoRep, sourceNodeId . outcome is the Solidity enumeration ordinal ( 0 Invalid, 1 Yes, 2 No). Leaf hashing uses standard ABI word padding through abi.encode ; parent hashing uses the 64 raw bytes from abi.encodePacked(left, right) , so the order of left and right is significant. There is no additional domain separator. leafHash = keccak256(abi.encode(depositor, outcome, amountAttoRep, parentDepositIndex, cumulativeAmountAttoRep, sourceNodeId)) parentHash(left, right) = keccak256(abi.encodePacked(left, right)) // peaks[0..peakCount-1] are the occupied peaks in ascending height order bagPeaks(peaks, peakCount): if peakCount == 0: return bytes32(0) root = peaks[peakCount - 1] for i = peakCount - 1 down to 1: root = parentHash(peaks[i - 1], root) return root The fold starts at the highest occupied peak and places each lower peak as the left operand. With occupied heights 0 , 2 , and 3 , the root is parentHash(peak0, parentHash(peak2, peak3)) . A single peak is the root itself, and an empty range has root bytes32(0) . Proof verification rejects each of the following with the quoted revert string: an outcome whose snapshot has no leaves: Carry peak absent ; a zero amountAttoRep : Proof amount zero ; leafIndex >= leafCount : Bad carry leaf ; a merkleMountainRangePeakIndex that is not the height of the peak containing leafIndex : Bad carry peak ; a sibling array whose length is not peakHeight + peakCount - 1 : Bad MMR proof length ; a reconstructed root that differs from the stored snapshot root: Bad carry proof ; in standalone games, a nullifier path whose length is not 64 : Bad nullifier length ; in standalone games, a nullifier path that does not reproduce the current nullifier root from an empty leaf, which includes an already-consumed nullifier: Bad nullifier proof .","title":"Merkle Mountain Range carry proofs","topic":"Mechanisms","weight":0},{"fragment":"encoding","heading":"Conformance vectors","keywords":[],"path":"reference/merkle-mountain-range.html","sectionTitle":"Reference","summary":"Carry-proof encoding, peak bagging, proof shape, nullifier tree, and bounds.","text":"Input or intermediate Value Leaf 0 ABI values 0x0000000000000000000000000000000000000001, 0, 1, 0, 1, 0 Leaf 0 hash 0xb01042d963a0c261d64893dfb7e1f221c93ef608653cb92f9ca2c8899ca4572b Leaf 1 ABI values 0x0000000000000000000000000000000000000002, 1, 2, 1, 2, 1 Leaf 1 hash 0xe0ca39e8852dc96783e8b8f0859cfbf155338f9144ba4b9fdd6af847299fc867 Two-leaf root 0x1c9c7f3c2c8bebf92ce393deb5dccdd253f2b10084af72defb5dcd4486832b27 Leaf 2 ABI values 0x0000000000000000000000000000000000000003, 2, 3, 2, 3, 2 Leaf 2 hash 0x92b2040f7e9b4f1e1b587eda2fb255419704a6ea1693789658adfddbf98389ae Three-leaf root 0x3488c83edd77c5259c9432e90f972d45d0ced819fd3691eddfe41fe05d676564 Empty nullifier root 0xe2c3ed4052eeb1d60514b4c38ece8d73a27f37fa5b36dcbf338e70de95798caa","title":"Merkle Mountain Range carry proofs","topic":"Mechanisms","weight":0},{"fragment":"peaks","heading":"Peaks and the 64-peak bound","keywords":[],"path":"reference/merkle-mountain-range.html","sectionTitle":"Reference","summary":"Carry-proof encoding, peak bagging, proof shape, nullifier tree, and bounds.","text":"The carry snapshot stores one peak per set bit in the leaf count, with MERKLE_MOUNTAIN_RANGE_MAX_PEAKS = 64 positions. A leaf count of thirteen is shown as binary one one zero one, corresponding to occupied peaks at heights zero, two, and three which are later bagged into one root. MMR peaks. The occupied peak heights are exactly the set bits in the snapshot leaf count. To form one root, the verifier collects the occupied peaks in ascending height order and folds them with bagPeaks . snapshotLeafCount < 2 64 Carry initialization rejects a larger leaf count with Leaf count high . Local carry appends behave like binary addition with carries: a new leaf merges upward through occupied lower peaks until it finds the first empty peak slot. If that upward carry would reach height 64 , the append reverts with MMR too tall .","title":"Merkle Mountain Range carry proofs","topic":"Mechanisms","weight":0},{"fragment":"proofs","heading":"Proof structure","keywords":[],"path":"reference/merkle-mountain-range.html","sectionTitle":"Reference","summary":"Carry-proof encoding, peak bagging, proof shape, nullifier tree, and bounds.","text":"Every carried-deposit proof authenticates membership in the inherited MMR snapshot for one outcome. In canonical games, whose REP is held by the security pool reporter ledger, appendOnlyCarry() returns true: live roots only append, finalized claims leave historical membership intact, and a per-game consumed-deposit check prevents replay. Builders pass an empty nullifierSiblings array. Standalone games, which hold REP directly, also require a nullifier proof that the inherited leaf has not been consumed. Component Purpose leafIndex , merkleMountainRangePeakIndex , merkleMountainRangeSiblings Proves the carried deposit belongs to the inherited snapshot root for that outcome. nullifierSiblings Standalone games: proves the nullifier leaf is still empty. Canonical games use the consumed-deposit check instead and accept an empty array. The membership lane combines the carried deposit leaf with bottom-up siblings inside its selected peak, then with other occupied peak roots in ascending height order to reconstruct the snapshot root. The nullifier lane uses the unique carry leaf position and combines it with a fixed-depth sibling path to prove the claim has not already been consumed. Standalone carry proof anatomy. The MMR sibling array first reconstructs one selected peak and then supplies every other occupied peak. The independent 64-level nullifier path uses the proof-bound MMR leaf index directly, so distinct leaves cannot collide in one slot. The position persists through descendant snapshots. merkleMountainRangePeakIndex is the occupied peak height, not the ordinal position of that peak among occupied peaks. leafIndex is the leaf's global position across the whole snapshot. The verifier derives the peak that contains that position, requires merkleMountainRangePeakIndex to equal its height, and orders the in-peak siblings by the in-peak offset leafIndex - peakStart , where peakStart is the peakStartIndex returned by getCurrentCarryPeakForLeaf . The game identifies inherited leaves and nullifier positions by this global index. For example, if the full snapshot has 13 leaves, the height- 3 peak holds global leaves 0 through 7 , the height- 2 peak holds 8 through 11 , and the height- 0 peak holds 12 . A proof for the third leaf of the height- 2 peak uses merkleMountainRangePeakIndex = 2 and leafIndex = 10 ; its in-peak offset is 2 . merkleMountainRangeSiblings is also ordered in two phases. The first merkleMountainRangePeakIndex entries are the bottom-up Merkle siblings inside the selected peak. The remaining entries are the other occupied peak roots in ascending peak-height order, skipping the selected peak height. That exact ordering is why the verifier checks merkleMountainRangeSiblings.length == peakHeight + peakCount - 1 .","title":"Merkle Mountain Range carry proofs","topic":"Mechanisms","weight":0},{"fragment":"nullifier-tree","heading":"Standalone nullifier tree","keywords":[],"path":"reference/merkle-mountain-range.html","sectionTitle":"Reference","summary":"Carry-proof encoding, peak bagging, proof shape, nullifier tree, and bounds.","text":"Each standalone continuation outcome keeps a binary Merkle tree of fixed depth NULLIFIER_DEPTH = 64 , hashed with the same parentHash . The path is the proof-bound leafIndex , which must fit 64 bits: bit d , counted from the least significant bit, selects the side at depth d , starting at the leaf. A 0 bit hashes parentHash(node, sibling) ; a 1 bit hashes parentHash(sibling, node) . An unconsumed leaf is bytes32(0) ; a consumed leaf is bytes32(uint256(1)) . The empty root is bytes32(0) hashed with itself 64 times: root = parentHash(root, root) . Its value is in the conformance vectors . nullifierSiblings lists the 64 siblings from the leaf upward. The verifier recomputes the current root with the empty leaf, then stores the root recomputed with the consumed leaf.","title":"Merkle Mountain Range carry proofs","topic":"Mechanisms","weight":0},{"fragment":"mmr-proof-planner","heading":"Plan a standalone carry proof","keywords":[],"path":"reference/merkle-mountain-range.html","sectionTitle":"Reference","summary":"Carry-proof encoding, peak bagging, proof shape, nullifier tree, and bounds.","text":"Choose a snapshot leaf count and one occupied peak. The planner reports the global leafIndex range of that peak, checks an in-peak offset, and reports the exact sibling-array lengths the verifier enforces. It does not construct hashes or replace an off-chain proof builder. Selection Valid in-peak offset Selected peak capacity 4 leaves; leafIndex 8…11 Snapshot Snapshot leaf count Occupied peak height Height 2 Proof position In-peak offset MMR sibling hashes required 4 Nullifier sibling hashes required 64 Binary leaf count 1101₂ Occupied peak heights 0, 2, 3","title":"Merkle Mountain Range carry proofs","topic":"Mechanisms","weight":0},{"fragment":"snapshots","heading":"Snapshots in child continuations","keywords":[],"path":"reference/merkle-mountain-range.html","sectionTitle":"Reference","summary":"Carry-proof encoding, peak bagging, proof shape, nullifier tree, and bounds.","text":"Each continuation outcome stores both an inherited snapshot baseline and a mutable current state. Field Meaning snapshotLeafCount and snapshotCarryRoot The immutable inherited carry commitment. currentLeafCount and currentPeaks The descendant carry snapshot after inherited initialization and local appends. Canonical claims retain these historical peaks. Standalone local claims remove their carry leaves; standalone inherited claims update currentNullifierRoot instead. inheritedUnresolvedTotalAttoRep and localUnresolvedTotalAttoRep The principal totals the current carry state must still represent. currentNullifierRoot The replay-protection root for standalone games; canonical games retain the empty root. getForkCarrySnapshot() reports the current carry peaks, leaf counts, totals, and nullifier roots after local appends and proof consumption. getCarryLeafPageByOutcome() pages only local unresolved leaves in newest-first order. Its maxEntries parameter bounds visited nodes, including consumed leaves. An empty or short page can return a nonzero nextPageNodeId ; pass that cursor as startNodeId to continue until the cursor is zero.","title":"Merkle Mountain Range carry proofs","topic":"Mechanisms","weight":0},{"fragment":"sources","heading":"Sources","keywords":[],"path":"reference/merkle-mountain-range.html","sectionTitle":"Reference","summary":"Carry-proof encoding, peak bagging, proof shape, nullifier tree, and bounds.","text":"MerkleMountainRange.sol EscalationGameProofVerifier.sol EscalationGameCarry.sol EscalationGameTypes.sol EscalationGameDepositDelegate.sol","title":"Merkle Mountain Range carry proofs","topic":"Mechanisms","weight":0},{"fragment":"","heading":"","keywords":["Statoblast","markets","security pools","question ledgers","overview"],"path":"explanation/statoblast.html","sectionTitle":"Explanations","summary":"A market lifecycle from creation through local resolution or fork migration to redemption.","text":"Prediction markets Follow one question: “Will the fictional Aurora spacecraft launch before 31 December 2030?” Statoblast creates a collateralized market with Yes, No, and Invalid shares, resolves ordinary disagreement locally, and uses Zoltar branches only when local resolution cannot produce a decision or another fork interrupts the question ledger.","title":"How Statoblast works","topic":"Protocol","weight":1},{"fragment":"mental-model","heading":"1. Mental model","keywords":[],"path":"explanation/statoblast.html","sectionTitle":"Explanations","summary":"A market lifecycle from creation through local resolution or fork migration to redemption.","text":"# Statoblast is the market layer. Each question ledger serves one question in one universe. Separate security pools keep separate question ledgers even when their question and configuration match. Every question ledger belongs to a security pool and uses its shared capital and settlement accounting. A fixed security pool containing one question uses the same contracts and rules as a security pool containing many questions. Zoltar owns the questions, universes, REP, and forks underneath it; Trading and OpenOracle sit beside it. The system overview introduces all four. A security pool shares capital across registered questions. Questions resolve locally through reporting. A universe fork uses shared owner choices and branch accounting; individual child questions are prepared on demand. Market lifecycle. Local resolution is the ordinary route. An own-question non-decision or an independent universe fork while the question ledger remains unresolved and operational enters the migration and collateral-repair route.","title":"How Statoblast works","topic":"Protocol","weight":0},{"fragment":"normal-lifecycle","heading":"2. Normal market lifecycle","keywords":[],"path":"explanation/statoblast.html","sectionTitle":"Explanations","summary":"A market lifecycle from creation through local resolution or fork migration to redemption.","text":"# Vault owners deposit REP to back markets. Traders buy complete sets with the security pool’s collateral token and can exchange the shares through Trading . After the question ends, the Escalation Game can determine which shares redeem. collateral mints Invalid, Yes, and No shares. Operational question ledgers redeem full sets, and finalized winning shares redeem through outcome settlement. Share lifecycle. One unit of collateral mints matching Invalid, Yes, and No claims; the complete set can be burned while the question ledger is operational or the winning share redeemed after finalization.","title":"How Statoblast works","topic":"Protocol","weight":0},{"fragment":"participants-assets","heading":"3. Participants and assets","keywords":[],"path":"explanation/statoblast.html","sectionTitle":"Explanations","summary":"A market lifecycle from creation through local resolution or fork migration to redemption.","text":"# Traders and liquidity providers Mint complete sets, hold or trade outcome shares, redeem claims, and optionally provide Yes/No liquidity. Vault owners Deposit REP, set a separate underwriting commitment in collateral, keep it backed, earn fees, and may report or migrate. Reporters Place REP-backed positions on Invalid, Yes, or No in the Escalation Game. Liquidation receivers and operators A funded receiver accepts commitment and a time-increasing takeover reward (REP taken from the liquidated owner’s pledged REP) into its vault, supplies the remaining backing and earns future fees. Collateral Repair Auction bidders Supply collateral in exchange for REP-backed accounting positions in the child security pool branch when a child needs collateral repair. Asset or claim What it represents Where it is held or recorded Settlement collateral Backing for outstanding complete sets The security pool settlement contract holds collateral; the settlement book records its liabilities. Migration allocates rather than copies collateral. REP Universe-specific reporting and security capital; never settlement collateral Wallets hold liquid REP, the capital ledger holds underwriting principal, and the reporter ledger holds reporting escrow. Escalation Games record the associated claims. Outcome shares Yes, No, or Invalid claims for one question ledger and universe An ERC-1155 ShareToken deployed once per origin question ledger and reused by all of its fork descendants Underwriting commitment collateral a vault owner agrees to underwrite; its share of the security pool's total underwriting commitment sets the vault's collateral obligations and fee rights The security pool capital ledger Escalation claims REP-backed positions on reported outcomes The Escalation Game until claimed or migrated Trading LP tokens A claim on one trading pool 's Yes and No reserves The trading pool's ERC-20 ledger","title":"How Statoblast works","topic":"Protocol","weight":0},{"fragment":"local-resolution","heading":"4. Local resolution","keywords":[],"path":"explanation/statoblast.html","sectionTitle":"Explanations","summary":"A market lifecycle from creation through local resolution or fork migration to redemption.","text":"# The game opens with the first REP deposit after the question ends. REP-backed balances then accumulate behind Invalid, Yes, and No. Their median is binding capital . Once the scheduled deadline has passed, exactly one strict balance leader produces the local winner. Winning shares redeem from that question ledger. If two outcomes reach the non-decision threshold , the game does not choose between them. Anyone can then fork the universe on the market question through Statoblast, which pays the fork threshold from the triggering question’s REP escrow in the security pool reporter ledger. The game fixes its non-decision threshold when it opens, while the genesis fork threshold follows the live external REP supply until the first fork. If that supply grew in between, the escrow falls short, and the caller pays the difference from REP approved to the reporter ledger. That REP joins the question’s escrow without a separate claim. See the Escalation Game explanation for the required-support curve, deadlines, payouts, and how games continue across forks.","title":"How Statoblast works","topic":"Protocol","weight":0},{"fragment":"forks-migration","heading":"5. Forks and migration","keywords":[],"path":"explanation/statoblast.html","sectionTitle":"Explanations","summary":"A market lifecycle from creation through local resolution or fork migration to redemption.","text":"A question's non-decision or a fork on another question enters the same shared branch process. Owners choose once per security pool and universe. Questions, outcome tokens and reporter continuations are prepared lazily when used. See the choice windows and recovery rules below .","title":"How Statoblast works","topic":"Protocol","weight":0},{"fragment":"fees-capacity-liquidations","heading":"6. Fees, underwriting, and liquidations","keywords":[],"path":"explanation/statoblast.html","sectionTitle":"Explanations","summary":"A market lifecycle from creation through local resolution or fork migration to redemption.","text":"A purchase needs enough healthy underwriting commitment and a fresh funded price. Returning complete sets reduces demand and can free capacity for retirement. Fees accrue separately from principal, while liquidation repairs move unsupported commitment to a funded receiver.","title":"How Statoblast works","topic":"Protocol","weight":0},{"fragment":"question-security-pools","heading":"7. Managing several questions","keywords":[],"path":"explanation/statoblast.html","sectionTitle":"Explanations","summary":"A market lifecycle from creation through local resolution or fork migration to redemption.","text":"# A SecurityPool lets traders buy outcomes for many questions against one aggregate backing. REP stays in a central capital ledger. Each owner has individually accounted principal and chooses how much standing commitment to authorize; liquidation can transfer pledged REP to a receiver. There is no common REP share price and no purchase from individual vaults. Questions retain separate outcome tokens and reporting games. All REP deposited for an owner in one security pool and universe backs that owner's single commitment, including any surplus above its required backing. While the position is healthy at the current liquidation price, depositing more REP immediately strengthens that backing; there is no separate pledge or top-up step. Each owner chooses a health target for authorizing commitment. A larger buffer supports less commitment with the same REP but gives more protection against price changes. Admission and surplus withdrawals use the conservative edge of the oracle’s configured error band. Repair eligibility uses the opposite edge and compares the entire deposit with required backing. Falling below the owner's chosen buffer alone does not trigger it. See liquidation repairs for the price limits. New purchases require enough healthy commitment at a fresh shared REP/collateral price. New commitments must fit a finalized report’s funded exposure. Independent report candidates prevent one pending report from blocking another sponsor. An owner can authorize growth using an independently finalized, sufficiently funded report; its conservative collateral requirement stays attached to that owner until a new shared report covers the enlarged security pool. The creator fixes an admission-policy contract, the annual time-fee rate, the question horizon and economic minimums. The interface offers either open admission or a permanently fixed question list. Other policies work onchain. Registration moves no REP and creates no claim obligation for owners. Every purchase checks admission, pays the configurable backing-based fee and prepaid resolution premium, and delivers its three outcomes atomically. Unused payment budget remains in the buyer’s wallet. A security pool admits new questions only while its origin universe remains unforked. Once that universe forks, admission closes permanently for that security pool, including its continuations in child universes. Children inherit the questions registered before the fork; continuing an existing question does not admit a new one. To introduce a new question in an unforked child universe, create a new security pool with that child as its origin universe. An open admission policy does not override this restriction. Holding fees accrue to healthy commitments. Purchase fees and released REP income vest over time ; already earned income belongs to the provider who earned it. Reporter REP has its own custody and cannot be taken from a vault's backing. Irrevocably released dispute surplus enters the separate REP income stream. It does not raise an entry or exit exchange rate. Final reporter claims and their required burns remain separately funded. The chosen health target protects voluntary changes: authorizing more commitment or withdrawing backing requires enough REP at that target and a fresh conservative price. Receiving a takeover checks the same health target using the last accepted conservative valuation; an auction already started can execute after that report expires. Raising the target on a live position also requires sufficient backing; an owner can deposit REP and raise it in one transaction. Lowering the target never increases commitment automatically. A price move that leaves the position below its health target does not itself cause liquidation, and deposits and earned-fee claims remain available. An owner can withdraw pledged surplus REP when healthy aggregate commitment covers demand and the remaining backing meets the owner's chosen target. Previously free principal and principal released by full retirement remain withdrawable. To reduce commitment, the owner sets a lower amount, including zero for a full exit, and the reduction is queued for retirement. Returning complete sets reduces aggregate demand, letting queued commitments retire before new purchases consume the spare capacity. Retirement waits for spare capacity after earlier requested reductions. Purchases reserve that capacity without processing the queue. An owner can withdraw principal released by retirement. See the underwriting reference for the queue and indexed accounting. Underbacked positions follow the liquidation rules . Repairs release unused commitment first. A receiver can take over the remainder with fresh backing and earn future fees, while old earnings stay with their owners. A native universe fork fixes an eight-week choice window. Each owner may make one irreversible branch choice for their commitment and its entire deposit in that universe, including the surplus buffer. Principal with no remaining commitment and old earnings stay separately claimable in the parent. After choices close, one shared migration prepares each requested branch. Each branch inherits the outstanding outcome claims, including tokens not yet materialized there. Source settlement collateral follows the fraction of total collateral commitment that chose the branch, so choosing excess REP cannot capture extra cash. Chosen providers retain their REP, commitment, and health target. REP in unchosen branches backs inherited outcome claims. A shared collateral repair auction can sell freshly unchosen REP to raise collateral. A prepared branch can activate at week nine even without a sale. Redeeming claims then pays proportional collateral and remaining recovery REP. A cash deficit closes new purchases and ordinary holding fees until backing is repaired. If recovery REP remains, new buyers contribute a proportional amount so existing claims keep their entitlement per share. Missing the choice cutoff does not select a default branch: the owner’s committed REP backs inherited claims in every child. For questions still live at the fork, holders recover unassigned source settlement collateral by combining separate Invalid, Yes and No recovery rights. These rights transfer independently of child-token migration rights. Questions already final retain their collateral and REP claims in the source question ledger; their shares redeem there and cannot migrate. A later fork migrates only the remaining live recovery REP, once per branch. Child forks cannot begin before week ten; late preparation does not restart these deadlines. Questions and reporter continuations are prepared lazily when used, and traders still migrate shares through each intervening universe. An intervening universe that forked before the question was prepared there needs no separate transaction: once its branch custody exists, the migration itself prepares the question’s ledger there. The security pool fork reference specifies the interfaces.","title":"How Statoblast works","topic":"Protocol","weight":0},{"fragment":"security","heading":"8. Security assumptions and non-guarantees","keywords":[],"path":"explanation/statoblast.html","sectionTitle":"Explanations","summary":"A market lifecycle from creation through local resolution or fork migration to redemption.","text":"# REP-backed incentives do not prove objective truth; users and applications decide which branches retain activity. OpenOracle depends on timely, profitable correction of inaccurate REP/collateral reports. A fork gives each child only the collateral that migrates to it ( Zoltar's non-guarantees ). Tokens or native ETH pushed into a question ledger outside its accounting create no collateral claim. Steps that anyone may trigger, such as forking or starting an auction, still need someone to send the transaction. Spot prices from trading pools are manipulable and are never protocol oracles. Normative assumptions and guarantees are listed in the security model and invariant catalog .","title":"How Statoblast works","topic":"Protocol","weight":0},{"fragment":"security","heading":"Design rationale","keywords":[],"path":"explanation/statoblast.html","sectionTitle":"Explanations","summary":"A market lifecycle from creation through local resolution or fork migration to redemption.","text":"Statoblast's ancestry includes the Arctic Tern Oracle , Sisyphean Exchange , Partial Colored Coins , and Isonzo . Those research ideas influenced the design; the deployed contracts govern current behavior.","title":"How Statoblast works","topic":"Protocol","weight":0},{"fragment":"","heading":"","keywords":["Zoltar","universes","questions","REP"],"path":"explanation/zoltar.html","sectionTitle":"Explanations","summary":"Universes, question encoding, global forks, and REP splitting.","text":"Branching universes Zoltar records global questions and lets an eligible parent universe branch into outcome-specific child universes . Each child universe has its own REP ledger, and REP holders move into children under fixed migration rules.","title":"Zoltar and branching universes","topic":"Protocol","weight":1},{"fragment":"branching","heading":"A parent becomes separate child ledgers","keywords":[],"path":"explanation/zoltar.html","sectionTitle":"Explanations","summary":"Universes, question encoding, global forks, and REP splitting.","text":"# For a binary market question, one forking parent universe creates separate Invalid, Yes, and No child ledgers. An unrelated fork instead follows its own fork question's valid answers. Forking parent Invalid branch Valid outcome branches Branch set. A binary launch question produces Invalid, Yes, and No children. Alice converts 100 parent REP into a migration balance , a parent-universe credit that can mint the same amount in each valid child, and uses it to mint 100 child REP in the Yes universe and 100 child REP in the No universe. These are separate branch-specific ledgers. They are not 200 globally interchangeable REP, they do not copy ETH, and Zoltar does not guarantee equal economic value across branches. Applications and users later determine where economic activity continues.","title":"Zoltar and branching universes","topic":"Protocol","weight":0},{"fragment":"questions","heading":"Global questions and fork eligibility","keywords":[],"path":"explanation/zoltar.html","sectionTitle":"Explanations","summary":"Universes, question encoding, global forks, and REP splitting.","text":"# Questions are global Zoltar objects and are not bound to a universe. Any account can fork a universe by naming a question when four conditions hold: the universe has not forked before, so each universe forks at most once; the question exists and its end time has passed; the caller supplies the full fork threshold in that universe's REP; and, for a child universe, ten weeks have passed since its parent fork. Zoltar does not check that the question concerns the universe, so applications such as Statoblast enforce any stricter relationship they need. The child cooldown reserves eight weeks for security pool branch choices, one week before shared branch activation and one week for responses. Native choice, bidding and child-fork deadlines derive from the fork timestamp; preparing a child late does not restart them. Statoblast reporting instead remains paused until the child branch becomes operational. Security pool branches distribute recovery assets to outcome claims and may auction freshly unchosen REP to repair their collateral shortfall. Prompt activation leaves time for reporting before another fork can interrupt the child; late activation does not extend the native cooldown. The fork question decides which child universes can exist: one for each of its valid answers. A categorical question allows Invalid and each of its labels. A scalar question allows Invalid or a well-formed packed scalar answer. Zoltar sets no migration deadline; REP holders can add to their migration balance at any time after the fork. Readable answer names are part of Zoltar’s on-chain interface. The question registry’s getAnswerOptionName exposes categorical labels and formatted scalar answers so integrating protocols can interpret outcomes directly from the contracts. Keeping this formatting on-chain is an intentional integration requirement; raw metadata and client-side formatting alone do not replace it.","title":"Zoltar and branching universes","topic":"Protocol","weight":0},{"fragment":"guarantees","heading":"What Zoltar guarantees","keywords":[],"path":"explanation/zoltar.html","sectionTitle":"Explanations","summary":"Universes, question encoding, global forks, and REP splitting.","text":"# Universe and child identities derive deterministically from their parent and outcome. Only valid outcomes for the fork question can create child universes or receive child REP. Each account cannot mint more REP into one child than its recorded migration balance permits. Child REP is controlled by Zoltar and remains tied to its universe identity. Every child starts with the parent's pre-fork theoretical REP supply , the per-universe supply figure from which fork thresholds are derived, less exactly the fork haircut . Categorical labels use printable ASCII and must be unique even when compared without regard to case. Categorical navigation labels, migration choices and confirmations display each label with its numeric outcome ID; check that ID before choosing a child. Before genesis first forks, Zoltar reads the external REP token’s current theoretical supply and subtracts REP sent to Zoltar’s burn address. The fork threshold therefore follows external supply changes instead of freezing the deployment-time value. The first fork freezes the admission base, net of its burn haircut, for every child. Child supply caps remain fixed afterward: later external inflation does not enlarge them and may crowd out late migration. Fungible REP cannot identify which holder’s tokens came from inflation, so fixed caps do not guarantee admission for each original holder. This policy does not rely on legacy REP conversion. Mainnet’s Yes deployment and No deployment use the corresponding Augur V2 fork child tokens. REP Yes supply can grow before its first Zoltar fork, increasing the threshold until child caps are fixed. REP No has a theoretical supply of about 1,786 REP, yielding a threshold of about 89 REP and a haircut of about 18 REP at the default divisors. This gives the No deployment a much lower cost to initiate a fork. Theoretical supply includes REP that never migrates into a child. With the default threshold divisor of 20, the fork threshold need not be 5% of circulating REP: at half turnout it is about 10% of the REP present, and it can exceed all circulating REP when less than 5% of the snapshot arrives. The same denominator sets the Escalation Game non-decision threshold. Incomplete migration compounds this constraint through later generations; open-ended migration does not reconcile the denominator to turnout.","title":"Zoltar and branching universes","topic":"Protocol","weight":0},{"fragment":"non-guarantees","heading":"What Zoltar does not guarantee","keywords":[],"path":"explanation/zoltar.html","sectionTitle":"Explanations","summary":"Universes, question encoding, global forks, and REP splitting.","text":"# It does not choose a truthful or canonical child. Applications control how collateral and state carry into each child. Each child receives only the collateral that migrates to it, so a Statoblast child question ledger can remain impaired if too little collateral follows and its Collateral Repair Auction cannot raise the difference. It does not guarantee equal REP value, liquidity, or redemption across branches. It does not prove a supplied global question is relevant to the universe being forked. It does not guarantee that applications continue in every branch.","title":"Zoltar and branching universes","topic":"Protocol","weight":0},{"fragment":"accounting","heading":"Deterministic identities and migration accounting","keywords":[],"path":"explanation/zoltar.html","sectionTitle":"Explanations","summary":"Universes, question encoding, global forks, and REP splitting.","text":"# A child universe ID is derived from its parent universe ID and selected outcome. Child REP uses a predictable deployment address derived from the Zoltar address and universe ID. Adding parent REP to a migration balance burns or transfers it from the parent ledger. That balance can then materialize the same branch-specific amount in multiple valid children, with per-child accounting preventing replay beyond the source amount. Starting a fork removes the full threshold from parent supply and credits the initiator with the remainder after the haircut. The divisors are fixed at deployment. With defaults of 20 and 5, the threshold is about 5% of supply and the haircut about 1% of supply. The minimum burn divisor is 5, which caps the haircut at 20% of the threshold. The full fork threshold leaves parent supply. Most becomes the initiator's migration balance and the fork haircut is not credited. Fork deposit. The default split, subject to integer rounding. fork threshold = ⌈live theoretical REP supply ÷ threshold divisor⌉ fork haircut = ⌊fork threshold ÷ burn divisor⌋ Each fork reduces theoretical supply by its fork haircut, so later thresholds decline along that lineage. Repeated forks. Integer-rounded fork haircuts reduce theoretical supply and therefore later fork thresholds.","title":"Zoltar and branching universes","topic":"Protocol","weight":0},{"fragment":"encodings","heading":"Question and outcome encodings","keywords":[],"path":"explanation/zoltar.html","sectionTitle":"Explanations","summary":"Universes, question encoding, global forks, and REP splitting.","text":"# Each question defines its valid outcome encodings. All-zero is the canonical Invalid answer. A malformed scalar answer is rejected. See ZoltarQuestionData for stored data and encoding details. A scalar answer contains reserved bits, a namespace bit, and first and second payout numerators. Valid payout fields sum to the question's tick count. Namespace bit First payout Second payout Packed scalar answer. Encoding validation keeps Invalid distinct from malformed input. Exact storage fields, permissions, and entrypoints are listed in the contract reference . Security assumptions are listed in the security model .","title":"Zoltar and branching universes","topic":"Protocol","weight":0},{"fragment":"","heading":"","keywords":["Trading","AMM","liquidity","prices"],"path":"explanation/trading.html","sectionTitle":"Explanations","summary":"Complete sets, directional liquidity, conditional prices, and market isolation.","text":"Explanation Trading is a separate on-chain exchange for Statoblast outcome shares , one of possibly many that can be built on Statoblast. Statoblast still owns market creation, collateral, migration, resolution, and redemption through each market's question ledger .","title":"How Trading works","topic":"Trading","weight":1},{"fragment":"complete-set-entry","heading":"From collateral to a directional position","keywords":[],"path":"explanation/trading.html","sectionTitle":"Explanations","summary":"Complete sets, directional liquidity, conditional prices, and market isolation.","text":"Each collateral deposit first creates a complete set : one Yes, one No, and one Invalid share. A market's trading pool , a Yes/No pair, is an automated market maker (AMM) that holds only Yes and No reserves. The router sends the unwanted directional share into the pair, receives more of the selected directional share, and returns the directional position and the Invalid share to the user. The Invalid share never enters the AMM; it remains a separate claim if the market later resolves Invalid.","title":"How Trading works","topic":"Trading","weight":0},{"fragment":"prices","heading":"What the prices mean","keywords":[],"path":"explanation/trading.html","sectionTitle":"Explanations","summary":"Complete sets, directional liquidity, conditional prices, and market isolation.","text":"Yes and No prices are conditional on valid resolution. They describe the relative price of the two directional outcomes; they are not estimates of invalidity. Interfaces should therefore label the Yes and No values directly and present Invalid separately. Spot prices come from current reserves and are not an oracle . Settlement uses the question ledger's resolved outcome, while solvency-sensitive REP/collateral operations use OpenOracle.","title":"How Trading works","topic":"Trading","weight":0},{"fragment":"market-isolation","heading":"One pair per question ledger","keywords":[],"path":"explanation/trading.html","sectionTitle":"Explanations","summary":"Complete sets, directional liquidity, conditional prices, and market isolation.","text":"Each question ledger maps to its own pair. Parent and child question ledgers therefore have separate pairs, reserves, prices, and liquidity-provider tokens; liquidity, like everything else in a fork , is not copied between them.","title":"How Trading works","topic":"Trading","weight":0},{"fragment":"reading-paths","heading":"Where to go next","keywords":[],"path":"explanation/trading.html","sectionTitle":"Explanations","summary":"Complete sets, directional liquidity, conditional prices, and market isolation.","text":"Make your first trade , follow the assets through an entry , or look up the contracts . See the documentation index for the other Trading pages.","title":"How Trading works","topic":"Trading","weight":0},{"fragment":"","heading":"","keywords":["Escalation Game","disputes","deadlines","accounting"],"path":"explanation/escalation-game.html","sectionTitle":"Explanations","summary":"How REP deposits resolve a market locally, when they force a fork, and how games continue across forks.","text":"The Escalation Game gives a finished Statoblast market a local, REP-backed resolution path before a universe-wide fork is necessary. A report that nobody challenges wins three days after the first deposit opens the game. For a binary launch question, suppose Yes has 8 REP, No has 5 REP, and Invalid has 2 REP. The median balance, 5 REP, is the game's binding capital : the stake the strongest challenger has committed, and the amount that sets the curve-based deadline. A deposit that raises only the leading Yes balance does not move that deadline; a deposit that raises Invalid above 5 REP raises the median and moves it later. After the deadline, Yes wins only if it remains the unique strict leader. If two outcomes each reach the non-decision threshold before local finalization, the game records non-decision and the question ledger follows the Zoltar fork path. The threshold is half of Zoltar's fork threshold , rounded up, and is fixed when the game opens. A tie is never treated as a local winner. The scheduled deadline is the last second before required support exceeds binding capital. An uninterrupted game has no additional response period and ends no later than 52 days after the first deposit. If a fork interrupts the game, the continuation clock preserves elapsed curve time. Any REP holder can fund a report from their wallet. Reporting deposits are separate from underwriting principal and generally must meet the start bond . The EscalationGame reference gives the deposit limits and tie handling. Wallet reporters fund their own report and claim gas. The prepaid resolution premium pays underwriting commitments, not reporters, and an unopposed report has no opposing stake from which to earn an escalation reward . A small question may remain unreported or unresolved if nobody values its answer enough to pay those costs. The protocol keeps reporting unsubsidized rather than promising an automatic reporter payment.","title":"Escalation Game","topic":"Protocol","weight":1},{"fragment":"escalation","heading":"Escalation","keywords":[],"path":"explanation/escalation-game.html","sectionTitle":"Explanations","summary":"How REP deposits resolve a market locally, when they force a fork, and how games continue across forks.","text":"The first deposit after the question ends opens the game. It stays open while required support is at or below binding capital. If challengers stop adding enough REP to raise the median, the curve passes binding capital and the strict leader wins. Required support is zero through day 3, starts at the start bond just after day 3, and reaches the non-decision threshold on day 52. Required support. Nothing is required for the first three days. From day 3 the requirement climbs from the start bond to the non-decision threshold, reaching it on day 52. The EscalationGame reference gives the exact curve.","title":"Escalation Game","topic":"Protocol","weight":0},{"fragment":"escalation-game-example","heading":"Try a simplified escalation round","keywords":[],"path":"explanation/escalation-game.html","sectionTitle":"Explanations","summary":"How REP deposits resolve a market locally, when they force a fork, and how games continue across forks.","text":"This simulator uses the contract model: a three-day activation delay, a 49-day escalation interval, and the fixed-point required-support curve. Change the configuration, then choose an outcome, a requested deposit and the day it is made to see clipping, tie adjustment, acceptance, or rejection. Once required support has passed the median and one outcome strictly leads, the game is closed and every deposit is rejected. The deadline after the deposit is where the curve crosses the new median. Deposit result accepted: 1 REP Contract-model state activation pending Game settings Start bond 1 REP Non-decision threshold 10 REP Player positions Current Yes balance 1 REP Current No balance 1 REP Current Invalid balance 0 REP Player move Deposit outcome Yes No Invalid Requested deposit 1 REP Timeline Days since start 0 days Yes 1 → 2 REP No 1 → 1 REP Invalid 0 → 0 REP Configured start bond 1 REP Configured threshold 10 REP Projected leader Yes Projected median balance 1 REP Deposit before → after Yes 1 / 2 REP; No 1 / 1 REP; Invalid 0 / 0 REP Activation 3 days Curve-based deadline (before → after) day 3.0 → day 3.0","title":"Escalation Game","topic":"Protocol","weight":0},{"fragment":"leader-margin","heading":"Why a leader holds more than the median","keywords":[],"path":"explanation/escalation-game.html","sectionTitle":"Explanations","summary":"How REP deposits resolve a market locally, when they force a fork, and how games continue across forks.","text":"A challenger can take the lead in the game's last open second. The old leader then has only the time the flip itself adds: the new median is the old leader's stake, and the deadline moves to where the curve crosses it. A leader that holds just above the runner-up therefore gets almost no time to answer. The contracts do not enforce a margin, but the escalation reward pays winning support up to the reward-eligible cap , one and a half times binding capital, so the design assumes a leader holds about that much. Against that margin, a flip must lift the challenger above the leader's larger stake, which raises the median and moves the curve deadline later, leaving time to answer.","title":"Escalation Game","topic":"Protocol","weight":0},{"fragment":"payouts","heading":"How the game ends","keywords":[],"path":"explanation/escalation-game.html","sectionTitle":"Explanations","summary":"How REP deposits resolve a market locally, when they force a fork, and how games continue across forks.","text":"The game ends in one of three ways: Strict local winner. A unique leading outcome can finalize once the scheduled deadline has passed and required support has overtaken binding capital, provided no fork interrupted the game. Local non-decision. Two outcomes reach the non-decision threshold. Statoblast uses its fork path instead of choosing one locally. Interruption by another fork. The question ledger's universe forks while the game remains unresolved. The game has no local result and continues in the child question ledgers instead; see forks and migration . Settlement rewards the winning side. Winning deposits get their principal back plus an escalation reward. The reward comes from the runner-up's forfeited stake, the binding capital: three fifths of it becomes the reward pool and two fifths, the escalation haircut , is burned, unless the fork this game triggered already burned it as the fork haircut . As ordinary reports increase the smallest of the three outcome balances, that increase leaves dispute escrow. The security pool distributes it as separate REP income . The other two balances remain in escrow to cover any winning outcome. Final settlement burns any extra beyond its reserved liabilities; a game carried across a fork also burns its terminal residual. The escalation reward applies only up to the reward-eligible cap , one and a half times binding capital. The game scales winning withdrawals down if the recorded fork threshold falls below the fixed non-decision threshold. This can leave a winner with less than their principal. The payout reference explains when the game records each value and works through an exact example.","title":"Escalation Game","topic":"Protocol","weight":0},{"fragment":"payouts","heading":"Late deposits and reward sharing","keywords":[],"path":"explanation/escalation-game.html","sectionTitle":"Explanations","summary":"How REP deposits resolve a market locally, when they force a fork, and how games continue across forks.","text":"Reward shares depend on eligible deposit amounts and their positions within the reward-eligible cap, not on how long REP was staked. A deposit accepted just before finality can therefore earn an escalation reward if capacity remains under the cap, reducing earlier reporters' shares despite spending less time at risk. Deposits beyond the cap earn no escalation reward, and a late depositor still risks losing REP if another outcome wins. This is an accepted design tradeoff: the game keeps amount-based rewards rather than adding time-weighted reward accounting, especially for claims inherited through forks. The cap limits eligible stake but does not eliminate late reward capture. See issue #1284 for the tradeoff considered.","title":"Escalation Game","topic":"Protocol","weight":0},{"fragment":"payouts","heading":"Escalation game continuation","keywords":[],"path":"explanation/escalation-game.html","sectionTitle":"Explanations","summary":"How REP deposits resolve a market locally, when they force a fork, and how games continue across forks.","text":"When a fork interrupts an unresolved game, Statoblast's QuestionForker restarts it in each child question ledger as a carried game with the parent's balances and elapsed time. In a child created by a fork on the question ledger's own question, the outcome is already fixed to that child's outcome, so the game only settles deposits on it. After an unrelated fork, the game resumes and can still be contested, with new wallet REP deposits. Depositors claim their positions in a child with a Merkle Mountain Range carry proof against the parent's deposit history, so no child copies every deposit onchain. An unrelated-fork continuation keeps the parent's elapsed curve time frozen until the child security pool branch becomes operational. All inherited games resume together at that activation transaction, with their remaining curve time and no additional three-day response period. Preparing an individual question later does not restart its clock. A lead change raises the median and adds only the corresponding curve time, as in an ordinary game. Branch activation is permissionless after the fixed recapitalization deadline; delaying it postpones reporting, but cannot consume the inherited reply time. The native child-fork cooldown still runs from the parent's fork timestamp, so late activation does not guarantee time before another fork. Responders still need transaction inclusion before their game's deadline; see the inclusion assumption .","title":"Escalation Game","topic":"Protocol","weight":0},{"fragment":"payouts","heading":"Design rationale","keywords":[],"path":"explanation/escalation-game.html","sectionTitle":"Explanations","summary":"How REP deposits resolve a market locally, when they force a fork, and how games continue across forks.","text":"Augur V2 cancelled active dispute games when a fork began and refunded their participants. Statoblast instead continues active games across the fork so a fork cannot erase the stakes players already put at risk. See the research note Zoltar forks should not cancel escalation games for the historical motivation.","title":"Escalation Game","topic":"Protocol","weight":0},{"fragment":"","heading":"","keywords":["liquidation","underwriting commitment","backing constraints","operator","receiver vault","approval","reservation","bad debt"],"path":"explanation/liquidations.html","sectionTitle":"Explanations","summary":"Funded commitment repairs, original REP ownership, receiver approvals, and remaining price risks.","text":"A funded repair transfers unsupported underwriting commitment to a consenting receiver. A one-hour auction transfers a growing takeover reward (REP taken from the target’s pledged REP) directly into the receiver’s vault. For the target this is a liquidation penalty. The receiver supplies any remaining backing and earns future fees on the transferred commitment.","title":"Vault liquidation","topic":"Economics","weight":1},{"fragment":"position-model","heading":"Target, receiver, and keeper","keywords":[],"path":"explanation/liquidations.html","sectionTitle":"Explanations","summary":"Funded commitment repairs, original REP ownership, receiver approvals, and remaining price risks.","text":"The target owns an underbacked position. The receiver funds the transferred commitment at its chosen health target. The takeover reward changes principal ownership inside the capital ledger; nothing is paid to the receiver’s wallet, and the protocol adds no REP of its own. Past earnings remain with their original owners. There is no protocol-funded gas reimbursement.","title":"Vault liquidation","topic":"Economics","weight":0},{"fragment":"capacity-and-health","heading":"When a position is liquidatable","keywords":[],"path":"explanation/liquidations.html","sectionTitle":"Explanations","summary":"Funded commitment repairs, original REP ownership, receiver approvals, and remaining price risks.","text":"All free and committed REP deposited for the owner in this security pool and universe counts toward its backing. Earned REP income is separate. The owner's voluntary health target controls new exposure and surplus withdrawals; merely falling below that buffer does not trigger liquidation. The protocol detects distress at the protective liquidation price. Admission and receiver backing use the conservative price. A fresh adverse observation starts an auction whose takeover reward grows from zero to all pledged REP over one hour. Partial fills consume vested rewards without restarting the clock. The auction remains fillable after the observation expires, and a later accepted price updates the valuation or ends the auction if health recovers.","title":"Vault liquidation","topic":"Economics","weight":0},{"fragment":"rule","heading":"Cure before takeover","keywords":[],"path":"explanation/liquidations.html","sectionTitle":"Explanations","summary":"Funded commitment repairs, original REP ownership, receiver approvals, and remaining price risks.","text":"The owner can add REP while healthy at the liquidation price. While underbacked, the owner can reduce unused commitment under the cure rules. A takeover first releases unused commitment without moving principal. If that restores backing at the conservative repair price, no transfer is needed. Otherwise the takeover reward reduces the backing left behind, and the receiver must back its resulting commitment at its own health target. A partial fill must either restore conservative backing or leave the source eligible for another takeover. Existing earned fees remain with their owner. Underbacked commitment stops earning new fees. Principal, fees, and reporter claims use separate accounting. See security pool capital and retirement for exits and the liquidation reference for exact contract bounds.","title":"Vault liquidation","topic":"Economics","weight":0},{"fragment":"receiver-approvals","heading":"Receiver consent","keywords":[],"path":"explanation/liquidations.html","sectionTitle":"Explanations","summary":"Funded commitment repairs, original REP ownership, receiver approvals, and remaining price risks.","text":"The receiver calls the capital ledger directly and chooses bounds on its own contribution and the resulting position. The liquidation reference gives the exact limits and retirement rules.","title":"Vault liquidation","topic":"Economics","weight":0},{"fragment":"queue-semantics","heading":"Execution and incentives","keywords":[],"path":"explanation/liquidations.html","sectionTitle":"Explanations","summary":"Funded commitment repairs, original REP ownership, receiver approvals, and remaining price risks.","text":"Quotes are checked against live state. Insufficient backing or changed consent bounds revert atomically. Receivers acquire pledged REP as well as future fee rights. The takeover reward reduces the new REP they must supply, but is not spendable gas funding. Fee income depends on demand, fees and continued health; participation is not guaranteed. Pledged principal cannot be withdrawn while healthy commitment is below demand. Previously free principal and separately earned income remain withdrawable. A wrong accepted oracle price can misclassify health and transfer the target’s pledged REP to a receiver; these rules do not establish the fair REP price.","title":"Vault liquidation","topic":"Economics","weight":0},{"fragment":"fork-behavior","heading":"Fork isolation","keywords":[],"path":"explanation/liquidations.html","sectionTitle":"Explanations","summary":"Funded commitment repairs, original REP ownership, receiver approvals, and remaining price risks.","text":"Positions belong to one security pool and universe. A fork stops ordinary parent operations. A child starts a new liquidation history after activation; parent auction age is not inherited. Each owner chooses a branch during the shared choice window; the receiver in a child must authorize its own child-universe exposure. See shared fork choices .","title":"Vault liquidation","topic":"Economics","weight":0},{"fragment":"","heading":"","keywords":["fees","holding fees","vesting","capital","securityPools"],"path":"explanation/fees.html","sectionTitle":"Explanations","summary":"How healthy security pool commitments earn fixed annual holding fees, how prepaid rewards vest, and when accrual stops.","text":"Each question in a security pool is charged an open-interest fee against its outstanding settlement collateral ; the security pool pays it to the vaults that underwrite that collateral. Every security pool uses funded linear fee streams so owners receive fees when they are earned.","title":"Open-interest fees","topic":"Economics","weight":1},{"fragment":"why-fees-exist","heading":"Why the fee exists","keywords":[],"path":"explanation/fees.html","sectionTitle":"Explanations","summary":"How healthy security pool commitments earn fixed annual holding fees, how prepaid rewards vest, and when accrual stops.","text":"Vault owners provide REP-backed security, keep their vaults healthy, and may need to take part in reporting and migration. In return, each fee-eligible underwriting commitment earns a share of the fee in proportion to its size. Owners claim aggregate earnings from the security pool without visiting each question.","title":"Open-interest fees","topic":"Economics","weight":0},{"fragment":"security-pool-fees","heading":"Fees in a shared security pool","keywords":[],"path":"explanation/fees.html","sectionTitle":"Explanations","summary":"How healthy security pool commitments earn fixed annual holding fees, how prepaid rewards vest, and when accrual stops.","text":"A security pool holds actual question collateral in a shared settlement contract. Each question has a linear fee stream, and an aggregate expiry index advances all streams without visiting them individually. Fees reduce both funded collateral and its internal accounting units. Outcome token supply remains separate, so a fee changes each remaining token's redemption value. Fractional claim weights let small USDC and USDT markets accrue fees below one raw token unit per second. See the settlement reference for rounding and precision limits. The creator fixes one annual rate, applied to each question's outstanding principal basis. It does not compound against shrinking collateral. Minting and redemption preserve that basis per remaining share. Fees continue after the question's calendar end until reporting starts or the fee budget above the backing floor runs out. Stale-price intervals and cash-impaired books do not charge ordinary holding fees, and later price recovery never backcharges those intervals. The creator fixes minimumOutcomeRate , the value reserved per outcome share. Holding fees can spend only the amount above this floor. For example, 1,000 complete sets retain at least 10 collateral tokens in a fully funded book with a 0.01 floor. The settlement reference gives the units and stream-expiry rules. This caps ordinary fee income; it does not release the remaining underwriting obligation or waive purchase fees and the prepaid resolution premium. Providers must account for a possible period without holding-fee income before redemption or finality. The floor protects against fee depletion, not an underfunded fork: recovery claims may pay less collateral plus REP, and tiny claims still face token-unit rounding. Each purchase also prepays 52 days of fees on its added principal: the maximum ordinary three-day reporting window plus seven-week extension. An Escalation Game that no fork interrupts always ends within that window, because its only deadline is the curve's crossing of binding capital. This resolution premium stays with the question. Once reporting starts, providers earn only the portion corresponding to time the question remains unresolved, up to the prepaid amount. An expiry index stops those earnings at finality without requiring an owner or keeper to visit each question. Unused premium is burned when the question becomes final or all trader complete sets are returned, because the contract does not record which buyer paid it. The premium is nonrefundable; later providers cannot collect an already resolved question’s remaining budget. Healthy providers earn a fraction based on their share of total commitment, including unhealthy commitment in the denominator. An eligibility change first credits income under the previous weights. Outstanding retirement commitments earn while healthy, and separately claimable earnings do not count as backing. Each purchase pays the greater of the flat minimum fee and the backing-based percentage. That fee and recognized REP income vest over at least 24 hours, rewarding commitments that are healthy when each portion unlocks. This minimum prevents providers from capturing a whole stream by supplying capacity for seconds. Providers who retire retain earned fees and give up future releases. Unassigned collateral and REP are burned. The protocol does not pay keepers or price sponsors from underwriting fees. A failed collateral transfer remains a funded claim that can be retried when transfers resume. The resolution premium goes to underwriting providers. Wallet reporters instead fund their own gas and rely on the Escalation Game’s REP incentives . A fork preserves existing purchase-fee and REP stream dates. Remaining resolution premiums follow branch allocations and can fund child reporting once the branch activates. See the underwriting reference for custody and burn rules.","title":"Open-interest fees","topic":"Economics","weight":0},{"fragment":"","heading":"","keywords":["collateral repair auction","clearing","settlement","repair"],"path":"explanation/collateral-repair-auctions.html","sectionTitle":"Explanations","summary":"Auction clearing, settlement, and weak-demand loss allocation.","text":"When a universe forks, each Statoblast security pool prepares branches, one per child universe ( forks and migration ). Each owner chooses one branch for its REP and its commitment-weighted share of the security pool’s settlement collateral , so a child receives only the collateral of the vaults that chose it. For a security pool using USDC, suppose the parent held 100 USDC of settlement collateral when it forked and a child receives only 70 USDC through migration. The child inherits the parent's full share supply, so its Collateral Repair Auction tries to raise the missing 30 USDC by selling child REP. Full repair: bidders contribute 30 USDC, so the child reaches its 100 USDC target. Partial repair: bidders contribute 12 USDC, so the child operates with 82 USDC and remains impaired. No demand: no qualifying bid contributes USDC, so the child remains at 70 USDC. The auction repairs collateral and never selects truth. It sells REP belonging to an already-defined child universe. The child resumes with only the collateral that migration and accepted bids actually provide.","title":"Collateral Repair Auctions","topic":"Economics","weight":1},{"fragment":"security-pool-auctions","heading":"Shared security pool auctions","keywords":[],"path":"explanation/collateral-repair-auctions.html","sectionTitle":"Explanations","summary":"Auction clearing, settlement, and weak-demand loss allocation.","text":"A security pool shares backing across several questions. It holds one auction for the branch’s combined collateral shortfall. Only freshly unchosen provider REP can be sold; chosen providers’ backing and REP already owed to older outcome claims are excluded. A fixed one-question security pool uses this same auction. Reporter escrow is never auction inventory. The auction rounds equal lots up so a full fill can cover the entire deficit. It prefers larger lots when smaller ones would add too much REP through rounding. The contract reference gives the lot-size and rounding limits. A bidder commits collateral for whole lots, sets a minimum REP per lot, and chooses a health target. The minimum REP must back the lot at the opening conservative price and the bidder’s target. Winners become vault owners with commitments equal to their accepted collateral. Lower REP-per-lot bids offer more collateral for the same REP, so they rank first. At closing, every winning lot must also receive at least the REP that backs it at the closing conservative price, rounded up to the auction’s REP price grid, the step between valid REP-per-lot prices. The auction accepts the largest number of lots that fit both its collateral target and the available REP when each lot receives the larger of the marginal winner’s minimum and that closing requirement. Any unsold REP remains with inherited claims. If no feasible lot exists, no REP is sold. At the same limit, earlier bids fill first. This prevents a late bid at the minimum viable price from crowding out earlier bids. A bid must be competitive when submitted. If later bids permanently rule it out, the bidder can recover its entire collateral before closing. Bids that could still win remain binding. See the auction reference for refunds. Winning backing and commitments count immediately at closing, even before an owner or keeper splits a winning position out of the shared accounting. Choices close at week eight and bidding at week nine, even if preparation starts late. Opening requires a fresh funded price and at least one full day to bid. Closing uses the accepted price only if its settlement-eligibility timestamp is on the final day and it still covers the auction’s exposure. A higher closing price raises the required REP per lot, which can reduce the number of fills. The auction reference lists cancellation conditions. A cash deficit puts the branch into runoff. Existing claims can settle while new purchases and ordinary holding fees remain closed. A sponsor can fund the remaining cash deficit if there is enough underwriting commitment. New buyers in an inherited question with recovery REP contribute their proportional REP so existing holders keep their entitlement per share. Winners take on ordinary vault obligations and liquidation risk . Later withdrawals and retirement can release backing, so a sale does not guarantee permanent security.","title":"Collateral Repair Auctions","topic":"Economics","weight":0},{"fragment":"","heading":"","keywords":["OpenOracle","price","coordinator","callback"],"path":"explanation/open-oracle.html","sectionTitle":"Explanations","summary":"Why a contestable REP/ETH price guards solvency-sensitive operations, and where it stops.","text":"Explanation Statoblast needs a fresh REP/collateral exchange rate (quoted as REP per collateral token) when it measures whether REP adequately secures collateral obligations. OpenOracle supplies an economically contestable price. A reporter posts a collateral/REP position at the claimed price, and anyone who believes the price is wrong can replace the report by trading against that position, so an inaccurate report is a profit opportunity. OpenOracle never decides whether a prediction-market answer is Yes, No, or Invalid.","title":"Why Statoblast uses OpenOracle","topic":"Protocol","weight":1},{"fragment":"protected-operations","heading":"Protected operations","keywords":[],"path":"explanation/open-oracle.html","sectionTitle":"Explanations","summary":"Why a contestable REP/ETH price guards solvency-sensitive operations, and where it stops.","text":"A shared security pool feed protects commitment growth, surplus REP withdrawals, purchases and takeovers. Each question reads that feed directly. Each capital or settlement operation still checks its own live safety conditions. Reporter deposits use separate REP custody.","title":"Why Statoblast uses OpenOracle","topic":"Protocol","weight":0},{"fragment":"cached-and-fresh","heading":"Cached and fresh prices","keywords":[],"path":"explanation/open-oracle.html","sectionTitle":"Explanations","summary":"Why a contestable REP/ETH price guards solvency-sensitive operations, and where it stops.","text":"An accepted report supplies a shared price for a limited freshness window. A sponsor can fund a new independent candidate when it expires. Child universes need their own accepted reports; a copied numeric price is not fresh evidence.","title":"Why Statoblast uses OpenOracle","topic":"Protocol","weight":0},{"fragment":"example","heading":"One request, dispute, and settlement","keywords":[],"path":"explanation/open-oracle.html","sectionTitle":"Explanations","summary":"Why a contestable REP/ETH price guards solvency-sensitive operations, and where it stops.","text":"Alice wants to increase her commitment while the shared REP/collateral price is stale. She funds an independent report. Bob can trade against an inaccurate report before it settles, restarting its settlement clock. Once a valid report is accepted, Alice can submit her capital change with explicit bounds. The security pool shares one accepted price across its questions, but each candidate has its own escrow. A pending dispute cannot lock another sponsor’s funds. A sponsor can attach capital operations to a report, though another candidate may settle first. See shared report coverage for funding and callback rules.","title":"Why Statoblast uses OpenOracle","topic":"Protocol","weight":0},{"fragment":"limitations","heading":"Security limitations","keywords":[],"path":"explanation/open-oracle.html","sectionTitle":"Explanations","summary":"Why a contestable REP/ETH price guards solvency-sensitive operations, and where it stops.","text":"OpenOracle relies on participants noticing and profitably correcting inaccurate reports. Congestion, censorship, incorrect gas assumptions, insufficient economic incentives, or repeated disputes can delay or undermine that process. A fresh price is not a guarantee that an operation will succeed, and a settled price says nothing about the market question's outcome. The security pool creator must choose the minimum report stake in the selected collateral when creating the security pool. The floor is immutable and inherited by children; reports also need funding proportional to exposure. The system assumes that the chosen floor keeps correcting harmful prices profitable after gas and other costs, even when exposure is low. The contracts do not establish this economic condition or query a collateral/ETH price feed. Assumption A18 states the requirement and what fails if it no longer holds. See the integration reference for funding, callbacks, and recovery, and the security model for participation assumptions.","title":"Why Statoblast uses OpenOracle","topic":"Protocol","weight":0},{"fragment":"","heading":"","keywords":["bots","liquidator","arbitrager","augurScan","research","README"],"path":"reference/further-reading.html","sectionTitle":"Reference","summary":"Operator, indexer, test, and research material that lives beside the code.","text":"Reference These links open repository documentation and research on GitHub.","title":"Further reading","topic":"Ecosystem","weight":1},{"fragment":"operators","heading":"Run something","keywords":[],"path":"reference/further-reading.html","sectionTitle":"Reference","summary":"Operator, indexer, test, and research material that lives beside the code.","text":"Document Who it is for What it covers Statoblast liquidator Security pool capital operators Monitoring security pool positions, taking over commitments and recapitalizing principal, and the bot's dashboard. OpenOracle arbitrager Price correctors Finding mispriced REP/ETH reports, funding disputes against Uniswap quotes, executor deployment, and settlement tracking. Chaos bot and its operator reference Testnet operators Exercising permissionless protocol paths with real transactions, safety limits, and runtime controls. augurScan , its operations guide , API reference , and state model Indexer operators and integrators The read-only protocol explorer: deployment, backup and upgrade, the HTTP API, and how retained evidence differs from the current view. Local test network and Sepolia Reth node Developers and node operators Docker Compose setups for the repository-pinned Anvil chain and a pruned Sepolia execution and consensus pair.","title":"Further reading","topic":"Ecosystem","weight":0},{"fragment":"code","heading":"Read the code","keywords":[],"path":"reference/further-reading.html","sectionTitle":"Reference","summary":"Operator, indexer, test, and research material that lives beside the code.","text":"Document Who it is for What it covers Repository README Contributors Setup, local development, simulation mode, common commands, and validation. Contract layout and the Statoblast contract directory Auditors and contract developers Why source paths are stable, which directory owns which contracts, and the OpenOracle vendoring boundary. Security regression suite Auditors The focused tests that reproduce past security findings and how to add one. Shared TypeScript packages and core UI ownership Front-end and tooling contributors Package boundaries, build dependencies, and which layer may import which. Statoblast test slices Contract developers How the split Statoblast suites are run individually and together. Shared security pool fork workflow tests Front-end contributors Test cases for the shared security pool fork workflow. Token authorization provenance Auditors Where the EIP-712 and ECDSA signature helpers come from and what was changed. augurScan backend boundaries Indexer contributors Which backend directory owns HTTP, scanning, storage, and evidence types. UI features , UI protocol client , and UI copy modules UI contributors Where feature components, typed contract reads and writes, and user-facing text live, and which may import which. Visual foundation UI contributors The shared design tokens, product accents, and status treatments used by every interface.","title":"Further reading","topic":"Ecosystem","weight":0},{"fragment":"research","heading":"Understand the design history","keywords":[],"path":"reference/further-reading.html","sectionTitle":"Reference","summary":"Operator, indexer, test, and research material that lives beside the code.","text":"The oracle-research repository holds the proposals, rejected alternatives, and open questions behind the protocol.","title":"Further reading","topic":"Ecosystem","weight":0},{"fragment":"","heading":"","keywords":["OpenOracle","parameters","funding","callbacks","estimators"],"path":"reference/open-oracle.html","sectionTitle":"Reference","summary":"Funding, report sizing, settlement validation, recovery, parameters, and shared security pool price coverage.","text":"OpenOracle supplies a fresh REP/collateral price for Statoblast's solvency-sensitive operations. It does not decide market truth; local Escalation Games and Zoltar remain responsible for truth and branching. The OpenOracle explanation covers why Statoblast uses a contestable price and which operations depend on it. This page specifies the integration: formulas, parameters, and settlement-callback behavior. Per-function callers, preconditions, and events are in the feed contract reference .","title":"OpenOracle integration reference","topic":"Mechanisms","weight":1},{"fragment":"openoracle-role","heading":"Token pair and cached price","keywords":[],"path":"reference/open-oracle.html","sectionTitle":"Reference","summary":"Funding, report sizing, settlement validation, recovery, parameters, and shared security pool price coverage.","text":"Statoblast configures the security pool’s immutable collateral token as token1 and universe REP as token2 . Reports quote their direct raw-unit ratio, without a collateral/ETH feed: lastPrice = amount2 ⋅ 10 18 amount1 The settled REP amount ( amount2 ) is scaled by the fixed price precision and divided by the settled collateral amount ( amount1 ). The price is raw REP units per raw collateral unit, scaled by 10 18 . The callback does not normalize decimals. For six-decimal USDC, one REP per whole collateral token is encoded as 10 30 ; for 18-decimal WETH it is 10 18 . The reference OpenOracle arbitrager always supports WETH collateral. It disputes and settles reports for a non-WETH security pool only when the token meets the bot’s transfer and allowance requirements and the operator allowlists it; it skips every other collateral. For an allowlisted token it hedges REP against that token and values profit and risk in WETH through the token's Uniswap V3 WETH pool. Security pools whose collateral no running bot allowlists still require an independent corrector and settler. An accepted report with a strictly newer eligibility timestamp becomes the feed's cached REP/collateral price. lastSettlementTimestamp records settlement eligibility: the final report timestamp plus settlementTime . The freshness window starts there, so settling late cannot extend it. The shared feed freshness window is settlementTime plus one hour on Sepolia or five minutes on other chains. With the mainnet eight-minute challenge period, a shared price is usable for 780 seconds after eligibility, or at most 21 minutes after the final report. priceValidUntil() returns the canonical expiry; a price is stale at that timestamp. The feed does not meter operation volume during the window. Independently challenged candidates with equal eligibility timestamps may each be accepted and execute their own authorized operation batches; equality alone is not a rejection reason. Only a strictly newer eligibility timestamp replaces the shared price, exposure limits and epoch. For equal timestamps, the first accepted report supplies those shared values; settlement ordering can therefore influence the initial choice, but later equal-time settlements cannot overwrite it. A shared feed's aggregate exposure is the largest of current security pool commitment, backing demand and the maximum commitment a preparing branch auction can add to chosen backing. The feed reads these aggregates without scanning questions or bidders. Escalation halts use aggregate exposure. A shared feed permits concurrent candidates, including renewal before the accepted price expires. Each candidate has its own SharedPoolReportEscrow , which holds its reporter identity and funding credits. pendingReportId and the associated pending getters identify only the latest candidate; they do not enumerate or exclude other pending candidates. A pending, disputed or rejected report never extends accepted-price freshness. Accepting a strictly newer shared observation atomically checkpoints security pool fees under the previous price and updates capital eligibility. A failed mandatory checkpoint rolls back that acceptance. Ordinary capital operations execute separately and recheck current safety constraints against the accepted price. An optional batch of sponsor-authorized capital operations is attempted immediately after acceptance. The request form shows current underwriting exposure, the exposure its selected report stake can cover, and the remaining growth headroom. Commitments can grow while a report waits to settle. Settlement therefore rechecks funding against current exposure: accepting an underfunded price could weaken the correction incentive just as more capital depends on it. An underfunded report is rejected. Any attached operations follow the canonical-price fallback . Headroom is shared and can change before inclusion; it is not a capacity reservation. REP exposure coverage remains a separate required bound. Accepted report capacity is shared across security pool owners; sponsoring a larger report does not reserve its added capacity. Another owner can consume it before a separate commitment-growth transaction. Attach growth to requestSecurityPoolPriceWithOperations to attempt it atomically after acceptance, with an operation gas allowance, deadline and price bounds. Each operation still rechecks safety constraints and can fail independently; inspect ReportOperationAttempted before treating growth as complete.","title":"OpenOracle integration reference","topic":"Mechanisms","weight":0},{"fragment":"security-pool-preparation-reports","heading":"Security pool branch preparation","keywords":[],"path":"reference/open-oracle.html","sectionTitle":"Reference","summary":"Funding, report sizing, settlement validation, recovery, parameters, and shared security pool price coverage.","text":"SecurityPoolFactory.prepareSecurityPoolPriceFeed(source, universeId) prepares the canonical child feed without deploying a child question. source must be a canonical question ledger already admitted to this security pool, with its security pool binding recorded by the factory. Security pool configuration is supplied directly at deployment; preparing its initial shared feed does not require a seed question. SecurityPool.isPriceReportingOpen(universeId) permits requests and acceptance after startBranch , until the fixed native recapitalization deadline at week nine. From that deadline until activation, the accepted observation is frozen. Reporting reopens on activation if the child universe has not forked; a forked universe rejects new prices. Late branch preparation does not extend the window. A strictly newer accepted preparation report caches the price and funded exposure; it does not activate trading, start holding fees or start imported positions’ liquidation history. Activation uses the cached price only while it remains fresh. A stale or absent price does not block branch activation. Callbacks during a closed window reject the candidate price while preserving report funding recovery .","title":"OpenOracle integration reference","topic":"Mechanisms","weight":0},{"fragment":"shared-report-coverage","heading":"Shared report coverage and capital prices","keywords":[],"path":"reference/open-oracle.html","sectionTitle":"Reference","summary":"Funding, report sizing, settlement validation, recovery, parameters, and shared security pool price coverage.","text":"minimumToken2ReportAttoRep() requires one hundredth of pledged underwriting REP plus any fresh fork-auction sale inventory, rounded up. Pledged REP is included because a liquidation auction can transfer all of it. minimumToken1ReportAttoEthForPrice(price) also raises the collateral minimum enough to fund that REP amount at the proposed price. The shared feed rejects a signed collateral budget below the required amount. Acceptance checks both sides: acceptedRepExposureLimitAttoRep is final REP funding times 100. An arbitrarily cheap proposed REP price therefore cannot remove the REP inventory challenge requirement. A canonical acceptance sets acceptedExposureLimitAttoEth to (final collateral − configured collateral stake floor) × 100 and increments acceptedEpoch . An ordinary setCommitment raise cannot lift aggregate exposure above that accepted limit. An owner can also call setCommitmentWithReport(reportId, commitment) using a fresh report the shared feed accepted whose final funding covers the projected exposure. reportAuthorization(reportId, newExposure) validates that proof even if a newer, smaller canonical report superseded it. For accepted price q , target error e , and percentage precision P = 10,000,000 , the upper bound is q × P / (P − e) , rounded up, and the liquidation bound is q × P / (P + e) , rounded down. Capital entry, withdrawals, capacity and fee eligibility use the conservative upper bound; rewarded adverse actions use the lower bound. Proof authorization also retains the higher of the owner’s current conservative price and the proof’s upper bound for that owner’s collateral checks until a later canonical epoch covers the enlarged live exposure. Selecting a smaller report cannot remove this owner-specific floor.","title":"OpenOracle integration reference","topic":"Mechanisms","weight":0},{"fragment":"optional-upstream-capabilities","heading":"Report flags","keywords":[],"path":"reference/open-oracle.html","sectionTitle":"Reference","summary":"Funding, report sizing, settlement validation, recovery, parameters, and shared security pool price coverage.","text":"The feed always submits flags 7 : FLAG_TIME_TYPE ( 1 , a timestamp clock), FLAG_TRACK_DISPUTES ( 2 , per-dispute history), and FLAG_STORE_ALL ( 4 , the complete report state in storage). It never sets the optional flags 8 , 16 , 32 , or 64 ; the OpenOracle provenance record identifies the vendored source and build profile.","title":"OpenOracle integration reference","topic":"Mechanisms","weight":0},{"fragment":"statoblast-integration","heading":"Integration architecture","keywords":[],"path":"reference/open-oracle.html","sectionTitle":"Reference","summary":"Funding, report sizing, settlement validation, recovery, parameters, and shared security pool price coverage.","text":"Each question references the canonical SharedPoolPriceFeed for its security pool and universe. The feed owns the accepted REP/collateral price and freshness clock. Every candidate has its own escrow. The factory binds each question to its canonical feed; exposure checkpoints authenticate the calling ledger against factory records. A child universe gets a new feed for its own REP token, initially with no accepted price. Adding a question does not reset an existing feed. Capital deposits, commitments and retirement use the security pool directly. Capital operations may also execute through an ordered sponsor-authorized batch attached to a shared report.","title":"OpenOracle integration reference","topic":"Mechanisms","weight":0},{"fragment":"report-lifecycle","heading":"Price request lifecycle","keywords":[],"path":"reference/open-oracle.html","sectionTitle":"Reference","summary":"Funding, report sizing, settlement validation, recovery, parameters, and shared security pool price coverage.","text":"The sponsor approves collateral and universe REP, chooses a candidate price and report budget, and opens a report directly through the shared feed. OpenOracle accepts valid corrections during the challenge window. A correction replaces the current reporter and restarts settlement timing. After the challenge window, anyone can settle the report. Its isolated escrow validates the result and offers it to the shared feed. The feed accepts only a qualifying, fresh observation with sufficient economic coverage. Rejected candidates cannot invalidate the previously accepted observation. Owners and keepers normally submit capital operations separately. A report may instead carry a sponsor-authorized operation batch; both paths recheck current safety constraints. Escalation reports use wallet REP in separate reporter custody. They do not consume underwriting REP or use an oracle operation queue.","title":"OpenOracle integration reference","topic":"Mechanisms","weight":0},{"fragment":"request-funding","heading":"Funding and report ownership","keywords":[],"path":"reference/open-oracle.html","sectionTitle":"Reference","summary":"Funding, report sizing, settlement validation, recovery, parameters, and shared security pool price coverage.","text":"The sponsor is the caller requesting a price report and funding its collateral, REP, transaction gas and public settlement bounty. Whoever settles earns the bounty as an OpenOracle ETH credit, including the sponsor if they settle their own report. Settlement does not immediately return that ETH to a wallet: the credited caller collects it through OpenOracle’s withdraw(address(0), amount) , available under Oracle balances in the OpenOracle app. Returned report collateral and REP belong to the recorded sponsor. A negligible bounty leaves settlement largely to sponsors. A sponsor can wait after a candidate becomes eligible and accept its older price only when that price favours a withdrawal or takeover. The gas-funded minimum encourages independent settlers to accept eligible reports promptly, reducing that timing advantage. It does not guarantee a settler, timely inclusion, or a current market price. The minimum bounty is the callback gas budget, plus its EIP-150 forwarding allowance and 310,000 gas for settlement overhead and reward collection, padded by 20% plus 20,000 gas for the two transaction margins, multiplied by the configured settlement fee allowance, plus 101 attoETH . The fee allowance uses the request block's base fee plus the configured priority fee, multiplied by the settlement base-fee multiplier. The ordinary callback budget is 4,000,000 gas ; attached operation batches add their declared gas budget. Sponsors can offer more. A rising inclusion-time base fee can require a new quote; the contract rejects an insufficient bounty instead of charging more silently. requestSecurityPoolPrice(proposedRepPerEthPrice, requestedInitialAttoWeth, bountyAttoEth) transfers exactly the reviewed collateral budget and its corresponding REP from the caller. It rejects a budget below the inclusion-time minimum instead of silently increasing it. Security pool settlement collateral does not fund reports. Both shared-feed request methods commit the specified bounty and refund excess native ETH to the caller after storing the candidate and any operation batch. Rejecting that refund reverts the whole request.","title":"OpenOracle integration reference","topic":"Mechanisms","weight":0},{"fragment":"sponsor-takeover","heading":"Sponsor-authorized operation batches","keywords":[],"path":"reference/open-oracle.html","sectionTitle":"Reference","summary":"Funding, report sizing, settlement validation, recovery, parameters, and shared security pool price coverage.","text":"requestSecurityPoolPriceWithOperations(price, wethBudget, bounty, batch) funds a direct report and records ordered capital-ledger calls. The funding caller supplies the owner authority for every call; withdrawal and earnings recipients remain explicit in the signed calldata. A batch fixes accepted-price bounds, an expiry, a total gas allowance, and an array of operations containing calldata and individual gas limits. There is no fixed operation-count cap; each operation's calldata is at most MAXIMUM_REPORT_OPERATION_DATA_BYTES (512 bytes). The ledger supports takeOver , takeOverWithBounds , takeOverRepairAuctionBid , depositFor , depositAndSetHealthTarget , withdrawPrincipal , setHealthTarget , setCommitment , setCommitmentWithReport , claimEarnings , serveRetirement and materializeRepairAuctionBid . Privileged accounting and fork entrypoints are excluded. Each call retains its ordinary owner, collateral, cohort, funding and reentrancy checks. A report-bound takeover may use zero for expectedAcceptedPriceEpoch to accept the settlement epoch; direct takeovers retain exact epoch checks. Funding can be deposited beforehand or by an earlier batch operation with the required REP approval. The declared batch allowance must cover each individual limit plus 100,000 gas per operation. The OpenOracle callback budget is 4,000,000 gas plus that allowance; ordinary reports retain 4,000,000. A request whose callback budget would exceed 10,000,000 gas reverts with Callback gas exceeds limit , which leaves at most 6,000,000 for the batch. After mandatory acceptance and security pool checkpoints, the feed attempts every operation in order through executeReportOperation , which accepts only the canonical feed and authenticates a ledger self-call as the sponsor. Failed operations, including exhausted individual allowances and reverted auction materialization, roll back individually; later operations still run. Expired or out-of-range batches emit failed outcomes without executing their calls. ReportOperationAttempted(reportId, sponsor, operationIndex, success) records each outcome. A settlement that forwards less than the callback budget cannot publish a price after skipping a promised attempt: it reverts as described under callback gas . The batch runs inside its report's acceptance transaction regardless of who settles. It receives no priority over another report that settles first, no exclusive target reservation, and no reimbursement. Practical transaction and block gas limits bound execution. sponsorOperationReport(sponsor) identifies the latest batch report; its terms are the arguments of the request transaction. Independent reports and ordinary recovery remain available.","title":"OpenOracle integration reference","topic":"Mechanisms","weight":0},{"fragment":"rolling-dispute-exclusivity","heading":"Independent candidates","keywords":[],"path":"reference/open-oracle.html","sectionTitle":"Reference","summary":"Funding, report sizing, settlement validation, recovery, parameters, and shared security pool price coverage.","text":"Reporting has no exclusive sponsor lane. Anyone may fund another candidate while earlier candidates are pending or the accepted price is still fresh. Disputing one candidate cannot reserve all reporting access.","title":"OpenOracle integration reference","topic":"Mechanisms","weight":0},{"fragment":"security-guarantee","heading":"Report sizing and correction incentives","keywords":[],"path":"reference/open-oracle.html","sectionTitle":"Reference","summary":"Funding, report sizing, settlement validation, recovery, parameters, and shared security pool price coverage.","text":"The creator sets a positive minimumReportCollateral in raw collateral units at security pool creation. It is immutable and inherited by every universe in the security pool. The minimum report adds that floor to one hundredth of aggregate collateral exposure, rounded up, whether or not the accepted price is fresh. Unused capacity from an oversized or escalated report does not raise renewal funding. A pending renewal does not reserve capacity or block independently funded growth. If actual exposure grows beyond its final funding before settlement, the renewal’s price is rejected as Report uneconomic . If a separately accepted canonical price is still fresh, the sponsor’s queued operations are attempted under that price and their signed bounds; the rejected report grants no growth authorization. Sponsors may fund headroom to tolerate growth. REP exposure coverage can raise the minimum further at the proposed price. minimumToken1ReportAttoEth = minimumReportCollateral + ⌈ aggregateExposure 100 ⌉ Amounts use raw collateral units, including accessors with legacy AttoEth names. The escalation halt remains the larger of one hundredth of aggregate exposure, rounded up, and the configured fraction of initial collateral funding. The creator must size this floor to satisfy A18: independent, gas-economic correction throughout the security pool’s use, including at low exposure. This is a system assumption, not an onchain gas-cost guarantee. No collateral/ETH feed is queried, and a higher floor requires a new security pool. The feed requires requestedInitialAttoWeth to meet minimumToken1ReportAttoEthForPrice(proposedRepPerEthPrice) at inclusion. It uses the signed collateral amount as OpenOracle's currentAmount1 and derives amount2 = ⌈currentAmount1 × proposedRepPerEthPrice / 1e18⌉ .","title":"OpenOracle integration reference","topic":"Mechanisms","weight":0},{"fragment":"formula-inputs","heading":"Report configuration","keywords":[],"path":"reference/open-oracle.html","sectionTitle":"Reference","summary":"Funding, report sizing, settlement validation, recovery, parameters, and shared security pool price coverage.","text":"Default report configuration comes from shared/statoblast/ts/initialReport/oracleInitialReport.ts and is copied into the shared feed configuration. Formula input Current value Source block.basefee Current request block EVM block context initialReportPriorityFeeAttoEthPerGas Origin question ledger parameter; the UI defaults to 10 nanoETH Immutable lineage configuration inherited by child question ledgers aggregateExposureAttoEth Current aggregate security pool exposure Configured security pool ( oracleExposure(universeId) ) OPEN_INTEREST_DIVIDER 100 (a 1% floor) Feed constant OraclePriceMath.DISPUTE_GAS_UNITS 300,000 gas Source constant used only for the priority-fee bound OraclePriceMath.REPORT_SECURITY_MULTIPLIER_BPS 100,000 bps ( 10x ) Source constant used only for the priority-fee bound targetPriceErrorForDispute 500,000 / 10,000,000 ( 5% ) Factory constructor; shared deployment configuration ORACLE_TARGET_PRICE_ERROR_FOR_DISPUTE protocolFee 100,000 / 10,000,000 ( 1% ) Factory constructor; shared deployment configuration ORACLE_PROTOCOL_FEE feePercentage (OpenOracle's reporter fee) 10,000 / 10,000,000 ( 0.1% ) Factory constructor; shared deployment configuration ORACLE_FEE_PERCENTAGE escalationHaltMultiplierBps 100,000 bps ( 10x ) Factory constructor; shared deployment configuration ORACLE_ESCALATION_HALT_MULTIPLIER_BPS The fixed dispute-gas and report-security factors bound the maximum priority fee; they do not size current report stakes. The configured priority fee sets the settlement base-fee acceptance cap. OpenOracle’s protocol and reporter fees still apply to corrections.","title":"OpenOracle integration reference","topic":"Mechanisms","weight":0},{"fragment":"security-guarantee","heading":"Construction bounds and parameter effects","keywords":[],"path":"reference/open-oracle.html","sectionTitle":"Reference","summary":"Funding, report sizing, settlement validation, recovery, parameters, and shared security pool price coverage.","text":"Feed factory construction requires: a deployed shared-feed deployment worker a target error strictly below 100%, with combined OpenOracle fees below that error a nonzero escalationHaltMultiplierBps settlementTime greater than disputeDelay , and a dispute multiplier of at least 100 maxSettlementBaseFeeMultiplierBps of at least 1x minLiquidationPriceDistanceBps no greater than 100% The priority-fee bound uses the fixed gas and security factors above and half the smaller of the maximum report amount and the escalation-halt capacity. A larger fee reverts with Initial report priority fee exceeds OpenOracle limits . SharedPoolPriceFeedFactory validates report timing, fees and risk configuration at construction. Each feed implementation validates its positive priority fee against that configuration.","title":"OpenOracle integration reference","topic":"Mechanisms","weight":0},{"fragment":"callback-rejection-and-recovery","heading":"Settlement validation, rejection, and recovery","keywords":[],"path":"reference/open-oracle.html","sectionTitle":"Reference","summary":"Funding, report sizing, settlement validation, recovery, parameters, and shared security pool price coverage.","text":"","title":"OpenOracle integration reference","topic":"Mechanisms","weight":0},{"fragment":"callback-rejection-and-recovery","heading":"Low-level callback failure","keywords":[],"path":"reference/open-oracle.html","sectionTitle":"Reference","summary":"Funding, report sizing, settlement validation, recovery, parameters, and shared security pool price coverage.","text":"OpenOracle invokes the price source through a low-level callback whose success is intentionally neither stored nor emitted. A failed callback does not automatically undo an otherwise valid OpenOracle settlement. A shared escrow authenticates OpenOracle and its own report ID; the feed accepts settlement only from that candidate’s registered escrow. Failure can leave an individually settled candidate unprocessed without blocking other candidates. If the settlement transaction cannot retain enough gas headroom after the callback attempt, OpenOracle reverts settlement with InvalidGasLimit . In that case, the report has not settled and feed recovery is not yet available. A shared report escrow enforces this for an under-funded settlement: when its callback is entered with less than the report's callback budget minus 100,000 gas, it consumes all the gas it was given, so the settlement reverts with InvalidGasLimit instead of settling without accepting the price.","title":"OpenOracle integration reference","topic":"Mechanisms","weight":0},{"fragment":"settlement-validation-scope","heading":"Price-source settlement validation","keywords":[],"path":"reference/open-oracle.html","sectionTitle":"Reference","summary":"Funding, report sizing, settlement validation, recovery, parameters, and shared security pool price coverage.","text":"A callback that successfully enters the price source can still produce a rejected price. The price source emits PriceReportRejected and leaves the price cache unchanged when the first applicable condition below holds. A shared rejection records reportOutcome(reportId) = 2 and clears the latest-candidate getters only if they still name that report: for a shared feed, the security pool reporting window is closed: Security pool reporting window closed ; storedGame(reportId).numReports is saturated at the uint24 maximum: Counter saturated ; numReports is zero, or the settled collateral amount is below the configured floor plus ⌈live aggregate exposure / 100⌉ : Report uneconomic ; final REP funding is below the live REP exposure minimum: Report REP inventory budget insufficient ; for a shared feed, settlement eligibility is earlier than the accepted observation ( Report timestamp not newer ); the freshness window measured from settlement eligibility has already closed: Report stale ; either settled token amount is zero: Empty oracle settlement ; the integer REP/collateral calculation produces a zero price: Oracle price is zero . Each report also records a settlement base-fee cap: request block.basefee + initialReportPriorityFeeAttoEthPerGas , scaled by maxSettlementBaseFeeMultiplierBps / 10000 . Because anyone may choose the settlement block, a base fee above the cap never rejects a report. When a candidate passes price validation, or its signed operations can use a fresh canonical price, and the base fee exceeds the cap, the feed emits PriceReportDeferred(reportId, baseFee, maxSettlementBaseFee) instead. OpenOracle completes settlement and pays the final reporter. Released funding remains available through the report escrow’s separate withdrawal methods . The report stays pending: reportOutcome(reportId) stays 0 , the latest-candidate getters keep naming it, and no price or sponsor operation is applied. Recovery completes it later, as described below. These checks establish only that the final collateral position meets the configured stake and exposure floors and that the callback data is structurally acceptable. They do not prove that the accepted price is externally correct, that an independent corrector exists, or that a correcting transaction will obtain inclusion. The shared feed emits PriceCandidateAccepted with the candidate price, the resulting canonical price, the observation timestamp and whether the canonical observation changed. Equal-time acceptance leaves the existing canonical observation in place. Sponsor operations still use the canonical price when checking their signed price bounds. PriceReported carries the resulting canonical price for each acceptance.","title":"OpenOracle integration reference","topic":"Mechanisms","weight":0},{"fragment":"callback-rejection-and-recovery","heading":"Rejected-price cleanup","keywords":[],"path":"reference/open-oracle.html","sectionTitle":"Reference","summary":"Funding, report sizing, settlement validation, recovery, parameters, and shared security pool price coverage.","text":"A rejected candidate leaves the accepted price unchanged. Economic insufficiency or an older observation does not discard signed operations when a separately accepted canonical price is still fresh. Those operations use the canonical price, original expiry and price bounds; rejection never grants report-based growth authority. Other rejection reasons do not attempt operations. Capital transactions retain their own state checks.","title":"OpenOracle integration reference","topic":"Mechanisms","weight":0},{"fragment":"callback-rejection-and-recovery","heading":"Recovering a settled report after callback failure or deferral","keywords":[],"path":"reference/open-oracle.html","sectionTitle":"Reference","summary":"Funding, report sizing, settlement validation, recovery, parameters, and shared security pool price coverage.","text":"Anyone can call recoverReport(reportId) on the shared feed for an unprocessed settled candidate. Call recoverSettledPendingReport() on the feed to recover the latest pending ID. Shared recovery retries acceptance from the amounts stored by OpenOracle, using the original settlement-eligibility timestamp and the usual freshness, funding and reporting-window checks. A still-valid candidate is accepted and attempts its sponsor operations, replacing the shared price only when its eligibility timestamp is strictly newer; an invalid candidate is rejected. Recovery never transfers funding to the sponsor, so a failed payout cannot roll back report processing. A deferred candidate is recovered the same way: in a block whose base fee is at or below its cap, recovery processes the report with the original settlement-eligibility timestamp, so waiting never extends freshness, and runs its operation batch. Recovery reverts with Base fee too high above the cap when either the candidate price is valid or its signed operations can use a fresh canonical price. The latter path can remain available after the candidate itself expires: validation can first classify it as economically insufficient or older than the canonical observation. A candidate with neither a valid price nor a canonical-operation path is rejected at any base fee.","title":"OpenOracle integration reference","topic":"Mechanisms","weight":0},{"fragment":"report-funding-withdrawal","heading":"Withdrawing released report funding","keywords":[],"path":"reference/open-oracle.html","sectionTitle":"Reference","summary":"Funding, report sizing, settlement validation, recovery, parameters, and shared security pool price coverage.","text":"Anyone can call withdrawFunding() on the report escrow to send both available tokens to the original sponsor. Call withdrawTokenFunding(token, recipient) on the escrow to withdraw collateral or REP independently. Anyone can send the token to the sponsor. The sponsor can specify a different recipient other than the escrow. Both methods can withdraw funds released by a dispute before settlement. Each escrow holds credits for one report.","title":"OpenOracle integration reference","topic":"Mechanisms","weight":0},{"fragment":"intentional-economic-tradeoffs","heading":"Liveness and exposure limits","keywords":[],"path":"reference/open-oracle.html","sectionTitle":"Reference","summary":"Funding, report sizing, settlement validation, recovery, parameters, and shared security pool price coverage.","text":"The challenge window assumes at least one funded corrector can obtain transaction inclusion before settlement eligibility. Excluding every correction throughout the window can let a wrong price settle; a higher gas bid cannot guarantee inclusion. The settlement base-fee cap can defer acceptance during expensive blocks, but does not prevent selective censorship. Recovery retains the original observation timestamp and cannot extend freshness. See A16 for the inclusion assumption and A18 for correction incentives. Capital growth requires the accepted coverage or an accepted report with sufficient funding . The feed does not impose an absolute deadline on an individual dispute game; shared candidates can proceed independently when another game remains disputed. See excluded guarantees EG03 and EG04 .","title":"OpenOracle integration reference","topic":"Mechanisms","weight":0},{"fragment":"dispute-escalation","heading":"Dispute escalation amounts","keywords":[],"path":"reference/open-oracle.html","sectionTitle":"Reference","summary":"Funding, report sizing, settlement validation, recovery, parameters, and shared security pool price coverage.","text":"In this integration, collateral is the escalation variable. Before escalationHalt , OpenOracle requires min(⌊oldAmount1 × multiplier / 100⌋, escalationHalt) . With the configured multiplier of 115 , integer flooring leaves token1 amounts from one through six raw collateral units unchanged; seven units is the first amount that grows. At or above the halt, each replacement instead requires oldAmount1 + 1 , adding one raw collateral unit.","title":"OpenOracle integration reference","topic":"Mechanisms","weight":0},{"fragment":"parameters","heading":"OpenOracle parameters used","keywords":[],"path":"reference/open-oracle.html","sectionTitle":"Reference","summary":"Funding, report sizing, settlement validation, recovery, parameters, and shared security pool price coverage.","text":"A source constant is fixed in the Solidity source. Deployment configuration is the shared configuration that the deployment passes to the feed factory, named by its constant in shared/statoblast/ts/initialReport/oracleInitialReport.ts ; the feed exposes its configured values through public getters. Fixed feed flags and internal math constants are identified separately. The line under each value says where it is set. Parameter Value and source Use in Statoblast token1Address / token2Address Collateral / REP The security pool’s collateral and its universe’s REP token Defines the REP/collateral price pair used for security-pool solvency checks. minimumToken1ReportAttoEth() See Report sizing and correction incentives . Configured collateral floor plus one hundredth of the aggregate exposure Minimum initial collateral position for a feed report. OpenOracle initial currentAmount1 The sponsor’s requestedInitialAttoWeth , which must meet the current minimum Initial collateral position; the sponsor may fund more than the minimum. escalationHalt See Report sizing and correction incentives . Derived per request Ends multiplicative escalation; see Dispute escalation amounts . settlerReward The committed bountyAttoEth ; at least the request cost Derived per request Paid to the account that settles the report. Stake sizing: minimumReportCollateral , OPEN_INTEREST_DIVIDER , escalationHaltMultiplierBps See Report sizing and correction incentives . The configured collateral floor plus one hundredth of the aggregate exposure sets the minimum. Pledged REP and sale inventory can raise it. The halt scales selected collateral funding. getRequestPriceCostAttoEth() Gas-funded bounty plus 101 attoETH Minimum public settlement bounty at the request-time fee allowance; sponsors also pay their own transaction gas. callbackGasLimit 4,000,000 gas for ordinary reports. Operation batches add their declared gas allowance, up to 10,000,000 gas in total. Gas forwarded to the settlement callback. Settlement also needs enough surrounding gas to satisfy OpenOracle's callback headroom check. maxSettlementBaseFeeMultiplierBps 30,000 bps ( 3x ) Deployment configuration ORACLE_MAX_SETTLEMENT_BASE_FEE_MULTIPLIER_BPS Scales the request-block base fee plus configured priority fee into the settlement base-fee cap . settlementTime 480 seconds Deployment configuration ORACLE_SETTLEMENT_TIME Report settlement delay on the timestamp clock. The feed factory constructor requires it to exceed disputeDelay . disputeDelay 0 Deployment configuration ORACLE_DISPUTE_DELAY Allows disputes immediately after each report and strictly before its settlement deadline. At the deadline, settlement is valid and a dispute is too late. multiplier 115 ( 1.15x ) Deployment configuration ORACLE_MULTIPLIER Growth of the collateral amount per dispute before the halt; see Dispute escalation amounts . The feed factory constructor requires at least 100 . protocolFeeRecipient 0x000000000000000000000000000000000000dEaD Deployment configuration ORACLE_PROTOCOL_FEE_RECIPIENT Receives OpenOracle protocol fees for feed-created reports. timeType / trackDisputes true / true Fixed SharedPoolPriceFeed constants Timestamps let the feed measure freshness from settlement eligibility; dispute history preserves the correction sequence. callbackContract The candidate's SharedPoolReportEscrow . Set when the report is created Callbacks reconcile candidate funding and submit the settled price to the feed, including any sponsor-authorized operation batch . PRICE_VALID_FOR_SECONDS / SEPOLIA_PRICE_VALID_FOR_SECONDS See Token pair and cached price . Source constant Keeper slack added to settlementTime by SharedPoolPriceFeed.priceValidityWindowSeconds() .","title":"OpenOracle integration reference","topic":"Mechanisms","weight":0},{"fragment":"","heading":"","keywords":["Zoltar","question","registry","screenshots"],"path":"tutorials/zoltar-first-question.html","sectionTitle":"Tutorials","summary":"Create a yes-or-no question in the Zoltar registry and find it again.","text":"Tutorial You will create a yes-or-no question in Zoltar's global registry and then find it again in the question browser. Every market and every fork starts from a question like this one.","title":"Your first Zoltar question","topic":"Zoltar","weight":1},{"fragment":"before-you-start","heading":"Before you start","keywords":[],"path":"tutorials/zoltar-first-question.html","sectionTitle":"Tutorials","summary":"Create a yes-or-no question in the Zoltar registry and find it again.","text":"Run bun run app:serve:zoltar and open http://localhost:4153/?simulate=1&genesis=yes&simScenario=deployed . That browser-local simulator has the contracts deployed and no questions yet; the screenshots below come from it. It never reflects live chain state.","title":"Your first Zoltar question","topic":"Zoltar","weight":0},{"fragment":"create","heading":"Write the question","keywords":[],"path":"tutorials/zoltar-first-question.html","sectionTitle":"Tutorials","summary":"Create a yes-or-no question in the Zoltar registry and find it again.","text":"Select Create question and keep the Binary question type. Enter a Title , for example Will it rain in Lisbon on 1 June 2027? In Description , name the source and say when the answer is Invalid , for example Resolves Yes if IPMA reports measurable rain at its Lisbon station on 1 June 2027 (UTC). Invalid if IPMA publishes no reading. Enter an End time after the event. Leave Start time blank to start immediately. The question form. The draft preview shows the question details and includes the implicit Invalid outcome. A question cannot be edited after it is created, so check the preview before you submit.","title":"Your first Zoltar question","topic":"Zoltar","weight":0},{"fragment":"submit","heading":"Create it","keywords":[],"path":"tutorials/zoltar-first-question.html","sectionTitle":"Tutorials","summary":"Create a yes-or-no question in the Zoltar registry and find it again.","text":"Select Create question below the preview and confirm the transaction. The confirmation card shows the Question ID , the end time, and the Outcomes Yes, No, and Invalid. The new question.","title":"Your first Zoltar question","topic":"Zoltar","weight":0},{"fragment":"find","heading":"Find it in the registry","keywords":[],"path":"tutorials/zoltar-first-question.html","sectionTitle":"Tutorials","summary":"Create a yes-or-no question in the Zoltar registry and find it again.","text":"Select Browse questions . Your question appears automatically under Downloaded (1) . The registry. Questions are global: any universe can use this one, and anyone can find it here. Next, back a shared security pool and mint shares in a separate simulation, or see Zoltar and branching universes to learn what Use for fork does after the question ends.","title":"Your first Zoltar question","topic":"Zoltar","weight":0},{"fragment":"","heading":"","keywords":["Statoblast","securityPool","REP","complete sets","screenshots"],"path":"tutorials/statoblast-first-pool.html","sectionTitle":"Tutorials","summary":"Add REP to a simulated security pool and purchase complete sets for one question.","text":"Tutorial Use the browser simulator to add 1 REP to a shared security pool , then purchase complete sets for one of its questions. A complete set contains one share of every outcome. You will see your principal rise from 5,000 to 5,001 REP and receive shares in your simulated wallet.","title":"Back a shared security pool and mint shares","topic":"Statoblast","weight":1},{"fragment":"before-you-start","heading":"Open the prepared security pool","keywords":[],"path":"tutorials/statoblast-first-pool.html","sectionTitle":"Tutorials","summary":"Add REP to a simulated security pool and purchase complete sets for one question.","text":"This exercise uses fake assets in a browser-local chain. From a prepared checkout, run bun run app:serve:statoblast and open http://localhost:12347/#/security-pools?simulate=1&genesis=yes&simScenario=securitypoolx2 . No wallet extension or live funds are needed. Wait for Price current in the Shared backing section. The prepared security pool has two questions. Your starting principal is 5,000 REP and your commitment is 10 WETH. Your starting account. The fixture has already authorized backing.","title":"Back a shared security pool and mint shares","topic":"Statoblast","weight":0},{"fragment":"deposit","heading":"Add one REP","keywords":[],"path":"tutorials/statoblast-first-pool.html","sectionTitle":"Tutorials","summary":"Add REP to a simulated security pool and purchase complete sets for one question.","text":"In Deposit REP , enter 1 in REP amount . If an approval is requested, approve the displayed amount and confirm its review. Select Deposit REP , review the transaction, and confirm it. Wait for Transaction confirmed . Under Your capital , check that Your total principal is now 5,001 REP. Your commitment remains 10 WETH: depositing does not automatically increase it.","title":"Back a shared security pool and mint shares","topic":"Statoblast","weight":0},{"fragment":"mint","heading":"Purchase complete sets","keywords":[],"path":"tutorials/statoblast-first-pool.html","sectionTitle":"Tutorials","summary":"Add REP to a simulated security pool and purchase complete sets for one question.","text":"In Mint security pool complete sets , choose a title under Question . Read the question details and verify its full ID. Use Find by question ID to enter an exact identifier instead. Enter 0.01 for Collateral (WETH) , 0.1 for Maximum purchase charge (WETH) , and 0 for Minimum complete sets . The zero minimum is suitable for this controlled simulation; a live purchase should set a minimum from its quote. Check Purchase charge (fee + premium) . If needed, approve the displayed WETH amount, which covers collateral plus the quoted charge, and wait for confirmation. Then select Mint security pool complete sets and confirm the review. Wait for Transaction confirmed . Select Open question shares to open the question's page in the same simulation, and inspect your outcome balances under Shares . Each outcome has the same newly minted quantity. Purchase controls. The fee budget is separate from collateral. See Fees for purchase costs and the capital guardrails for withdrawal rules.","title":"Back a shared security pool and mint shares","topic":"Statoblast","weight":0},{"fragment":"","heading":"","keywords":["Trading","liquidity","initialize","pair","screenshots"],"path":"tutorials/trading-first-market.html","sectionTitle":"Tutorials","summary":"Create and seed the trading pool for a QuestionLedger in the UI.","text":"Tutorial You will open trading for a question ledger by creating its trading pool and seeding it at a 70% conditional Yes price (the Yes price if the market does not resolve Invalid) with 0.01 ETH. You finish with LP tokens (the trading pool's liquidity-provider tokens) and separately held Invalid shares in your wallet, and the market listed as open.","title":"Your first Trading market","topic":"Trading","weight":1},{"fragment":"before-you-start","heading":"Before you start","keywords":[],"path":"tutorials/trading-first-market.html","sectionTitle":"Tutorials","summary":"Create and seed the trading pool for a QuestionLedger in the UI.","text":"Trading is an exchange for Statoblast outcome shares: a trading pool swaps the Yes and No shares of exactly one question ledger, and a question ledger without one has no market yet. In the UI, creating the trading pool is called creating a market . To practise without a wallet, run bun run app:serve:trading and open http://localhost:4163/?simulate=1&genesis=yes&simScenario=deployed#/create-market . That simulator scenario has one question ledger, at 0x44b053EFC92944Cc673d5533fFf9D4Db99843B7f , and no trading pool; the screenshots below come from it. For a live network, follow Configure the Trading UI , and if the UI opens the deployment screen, complete Deploy the Trading contracts first. Fund the wallet with the deposit plus gas.","title":"Your first Trading market","topic":"Trading","weight":0},{"fragment":"find-pool","heading":"Open a question without a market","keywords":[],"path":"tutorials/trading-first-market.html","sectionTitle":"Tutorials","summary":"Create and seed the trading pool for a QuestionLedger in the UI.","text":"Select the Create tab and paste the question ledger address into Search questions ; the UI lists each question ledger as a question. In the simulator, use the address above; on a live network, copy it from Statoblast. Select Open question . The form opens with the badge MARKET NOT CREATED and Create market selected, and the question ledger is saved as a favorite. To check eligibility before seeding, return to the Create tab and open Details on the question card. Review Minting capacity , then select Create market to return to the form. If the button is disabled, the page gives the reason. Questions without a market. Create market reopens the form; Details opens the details page. An eligible question ledger. The question ledger is operational, its universe has not forked, and it has capacity for new collateral.","title":"Your first Trading market","topic":"Trading","weight":0},{"fragment":"initialize","heading":"Create and seed the trading pool","keywords":[],"path":"tutorials/trading-first-market.html","sectionTitle":"Tutorials","summary":"Create and seed the trading pool for a QuestionLedger in the UI.","text":"Enter 0.01 in Amount and 70 in Yes price if not Invalid . Read the estimate: You provide 0.01 ETH, You receive LP tokens, about 0.0089 Invalid after the minting fee, and the Yes shares the trading pool did not need. Select Create market and add liquidity and confirm the one transaction that creates and seeds the trading pool. It reverts if it would mint fewer LP tokens than the estimate allows after your slippage tolerance, which you set under Settings . Seeding at a 70% conditional Yes price. The LP tokens represent only the Yes and No left in the trading pool; the Invalid and spare Yes return to your wallet.","title":"Your first Trading market","topic":"Trading","weight":0},{"fragment":"check","heading":"Check the result","keywords":[],"path":"tutorials/trading-first-market.html","sectionTitle":"Tutorials","summary":"Create and seed the trading pool for a QuestionLedger in the UI.","text":"Open the Markets tab. The market is listed with the badge TRADING OPEN and a conditional price of 70% Yes. Then open Portfolio : the market shows your LP tokens, your Invalid, and the spare Yes as separate holdings. Portfolio after seeding. The new LP tokens and spare outcomes appear alongside the simulation wallet’s existing complete set. Fees reduce the amount minted from the 0.01 ETH deposit. See Conditional pricing for how the reserves set the displayed price. Next: make your first trade against the trading pool you just created.","title":"Your first Trading market","topic":"Trading","weight":0},{"fragment":"","heading":"","keywords":["Trading","buy","sell","enterPosition","slippage"],"path":"tutorials/trading-first-trade.html","sectionTitle":"Tutorials","summary":"Buy Yes shares in the UI and sell part of them back for ETH.","text":"Tutorial Buy Yes in the simulator and sell part of it for ETH. After the purchase, both Yes and Invalid balances rise. The sale spends some Invalid.","title":"Your first trade","topic":"Trading","weight":1},{"fragment":"before-you-start","heading":"Before you start","keywords":[],"path":"tutorials/trading-first-trade.html","sectionTitle":"Tutorials","summary":"Buy Yes shares in the UI and sell part of them back for ETH.","text":"Run bun run app:serve:trading and open http://localhost:4163/?simulate=1&genesis=yes#/market/0x44b053EFC92944Cc673d5533fFf9D4Db99843B7f . No wallet extension or live funds are needed. For a live network, see Configure the Trading UI .","title":"Your first trade","topic":"Trading","weight":0},{"fragment":"choose","heading":"Check the market","keywords":[],"path":"tutorials/trading-first-trade.html","sectionTitle":"Tutorials","summary":"Buy Yes shares in the UI and sell part of them back for ETH.","text":"Check for the TRADING OPEN badge on the seeded market. The seeded market.","title":"Your first trade","topic":"Trading","weight":0},{"fragment":"protect","heading":"Set your protection","keywords":[],"path":"tutorials/trading-first-trade.html","sectionTitle":"Tutorials","summary":"Buy Yes shares in the UI and sell part of them back for ETH.","text":"Keep the default Slippage tolerance of 0.5% and Transaction valid for of 20 minutes. Trade settings. Every quote repeats the current values, for example Slippage 0.5% · valid up to 20 min .","title":"Your first trade","topic":"Trading","weight":0},{"fragment":"buy","heading":"Buy Yes","keywords":[],"path":"tutorials/trading-first-trade.html","sectionTitle":"Tutorials","summary":"Buy Yes shares in the UI and sell part of them back for ETH.","text":"In the market's Trade tab, select Buy and Yes . Enter 0.002 in You pay . Check You receive , Invalid insurance , and Minimum received . The simulated trade includes the pool’s minimum purchase fee, so its profit estimate is negative. Check that Price impact reads 4.93%. Select Buy Yes and wait for confirmation. A Yes purchase. The bar at the top of the estimate shows how far the purchase moves the conditional price. After confirmation, check that Yes and Invalid increased under Your position . See Invalid insurance for how entry creates both balances.","title":"Your first trade","topic":"Trading","weight":0},{"fragment":"sell","heading":"Sell Yes","keywords":[],"path":"tutorials/trading-first-trade.html","sectionTitle":"Tutorials","summary":"Buy Yes shares in the UI and sell part of them back for ETH.","text":"Select Sell and Yes , then enter 0.001 in Shares to sell . Check You receive in ETH, Minimum received , and Invalid used . Select Sell Yes and wait for confirmation. No approval is needed: the sale is one ShareToken transfer of your Yes and Invalid to the router . A Yes sale. Invalid used is the insurance the sale spends: it is burned with matching Yes and No as complete sets, which pay out the ETH. After confirmation, check that your wallet received ETH and your Invalid balance fell by the quoted amount. To sell a larger amount, see Exit an insured position .","title":"Your first trade","topic":"Trading","weight":0},{"fragment":"","heading":"","keywords":["Trading","development","commands","Docker"],"path":"how-to/trading-set-up-development.html","sectionTitle":"How-to guides","summary":"Compile, test, build, and serve the Trading subsystem locally.","text":"How-to guide","title":"Set up Trading development","topic":"Trading","weight":1},{"fragment":"build-and-run","heading":"Build and run","keywords":[],"path":"how-to/trading-set-up-development.html","sectionTitle":"How-to guides","summary":"Compile, test, build, and serve the Trading subsystem locally.","text":"From the repository root, confirm dependencies with test -d node_modules . If they are missing, run bun install --frozen-lockfile && bun run setup . Run bun run trading:compile , bun run trading:test , and bun run trading:ui:build . Start bun run app:serve:trading , then open http://localhost:4163/?simulate=1&genesis=yes for the browser-local simulator. Configure the Trading UI lists the Trading scenarios. The setup works when the tests pass and the simulator page loads. To run the standalone UI in Docker instead, complete the repository-root setup first, then from ui/trading : docker network inspect zoltar >/dev/null 2>&1 \\ || docker network create zoltar docker compose up --build --force-recreate Open http://localhost:4163/#/market . On Windows, use ui/trading/start.bat to start Compose.","title":"Set up Trading development","topic":"Trading","weight":0},{"fragment":"external-node","heading":"Test transactions against an external node","keywords":[],"path":"how-to/trading-set-up-development.html","sectionTitle":"How-to guides","summary":"Compile, test, build, and serve the Trading subsystem locally.","text":"To exercise the contracts on a local Anvil node, follow Use local Anvil ; for script and integration tests, Deploy for script and integration tests covers bun run trading:deploy:local .","title":"Set up Trading development","topic":"Trading","weight":0},{"fragment":"commands","heading":"Other commands","keywords":[],"path":"how-to/trading-set-up-development.html","sectionTitle":"How-to guides","summary":"Compile, test, build, and serve the Trading subsystem locally.","text":"See the root package scripts for the Trading check, contract coverage, and gas report commands. After a change that alters what the UI shows, run bun run docs:screenshots -- --app trading to recapture the documentation screenshots.","title":"Set up Trading development","topic":"Trading","weight":0},{"fragment":"","heading":"","keywords":["Trading","UI","simulate","RPC","settings"],"path":"how-to/trading-configure-ui.html","sectionTitle":"How-to guides","summary":"Live and simulation modes; network and RPC selection.","text":"How-to guide","title":"Configure the Trading UI","topic":"Trading","weight":1},{"fragment":"modes","heading":"Choose a mode","keywords":[],"path":"how-to/trading-configure-ui.html","sectionTitle":"How-to guides","summary":"Live and simulation modes; network and RPC selection.","text":"Without a mode parameter, the UI reads live RPC and wallet state. Add ?simulate=1 to run a browser-local chain. A banner identifies simulation mode. Set simScenario to choose a starting state. trading-funded starts with one funded market by default. deployed starts with a question ledger with no trading pool . baseline starts without Statoblast or Trading contracts. trading-forked opens the funded market after its universe forks. trading-usdc opens a funded market using six-decimal USDC and separate router approvals. Set up Trading development to serve the UI locally.","title":"Configure the Trading UI","topic":"Trading","weight":0},{"fragment":"genesis","heading":"Choose the truthful universe","keywords":[],"path":"how-to/trading-configure-ui.html","sectionTitle":"How-to guides","summary":"Live and simulation modes; network and RPC selection.","text":"Choose Yes or No for the displayed Augur v2 fork question. To open that choice directly, add genesis=yes or genesis=no to the URL query, for example ?simulate=1&genesis=yes . The selected universe uses its own REP token and protocol deployment. To change the choice, open Universe under More , follow the selected root's Parent universe link to Augur v2 universe , and open the other child. Returning to the Augur v2 universe clears the previous deployment's market and fork selection.","title":"Configure the Trading UI","topic":"Trading","weight":0},{"fragment":"network","heading":"Select a network and RPC","keywords":[],"path":"how-to/trading-configure-ui.html","sectionTitle":"How-to guides","summary":"Live and simulation modes; network and RPC selection.","text":"Open Settings in the header. Choose the RPC network , and optionally enter a Fallback RPC URL and select Save RPC . A custom RPC must use HTTPS, or HTTP on a loopback address, and must serve the selected chain. Trading uses this endpoint for deployment checks even when a wallet is connected. Once connected on the selected chain, the wallet serves ordinary market reads and submits transactions. The UI then checks the RPC chain and the core contracts, and derives the Trading factory and router addresses for that network. If either is not deployed, the deployment screen opens; Deploy the Trading contracts covers it. The configuration works when the Markets tab opens. The configuration reference describes what the client derives and checks.","title":"Configure the Trading UI","topic":"Trading","weight":0},{"fragment":"","heading":"","keywords":["Trading","deploy","factory","router","Anvil"],"path":"how-to/trading-deploy-contracts.html","sectionTitle":"How-to guides","summary":"Deploy the Trading factory and router from the browser, with the testnet deployer on local Anvil, or as script-only copies for tests.","text":"How-to guide Trading needs one factory and one router at addresses derived from the Statoblast deployment. Use this guide to deploy them from the browser on a network where Statoblast is installed, or with the testnet deployer on a local Anvil node.","title":"Deploy the Trading contracts","topic":"Trading","weight":1},{"fragment":"before-you-start","heading":"Before you start","keywords":[],"path":"how-to/trading-deploy-contracts.html","sectionTitle":"How-to guides","summary":"Deploy the Trading factory and router from the browser, with the testnet deployer on local Anvil, or as script-only copies for tests.","text":"Confirm reviewed Statoblast core code, including its SecurityPoolFactory , for your selected network and Yes or No universe. Use the matching manifest listed in the deployment status reference ; planned addresses do not prove the contracts are live. To install the core on a test network first, follow Deploy testnet contracts .","title":"Deploy the Trading contracts","topic":"Trading","weight":0},{"fragment":"deploy","heading":"Deploy from the browser","keywords":[],"path":"how-to/trading-deploy-contracts.html","sectionTitle":"How-to guides","summary":"Deploy the Trading factory and router from the browser, with the testnet deployer on local Anvil, or as script-only copies for tests.","text":"Serve the Trading UI as Set up Trading development describes, from source or in Docker. Open http://localhost:4163/#/deploy and choose the truthful universe if prompted. To use another network or RPC URL, set it under Settings before connecting. Select Connect wallet . The Trading contracts panel shows the SecurityPoolFactory address and the status of the Trading factory ( TwoWayConstantProductFactory ) and Trading router ( TwoWayConstantProductRouter ). Select Deploy trading factory and confirm, then select Deploy trading router and confirm. Deployment progress reaches 2 / 2 once both contracts and their links are verified. If you leave and return with the same settings, the flow resumes at the first missing contract. A missing prerequisite. The SECURITY POOL FACTORY IS NOT DEPLOYED badge and the disabled deploy buttons mean the selected Statoblast SecurityPoolFactory is not deployed. See the factory reference for the fixed trading fee and Your first Trading market to create a pool.","title":"Deploy the Trading contracts","topic":"Trading","weight":0},{"fragment":"use-local-anvil","heading":"Use local Anvil","keywords":[],"path":"how-to/trading-deploy-contracts.html","sectionTitle":"How-to guides","summary":"Deploy the Trading factory and router from the browser, with the testnet deployer on local Anvil, or as script-only copies for tests.","text":"The UIs do not offer Ethereum mainnet, so start Anvil with Sepolia's chain ID, 11155111 , and use the Sepolia address profile: bun run anvil -- --chain-id 11155111 --hardfork osaka Deploy to the node with the testnet deployer, following Deploy locally with --rpc-url=http://127.0.0.1:8545 and an Anvil account's key. It installs the core and the Trading factory and router. Its final explorer-verification step targets public Sepolia, so a failure there does not mean the local deployment failed. Start the Trading UI as Set up Trading development describes. In Settings , set the Sepolia RPC URL to http://127.0.0.1:8545 . The deploy page reads deployment status through this RPC URL, not through the wallet, even after a wallet connects. In your wallet, point the chain 11155111 network at http://127.0.0.1:8545 and import an Anvil account. The wallet sends the deployment transactions through its own RPC, so a wallet still on public Sepolia would send them to the real network. Connect the wallet in the running Trading UI and open #/deploy . Deployment progress shows 2 / 2 .","title":"Deploy the Trading contracts","topic":"Trading","weight":0},{"fragment":"deploy-script","heading":"Deploy for script and integration tests","keywords":[],"path":"how-to/trading-deploy-contracts.html","sectionTitle":"How-to guides","summary":"Deploy the Trading factory and router from the browser, with the testnet deployer on local Anvil, or as script-only copies for tests.","text":"The local deployment script deploys independent copies at ordinary CREATE addresses. The UI never finds them, because it looks only at the derived addresses; use them from scripts and integration tests only. The script needs a node whose chain ID equals the core manifest's, code at that manifest's SecurityPoolFactory , and an unlocked account on the node. For the Sepolia manifest below, complete step 1 of Use local Anvil first. ZOLTAR_DEPLOYMENT_MANIFEST=docs/sepolia-deployment-addresses.json \\ TRADING_RPC_URL=http://127.0.0.1:8545 \\ bun run trading:deploy:local Find the deployment addresses in solidity/deployments/trading/local.json . See the manifest reference for the output fields.","title":"Deploy the Trading contracts","topic":"Trading","weight":0},{"fragment":"","heading":"","keywords":["Trading","odds","initialize","basis points"],"path":"how-to/trading-seed-alternative-odds.html","sectionTitle":"How-to guides","summary":"Seed a new trading pool at a chosen Conditional Yes price by calling its contract directly.","text":"How-to guide Use this guide when you call the trading pool contract (the pair) yourself and want a new trading pool to open at a specific Conditional Yes price rather than 50%.","title":"Seed alternative conditional odds","topic":"Trading","weight":1},{"fragment":"prerequisites","heading":"Before you call initialize","keywords":[],"path":"how-to/trading-seed-alternative-odds.html","sectionTitle":"How-to guides","summary":"Seed a new trading pool at a chosen Conditional Yes price by calling its contract directly.","text":"Creating and initializing the trading pool in separate transactions lets someone seed it first. Prefer the router’s createPairAndInitializeWithEth for atomic initialization with deposit and liquidity bounds. The first-market tutorial shows the UI path. The question has not ended, its universe has not forked, and the question ledger is Operational and unresolved. The smaller deposit must exceed MINIMUM_LIQUIDITY (1,000 share base units). The pair reference lists the remaining preconditions of initialize .","title":"Seed alternative conditional odds","topic":"Trading","weight":0},{"fragment":"steps","heading":"Seed the trading pool","keywords":[],"path":"how-to/trading-seed-alternative-odds.html","sectionTitle":"How-to guides","summary":"Seed a new trading pool at a chosen Conditional Yes price by calling its contract directly.","text":"Look up the security pool with securityPoolOf(questionLedger) on the question ledger’s factory. Call the security pool’s purchaseQuote(universeId, questionId, cash) . Approve the security pool’s services() to spend the collateral for cash plus the quoted fee, including the resolution premium. Call the security pool’s createCompleteSet(universeId, questionId, cash, recipient, minimumShares, maximumFee) with your address as the recipient. Let q be the Yes share amount minted by this purchase. Create the trading pool with the factory's createPair(questionLedger) , unless it already exists. Choose the Conditional Yes price p in basis points with 0 < p < 10,000 and compute the deposits: If p ≥ 5,000 , deposit all q No and ⌊q × (10,000 − p) / p⌋ Yes. If p < 5,000 , deposit all q Yes and ⌊q × p / (10,000 − p)⌋ No. Approve the trading pool as an operator on the canonical ShareToken with setApprovalForAll ; initialize pulls both deposits from the caller. Call initialize(yesAmount, noAmount, minLiquidity, recipient, deadline) with those deposits. Set minLiquidity to the smaller deposit minus the 1,000 LP base units minted to the lock address. Set deadline to the latest timestamp at which the call may still execute. Check the result: getReserves() returns your two deposits and tradingStatus() returns Open . If initialize reverted with Already initialized , someone else seeded the trading pool first. Conditional pricing works through the 70% example and explains why the larger reserve is the side you are betting against.","title":"Seed alternative conditional odds","topic":"Trading","weight":0},{"fragment":"","heading":"","keywords":["Trading","exit","sell","maximumInsuredExit","Invalid"],"path":"how-to/trading-exit-a-position.html","sectionTitle":"How-to guides","summary":"Sell shares for ETH in the UI or find the largest insured exit with the SDK.","text":"How-to guide Use this guide to turn Yes or No shares back into ETH while the market's trading pool is open. The exit spends one Invalid share for each complete set it redeems, so your Invalid balance limits it. Connect your wallet and find the market in Portfolio . Switch universes if the holding is in another branch.","title":"Exit an insured position","topic":"Trading","weight":1},{"fragment":"in-the-ui","heading":"Exit in the UI","keywords":[],"path":"how-to/trading-exit-a-position.html","sectionTitle":"How-to guides","summary":"Sell shares for ETH in the UI or find the largest insured exit with the SDK.","text":"Open the market, select Sell and the side you hold. Select Max to fill Shares to sell with the largest amount you can sell now, or enter a smaller amount. The hint under the field shows your holding and that limit. Check You receive ≈ , Minimum received , and Invalid used , then select Sell Yes (or Sell No ) and confirm. In a market in a child universe whose complete sets also pay REP, the estimate adds Recovery REP and Minimum recovery REP ; the sale reverts if it would pay less REP than that minimum. An insured Yes exit. The hint under the amount is the limit set by your Invalid, your Yes, and the trading pool's liquidity. If Invalid is the binding limit and you enter more than it covers, the ticket shows Not enough Invalid shares to insure this sale. and offers Sell … instead with the largest amount. The shares you do not sell stay in your wallet. Acquiring more Invalid helps only when Invalid is the binding limit; Why early ETH exits are limited works through the bounds. When the sale confirms, the Invalid balance under Your position has dropped by Invalid used and the ETH is in your wallet.","title":"Exit an insured position","topic":"Trading","weight":0},{"fragment":"through-the-sdk","heading":"Exit through the SDK","keywords":[],"path":"how-to/trading-exit-a-position.html","sectionTitle":"How-to guides","summary":"Sell shares for ETH in the UI or find the largest insured exit with the SDK.","text":"Call maximumInsuredExit from the SDK with your balances, the reserves, and the trading fee. It returns the maximum number of complete sets q you can redeem. Choose maxLongSharesIn , minEthOut , minRepOut (the least recovery REP you accept; zero where complete sets pay none), payoutRecipient , refundRecipient , and deadline explicitly, and encode the request with encodeReceiveRequest . Send a ShareToken safeBatchTransferFrom to the router carrying q Invalid and maxLongSharesIn long shares with the encoded request. Simulate it, simulate again immediately before submission, and submit only if the result still satisfies maxLongSharesIn , minEthOut , and minRepOut . The router reference describes the request constraints and exit outputs.","title":"Exit an insured position","topic":"Trading","weight":0},{"fragment":"","heading":"","keywords":["Trading","liquidity","removeLiquidity","permit"],"path":"how-to/trading-remove-liquidity.html","sectionTitle":"How-to guides","summary":"Withdraw an LP position as raw Yes and No in any lifecycle phase.","text":"How-to guide Removing liquidity from a trading pool returns Yes and No shares, including after question end, finalization, or a fork.","title":"Remove liquidity","topic":"Trading","weight":1},{"fragment":"in-the-ui","heading":"Remove in the UI","keywords":[],"path":"how-to/trading-remove-liquidity.html","sectionTitle":"How-to guides","summary":"Withdraw an LP position as raw Yes and No in any lifecycle phase.","text":"Open the market and select the Liquidity tab, then Remove . Enter the LP tokens to remove in Amount . You receive ≈ shows the Yes and No you get back. Select Remove liquidity and confirm. The transaction reverts if it would return less than the estimate allows after your slippage tolerance. Removing liquidity. The UI calls the trading pool directly, so it never asks for a router allowance.","title":"Remove liquidity","topic":"Trading","weight":0},{"fragment":"through-the-pair","heading":"Remove through the trading pool contract","keywords":[],"path":"how-to/trading-remove-liquidity.html","sectionTitle":"How-to guides","summary":"Withdraw an LP position as raw Yes and No in any lifecycle phase.","text":"Compute the outputs from getEffectiveReserves() : each reserve × liquidity ÷ LP total supply, rounded down. Removal uses current balances, so donated shares count. Call the trading pool's removeLiquidity(liquidity, minYes, minNo, recipient, deadline) with minYes and minNo set from those outputs and your tolerance. The deadline is enforced on chain. After either path, your LP balance has fallen by liquidity , your Yes and No balances have risen by the outputs, and the trading pool has emitted LiquidityRemoved . Integrations that need router-based removal call the router's removeLiquidityWithPermit , whose bounds are named minYesOut and minNoOut . The router reference gives the values the permit must sign and the allowance fallback. Never grant a permanent or infinite allowance.","title":"Remove liquidity","topic":"Trading","weight":0},{"fragment":"what-removal-does-not-do","heading":"Use the returned shares","keywords":[],"path":"how-to/trading-remove-liquidity.html","sectionTitle":"How-to guides","summary":"Withdraw an LP position as raw Yes and No in any lifecycle phase.","text":"See Handle resolution to redeem eligible shares or Handle a universe fork to migrate them.","title":"Remove liquidity","topic":"Trading","weight":0},{"fragment":"","heading":"","keywords":["Trading","resolution","settlement","redeemShares","Invalid"],"path":"how-to/trading-handle-resolution.html","sectionTitle":"How-to guides","summary":"Unwind LP and redeem complete sets after question end, then claim winning shares once the market finalizes.","text":"How-to guide A trading pool closes to swaps and added liquidity when its question ends; the market finalizes later, when its outcome is final. Use this guide from question end onward to turn LP tokens, complete sets, and winning shares into their redeemable assets.","title":"Handle resolution","topic":"Trading","weight":1},{"fragment":"steps","heading":"Unwind after question end","keywords":[],"path":"how-to/trading-handle-resolution.html","sectionTitle":"How-to guides","summary":"Unwind LP and redeem complete sets after question end, then claim winning shares once the market finalizes.","text":"Remove your LP tokens from the trading pool into Yes and No shares ( Remove liquidity ). Redeem matching Invalid, Yes, and No as complete sets while the question ledger is Operational and its universe has not forked. If the universe forks while the question is unresolved, follow Handle a universe fork . After the market finalizes, redeem the winning share through QuestionLedger.redeemShares , which requires the question ledger to be Operational . Keep redeeming through this question ledger if its universe forks later; resolved shares cannot migrate. On an Invalid result, Invalid is the only winning share. A redemption burns the shares and pays ETH at the question ledger’s current collateral rate. A shared security pool child can also pay recovery REP , even when its ETH payout is zero. Redemption requires neither a new report nor an auction buyer.","title":"Handle resolution","topic":"Trading","weight":0},{"fragment":"in-the-ui","heading":"In the UI","keywords":[],"path":"how-to/trading-handle-resolution.html","sectionTitle":"How-to guides","summary":"Unwind LP and redeem complete sets after question end, then claim winning shares once the market finalizes.","text":"Open the market. A closed market opens on its Settlement tab. To redeem complete sets, select Redeem sets , enter an amount in ETH to redeem (or Complete sets to redeem when the claim includes REP), check the ETH estimate and any Recovery REP , and select Redeem complete sets . Once the market finalizes, a Redeem Yes , Redeem No , or Redeem Invalid option appears for the winning outcome. It redeems your entire balance of that share through this exact question ledger. Before finalization the option is absent. Settlement after the question ends. The market has not finalized yet, so only complete-set redemption and fork migration are offered. Check both asset balances in the selected universe after confirmation. Portfolio shows recovery REP separately from ETH.","title":"Handle resolution","topic":"Trading","weight":0},{"fragment":"","heading":"","keywords":["Trading","fork","migrate","ShareToken"],"path":"how-to/trading-handle-a-fork.html","sectionTitle":"How-to guides","summary":"Migrate parent shares into chosen child branches and open a child trading pool.","text":"How-to guide A fork closes the parent trading pool for swaps and additions. Holders choose where to migrate their shares.","title":"Handle a universe fork","topic":"Trading","weight":1},{"fragment":"steps","heading":"Check the position","keywords":[],"path":"how-to/trading-handle-a-fork.html","sectionTitle":"How-to guides","summary":"Migrate parent shares into chosen child branches and open a child trading pool.","text":"If the question has finalized, redeem winning shares through its question ledger. For an unresolved question, remove parent LP into Yes and No before migrating it. Migration copies the selected source token’s balance to each child you choose, less any shares you already migrated there. At least one child must receive additional shares. The parent balance stays visible and is permanently locked against outgoing transfers.","title":"Handle a universe fork","topic":"Trading","weight":0},{"fragment":"in-the-ui","heading":"Migrate in the UI","keywords":[],"path":"how-to/trading-handle-a-fork.html","sectionTitle":"How-to guides","summary":"Migrate parent shares into chosen child branches and open a child trading pool.","text":"Open the market’s Settlement tab and select Migrate . Choose the Share to migrate (Invalid, Yes, or No), then choose your child universes . Tick the balance and transfer-lock acknowledgment. Select Migrate to N child universes and confirm. Check the expected child token IDs and balances. Repeat for each source token you want to migrate.","title":"Handle a universe fork","topic":"Trading","weight":0},{"fragment":"choosing-targets","heading":"Choose fork targets","keywords":[],"path":"how-to/trading-handle-a-fork.html","sectionTitle":"How-to guides","summary":"Migrate parent shares into chosen child branches and open a child trading pool.","text":"The fork outcome identifies the child universe. Your migrated share keeps its original outcome. Select Invalid or a labeled answer for a categorical fork, or Invalid or an exact tick for a scalar fork. One child with a ledger. Select it directly. One child without a ledger. Activate the child’s security pool branch first. Until then migration is blocked ( Fork branch not active ). A single migration can create the canonical child question ledger after activation. In Statoblast’s Fork choices and recovery , prepare the branch, start it after the eight-week fork-choice window closes, and activate it after the recapitalization deadline. Each step is a separate transaction. Multiple children. Each must already have a canonical question ledger. Migrate to each missing ledger in its own call. A token locked by an earlier migration can still migrate to a new child. To migrate directly, call ShareToken.migrate(fromId, targetOutcomeIndexes) for each source token ID with strictly increasing target outcome indexes. A child can open a trading pool only if its question ledger returns to Operational before question end. To recover collateral without trading, follow Handle resolution . An eligible complete set or winning share can pay ETH and REP together or REP alone, without a trading pool or auction bid. Forks and resolution explains why the trading pool behaves this way.","title":"Handle a universe fork","topic":"Trading","weight":0},{"fragment":"","heading":"","keywords":["Trading","constant product","complete set","Yes","No"],"path":"explanation/trading/two-way-market.html","sectionTitle":"Explanations","summary":"One ETH entry as a complete set, a swap, and Invalid insurance.","text":"Explanation Trading is the two-reserve constant-product design from How To Build: Augur Constant Product with Invalid Insurance , adapted to Statoblast. This page explains what happens inside one entry.","title":"The two-way market","topic":"Trading","weight":1},{"fragment":"mental-model","heading":"Mental model","keywords":[],"path":"explanation/trading/two-way-market.html","sectionTitle":"Explanations","summary":"One ETH entry as a complete set, a swap, and Invalid insurance.","text":"A question ledger exposes the outcome shares for one question and universe. Creating a complete set mints equal Invalid, Yes, and No shares. Redeeming a set burns those shares and returns collateral at the question ledger’s current rate. The pair holds Yes and No reserves. The router combines complete-set creation or redemption with a swap, then delivers the resulting assets to the user. The pair’s Invalid balance stays zero: shareToken.balanceOf ( pair , invalidTokenId ) = 0 Swaps and added liquidity are open only while the market is live; Forks and resolution lists what closes a pair. Parent and child question ledgers are distinct markets and therefore use distinct pairs.","title":"The two-way market","topic":"Trading","weight":0},{"fragment":"one-entry","heading":"One entry, step by step","keywords":[],"path":"explanation/trading/two-way-market.html","sectionTitle":"Explanations","summary":"One ETH entry as a complete set, a swap, and Invalid insurance.","text":"The pair maintains k = Yes reserve × No reserve . Entering Yes looks like this; entering No is the mirror image. User → Router: enterPosition(pair, YES) with ETH Router → Security pool: approve collateral and purchase complete sets Question ledger → Router: q Invalid + q Yes + q No Router → Pair: swap q No for Yes Pair → Router: additional Yes Router → User: q Invalid + total Yes Entering a Yes position. The trader ends up holding the Invalid shares from the complete set; the pair never sees them. Suppose the reserves are 1,000 Yes and 1,000 No, the fee is zero, and the trader mints 100 complete sets. Swapping 100 No yields about 90.909 Yes. The wallet receives about 190.909 Yes and exactly 100 Invalid; the reserves become about 909.091 Yes and exactly 1,100 No. See units and rounding for raw precision. A No purchase simply reverses the reserves. No position record is created anywhere: wallet Yes, No, Invalid, and LP balances are the complete accounting state.","title":"The two-way market","topic":"Trading","weight":0},{"fragment":"","heading":"","keywords":["Trading","Invalid","insurance"],"path":"explanation/trading/invalid-insurance.html","sectionTitle":"Explanations","summary":"Why Invalid is held rather than traded and what it pays.","text":"Explanation An Invalid share can pay out when neither directional outcome wins.","title":"Invalid insurance","topic":"Trading","weight":1},{"fragment":"how-it-works","heading":"How it works","keywords":[],"path":"explanation/trading/invalid-insurance.html","sectionTitle":"Explanations","summary":"Why Invalid is held rather than traded and what it pays.","text":"Each ordinary ETH entry returns one Invalid share for each complete set created. If the question resolves Invalid, the holder redeems those shares with the question ledger 's redeemShares() at the current collateral value. The two-reserve design keeps Invalid in the holder’s wallet. The pair therefore supplies no price for Invalid that a trader could use to judge the risk of that result.","title":"Invalid insurance","topic":"Trading","weight":0},{"fragment":"worked-example","heading":"A worked Invalid result","keywords":[],"path":"explanation/trading/invalid-insurance.html","sectionTitle":"Explanations","summary":"Why Invalid is held rather than traded and what it pays.","text":"Alice entered with enough ETH to mint 100 complete-set shares, received 190 Yes after her swap, and kept 100 Invalid. The market resolves Invalid. The pair's Yes and No balances do not become valuable merely because they are nonzero. Alice redeems her 100 winning Invalid shares through the question ledger. Because of protocol holding fees , the refund is the current net collateral value, not necessarily her original gross ETH. Liquidity providers hold Invalid shares separately in exactly the same way. Selling or transferring LP tokens never moves that insurance.","title":"Invalid insurance","topic":"Trading","weight":0},{"fragment":"","heading":"","keywords":["Trading","price","conditional","oracle"],"path":"explanation/trading/pricing.html","sectionTitle":"Explanations","summary":"What Conditional Yes and No mean and why the spot price is not an oracle.","text":"Explanation A two-reserve pair produces exactly two prices, and both are conditional on the market resolving validly.","title":"Conditional pricing","topic":"Trading","weight":1},{"fragment":"formula","heading":"What the prices are","keywords":[],"path":"explanation/trading/pricing.html","sectionTitle":"Explanations","summary":"What Conditional Yes and No mean and why the spot price is not an oracle.","text":"The constant-product marginal relationship prices one outcome using the opposite reserve: conditionalYes = noReserve yesReserve + noReserve conditionalNo = yesReserve yesReserve + noReserve The two values sum to 100% because they partition the valid outcomes only. That does not imply Invalid has zero probability; there is no unconditional Yes or No probability in this AMM. At 428.571 Yes and 1,000 No, Conditional Yes is about 70% and Conditional No about 30%. The larger No reserve makes Yes the more expensive side, which is why initializing at 70% Conditional Yes deposits more No than Yes.","title":"Conditional pricing","topic":"Trading","weight":0},{"fragment":"not-an-oracle","heading":"Not an oracle","keywords":[],"path":"explanation/trading/pricing.html","sectionTitle":"Explanations","summary":"What Conditional Yes and No mean and why the spot price is not an oracle.","text":"Reserve spot values can be moved within a block, donated to, sandwiched, or go stale before inclusion. Protocol logic must never consume this price as an oracle; Trading includes no TWAP.","title":"Conditional pricing","topic":"Trading","weight":0},{"fragment":"","heading":"","keywords":["Trading","LP","liquidity","minimum liquidity"],"path":"explanation/trading/liquidity-provider-position.html","sectionTitle":"Explanations","summary":"How LP supply is scaled and what an LP token claims.","text":"Explanation An LP token represents a proportional claim on a pair ’s Yes and No reserves.","title":"The liquidity-provider position","topic":"Trading","weight":1},{"fragment":"supply","heading":"How LP supply is scaled","keywords":[],"path":"explanation/trading/liquidity-provider-position.html","sectionTitle":"Explanations","summary":"How LP supply is scaled and what an LP token claims.","text":"Initial supply uses the smaller reserve, avoiding a geometric-mean multiplication that can overflow at raw share scales. After a permanent minimum lock , the initializer receives the remaining LP tokens. Ownership depends on the share of total LP supply. LP → Router: addLiquidityWithEth with ETH Router → Security pool: approve collateral and purchase complete sets Question ledger → Router: equal Invalid, Yes, No Router → Pair: add largest proportional Yes + No Pair → LP: LP tokens Router → LP: all Invalid + unused Yes/No Adding liquidity with ETH. The router returns any shares that the pair does not take.","title":"The liquidity-provider position","topic":"Trading","weight":0},{"fragment":"what-it-claims","heading":"What the token claims","keywords":[],"path":"explanation/trading/liquidity-provider-position.html","sectionTitle":"Explanations","summary":"How LP supply is scaled and what an LP token claims.","text":"A holder of wallet LP / total LP can withdraw that fraction of both reserves as raw Yes and No shares at any time, including after trading closes. Swap fees are taken from the input and stay in the reserves, and direct Yes or No donations immediately increase reserves, so both accrue to existing LPs. Trades change the mix of Yes and No. Neither share redeems on an Invalid result. The provider holds Invalid insurance separately from the LP token.","title":"The liquidity-provider position","topic":"Trading","weight":0},{"fragment":"","heading":"","keywords":["Trading","exit","bounds","Invalid"],"path":"explanation/trading/early-exit-limit.html","sectionTitle":"Explanations","summary":"The three bounds on redeeming complete sets from a directional wallet.","text":"Explanation Turning a directional position back into ETH means rebuilding complete sets. Three wallet and pair quantities cap how many you can rebuild.","title":"Why early ETH exits are limited","topic":"Trading","weight":1},{"fragment":"bounds","heading":"The three bounds","keywords":[],"path":"explanation/trading/early-exit-limit.html","sectionTitle":"Explanations","summary":"The three bounds on redeeming complete sets from a directional wallet.","text":"An insured exit of q complete sets from a wallet holding long Yes shares needs q Invalid shares, q Yes shares for the sets, and extra Yes shares to buy exactly q No shares from the pair : q ≤ wallet Invalid q + required Yes input(q) ≤ wallet Yes q < pair No reserve All three bounds presume a live market. The swap closes with the pair, and the question ledger redeems complete sets only while operational. See what closes a pair . The exit starts with a share transfer to the router . The SDK finds an exit amount within the bounds.","title":"Why early ETH exits are limited","topic":"Trading","weight":0},{"fragment":"example","heading":"Example","keywords":[],"path":"explanation/trading/early-exit-limit.html","sectionTitle":"Explanations","summary":"The three bounds on redeeming complete sets from a directional wallet.","text":"A wallet holds 150 Invalid and 260 Yes shares. If buying 100 No requires 112 Yes, redeeming 100 sets needs 212 Yes and succeeds. Trying 150 might require 181 extra Yes, or 331 in total, and fails on Yes even though Invalid would cover it. If the wallet instead had only 60 Invalid, at most 60 sets are insured. Whatever Yes cannot be insured remains a valid directional asset; it has not disappeared.","title":"Why early ETH exits are limited","topic":"Trading","weight":0},{"fragment":"","heading":"","keywords":["Trading","fork","resolution","lifecycle"],"path":"explanation/trading/forks-and-resolution.html","sectionTitle":"Explanations","summary":"What closes a pair and what a holder does after a fork or resolution.","text":"Explanation When trading closes, holders can still withdraw their Yes and No reserves. What they can do with those shares depends on whether the question resolved or its universe forked.","title":"Forks and resolution in Trading","topic":"Trading","weight":1},{"fragment":"what-closes","heading":"What closes a pair","keywords":[],"path":"explanation/trading/forks-and-resolution.html","sectionTitle":"Explanations","summary":"What closes a pair and what a holder does after a fork or resolution.","text":"Swaps, initialization, and added liquidity close permanently at question end, when the universe forks, or when the QuestionForker exposes a final outcome . They are also blocked while the question ledger is not operational or is awaiting fork continuation . LP removal deliberately has none of those guards. tradingStatus() reports which condition applies.","title":"Forks and resolution in Trading","topic":"Trading","weight":0},{"fragment":"after-a-fork","heading":"After a fork","keywords":[],"path":"explanation/trading/forks-and-resolution.html","sectionTitle":"Explanations","summary":"What closes a pair and what a holder does after a fork or resolution.","text":"Withdrawing parent liquidity returns parent shares. Holders choose which child branches to migrate their shares into and supply liquidity to distinct child pairs. A child pair can open only if the question ledger returns to Operational before the question ends. A fork over this question occurs after its end, so only a fork over a different question can leave time for child trading. See Handle a universe fork for the steps.","title":"Forks and resolution in Trading","topic":"Trading","weight":0},{"fragment":"after-resolution","heading":"After resolution","keywords":[],"path":"explanation/trading/forks-and-resolution.html","sectionTitle":"Explanations","summary":"What closes a pair and what a holder does after a fork or resolution.","text":"Settlement takes place at the question ledger. The pair only returns Yes and No shares, which can redeem there if they win. On an Invalid result, only the separately held Invalid shares redeem, so fee growth in the pair adds no redemption value. See Handle resolution for the steps.","title":"Forks and resolution in Trading","topic":"Trading","weight":0},{"fragment":"","heading":"","keywords":["Trading","security","permit","reentrancy","reporting"],"path":"reference/trading/security-model.html","sectionTitle":"Reference","summary":"Authorization, custody, assumptions, known risks, and vulnerability reporting.","text":"Reference This page states what the contracts defend against, what they assume, and what they leave to the user.","title":"Trading security model","topic":"Trading","weight":1},{"fragment":"authorization","heading":"Signature and callback authorization","keywords":[],"path":"reference/trading/security-model.html","sectionTitle":"Reference","summary":"Authorization, custody, assumptions, known risks, and vulnerability reporting.","text":"LP permits use the standard ERC-2612 nonce, deadline, chain-bound EIP-712 domain, owner, spender, and exact value. The LP ledger is the repository's vendored ERC20.sol , sourced from the MIT-licensed OpenZeppelin Contracts v5.2.0 release . TradingLiquidityToken.sol adapts the MIT-licensed v5.2.0 ERC20Permit by using the repository's shared OpenZeppelin-derived EIP-712/ECDSA primitive, a fixed version 1 , and the stable Zoltar Two-Way LP domain name. It omits Nonces , IERC20Permit , and EIP712 inheritance only because their exact storage and hashing behavior is implemented directly by the local nonce mapping and shared authorization primitive. No signature or curve algorithm is reimplemented in the pair. OpenZeppelin ERC-20 prohibits minting to the zero address. The pair therefore locks MINIMUM_LIQUIDITY at MINIMUM_LIQUIDITY_LOCK , address(1) , which no key controls, instead of treating the zero address as an LP holder. Only pair operations can invoke the inherited internal mint and burn functions. A signature is authorization, not transaction privacy: integrations should use short deadlines and exact amounts. The router reference gives the allowance alternative for signers without ECDSA permits. Receive-based ERC-1155 operations use no off-chain signature. The ShareToken call must be submitted by the owner, while the versioned payload binds all contract identities, operation inputs, recipients, slippage, and deadline. Copying an observed payload cannot redirect assets and cannot separately consume an allowance because the router requires operator == from .","title":"Trading security model","topic":"Trading","weight":0},{"fragment":"custody","heading":"Custody and reentrancy","keywords":[],"path":"reference/trading/security-model.html","sectionTitle":"Reference","summary":"Authorization, custody, assumptions, known risks, and vulnerability reporting.","text":"The pair rejects foreign ShareTokens, Invalid shares, and noncanonical universe IDs in both single and batch callbacks. A reentrancy lock covers every reserve-changing operation and every LP mint or burn; LP transfer , approve , and permit change no reserves and are not locked. Recipient callbacks can execute arbitrary code, so final reserves are read from authoritative balances after transfers. There is no persistent reserve cache. Operations check their resulting balances against the amounts calculated from their starting balances. Liquidity removal transfers both nonzero outputs in one batch, so the recipient callback sees both debits. Valid Yes and No donations accrue to current LPs. The router recognizes pairs only through its immutable factory, opens callback state only around one expected question ledger and share token, and restores its starting share balances. Its ETH receiver opens only around expected redemption callbacks from that question ledger or its canonical settlement engine, and around the WETH wrapper’s withdrawal callback when redeemed WETH is unwrapped. This prevents unsolicited callbacks and keeps forced ETH separate from exit proceeds. Factory checks prevent a malicious question ledger from borrowing another question's ShareToken or universe identity. CREATE2 plus one immutable fee gives one canonical pair per exact question ledger without privileged replacement. Because an ERC-1155 transfer to an address without code cannot invoke a receiver callback, an attacker can send shares to a predicted CREATE2 address before deployment. The factory's predeployment quarantine removes those canonical balances during pair construction, so initialization starts with zero canonical balances, including zero Invalid. Foreign token contracts cannot be enumerated and may still assign irrelevant balances to the address; the pair never recognizes or moves them.","title":"Trading security model","topic":"Trading","weight":0},{"fragment":"assumptions","heading":"Assumptions","keywords":[],"path":"reference/trading/security-model.html","sectionTitle":"Reference","summary":"Authorization, custody, assumptions, known risks, and vulnerability reporting.","text":"The configured Statoblast SecurityPoolFactory , question ledger, ShareToken , question forker , question data, and universe state are canonical and behave as implemented in the matching core release. Pair registration authenticates the ledger within that factory. Verify the intended network and Yes or No genesis deployment under A23 . Wallets and RPC endpoints accurately report account, chain, balances, approvals, and receipts, as described in A24 .","title":"Trading security model","topic":"Trading","weight":0},{"fragment":"known-risks","heading":"Known risks","keywords":[],"path":"reference/trading/security-model.html","sectionTitle":"Reference","summary":"Authorization, custody, assumptions, known risks, and vulnerability reporting.","text":"Before signing, set a limit on what you will pay or accept, choose the latest time the trade is still useful, simulate the transaction and record the block hash. For an insured exit , check the minimum collateral ( minEthOut ) and any minimum recovery REP ( minRepOut ) separately. Spot prices are not an oracle and expose users to front-running and sandwiches. A lifecycle transition, fork, resolution, reserve donation, holding-fee refresh, or intervening trade can invalidate a quote; a changed quote can execute within the signed bounds, while a breached minimum reverts the transaction. Initial liquidity determines the starting conditional price and should be initialized atomically. LP tokens cover only Yes/No reserves; separately held Invalid can be lost, transferred, or insufficient. A valid-resolution LP can suffer directional loss, and Yes/No reserves may have no redemption value after an Invalid result. Other risks include recipient rejection of ETH or ERC-1155 transfers, core-contract bugs, and approval misuse. The immutable factory fee is a deployment choice, not a claim of economic optimality.","title":"Trading security model","topic":"Trading","weight":0},{"fragment":"reporting","heading":"Reporting a vulnerability","keywords":[],"path":"reference/trading/security-model.html","sectionTitle":"Reference","summary":"Authorization, custody, assumptions, known risks, and vulnerability reporting.","text":"Report a suspected vulnerability through GitHub private vulnerability reporting . Include the affected commit, contracts and functions, prerequisites, a minimal reproduction, expected impact, and whether funds or deployment keys may be at immediate risk. If private reporting is unavailable, open a public issue asking maintainers to establish a private channel, but do not include vulnerability details. Do not exploit a live deployment or publish details before maintainers have had a reasonable opportunity to respond.","title":"Trading security model","topic":"Trading","weight":0},{"fragment":"","heading":"","keywords":["Trading","design","limitations","non-goals"],"path":"explanation/trading/architecture-decisions.html","sectionTitle":"Explanations","summary":"The seven design decisions and the features the MVP leaves out.","text":"Explanation Trading uses a small, immutable pair. Each design choice keeps market identity, collateral accounting, or migration explicit.","title":"Trading architecture decisions and limitations","topic":"Trading","weight":1},{"fragment":"decisions","heading":"Decisions","keywords":[],"path":"explanation/trading/architecture-decisions.html","sectionTitle":"Explanations","summary":"The seven design decisions and the features the MVP leaves out.","text":"Two reserves only. A pair holding Yes × No shares avoids the three-way quadratic and does not price Invalid . Exact question ledger identity. A pair is keyed by the question ledger address, so parent and child branch question ledgers always map to different pairs even when they share one ShareToken . The factory's canonical validation stops a question ledger from borrowing another market's identity. One immutable fee per factory. There is no owner, protocol fee switch, or mutable parameter. Balance-delta integration. The router measures complete-set creation and redemption by observed share and ETH balance deltas because holding fees change the ETH-to-share rate; it never assumes a fixed ratio. Its custody checks are listed in the router reference. Minimum-reserve LP scale. Initial LP supply follows the smaller reserve rather than a geometric mean, which would overflow at large share scales; only proportional ownership matters. The liquidity-provider position gives the rule. Donations accrue to LPs. Reserve-dependent operations read actual ShareToken balances, so unsolicited Yes or No shares benefit existing LPs instead of the next trader. The security model covers the related custody defenses. No automatic fork behavior. Parent liquidity is removed as parent shares. The user explicitly chooses migration targets and initializes distinct child pairs.","title":"Trading architecture decisions and limitations","topic":"Trading","weight":0},{"fragment":"limitations","heading":"Limitations and non-goals","keywords":[],"path":"explanation/trading/architecture-decisions.html","sectionTitle":"Explanations","summary":"The seven design decisions and the features the MVP leaves out.","text":"The Trading security model lists its defenses and assumptions.","title":"Trading architecture decisions and limitations","topic":"Trading","weight":0},{"fragment":"","heading":"","keywords":["Trading","factory","pair","tradingStatus","CREATE2"],"path":"reference/trading/contracts.html","sectionTitle":"Reference","summary":"Factory, pair, math library, and predeployment sink constructors, entrypoints, and revert conditions.","text":"Reference All Trading contracts compile with Solidity 0.8.35 for Osaka. Revert strings are operational diagnostics, not stable compatibility commitments.","title":"Trading contracts","topic":"Trading","weight":1},{"fragment":"factory","heading":"TwoWayConstantProductFactory","keywords":[],"path":"reference/trading/contracts.html","sectionTitle":"Reference","summary":"Factory, pair, math library, and predeployment sink constructors, entrypoints, and revert conditions.","text":"constructor(ISecurityPoolFactory, uint256 feeBps) fixes the core factory and fee; feeBps < 10,000 . createPair(IQuestionLedger) validates the exact canonical question ledger, deploys with CREATE2, and returns the existing canonical pair on duplicate calls. getPair(IQuestionLedger) → pair maps exact question ledger identity to a pair. isPair(address) → bool recognizes router-eligible pairs. predictPair(IQuestionLedger) → address calculates the CREATE2 address. securityPoolFactory() , feeBps() , and predeploymentShareSink() expose immutable configuration. Canonical validation requires the question ledger's own securityPoolFactory , the factory's (originId, universeId) lookup, and shareToken.canonicalQuestionLedgerByUniverse(universeId) all to agree. CREATE2 pair addresses exist counterfactually before code is deployed, when ERC-1155 receiver checks cannot run. Each factory therefore deploys an ownerless PredeploymentShareSink . A pair constructor transfers any canonical Invalid, Yes, or No already present at its future address into that sink before returning. Those quarantined shares are permanently inaccessible; do not send shares to a predicted pair address.","title":"Trading contracts","topic":"Trading","weight":0},{"fragment":"pair","heading":"TwoWayConstantProductPair","keywords":[],"path":"reference/trading/contracts.html","sectionTitle":"Reference","summary":"Factory, pair, math library, and predeployment sink constructors, entrypoints, and revert conditions.","text":"Immutable accessors expose factory , questionLedger , shareToken , universeId , questionId , invalidTokenId , yesTokenId , noTokenId , and feeBps . Constants are MINIMUM_LIQUIDITY (1,000) and MINIMUM_LIQUIDITY_LOCK ( address(1) ), which holds the locked minimum liquidity. LP ERC-20 accessors are name , symbol , decimals , totalSupply , balanceOf , allowance , approve , transfer , and transferFrom . ERC-2612 accessors are permit(owner, spender, value, deadline, v, r, s) , nonces(owner) , and DOMAIN_SEPARATOR() . initialize , addLiquidity , removeLiquidity , swapExactInput , and swapExactOutput revert with Deadline expired when block.timestamp > deadline . constructor(factory, questionLedger, feeBps, predeploymentShareSink) is called only by the factory. It requires nonzero addresses and feeBps < 10,000 , reads the ShareToken, universe, question, and token IDs from the question ledger, and quarantines any canonical shares already at its address, emitting PredeploymentSharesQuarantined . getReserves() reads the current Yes and No balances directly from ShareToken. Reserves are not cached in pair storage. getEffectiveReserves() returns the pair's current Yes and No balances, including donations, and reverts if the pair holds Invalid shares. The router's enterPosition reads it for the before-trade Conditional Yes price. tradingStatus() returns the first matching lifecycle status . initialize(yesAmount, noAmount, minLiquidity, recipient, deadline) pulls the first reserves, locks MINIMUM_LIQUIDITY , and mints the remainder once. It requires an uninitialized pair ( Already initialized ), a nonzero recipient, zero Invalid, Yes, and No balances at the pair ( Nonzero initial balance ), min(yesAmount, noAmount) > MINIMUM_LIQUIDITY ( Initial liquidity too small ), and minted liquidity of at least minLiquidity ( Minimum liquidity ). addLiquidity(maxYes, maxNo, minLiquidity, recipient, deadline) reads current balances, including donations, selects the largest proportional amounts, pulls them, and mints LP. It reverts with Liquidity rounds to zero when either used amount is zero and with Minimum liquidity when the minted LP is zero or below minLiquidity . removeLiquidity(liquidity, minYes, minNo, recipient, deadline) burns caller LP and returns proportional raw shares. It has no lifecycle-close guard. It reverts with Liquidity slippage below minYes or minNo and with Liquidity output is zero when both outputs round to zero. When only one output rounds to zero, that transfer is skipped and the removal succeeds, so a dust LP amount can still exit. swapExactInput(yesForNo, amountIn, minAmountOut, recipient, deadline) pulls the gross input and sends the floor-rounded output; it reverts with Swap slippage below minAmountOut . swapExactOutput(yesForNo, amountOut, maxAmountIn, recipient, deadline) pulls the ceil-rounded required input and sends the exact output; it reverts with Swap slippage above maxAmountIn . quoteExactInput(yesForNo, amountIn) → (amountOut, feeAmount) and quoteExactOutput(yesForNo, amountOut) → (amountIn, feeAmount) quote against effective reserves. sync() emits current balances in a Sync event. It reverts when the pair is uninitialized or holds Invalid shares. onERC1155Received , onERC1155BatchReceived , and supportsInterface implement receiver discovery. Only the canonical ShareToken 's exact Yes/No IDs are accepted. Pre-initialization transfers must be initiated by the pair's own pull. initialize , addLiquidity , and both swaps require an open lifecycle and otherwise revert with Question ended , Universe forked , Fork continuation pending , Pool inactive , or Question resolved ; all but initialize also revert with Uninitialized before the first deposit. tradingStatus() is a concise action blocker, not an authoritative settlement summary. The first matching condition wins in this order: Uninitialized , UniverseForked , AwaitingForkContinuation , PoolInactive , QuestionResolved , QuestionEnded , then Open . An uninitialized forked pair therefore reports Uninitialized , and a finalized question reports QuestionResolved even after its end time. Read QuestionForker.getQuestionOutcome(questionLedger) when the finalized outcome itself is required.","title":"Trading contracts","topic":"Trading","weight":0},{"fragment":"sink","heading":"PredeploymentShareSink","keywords":[],"path":"reference/trading/contracts.html","sectionTitle":"Reference","summary":"Factory, pair, math library, and predeployment sink constructors, entrypoints, and revert conditions.","text":"Accepts ERC-1155 transfers and has no withdrawal or administrative method. It exists only to preserve clean pair initialization and the zero-Invalid invariant after counterfactual-address poisoning attempts.","title":"Trading contracts","topic":"Trading","weight":0},{"fragment":"math","heading":"TwoWayConstantProductMath","keywords":[],"path":"reference/trading/contracts.html","sectionTitle":"Reference","summary":"Factory, pair, math library, and predeployment sink constructors, entrypoints, and revert conditions.","text":"The internal library provides exact-input and exact-output quotes, ceil rounding through the repository's full-precision Math.mulDiv , proportional deposits, initial odds, and conditional price basis points. Production paths never multiply reserve products. Rounding directions are listed in Units and rounding .","title":"Trading contracts","topic":"Trading","weight":0},{"fragment":"","heading":"","keywords":["Trading","router","enterPosition","receive request","permit"],"path":"reference/trading/router.html","sectionTitle":"Reference","summary":"Router entrypoints, custody guarantees, and approval-free share operations.","text":"Reference Trading deploys one canonical factory and one canonical router. The router handles entry and liquidity operations, share-transfer exits and complete-set redemption, and permit-assisted LP removal.","title":"Trading router","topic":"Trading","weight":1},{"fragment":"entrypoints","heading":"Entrypoints","keywords":[],"path":"reference/trading/router.html","sectionTitle":"Reference","summary":"Router entrypoints, custody guarantees, and approval-free share operations.","text":"TwoWayConstantProductRouter(factory) fixes the only eligible factory. All user mutations enforce a deadline and a reentrancy guard. The router's receiver callbacks accept only the active question ledger's canonical share token and universe IDs. receive() accepts ETH only while an expected callback is active: from the selected question ledger, its canonical security pool settlement engine, or the WETH wrapper during the router’s withdrawal of redeemed WETH. enterPosition(pair, mintAttoEth, longOutcome, minLongSharesOut, recipient, deadline) is payable. It mints mintAttoEth of complete sets from the fee-inclusive msg.value budget, refunds excess ETH, verifies equal Invalid/Yes/No deltas, swaps all opposite shares, and delivers long shares plus Invalid. It reverts with Minimum long shares below minLongSharesOut . It returns EnterResult : ethSpent , completeSetShares , oppositeSharesSwapped , additionalLongShares , totalLongShares , invalidInsurance , feeAmount , conditionalYesBpsBefore , and conditionalYesBpsAfter . createPairAndInitializeWithEth(pool, mintAttoEth, conditionalYesBpsValue, maxYesUsed, maxNoUsed, minYesUsed, minNoUsed, minLiquidity, recipient, deadline) creates or reuses the deterministic pair. An uninitialized pair is seeded atomically at a Conditional Yes price strictly between 0 and 10,000 basis points. If anyone has already initialized the pair, the call instead adds liquidity at the pair's price exactly as addLiquidityWithEth does, so it reverts only on the caller's own bounds. Both initialization paths enforce minYesUsed ≤ yesUsed ≤ maxYesUsed and minNoUsed ≤ noUsed ≤ maxNoUsed , reverting with Liquidity price slippage outside these bounds. Set both lower and upper bounds from the deposits previewed at your price and your slippage tolerance; minimum LP output alone does not protect the initial price. initializeWithEth(pair, mintAttoEth, conditionalYesBpsValue, maxYesUsed, maxNoUsed, minYesUsed, minNoUsed, minLiquidity, recipient, deadline) does the same for an already created canonical pair. addLiquidityWithEth(pair, mintAttoEth, maxYesUsed, maxNoUsed, minLiquidity, recipient, deadline) creates complete sets, deposits the authoritative proportional maximum, and returns Invalid and unused shares. It reverts with Liquidity price slippage when yesUsed > maxYesUsed or noUsed > maxNoUsed . minLiquidity alone does not bound the price: a swap toward even odds before the call raises both the deposit of the side with the smaller reserve and the minted LP. removeLiquidityWithPermit(pair, liquidity, minYesOut, minNoOut, recipient, deadline, v, r, s) calls the pair's permit for the caller as owner, the router as spender, exactly liquidity as value, and the same deadline ; the signature must cover those values. A failing permit is ignored when the router's allowance already covers liquidity ; otherwise the call reverts with LP permit or allowance . The router then pulls the LP and returns raw Yes/No directly to the recipient. enterPositionWithCollateral(pair, cash, outcome, minimum, recipient, deadline, budget) , createPairAndInitializeWithCollateral(pool, cash, probability, maxYes, maxNo, minYes, minNo, minimum, recipient, deadline, budget) , initializeWithCollateral(pair, cash, probability, maxYes, maxNo, minYes, minNo, minimum, recipient, deadline, budget) and addLiquidityWithCollateral(pair, cash, maxYes, maxNo, minimum, recipient, deadline, budget) provide the same operations for ERC-20 collateral. cash is the mint amount in raw collateral units. Approve the router for the fee-inclusive budget ; the router pulls only the mint amount plus its purchase fee; unused allowance stays with the caller. These nonpayable entrypoints require a positive budget and cannot be nested ( Invalid token budget ). factory() exposes the immutable factory. onERC1155Received , onERC1155BatchReceived , and supportsInterface exist only for the receive-based operations described below and for the pair callbacks inside an operation. The payable entrypoints require a positive mint amount ( Mint amount is zero ) and a fee-inclusive budget sufficient for that mint ( Purchase fee exceeds budget ). They require WETH collateral ( Use collateral token entry ). Every entrypoint requires a recipient that is neither zero nor the router ( Invalid recipient ) and block.timestamp ≤ deadline ( Deadline expired ); those that take a pair require it to be the factory's canonical pair for its question ledger ( Unrecognized pair , Noncanonical pair ). The three liquidity entrypoints that take ETH return LiquidityResult : pair , completeSetShares , yesUsed , noUsed , yesReturned , noReturned , invalidInsurance , and liquidity . Every successful share-custody operation, including the receive-based operations below, asserts that the router's Invalid, Yes, and No balances equal their starting values, preserving pre-existing forced shares.","title":"Trading router","topic":"Trading","weight":0},{"fragment":"approval-free","heading":"Exits and complete-set redemption by share transfer","keywords":[],"path":"reference/trading/router.html","sectionTitle":"Reference","summary":"Router entrypoints, custody guarantees, and approval-free share operations.","text":"To exit or redeem, call the ShareToken ’s safeBatchTransferFrom as caller and owner, with the router as recipient and an encoded request in data . The callback requires operator == from for top-level transfers. It authenticates the ledger through the factory and checks the actual token and IDs against the canonical ledger. No setApprovalForAll is required. encodeReceiveRequest produces the 12-word ABI payload in this order: version, operation, questionLedger, pair, longOutcome, completeSetShares, maxLongSharesIn, minEthOut, payoutRecipient, refundRecipient, deadline, minRepOut Use CALLBACK_VERSION (3) and operation 0 for ExitPosition or 1 for RedeemCompleteSet . Insured exit . The transfer carries exactly two IDs in the order [invalidTokenId, long token ID] with the values [q, maxLongSharesIn] , where q is completeSetShares , and longOutcome is Yes (1) or No (2). The router buys exactly q opposite shares with an exact-output swap, redeems q complete sets at the question ledger, and returns any unused long shares to the bound refund recipient. It emits PositionExitedByTransfer . Complete-set redemption. The transfer carries exactly three IDs in the order [invalidTokenId, yesTokenId, noTokenId] , each with the value completeSetShares ; longOutcome must be None (3) and maxLongSharesIn zero. The router redeems them at the exact canonical question ledger and emits CompleteSetRedeemedByTransfer . Both operations require completeSetShares > 0 , a payoutRecipient and a refundRecipient that are neither zero nor the router, and canonical question ledger, share-token, universe, question and outcome IDs. An insured exit requires the factory's canonical pair. Complete-set redemption permits a zero pair ; a supplied nonzero pair must still be canonical. A child with no trading pair can therefore redeem directly through this transfer route. Both operations measure the collateral and native REP produced by redemption and forward those deltas to the payout recipient. The collateral output must meet minEthOut in raw collateral units, and the REP output must meet minRepOut in attoREP. At least one asset must be positive. For a REP-only basket, set the collateral minimum to zero and set the REP minimum to the smallest payout you accept. Wrapped ETH collateral is unwrapped before payout. For LP removal through the router, Safes and integrations without signature support may use ordinary exact allowances instead of a permit.","title":"Trading router","topic":"Trading","weight":0},{"fragment":"","heading":"","keywords":["Trading","events","Swap","indexer"],"path":"reference/trading/events.html","sectionTitle":"Reference","summary":"Factory, pair, and router events and indexer attribution rules.","text":"Reference The factory logs pair creation, and each pair logs reserve updates from its own operations and changes in LP ownership. Parameters prefixed with indexed are log topics.","title":"Trading events","topic":"Trading","weight":1},{"fragment":"factory","heading":"Factory","keywords":[],"path":"reference/trading/events.html","sectionTitle":"Reference","summary":"Factory, pair, and router events and indexer attribution rules.","text":"PairCreated(indexed questionLedger, indexed shareToken, indexed universeId, pair, feeBps) identifies one exact branch market and its immutable fee.","title":"Trading events","topic":"Trading","weight":0},{"fragment":"pair","heading":"Pair","keywords":[],"path":"reference/trading/events.html","sectionTitle":"Reference","summary":"Factory, pair, and router events and indexer attribution rules.","text":"LiquidityInitialized(indexed provider, indexed recipient, yesAmount, noAmount, liquidity) records the first reserve contribution and circulating LP minted; locked minimum liquidity is separate. LiquidityAdded(indexed provider, indexed recipient, yesAmount, noAmount, liquidity) records proportional reserve use. LiquidityRemoved(indexed provider, indexed recipient, yesAmount, noAmount, liquidity) records raw-share output. Swap(indexed sender, indexed recipient, yesForNo, exactOutput, amountIn, amountOut, feeAmount, resultingYesReserve, resultingNoReserve) provides authoritative direction, mode, amounts, fee, and resulting state. Sync(yesReserve, noReserve) records current Yes and No balances after a pair mutation or a call to sync() . Direct share transfers can change the live reserves between Sync events. To reconstruct those changes, apply the canonical Yes and No token amounts from the share token’s TransferSingle and TransferBatch events involving the pair. PredeploymentSharesQuarantined(invalidAmount, yesAmount, noAmount) records canonical shares removed from a counterfactual pair address during construction. The destination is the factory's immutable predeploymentShareSink . Standard ERC-20 Transfer and Approval events describe LP ownership and allowances.","title":"Trading events","topic":"Trading","weight":0},{"fragment":"router","heading":"Router","keywords":[],"path":"reference/trading/events.html","sectionTitle":"Reference","summary":"Factory, pair, and router events and indexer attribution rules.","text":"PositionExitedByTransfer(indexed owner, indexed pair, completeSetShares, longSharesUsed, longSharesRefunded, payoutRecipient) records an insured exit initiated by owner 's share transfer. CompleteSetRedeemedByTransfer(indexed questionLedger, completeSetShares, payoutRecipient) records a complete-set redemption initiated by share transfer.","title":"Trading events","topic":"Trading","weight":0},{"fragment":"attribution","heading":"Attributing router operations","keywords":[],"path":"reference/trading/events.html","sectionTitle":"Reference","summary":"Factory, pair, and router events and indexer attribution rules.","text":"Immediate-caller fields identify contracts: pair provider / sender and ledger redeemer name the router, while ledger creator names SecurityPoolServices . The router is the recipient in CompleteSetsFunded and in the pair’s entry and exit swaps. Pair recipient names the explicit asset recipient for direct pair calls and router liquidity operations. Insured exit. Use PositionExitedByTransfer.owner . Complete-set redemption. Use the ShareToken TransferBatch log’s from field. CompleteSetRedeemedByTransfer carries the ledger and payout recipient. Entry and liquidity. Combine the transaction sender, router method, pair and core logs, and recipient from calldata or TransferBatch . Router return tuples are available from authoritative call simulation only; they are not stored in a transaction receipt. Complete-set and ETH-flow evidence comes from the question ledger's CompleteSetCreated and CompleteSetRedeemed events plus transaction logs.","title":"Trading events","topic":"Trading","weight":0},{"fragment":"","heading":"","keywords":["Trading","SDK","quoteExactOutput","maximumInsuredExit","encodeReceiveRequest"],"path":"reference/trading/sdk.html","sectionTitle":"Reference","summary":"Shared bigint math, exit search, receive-request encoding, and simulation-pinning helpers.","text":"Reference The private workspace package @zoltar/trading-shared holds the pure Trading math that the UI and the Solidity parity tests share. All chain quantities are bigint .","title":"Trading SDK","topic":"Trading","weight":1},{"fragment":"pure-math","heading":"Pure math","keywords":[],"path":"reference/trading/sdk.html","sectionTitle":"Reference","summary":"Shared bigint math, exit search, receive-request encoding, and simulation-pinning helpers.","text":"Module @zoltar/trading-shared/trading/math mirrors the quote and price functions of TwoWayConstantProductMath . It has no counterpart of the library's initial-odds and proportional-deposit functions; the UI estimates those in ui/trading/ts/features/live/liquidityEstimate.ts . quoteExactInput(reserveIn, reserveOut, amountIn, feeBps) and quoteExactOutput(reserveIn, reserveOut, amountOut, feeBps) return a SwapQuote , { amountIn, amountOut, netInput, feeAmount } , with the contract's floor (exact input) and ceil (exact output) rounding. They throw on empty reserves, an out-of-range output, or feeBps ≥ 10,000 . conditionalYesBps(yesReserve, noReserve) returns the Conditional Yes price in basis points, floor-rounded.","title":"Trading SDK","topic":"Trading","weight":0},{"fragment":"positions","heading":"Position quotes","keywords":[],"path":"reference/trading/sdk.html","sectionTitle":"Reference","summary":"Shared bigint math, exit search, receive-request encoding, and simulation-pinning helpers.","text":"Module @zoltar/trading-shared/trading/positions takes { yesReserve, noReserve, feeBps } and a longOutcome of 'YES' or 'NO' : quoteEnterPosition(longOutcome, completeSetShares, reserves) mirrors enterPosition after minting and returns an EnterPositionQuote : longOutcome , completeSetShares , oppositeSharesSwapped , additionalLongShares , totalLongShares , invalidInsurance , feeAmount , conditionalYesBpsBefore , and conditionalYesBpsAfter . quoteExitPosition(longOutcome, completeSetShares, reserves) mirrors the insured exit and returns an ExitPositionQuote : longOutcome , completeSetShares , longSharesSwapped , totalLongShares , invalidRequired , feeAmount , conditionalYesBpsBefore , and conditionalYesBpsAfter . largestExitForLongShares({ ...reserves, longOutcome, longShares, completeSetCap? }) binary-searches the largest complete-set amount whose total long-share cost fits within longShares and stays below the opposite reserve. The optional completeSetCap bounds the result further. maximumInsuredExit({ ...reserves, longOutcome, longBalance, invalidBalance }) calls largestExitForLongShares with the long balance as longShares and the Invalid balance as completeSetCap .","title":"Trading SDK","topic":"Trading","weight":0},{"fragment":"receive-request","heading":"Receive request encoding","keywords":[],"path":"reference/trading/sdk.html","sectionTitle":"Reference","summary":"Shared bigint math, exit search, receive-request encoding, and simulation-pinning helpers.","text":"Module @zoltar/trading-shared/trading/receiveRequest exports encodeReceiveRequest(request) , which ABI-encodes the data argument for a share transfer to the router. The request, typed ReceiveRequest , is a positional tuple in the field order of the router's ReceiveRequest struct, which the router reference lists.","title":"Trading SDK","topic":"Trading","weight":0},{"fragment":"transaction-helpers","heading":"Transaction helpers","keywords":[],"path":"reference/trading/sdk.html","sectionTitle":"Reference","summary":"Shared bigint math, exit search, receive-request encoding, and simulation-pinning helpers.","text":"Transaction-facing helpers live in the Trading UI at ui/trading/ts/protocol/tradeQuote.ts , not in the shared package: minimumAfterSlippage(amount, slippageBps?) floors and maximumAfterSlippage(amount, slippageBps?) ceils a bound. requireTransactionSlippageBps checks the configured slippage range . stableSimulation(client, simulate) reads the latest block with latestBlockIdentity(client) and passes its blockNumber , blockHash , and blockTimestamp to simulate , whose reads must all use that block number. A reorg during those reads is not detected. simulateWithDeadline(client, expiry, simulate, resolveDeadline?) does the same and also resolves expiry , a TransactionExpiry that is either an absolute deadline or { validityMinutes } checked by requireTransactionValidityMinutes , against the pinned block's timestamp. The optional resolveDeadline(block, requestedDeadline) can replace that deadline, and simulate receives (block, deadline) . retainApprovedMinimum(approved, refreshed, label) and retainApprovedMaximum(approved, refreshed, label) return the approved bound, or throw when the submission-time simulation no longer satisfies it. Before wallet submission, the client resimulates at the latest block and requires the approved bounds. Trades retain the approved deadline. Liquidity and settlement derive a fresh deadline from the latest block and the selected validity period.","title":"Trading SDK","topic":"Trading","weight":0},{"fragment":"","heading":"","keywords":["Trading","configuration","manifest","environment"],"path":"reference/trading/configuration.html","sectionTitle":"Reference","summary":"Deployment script environment variables, the local manifest, and what the client derives and stores.","text":"Reference The local deployment script solidity/ts/trading/deploy/deploy-local.mts reads the environment variables below. No public trading deployment manifest is bundled; the client derives and checks its contracts at startup.","title":"Trading deployment configuration","topic":"Trading","weight":1},{"fragment":"environment","heading":"Environment variables","keywords":[],"path":"reference/trading/configuration.html","sectionTitle":"Reference","summary":"Deployment script environment variables, the local manifest, and what the client derives and stores.","text":"Name Meaning Default TRADING_RPC_URL JSON-RPC endpoint http://127.0.0.1:8545 TRADING_DEPLOYER Unlocked RPC account First eth_accounts entry ZOLTAR_DEPLOYMENT_MANIFEST Existing matching core manifest Required TRADING_FEE_BPS Immutable trading fee 30 TRADING_FEE_BPS must be a whole number from 0 to 9999; only an unset variable selects the default, and an empty value stops the script. The script stops when the RPC chain ID differs from the core manifest's, when the manifest's SecurityPoolFactory has no code on that chain, or when the RPC exposes no account. Manifests must not contain secrets.","title":"Trading deployment configuration","topic":"Trading","weight":0},{"fragment":"manifest","heading":"Deployment manifest","keywords":[],"path":"reference/trading/configuration.html","sectionTitle":"Reference","summary":"Deployment script environment variables, the local manifest, and what the client derives and stores.","text":"bun run trading:deploy:local writes solidity/deployments/trading/local.json for scripts and integration environments. The file is git-ignored because local addresses are ephemeral. A generated manifest contains: network : decimal and hexadecimal chain IDs plus the deployment RPC; core : the SecurityPoolFactory address, checked to have code on the RPC chain, and the source manifest path; trading : factory, router, and immutable feeBps ; transactions : factory and router objects shaped as { hash, blockNumber: \"<base-10 integer string>\" } . blockNumber is a JSON string, not a JavaScript number, so large receipt block numbers remain exact; compilerProfiles : exact versions and settings from the main Solidity artifact; bytecodeHashes : creation-bytecode commitments; deployer and deployedAt . Discover and verify pairs through the Trading factory . Review a manifest before publishing it and keep local addresses out of public-network files.","title":"Trading deployment configuration","topic":"Trading","weight":0},{"fragment":"client","heading":"What the client derives","keywords":[],"path":"reference/trading/configuration.html","sectionTitle":"Reference","summary":"Deployment script environment variables, the local manifest, and what the client derives and stores.","text":"The standalone client reads no environment variables. At startup it uses: Networks: the build writes core-deployments.json from the Yes and No deployment manifests for mainnet and Sepolia in docs/ , with each network's default public RPC URL. Mainnet is listed but disabled in the UI build, and the default network is Sepolia. RPC override: Settings beside the wallet selects another supported network or accepts an optional HTTPS or loopback HTTP RPC URL. URL parameters: genesis ( yes or no ) selects the genesis deployment; an absent or invalid choice opens the fork-question picker before startup. network ( sepolia ) pins the network instead of following the wallet, rpcUrl overrides the saved RPC URL, universe selects a universe within the selected genesis deployment, and simulate=1 with an optional simScenario selects the simulator . Trading contracts: the factory and router addresses are computed with the fixed 30 basis-point fee through the core deployment's canonical CREATE2 proxy, and each address is checked for code. If either contract is missing, the connected wallet can deploy it. Markets: the client lists registered question ledgers with Trading pairs in the selected universe. When opening a ledger address it checks the ledger against the canonical SecurityPoolFactory , Zoltar and ShareToken. Mint capacity follows capital health . Trade settings: slippage tolerance (1 to 500 basis points, default 50) and transaction validity (1 to 1,440 minutes, default 20) are kept in browser local storage under zoltar.trading.tradeSettings . Price impact: the trade ticket cautions above 2%, requires an explicit confirmation above 5%, and refuses the trade above 15%. Quote and simulation behavior is described in the Trading SDK .","title":"Trading deployment configuration","topic":"Trading","weight":0},{"fragment":"","heading":"","keywords":["Trading","invariants","custody","k"],"path":"reference/trading/invariants.html","sectionTitle":"Reference","summary":"Custody, swap, and lifecycle conditions at every state transition.","text":"Reference These conditions are required at every successful external state transition. Reverting calls leave the prior state unchanged. Protocol-wide invariants are catalogued separately in Protocol invariants .","title":"Trading invariants","topic":"Trading","weight":1},{"fragment":"pair-custody","heading":"Pair custody","keywords":[],"path":"reference/trading/invariants.html","sectionTitle":"Reference","summary":"Custody, swap, and lifecycle conditions at every state transition.","text":"Yes and No reserves are the current ShareToken balances. Donations immediately increase reserves and accrue to existing LPs. The pair's Invalid balance is exactly zero. Transferring LP tokens moves the liquidity claim between holders and preserves total LP supply and pair reserves. An LP withdrawal cannot claim more Yes or No shares than the pair owns. Withdrawal amounts are proportional to current balances and the liquidity burned.","title":"Trading invariants","topic":"Trading","weight":0},{"fragment":"router-custody","heading":"Router custody","keywords":[],"path":"reference/trading/invariants.html","sectionTitle":"Reference","summary":"Custody, swap, and lifecycle conditions at every state transition.","text":"Every successful router operation ends with the same Invalid, Yes, and No balances with which that operation began. This deliberately preserves any pre-existing balances while preventing the operation from accumulating new share residue.","title":"Trading invariants","topic":"Trading","weight":0},{"fragment":"swaps","heading":"Swaps","keywords":[],"path":"reference/trading/invariants.html","sectionTitle":"Reference","summary":"Custody, swap, and lifecycle conditions at every state transition.","text":"For every successful exact-input or exact-output swap, k_after ≥ k_before , where k is the Yes balance multiplied by the No balance. For unchanged reserves, an exact-input swap delivers the quoted output and reverts when the requested minimum exceeds that quote. An exact-output swap delivers exactly the requested output amount. An exact-output swap never charges more than the caller's maxAmountIn . The product comparison starts from effective reserves so a donation cannot be mistaken for swap-created growth.","title":"Trading invariants","topic":"Trading","weight":0},{"fragment":"lifecycle","heading":"Lifecycle","keywords":[],"path":"reference/trading/invariants.html","sectionTitle":"Reference","summary":"Custody, swap, and lifecycle conditions at every state transition.","text":"Once the question has ended, the question has resolved, or the universe has forked, initialization, liquidity addition, and both swap forms revert permanently. While fork continuation is pending or the question ledger is not operational, the same operations also revert for as long as that state lasts. Proportional liquidity removal has no lifecycle guard.","title":"Trading invariants","topic":"Trading","weight":0},{"fragment":"swap-and-price-arithmetic","heading":"Swap and price arithmetic","keywords":[],"path":"reference/trading/invariants.html","sectionTitle":"Reference","summary":"Custody, swap, and lifecycle conditions at every state transition.","text":"Comparison conditions Use effective reserves and a fixed fee. Sequential swap comparisons assume no intervening trades, liquidity changes or donations. Least sufficient input The exact-output input quote is the least gross input whose exact-input quote meets or exceeds the target. One raw unit less yields less than the target or rounds below the minimum valid output. For reserves 1 and 4 with zero fees, a target of 1 costs 1, and that input buys 2 under exact-input rules. tradingArithmetic.test.ts . Splitting a trade Splitting a same-direction exact-input trade into two successful swaps cannot increase total output relative to a single trade with the same gross input. Each leg uses the updated reserves, including fees retained on the first leg. tradingArithmetic.test.ts . Immediate reverse trade Sending all output from a successful exact-input swap straight back through the same pair yields at most the original input. Equality can occur in fee-free, exactly divisible examples. auditTradingMathFuzz.test.ts . Directional price symmetry For the two directional spot prices rounded down in basis points, their sum is either 9,999 or 10,000. It is exactly 10,000 iff the unrounded basis-point price is integral. Multiplying both reserves by the same positive integer leaves both displayed prices unchanged if the scaled reserves fit. tradingArithmetic.test.ts . Initial price rounding On a successful initializer quote, reflecting the requested probability around 5,000 basis points exchanges the Yes and No amounts exactly. The resulting displayed Yes price rounds to the requested side of 50%, at or below the request for a request below 50%, and at or above it for a request above 50%. tradingArithmetic.test.ts .","title":"Trading invariants","topic":"Trading","weight":0},{"fragment":"liquidity-and-position-accounting","heading":"Liquidity and position accounting","keywords":[],"path":"reference/trading/invariants.html","sectionTitle":"Reference","summary":"Custody, swap, and lifecycle conditions at every state transition.","text":"Backing per LP token Let Y,N be effective reserves and S outstanding LP tokens. Across successful swaps, liquidity additions, removals and ordinary donations to an initialized pair, Y_after × N_after × S_before² ≥ Y_before × N_before × S_after² . For a liquidity addition or removal, each reserve per outstanding LP token is individually nondecreasing. auditTradingMathFuzz.test.ts . Permanently locked liquidity Once a pair initializes, at least 1,000 LP units remain at the lock address under the protocol’s ordinary transfer assumptions. Returning all circulating liquidity cannot reset initialization or permit a second initial-price selection. Additional LP tokens sent to the lock can increase its balance. pairRouter.integration.test.ts . Directional entry A successful router entry that mints m complete sets sends the recipient m Invalid insurance shares and m + swapOut shares of the chosen direction. It sells exactly m opposite shares. Here m is the observed mint, which can differ from the cash amount. realCore.integration.test.ts . Directional exit To exit m complete sets by transfer, the router consumes m Invalid shares and exactly m + quoteExactOutput(m) long shares at the unchanged pre-swap reserves. Supplied long shares above that amount go to the designated refund recipient. Minimum cash and REP outputs apply independently to what redemption actually produces. realCore.integration.test.ts and pairRouter.integration.test.ts . Predeployment shares and initial supply Canonical Invalid, Yes and No shares sent to the predicted pair before deployment move to the share sink during construction. They buy no LP supply. At initialization the scale is exactly the smaller of the two supplied reserves. The designated LP recipient receives that scale less 1,000 locked LP units. pairRouter.integration.test.ts .","title":"Trading invariants","topic":"Trading","weight":0},{"fragment":"test-evidence","heading":"Executable evidence","keywords":[],"path":"reference/trading/invariants.html","sectionTitle":"Reference","summary":"Custody, swap, and lifecycle conditions at every state transition.","text":"Pair and router Stateful swaps compare effective reserve products, outcome balances and router residue. Separate cases check exact-output bounds, proportional removal, counterfactual donations, temporary inactivity and permanent closure. pairRouter.integration.test.ts . Arithmetic and collateral Integer-model checks exercise trading math across generated inputs. Router collateral checks verify that calls leave no wrapped collateral or services allowance. auditTradingMathFuzz.test.ts , audit4TradingRouterCollateralResidue.test.ts .","title":"Trading invariants","topic":"Trading","weight":0},{"fragment":"","heading":"","keywords":["Trading","attoShares","rounding","token ID"],"path":"reference/trading/units-and-rounding.html","sectionTitle":"Reference","summary":"Atomic units, token ID layout, and rounding directions.","text":"Reference Every Trading quantity is an integer in an atomic unit, and swap rounding always favors existing liquidity providers.","title":"Trading units and rounding","topic":"Trading","weight":1},{"fragment":"units","heading":"Units","keywords":[],"path":"reference/trading/units-and-rounding.html","sectionTitle":"Reference","summary":"Atomic units, token ID layout, and rounding directions.","text":"Collateral amounts use the security pool collateral token's atomic units: 10 6 units per USDC and 10 18 units per WETH. Native ETH amounts use attoETH (10 −18 ETH). Outcome shares use the collateral token's display precision. At an empty question ledger, one collateral atomic unit creates one atomic share of every outcome. Protocol holding fees make the later collateral-to-share ratio dynamic, so clients must use the current mutation result rather than assuming this initial rate. The Trading app reads and displays LP quantities in the collateral’s share units, such as six decimal places for USDC. Contract calls and balances use raw LP units. The ERC-20 metadata still reports 18 decimals. Fees, slippage, initial conditional price, and displayed conditional price use basis points over 10,000 where applicable. Deadlines and question end times are Unix seconds. Universe IDs are uint248 . A token ID is (universeId << 8) | outcome , with local outcomes Invalid = 0, Yes = 1, No = 2.","title":"Trading units and rounding","topic":"Trading","weight":0},{"fragment":"rounding","heading":"Rounding","keywords":[],"path":"reference/trading/units-and-rounding.html","sectionTitle":"Reference","summary":"Atomic units, token ID layout, and rounding directions.","text":"Exact input computes net = ⌊gross × (10,000 − fee) / 10,000⌋ and out = ⌊reserveOut × net / (reserveIn + net)⌋ . Exact output computes net = ⌈reserveIn × out / (reserveOut − out)⌉ and gross = ⌈net × 10,000 / (10,000 − fee)⌉ . Liquidity deposits round reserve use down, and the LP minted is the smaller of ⌊yesUsed × supply / yesReserve⌋ and ⌊noUsed × supply / noReserve⌋ . Removal rounds each reserve output down. Initial alternative odds round the smaller deposited reserve down. Conditional Yes is NO / (YES + NO) and Conditional No is YES / (YES + NO) ; in basis points, Conditional Yes is ⌊10,000 × NO / (YES + NO)⌋ . Client slippage bounds round a minimum down and a maximum up.","title":"Trading units and rounding","topic":"Trading","weight":0}]
